(define chez-docs-data (quote ((csug ("binding:s15" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "internal-defines-as-letrec*") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to " (code "#t") " (the default), internal variable definitions are evaluated using " (code "letrec*") " semantics. It may be set to " (code "#f") " to revert to the " (code "letrec") " semantics for internal variable definitions, for backward compatibility.")) ("binding:s16" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-values " (var "formals") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "define-values") " form is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear. It is like a " (code "define") " form but permits an arbitrary formals list (like " (code "lambda") ") on the left-hand side. It evaluates " (code (var "expr")) " and binds the variables appearing in " (code (var "formals")) "  to the resulting values, in the same manner as the formal parameters of a procedure are bound to its arguments.") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (define-values (x y) (values 1 2))" (br) "  (list x y)) " (math "=>") " (1 2)" (br) "(let ()" (br) "  (define-values (x y . z) (values 1 2 3 4))" (br) "  (list x y z)) " (math "=>") " (1 2 (3 4))")) ("A " (code "define-values") " form expands into a sequence of definitions, the first for a hidden temporary bound to a data structure holding the values returned by " (code (var "expr")) " and the remainder binding each of the formals to the corresponding value or list of values, extracted from the data structure via a reference to the temporary. Because the temporary must be defined before the other variables are defined, this works for internal " (code "define-values") " forms only if " (code "internal-defines-as-letrec*") " is set to the default value " (code "#t") ".")) ("binding:s17" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(rec " (var "var") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "value of " (code (var "expr")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The syntactic form " (code "rec") " creates a recursive object from " (code (var "expr")) " by establishing a binding of " (code (var "var")) " within " (code (var "expr")) " to the value of " (code (var "expr")) ". In essence, it is a special case of " (code "letrec") " for self-recursive objects.") ("This form is useful for creating recursive objects (especially procedures) that do not depend on external variables for the recursion, which are sometimes undesirable because the external bindings can change. For example, a recursive procedure defined at top level depends on the value of the top-level variable given as its name. If the value of this variable should change, the meaning of the procedure itself would change. If the procedure is defined instead with " (code "rec") ", its meaning is independent of the variable to which it is bound.") ((code "(map (rec sum" (br) "       (lambda (x)" (br) "         (if (= x 0)" (br) "             0" (br) "             (+ x (sum (- x 1))))))" (br) "     '(0 1 2 3 4 5)) " (math "=>") " (0 1 3 6 10 15)" (br) (br) "(define cycle" (br) "  (rec self" (br) "    (list (lambda () self))))" (br) (br) "(eq? ((car cycle)) cycle) " (math "=>") " #t")) ("The definition below expands " (code "rec") " in terms of " (code "letrec") ".") ((code "(define-syntax rec" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ x e) (letrec ((x e)) x)]))"))) ("binding:s19" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(fluid-let ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The syntactic form " (code "fluid-let") "  provides a way to temporarily assign values to a set of variables. The new values are in effect only during the evaluation of the body of the " (code "fluid-let") " expression. The scopes of the variables are not determined by " (code "fluid-let") "; as with " (code "set!") ", the variables must be bound at top level or by an enclosing " (code "lambda") " or other binding form. It is possible, therefore, to control the scope of a variable with " (code "lambda") " or " (code "let") " while establishing a temporary value with " (code "fluid-let") ".") ("Although it is similar in appearance to " (code "let") ", its operation is more like that of " (code "set!") ". Each " (code (var "var")) " is assigned, as with " (code "set!") ", to the value of the corresponding " (code (var "expr")) " within the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") ". Should the body exit normally or by invoking a continuation made outside of the body (see " (code "call/cc") "), the values in effect before the bindings were changed are restored. Should control return back to the body by the invocation of a continuation created within the body, the bindings are changed once again to the values in effect when the body last exited.") ("Fluid bindings are most useful for maintaining variables that must be shared by a group of procedures. Upon entry to the group of procedures, the shared variables are fluidly bound to a new set of initial values so that on exit the original values are restored automatically. In this way, the group of procedures itself can be reentrant; it may call itself directly or indirectly without affecting the values of its shared variables.") ("Fluid bindings are similar to " (var "special") " bindings in Common Lisp [" (link "./bibliography.html#g190" "30") "], except that (1) there is a single namespace for both lexical and fluid bindings, and (2) the scope of a fluidly bound variable is not necessarily global.") ((code "(let ([x 3])" (br) "  (+ (fluid-let ([x 5])" (br) "       x)" (br) "     x)) " (math "=>") " 8" (br) (br) "(let ([x 'a])" (br) "  (letrec ([f (lambda (y) (cons x y))])" (br) "    (fluid-let ([x 'b])" (br) "      (f 'c)))) " (math "=>") " (b . c)" (br) (br) "(let ([x 'a])" (br) "  (call/cc" (br) "    (lambda (k)" (br) "       (fluid-let ([x 'b])" (br) "         (letrec ([f (lambda (y) (k '*))])" (br) "           (f '*)))))" (br) "  x) " (math "=>") " a")) ((code "fluid-let") " may be defined in terms of " (code "dynamic-wind") " as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax fluid-let" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(_ () b1 b2 ...) #'(let () b1 b2 ...)]" (br) "      [(_ ((x e) ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (andmap identifier? #'(x ...))" (br) "       (with-syntax ([(y ...) (generate-temporaries #'(x ...))])" (br) "         #'(let ([y e] ...)" (br) "             (let ([swap (lambda ()" (br) "                           (let ([t x]) (set! x y) (set! y t))" (br) "                           ...)])" (br) "               (dynamic-wind swap (lambda () b1 b2 ...) swap))))])))"))) ("binding:s24" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(define-top-level-value " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(define-top-level-value " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "define-top-level-value") " is used to establish a binding for the variable named by " (code (var "symbol")) " to the value " (code (var "obj")) "  in the environment " (code (var "env")) ". If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if " (code (var "env")) " is not mutable.") ("A call to " (code "define-top-level-value") " is similar to a top-level " (code "define") " form, except that a call to " (code "define-top-level-value") " need not occur at top-level and the variable for which the binding is to be established can be determined at run time, as can the environment.") ((code "(begin" (br) "  (define-top-level-value 'xyz \"hi\")" (br) "  xyz) " (math "=>") " \"hi\"" (br) (br) "(let ([var 'xyz])" (br) "  (define-top-level-value var \"mom\")" (br) "  (list var xyz)) " (math "=>") " (xyz \"mom\")"))) ("binding:s26" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-top-level-value! " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-top-level-value! " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "set-top-level-value!") " assigns  the variable named by " (code (var "symbol")) " to the value " (code (var "obj")) "  in the environment " (code (var "env")) ". If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the identifier named by " (code (var "symbol")) " is not defined as a variable in " (code (var "env")) "  or if the variable or environment is not mutable.") ((code "set-top-level-value!") " is similar to " (code "set!") " when " (code "set!") "  is used on top-level variables except that the variable to be assigned can be determined at run time, as can the environment.") ((code "(let ([v (let ([cons list])" (br) "           (set-top-level-value! 'cons +)" (br) "           (cons 3 4))])" (br) "  (list v (cons 3 4))) " (math "=>") " ((3 4) 7)"))) ("binding:s29" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-value " (var "symbol") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-value " (var "symbol") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the top-level value of the variable named by " (code (var "symbol")) " in " (code (var "env")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the identifier named by " (code (var "symbol")) " is not defined as a variable in " (code (var "env")) ".") ((code "top-level-value") " is similar to a top-level variable reference except that the variable to be referenced can be determined at run time, as can the environment.") ((code "(let ([cons +])" (br) "  (list (cons 3 4)" (br) "        ((top-level-value 'cons) 3 4))) " (math "=>") " (7 (3 . 4))" (br) (br) "(define e (copy-environment (scheme-environment)))" (br) "(define-top-level-value 'pi 3.14 e)" (br) "(top-level-value 'pi e) " (math "=>") " 3.14" (br) "(set-top-level-value! 'pi 3.1416 e)" (br) "(top-level-value 'pi e) " (math "=>") " 3.1416"))) ("binding:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-bound? " (var "symbol") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-bound? " (var "symbol") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "symbol")) " is defined as a variable in " (code (var "env")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("This predicate is useful in an interpreter to check for the existence of a top-level binding before requesting the value with " (code "top-level-value") ".") ((code "(top-level-bound? 'xyz) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(begin" (br) "  (define-top-level-value 'xyz 3)" (br) "  (top-level-bound? 'xyz)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(define e (copy-environment (interaction-environment)))" (br) "(define-top-level-value 'pi 3.14 e)" (br) "(top-level-bound? 'pi) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(top-level-bound? 'pi e) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("binding:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-mutable? " (var "symbol") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-mutable? " (var "symbol") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "symbol")) " is mutable in " (code (var "env")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("This predicate is useful in an interpreter to check whether a variable can be assigned before assigning it with " (code "set-top-level-value!") ".") ((code "(define xyz 3)" (br) "(top-level-mutable? 'xyz) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(set-top-level-value! 'xyz 4)" (br) "(top-level-value 'xyz) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) (br) "(define e (copy-environment (interaction-environment) #f))" (br) "(top-level-mutable? 'xyz e) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(set-top-level-value! 'xyz e) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: xyz is immutable")))) ("binding:s32" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(define-top-level-syntax " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(define-top-level-syntax " (var "symbol") " " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "define-top-level-syntax") " is used to establish a top-level binding for the identifier named by " (code (var "symbol")) " to the value of " (code (var "obj")) "  in the environment " (code (var "env")) ". The value must be a procedure, the result of a call to " (code "make-variable-transformer") ", or the result of a call to " (code "top-level-syntax") ". If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if " (code (var "env")) " is not mutable.") ("A call to " (code "define-top-level-syntax") " is similar to a top-level " (code "define-syntax") " form, except that a call to " (code "define-top-level-syntax") " need not occur at top-level and the identifier for which the binding is to be established can be determined at run time, as can the environment.") ((code "(define-top-level-syntax 'let1" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ x e b1 b2 ...) (let ([x e]) b1 b2 ...)]))" (br) "(let1 a 3 (+ a 1)) " (math "=>") " 4")) ((code "define-top-level-syntax") " can also be used to attach to an identifier arbitrary compile-time bindings obtained  via " (code "top-level-syntax") ".")) ("binding:s34" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-syntax " (var "symbol") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-syntax " (var "symbol") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "top-level-syntax") " is used to retrieve the transformer, compile-time value, or other compile-time binding to which the identifier named by " (code (var "symbol")) " is bound in the environment " (code (var "env")) ". If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") "). All identifiers bound in an environment have compile-time bindings, including variables.") ("An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the identifier named by " (code (var "symbol")) " is not defined as a keyword in " (code (var "env")) ".") ((code "(define-top-level-syntax 'also-let (top-level-syntax 'let))" (br) "(also-let ([x 3] [y 4]) (+ x y)) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) (br) "(define foo 17)" (br) "(define-top-level-syntax 'also-foo (top-level-syntax 'foo))" (br) "also-foo " (math "=>") " 17" (br) "(set! also-foo 23)" (br) "also-foo " (math "=>") " 23" (br) "foo " (math "=>") " 23")) ("The effect of the last example can be had more clearly with " (code "alias") ":") ((code "(define foo 17)" (br) "(alias also-foo foo)" (br) "also-foo " (math "=>") " 17" (br) "(set! also-foo 23)" (br) "also-foo " (math "=>") " 23" (br) "foo " (math "=>") " 23"))) ("binding:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-syntax? " (var "symbol") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(top-level-syntax? " (var "symbol") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "symbol")) " is bound as a keyword in " (code (var "env")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "env")) " is not provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ", i.e., the top-level evaluation environment (Section " (link "./system.html#g119" "12.3") ").") ("All identifiers bound in an environment have compile-time bindings, including variables, so this predicate amounts to a bound check, but is more general than " (code "top-level-bound?") ", which returns true only for bound variables.") ((code "(define xyz 'hello)" (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'cons) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'lambda) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'hello) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'cons (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'lambda (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(top-level-syntax? 'hello (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("compat:s0" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-hash-table)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-hash-table " (var "weak?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new hash table" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "weak?")) " is provided and is non-false, the hash table is a weak hash table, which means that it does not protect keys from the garbage collector. Keys reclaimed by the garbage collector are removed from the table, and their associated values are dropped the next time the table is modified, if not sooner.")) ("compat:s1" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(hash-table? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a hash table, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("compat:s2" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-hash-table! " (var "ht") " " (var "k") " " (var "v") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "ht")) " must be a hash table. " (code (var "k")) " and " (code (var "v")) " may be any Scheme values.") ((code "put-hash-table!") " associates the value " (code (var "v")) " with the key " (code (var "k")) " in " (code (var "ht")) ".")) ("compat:s3" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-hash-table " (var "ht") " " (var "k") " " (var "d") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "get-hash-table") " returns the value associated with " (code (var "k")) " in " (code (var "ht")) ". If no value is associated with " (code (var "k")) " in " (code (var "ht")) ", " (code "get-hash-table") " returns " (code (var "d")) ".") ("Key comparisons are performed with " (code (var "eq?")) ".") ("Because objects may be moved by the garbage collector, " (code "get-hash-table") "  may need to rehash some objects and therefore cause side effects in the hash table. Thus, it is not safe to perform concurrent accesses of the same hash table from multiple threads using " (code "get-hash-table") ".")) ("compat:s4" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(remove-hash-table! " (var "ht") " " (var "k") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "remove-hash-table!") " drops any association for " (code (var "k")) " from " (code (var "ht")) ".")) ("compat:s5" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(hash-table-map " (var "ht") " " (var "p") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "hash-table-map") " applies " (code (var "p")) " to each key, value association in " (code (var "ht")) ", in no particular order, and returns a list of the resulting values, again in no particular order. " (code (var "p")) " should accept two arguments, a key and a value.")) ("compat:s6" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(hash-table-for-each " (var "ht") " " (var "p") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "hash-table-for-each") " applies " (code (var "p")) " to each key, value association in " (code (var "ht")) ", in no particular order. Unlike " (code "hash-table-map") ", it does not create a list of the values; instead, it's value is unspecified. " (code (var "p")) " should accept two arguments, a key and a value.")) ("compat:s8" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(extend-syntax (" (var "name") " " (var "key") " ...) (" (var "pat") " " (var "fender") " " (var "template") ") ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The identifier " (code (var "name")) " is the name, or syntax keyword, for the syntactic extension to be defined. When the system expander processes any list expression whose car is " (code (var "name")) ", the syntactic transformation procedure generated by " (code "extend-syntax") " is invoked on this expression. The remaining identifiers " (code (var "key") " ...") " are additional keywords to be recognized within input expressions during expansion (such as " (code "else") " in " (code "cond") " or " (code "case") ").") ("Each clause after the list of keys consists of a pattern " (code (var "pat")) ", an optional " (code (var "fender")) ", and a " (code (var "template")) ". The optional " (code (var "fender")) " is omitted more often than not. The " (code (var "pat")) " specifies the syntax the input expression must have for the clause to be chosen. Identifiers within the pattern that are not keywords (" (var "pattern variables") ") are bound to corresponding pieces of the input expression. If present, the " (code (var "fender")) " is a Scheme expression that specifies additional constraints on the input expression (accessed through the pattern variables) that must be satisfied in order for the clause to be chosen. The " (code (var "template")) " specifies what form the output takes, usually in terms of the pattern variables.") ("During expansion, the transformation procedure " (code "extend-syntax") "  generates attempts to match the input expression against each pattern in the order the clauses are given. If the input expression matches the pattern, the pattern variables are bound to the corresponding pieces of the input expression and the fender for the clause, if any, is evaluated. If the fender returns a true value, the given expansion is performed. If input does not match the pattern or if the fender returns a false value, the transformation procedure tries the next clause. An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if no clause can be chosen.") ("Within the pattern, " (var "ellipsis") "  (" (code "...") ") may be used to specify zero or more occurrences of the preceding pattern fragment, or prototype. Similarly, ellipses may be used in the output to specify the construction of zero or more expansion prototypes. In this case, the expansion prototype must contain part of an input pattern prototype. The use of patterns, templates, ellipses within patterns and templates, and fenders is illustrated in the following sequence of examples.") ("The first example, defining " (code "rec") ", uses a single keyword, a single clause with no fender, and no ellipses.") ((code "(extend-syntax (rec)" (br) "  [(rec id val)" (br) "   (let ([id #f])" (br) "     (set! id val)" (br) "     id)])")) ("The second example, defining " (code "when") ", shows the use of ellipses.") ((code "(extend-syntax (when)" (br) "  [(when test exp1 exp2 ...)" (br) "   (if test (begin exp1 exp2 ...) #f)])")) ("The next example shows the definition of " (code "let") ". The definition of " (code "let") " shows the use of multiple ellipses, employing one for the identifier/value pairs and one for the expressions in the body. It also shows that the prototype need not be a single identifier, and that pieces of the prototype may be separated from one another in the template.") ((code "(extend-syntax (let)" (br) "  [(let ([x e] ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "   ((lambda (x ...) b1 b2 ...) e ...)])")) ("The next example shows " (code "let*") ", whose syntax is the same as for " (code "let") ", but which is defined recursively in terms of " (code "let") " with two clauses (one for the base case, one for the recursion step) since it must produce a nested structure.") ((code "(extend-syntax (let*)" (br) "  [(let* () b1 b2 ...)" (br) "   (let () b1 b2 ...)]" (br) "  [(let* ([x e] more ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "   (let ([x e]) (let* (more ...) b1 b2 ...))])")) ("The first pattern/template pair matches any " (code "let*") " expression with no identifier/value pairs and maps it into the equivalent " (code "begin") " expression. This is the base case. The second pattern/template pair matches any " (code "let*") " expression with one or more identifier/value pairs and transforms it into a " (code "let") " expression binding the first pair whose body is a " (code "let*") " expression binding the remaining pairs. This is the recursion step, which will eventually lead us to the base case because we remove one identifier/value pair at each step. Notice that the second pattern uses the pattern variable " (code "more") " for the second and later identifier/value pairs; this makes the pattern and template less cluttered and makes it clear that only the first identifier/value pair is dealt with explicitly.") ("The definition for " (code "and") " requires three clauses. The first clause is necessary to recognize " (code "(and)") ", and the second two define all other " (code "and") " forms recursively.") ((code "(extend-syntax (and)" (br) "  [(and) #t]" (br) "  [(and x) x]" (br) "  [(and x y ...) (if x (and y ...) #f)])")) ("The definition for " (code "cond") " requires four clauses. As with " (code "let*") ", " (code "cond") " must be described recursively, partly because it produces nested " (code "if") " expressions, and partly because one ellipsis prototype would not be sufficient to describe all possible " (code "cond") " clauses. The definition of " (code "cond") " also requires that we specify " (code "else") " as a keyword, in addition to " (code "cond") ". Here is the definition:") ((code "(extend-syntax (cond else)" (br) "  [(cond) #f]" (br) "  [(cond (else e1 e2 ...))" (br) "   (begin e1 e2 ...)]" (br) "  [(cond (test) more ...)" (br) "   (or test (cond more ...))]" (br) "  [(cond (test e1 e2 ...) more ...)" (br) "   (if test" (br) "       (begin e1 e2 ...)" (br) "       (cond more ...))])")) ("Two of the clauses are base cases and two are recursion steps. The first base case is an empty " (code "cond") ". The value of " (code "cond") " in this case is unspecified, so the choice of " (code "#f") " is somewhat arbitrary. The second base case is a " (code "cond") " containing only an " (code "else") " clause; this is transformed to the equivalent " (code "begin") " expression. The two recursion steps differ in the number of expressions in the " (code "cond") "  clause. The value of " (code "cond") " when the first true clause contains only the test expression is the value of the test. This is similar to what " (code "or") " does, so we expand the " (code "cond") " clause into an " (code "or") " expression. On the other hand, when there are expressions following the test expression, the value of the last expression is returned, so we use " (code "if") " and " (code "begin") ".") ("To be absolutely correct about the syntax of " (code "let") ", we actually must require that the bound identifiers in the input are symbols. If we typed something like " (code "(let ([3 x]) x)") " we would not get an error from " (code "let") " because it does not check to verify that the objects in the identifier positions are symbols. Instead, " (code "lambda") " may complain, or perhaps the system evaluator long after expansion is complete.  This is where fenders are useful.") ((code "(extend-syntax (let)" (br) "  [(let ([x e] ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "   (andmap symbol? '(x ...))" (br) "   ((lambda (x ...) b1 b2 ...) e ...)])")) ("The " (code "andmap") " of " (code "symbol?") "  over " (code "'(x ...)") " assures that each bound identifier is a symbol. A fender is simply a Scheme expression. Within that expression, any quoted object is first expanded by the same rules as the template part of the clause. In this case, " (code "'(x ...)") " is expanded to the list of identifiers from the identifier/value pairs.") ((code "extend-syntax") " typically handles everything you need it for, but some syntactic extension definitions require the ability to include the result of evaluating an arbitrary Scheme expression. This ability is provided by " (code "with") ".")) ("compat:s20" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(with ((" (var "pat") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "template") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "processed " (code (var "template"))) ((code "with") " is valid only within an template inside of " (code "extend-syntax") ". " (code "with") " patterns are the same as " (code "extend-syntax") " patterns, " (code "with") "  expressions are the same as " (code "extend-syntax") " fenders, and " (code "with") "  templates are the same as " (code "extend-syntax") " templates.") ((code "with") " can be used to introduce new pattern identifiers bound to expressions produced by arbitrary Scheme expressions within " (code "extend-syntax") " templates. That is, " (code "with") " allows an escape from the declarative style of " (code "extend-syntax") " into the procedural style of full Scheme.") ("One common use of " (code "with") " is the introduction of a temporary identifier or list of temporary identifiers into a template. " (code "with") " is also used to perform complex transformations that might be clumsy or inefficient if performed within the " (code "extend-syntax") "  framework.") ("For example, " (code "or") " requires the use of a temporary identifier. We could define " (code "or") " as follows.") ((code "(extend-syntax (or)" (br) "  [(or) #f]" (br) "  [(or x) x]" (br) "  [(or x y ...)" (br) "   (let ([temp x])" (br) "     (if temp temp (or y ...)))])")) ("This would work until we placed an " (code "or") " expression within the scope of an occurrence of " (code "temp") ", in which case strange things could happen, since " (code "extend-syntax") " does not respect lexical scoping. (This is one of the reasons that " (code "define-syntax") " is preferable to " (code "extend-syntax") ".)") ((code "(let ([temp #t])" (br) "  (or #f temp)) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("One solution is to use " (code "gensym") " and " (code "with") " to create a temporary identifier, as follows.") ((code "(extend-syntax (or)" (br) "  [(or) #f]" (br) "  [(or x) x]" (br) "  [(or x y ...)" (br) "   (with ([temp (gensym)])" (br) "     (let ([temp x])" (br) "       (if temp temp (or y ...))))])")) ("Also, " (code "with") " can be used to combine elements of the input pattern in ways not possible directly with " (code "extend-syntax") ", such as the following " (code "folding-plus") " example.") ((code "(extend-syntax (folding-plus)" (br) "  [(folding-plus x y)" (br) "   (and (number? 'x) (number? 'y))" (br) "   (with ([val (+ 'x 'y)])" (br) "      val)]" (br) "  [(folding-plus x y) (+ x y)])")) ((code "folding-plus") " collapses into the value of " (code "(+ x y)") " if both " (code "x") " and " (code "y") " are numeric constants. Otherwise, " (code "folding-plus") " is transformed into " (code "(+ x y)") " for later evaluation. The fender checks that the operands are numbers at expansion time, and the " (code "with") " performs the evaluation. As with fenders, expansion is performed only within a quoted expressions, since " (code "quote") " sets the data apart from the remainder of the Scheme expression.") ("The example below binds a list of pattern variables to a list of temporary symbols, taking advantage of the fact that " (code "with") " allows us to bind patterns to expressions. This list of temporaries helps us to implement the " (code "sigma") " syntactic extension. " (code "sigma") " is similar to " (code "lambda") ", except it assigns the identifiers in the identifier list instead of creating new bindings. It may be used to perform a series of assignments in parallel.") ((code "(extend-syntax (sigma)" (br) "  [(sigma (x ...) e1 e2 ...)" (br) "   (with ([(t ...) (map (lambda (x) (gensym)) '(x ...))])" (br) "     (lambda (t ...)" (br) "       (set! x t) ..." (br) "       e1 e2 ...))])" (br) (br) "(let ([x 'a] [y 'b])" (br) "  ((sigma (x y) (list x y)) y x)) " (math "=>") " (b a)")) ("The final example below uses " (code "extend-syntax") " to implement " (code "define-structure") ", following a similar example using " (code "syntax-case") " in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./syntax.html#g137" "8.4") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ".") ("The definition of " (code "define-structure") " makes use of two pattern/template clauses. Two clauses are needed to handle the optionality of the second subexpression. The first clause matches the form without the second subexpression and merely converts it into the equivalent form with the second subexpression present, but empty.") ("The definition also makes heavy use of " (code "with") " to evaluate Scheme expressions at expansion time. The first four " (code "with") " clauses are used to manufacture the identifiers that name the automatically defined procedures. (The procedure " (code "format") " is particularly useful here, but it could be replaced with " (code "string-append!") ", using " (code "symbol->string") " as needed.) The first two clauses yield single identifiers (for the constructor and predicate), while the next two yield lists of identifiers (for the field access and assignment procedures). The fifth " (code "with") " clause (the final clause in the outer " (code "with") ") is used to count the total length vector needed for each instance of the structure, which must include room for the name and all of the fields. The final " (code "with") " clause (the only clause in the inner " (code "with") ") is used to create a list of vector indexes, one for each field (starting at 1, since the structure name occupies position 0).") ((code "(extend-syntax (define-structure)" (br) "  [(define-structure (name id1 ...))" (br) "   (define-structure (name id1 ...) ())]" (br) "  [(define-structure (name id1 ...) ([id2 val] ...))" (br) "   (with ([constructor" (br) "           (string->symbol (format \"make-~a\" 'name))]" (br) "          [predicate" (br) "           (string->symbol (format \"~a?\" 'name))]" (br) "          [(access ...)" (br) "           (map (lambda (x)" (br) "                  (string->symbol" (br) "                    (format \"~a-~a\" 'name x)))" (br) "                '(id1 ... id2 ...))]" (br) "          [(assign ...)" (br) "           (map (lambda (x)" (br) "                  (string->symbol" (br) "                    (format \"set-~a-~a!\" 'name x)))" (br) "                '(id1 ... id2 ...))]" (br) "          [count (length '(name id1 ... id2 ...))])" (br) "     (with ([(index ...)" (br) "             (let f ([i 1])" (br) "               (if (= i 'count)" (br) "                   '()" (br) "                   (cons i (f (+ i 1)))))])" (br) "       (begin" (br) "         (define constructor" (br) "           (lambda (id1 ...)" (br) "             (let* ([id2 val] ...)" (br) "               (vector 'name id1 ... id2 ...))))" (br) "         (define predicate" (br) "           (lambda (obj)" (br) "             (and (vector? obj)" (br) "                  (= (vector-length obj) count)" (br) "                  (eq? (vector-ref obj 0) 'name))))" (br) "         (define access" (br) "           (lambda (obj)" (br) "             (vector-ref obj index)))" (br) "         ..." (br) "         (define assign" (br) "           (lambda (obj newval)" (br) "             (vector-set! obj index newval)))" (br) "         ...)))])"))) ("compat:s25" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-structure (" (var "name") " " (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...) ((" (var "id" (sub "2")) " " (var "expr") ") ...))") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "define-structure") " form is a definition and may appear anywhere and only where other definitions may appear.") ((code "define-structure") " defines a new data structure, " (code (var "name")) ", and creates a set of procedures for creating and manipulating instances of the structure. The identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " and " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") "  name the fields of the data structure.") ("The following procedures are defined by " (code "define-structure") ":   " (ul (li "a constructor procedure whose name is " (code "make-" (var "name")) ",") (li "a type predicate whose name is " (code (var "name") "?") ",") (li "an access procedure whose name is " (code (var "name") "-" (var "field")) "  for each field name " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " and " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") ", and") (li "an assignment procedure whose name is " (code "set-" (var "name") "-" (var "field") "!") "  for each field name " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " and " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") "."))) ("The fields named by the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " are initialized by the arguments to the constructor procedure. The fields named by the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") " are initialized explicitly to the values of the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") ". Each expression is evaluated within the scope of the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " (bound to the corresponding field values) and any of the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") " (bound to the corresponding field values) appearing before it (as if within a " (code "let*") ").") ("To clarify, the constructor behaves as if defined as") ((code "(define make-" (var "name") (br) "  (lambda (" (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...)" (br) "    (let* ([" (var "id" (sub "2")) " " (var "expr") "] ...)" (br) "      " (var "body") ")))")) ("where " (code (var "body")) " builds the structure from the values of the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " and " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") ".") ("If no fields other than those initialized by the arguments to the constructor procedure are needed, the second subexpression, " (code "((" (var "id" (sub "2")) " " (var "expr") ") ...)") ", may be omitted.") ("The following simple example demonstrates how pairs might be defined in Scheme if they did not already exist. Both fields are initialized by the arguments to the constructor procedure.") ((code "(define-structure (pare car cdr))" (br) "(define p (make-pare 'a 'b))" (br) (br) "(pare? p) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(pair? p) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(pare? '(a . b)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(pare-car p) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "(pare-cdr p) " (math "=>") " b" (br) (br) "(set-pare-cdr! p (make-pare 'b 'c))" (br) (br) "(pare-car (pare-cdr p)) " (math "=>") " b" (br) "(pare-cdr (pare-cdr p)) " (math "=>") " c")) ("The following example defines a handy string data structure, called a " (var "stretch-string") ", that grows as needed. This example uses a field explicitly initialized to a value that depends on the value of the constructor argument fields.") ((code "(define-structure (stretch-string length fill)" (br) "  ([string (make-string length fill)]))" (br) (br) "(define stretch-string-ref" (br) "  (lambda (s i)" (br) "    (let ([n (stretch-string-length s)])" (br) "      (when (>= i n) (stretch-stretch-string! s (+ i 1) n))" (br) "      (string-ref (stretch-string-string s) i))))" (br) (br) "(define stretch-string-set!" (br) "  (lambda (s i c)" (br) "    (let ([n (stretch-string-length s)])" (br) "      (when (>= i n) (stretch-stretch-string! s (+ i 1) n))" (br) "      (string-set! (stretch-string-string s) i c))))" (br) (br) "(define stretch-string-fill!" (br) "  (lambda (s c)" (br) "    (string-fill! (stretch-string-string s) c)" (br) "    (set-stretch-string-fill! s c)))" (br) (br) "(define stretch-stretch-string!" (br) "  (lambda (s i n)" (br) "    (set-stretch-string-length! s i)" (br) "    (let ([str (stretch-string-string s)]" (br) "          [fill (stretch-string-fill s)])" (br) "      (let ([xtra (make-string (- i n) fill)])" (br) "        (set-stretch-string-string! s" (br) "          (string-append str xtra))))))")) ("As often happens, most of the procedures defined automatically are used only to define more specialized procedures, in this case the procedures " (code "stretch-string-ref") " and " (code "stretch-string-set!") ". " (code "stretch-string-length") " and " (code "stretch-string-string") " are the only automatically defined procedures that are likely to be called directly in code that uses stretch strings.") ((code "(define ss (make-stretch-string 2 #\\X))" (br) (br) "(stretch-string-string ss) " (math "=>") " \"XX\"" (br) "(stretch-string-ref ss 3) " (math "=>") " #\\X" (br) "(stretch-string-length ss) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) "(stretch-string-string ss) " (math "=>") " \"XXXX\"" (br) (br) "(stretch-string-fill! ss #\\@)" (br) "(stretch-string-string ss) " (math "=>") " \"@@@@\"" (br) "(stretch-string-ref ss 5) " (math "=>") " #\\@" (br) "(stretch-string-string ss) " (math "=>") " \"@@@@@@\"" (br) (br) "(stretch-string-set! ss 7 #\\=)" (br) "(stretch-string-length ss) " (math "=>") " 8" (br) "(stretch-string-string ss) " (math "=>") " \"@@@@@@@=\"")) ("Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./syntax.html#g137" "8.4") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") " defines a simplified variant of " (code "define-structure") " as an example of the use of " (code "syntax-case") ". The definition given below implements the complete version.") ((code "define-structure") " expands into a series of definitions for names generated from the structure name and field names. The generated identifiers are created with " (code "datum->syntax") " to make the identifiers visible where the " (code "define-structure") "  form appears. Since a " (code "define-structure") " form expands into a " (code "begin") "  containing definitions, it is itself a definition and can be used wherever definitions are valid.") ((code "(define-syntax define-structure" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (define gen-id" (br) "      (lambda (template-id . args)" (br) "        (datum->syntax template-id" (br) "          (string->symbol" (br) "            (apply string-append" (br) "                   (map (lambda (x)" (br) "                          (if (string? x)" (br) "                              x" (br) "                              (symbol->string" (br) "                                (syntax->datum x))))" (br) "                        args))))))" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      ((_ (name field1 ...))" (br) "       (andmap identifier? #'(name field1 ...))" (br) "       #'(define-structure (name field1 ...) ()))" (br) "      ((_ (name field1 ...) ((field2 init) ...))" (br) "       (andmap identifier? #'(name field1 ... field2 ...))" (br) "       (with-syntax" (br) "         ((constructor (gen-id #'name \"make-\" #'name))" (br) "          (predicate (gen-id #'name #'name \"?\"))" (br) "          ((access ...)" (br) "           (map (lambda (x) (gen-id x #'name \"-\" x))" (br) "                #'(field1 ... field2 ...)))" (br) "          ((assign ...)" (br) "           (map (lambda (x) (gen-id x \"set-\" #'name \"-\" x \"!\"))" (br) "                #'(field1 ... field2 ...)))" (br) "          (structure-length" (br) "           (+ (length #'(field1 ... field2 ...)) 1))" (br) "          ((index ...)" (br) "           (let f ([i 1] [ids #'(field1 ... field2 ...)])" (br) "             (if (null? ids)" (br) "                 '()" (br) "                 (cons i (f (+ i 1) (cdr ids)))))))" (br) "         #'(begin" (br) "             (define constructor" (br) "               (lambda (field1 ...)" (br) "                 (let* ([field2 init] ...)" (br) "                   (vector 'name field1 ... field2 ...))))" (br) "             (define predicate" (br) "               (lambda (x)" (br) "                 (and (vector? x)" (br) "                      (#3%fx= (vector-length x) structure-length)" (br) "                      (eq? (vector-ref x 0) 'name))))" (br) "             (define access (lambda (x) (vector-ref x index)))" (br) "             ..." (br) "             (define assign" (br) "               (lambda (x update) (vector-set! x index update)))" (br) "             ...))))))"))) ("control:s0" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(exclusive-cond " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "exclusive-cond") " is a version of " (code "cond") "  (Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./control.html#g99" "5.3") " of TSPLFOUR) that differs from " (code "cond") " in that the tests embedded within the clauses are assumed to be exclusive in the sense that if one of the tests is true, the others are not. This allows the implementation to reorder clauses when profiling information is available at expansion time (Section " (link "./system.html#g123" "12.7") ").") ("The " (code "(" (var "test") ")") " form of clause is not supported. The order chosen when profiling information is available is based on the relative numbers of times the RHS of each clause is executed, and " (code "(" (var "test") ")") " has no RHS. " (code "(" (var "test") " => values)") " is equivalent, albeit less concise.")) ("control:s1" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(case " (var "expr" (sub "0")) " " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each clause but the last must take one of the forms:") ((code "((" (var "key") " ...) " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)" (br) "(" (var "key") " " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("where each " (code (var "key")) " is a datum distinct from the other keys. The last clause may be in the above form or it may be an " (code "else") " clause of the form") ((code "(else " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ((code (var "expr" (sub "0"))) " is evaluated and the result is compared (using " (code "equal?") ") against the keys of each clause in order. If a clause containing a matching key is found, the expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") " are evaluated in sequence and the values of the last expression are returned.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and an " (code "else") " clause is present, the expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") " of the " (code "else") " clause are evaluated in sequence and the values of the last expression are returned.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and no " (code "else") " clause is present, the value or values are unspecified.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "case") " does not support singleton keys (the second of the first two clause forms above) and uses " (code "eqv?") " rather than " (code "equal?") " as the comparison procedure. Both versions are defined in terms of " (code "exclusive-cond") " so that if profiling information is available at expansion time, the clauses will be reordered to put those that are most frequently executed first.") ((code "(let ([ls '(ii iv)])" (br) "  (case (car ls)" (br) "    [i 1]" (br) "    [ii 2]" (br) "    [iii 3]" (br) "    [(iiii iv) 4]" (br) "    [else 'out-of-range])) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) (br) "(define p" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (case x" (br) "      [(\"abc\" \"def\") 'one]" (br) "      [((a b c)) 'two]" (br) "      [else #f])))" (br) (br) "(p (string #\\d #\\e #\\f)) " (math "=>") " one" (br) "(p '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " two"))) ("control:s3" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(record-case " (var "expr") " " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "record-case") " is a restricted form of " (code "case") " that supports the destructuring of " (var "records") ", or " (var "tagged lists") ". A record has as its first element a tag that determines what \"type\" of record it is; the remaining elements are the fields of the record.") ("Each clause but the last must take the form") ((code "((" (var "key") " ...) " (var "formals") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("where each " (code (var "key")) " is a datum distinct from the other keys. The last clause may be in the above form or it may be an " (code "else") " clause of the form") ((code "(else " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ((code (var "expr")) " must evaluate to a pair. " (code (var "expr")) " is evaluated and the car of its value is compared (using " (code "eqv?") ") against the keys of each clause in order. If a clause containing a matching key is found, the variables in " (code (var "formals")) " are bound to the remaining elements of the list and the expressions " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") " are evaluated in sequence. The value of the last expression is returned. The effect is identical to the application of") ((code "(lambda " (var "formals") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("to the cdr of the list.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and an " (code "else") " clause is present, the expressions " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") " of the " (code "else") " clause are evaluated in sequence and the value of the last expression is returned.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and no " (code "else") " clause is present, the value is unspecified.") ((code "(define calc" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (record-case x" (br) "      [(add) (x y) (+ x y)]" (br) "      [(sub) (x y) (- x y)]" (br) "      [(mul) (x y) (* x y)]" (br) "      [(div) (x y) (/ x y)]" (br) "      [else (assertion-violationf 'calc \"invalid expression ~s\" x)])))" (br) (br) "(calc '(add 3 4)) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) "(calc '(div 3 4)) " (math "=>") " 3/4"))) ("control:s7" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ormap " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "ormap") " is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "exists") ".")) ("control:s8" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(andmap " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "andmap") " is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "for-all") ".")) ("control:s9" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call/1cc " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "call/1cc") " obtains its continuation and passes it to " (code (var "procedure")) ", which should accept one argument. The continuation itself is represented by a procedure. This procedure normally takes one argument but may take an arbitrary number of arguments depending upon whether the context of the call to " (code "call/1cc") "  expects multiple return values or not. When this procedure is applied to a value or values, it returns the values to the continuation of the " (code "call/1cc") " application.") ("The continuation obtained by " (code "call/1cc") " is a \"one-shot continuation.\" A one-shot continuation should not be returned to multiple times, either by invoking the continuation or returning normally from " (code (var "procedure")) " more than once. A one-shot continuation is \"promoted\" into a normal (multishot) continuation, however, if it is  still active when a normal continuation is obtained by " (code "call/cc") ". After a one-shot continuation is promoted into a multishot continuation, it behaves exactly as if it had been obtained via " (code "call/cc") ". This allows " (code "call/cc") " and " (code "call/1cc") " to be used together transparently in many applications.") ("One-shot continuations may be more efficient for some applications than multishot continuations. See the paper \"Representing control in the presence of one-shot continuations\" [" (link "./bibliography.html#g163" "3") "] for more information about one-shot continuations, including how they are implemented in " (var "Chez Scheme") ".") ("The following examples highlight the similarities and differences between one-shot and normal continuations.") ((code "(define prod" (br) " ; compute the product of the elements of ls, bugging out" (br) " ; with no multiplications if a zero element is found" (br) "  (lambda (ls)" (br) "    (lambda (k)" (br) "      (if (null? ls)" (br) "          1" (br) "          (if (= (car ls) 0)" (br) "              (k 0)" (br) "              (* (car ls) ((prod (cdr ls)) k)))))))" (br) (br) "(call/cc (prod '(1 2 3 4))) " (math "=>") " 24" (br) "(call/1cc (prod '(1 2 3 4))) " (math "=>") " 24" (br) (br) "(call/cc (prod '(1 2 3 4 0))) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(call/1cc (prod '(1 2 3 4 0))) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) (br) "(let ([k (call/cc (lambda (x) x))])" (br) "  (k (lambda (x) 0))) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) (br) "(let ([k (call/1cc (lambda (x) x))])" (br) "  (k (lambda (x) 0))) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("control:s11" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(dynamic-wind " (var "in") " " (var "body") " " (var "out") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(dynamic-wind " (var "critical?") " " (var "in") " " (var "body") " " (var "out") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "values resulting from the application of " (code (var "body")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The first form is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "dynamic-wind") ". When the optional " (code (var "critical?")) " argument is present and non-false, the " (code (var "in")) " thunk is invoked in a critical section along with the code that records that the body has been entered, and the " (code (var "out")) " thunk is invoked in a critical section along with the code that records that the body has been exited. Extreme caution must be taken with this form of " (code "dynamic-wind") ", since an error or long-running computation can leave interrupts and automatic garbage collection disabled.")) ("control:s12" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(with-continuation-mark " (var "key") " " (var "val") " " (var "body") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value of the " (code (var "body")) " expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "with-continuation-mark") " updates the table of marks on the current continuation to map the result of the " (code (var "key")) "  expression to the result of the " (code (var "val")) " expression. If the current continuation's table of marks already has a mapping for " (code (var "key")) "  (based on " (code "eq?") " comparison), then the mark's value is replaced with " (code (var "val")) ", otherwise a mapping from " (code (var "key")) " to " (code (var "val")) " is added to the table.") ((code "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " \"hello\") ; => \"hello\"" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                           'key)) ; => \"val\"" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                           'other-key)) ; => #f" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (with-continuation-mark" (br) "  'key \"val2\"" (br) "  (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                            'key))) ; => \"val2\"" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (with-continuation-mark" (br) "  'key \"val2\"" (br) "  (continuation-marks->list (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                            'key))) ; => (\"val2\")" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (values" (br) "  (with-continuation-mark" (br) "   'key \"val2\"" (br) "   (continuation-marks->list (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                             'key)))) ; => (\"val2\" \"val\")"))) ("control:s13" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "boolean" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A predicate that recognizes a continuation mark sequence, which can be produced by the functions " (code "current-continuation-marks") " and " (code "continuation-next-marks") ".")) ("control:s14" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-continuation-marks)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-next-marks " (var "cont") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a continuation mark sequence" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Returns a captured sequence of mark tables, either the current continuation's marks in the case of " (code "current-continuation-marks") " or the marks of the rest of " (code (var "cont")) " in the case of " (code "continuation-next-marks") ". In the latter case, " (code (var "cont")) " must be a continuation.") ("This function takes constant time. The size of the resulting mark sequence is proportional to the number of distinct key-value mappings in the overall mark-table sequence; that size is bounded by the length of the continuation times the number of distinct values used as keys, but since many continuations have no keys or fewer than all possible keys in their tables, the size tends to be much less than the bound.") ((code "(continuation-marks? (current-continuation-marks)) ; => #t" (br) "(continuation-marks? (continuation-next-marks" (br) "                      (call/cc (lambda (k) k)))) ; => #t"))) ("control:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks-first " (var "marks") " " (var "key") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks-first " (var "marks") " " (var "key") " " (var "none-val") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value for " (code (var "key")) " in " (code (var "marks")) " or " (code (var "none-val")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Extracts the first value found for " (code (var "key")) " in " (code (var "marks")) ", checking the mark table of a continuation before checking the table of the continuation that it extends (if any). Keys are compared using " (code "eq?") ". If no mark for " (code (var "key")) " is found, " (code (var "none-val")) " is returned; if " (code (var "none-val")) " is not provided, it defaults to " (code "#f") ".") ("This function takes amortized time proportional to the number of distinct values used as keys in " (code (var "marks")) ". Typically the number of keys used in an application is bounded, which makes the computation amortized constant-time for those applications.") ((code "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (values" (br) "  (with-continuation-mark" (br) "   'key \"val2\"" (br) "   (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                             'key)))) ; => \"val2\"" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                           'other" (br) "                           \"nope\")) ; => \"nope\""))) ("control:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks->list " (var "marks") " " (var "key") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Returns the list of all values associated with " (code (var "key")) " in " (code (var "marks")) ", with the value from a continuation's mark table appearing before the values from the mark tables of any other continuation that it extends.  Keys are compared using " (code "eq?") ".") ("This function takes time proportional to the size of the captured mark sequence.") ((code "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (values" (br) "  (with-continuation-mark" (br) "   'key \"val2\"" (br) "   (continuation-marks->list (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                             'key)))) ; => (\"val2\" \"val\")" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (continuation-marks->list (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                           'other)) ; => ()"))) ("control:s17" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks->iterator " (var "marks") " " (var "key-vector") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-marks->iterator " (var "marks") " " (var "key-vector") " " (var "none-val") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Generalizes the mark sequence traversal of " (code "continuation-marks->list") " to a functional iterator. The " (code (var "marks")) " argument must be a continuation mark sequence, and the " (code (var "key-vector")) " argument must be a vector of values to be used as keys. The result is an iterator procedure of zero arguments.") ("Calling the result iterator procedure (with no arguments) returns two values:   " (ul (li "The first result is either a vector of values, one for each key in  " (code (var "key-vector")) " (in the order given in " (code (var "key-vector")) ") and drawn from a single continuation's mark table, or " (code "#f") " if no more values for any keys are available. The given " (code (var "none-val")) ", which defaults to  " (code "#f") ", is used for each key that has no value in the table. Only mark tables with mappings for at least one of the keys in  " (code (var "key-vector")) " are represented in the iteration, so a result vector will never consist solely of " (code (var "none-val")) " values (unless one or more of the keys is explicitly mapped to " (code (var "none-val")) ").") (li "The second result is a new iterator procedure to obtain the next vector of values, and so on. When the first result is " (code "#f") ", the second result is an iterator procedure that will still return " (code "#f") " as its first result (and a procedure functionally equivalent to itself as the second result).   "))) ("Obtaining an iterator from " (code "continuation-marks->iterator") " takes constant time. Each call to an iterator takes time proportional to the size of continuation mark tables that are traversed to find one of the keys in " (code (var "key-vector")) ".") ((code "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (with-continuation-mark" (br) "  'other \"also\"" (br) "  (values" (br) "   (with-continuation-mark" (br) "    'key \"val2\"" (br) "    (let loop ([iter (continuation-marks->iterator" (br) "                      (current-continuation-marks)" (br) "                      '#(key other))])" (br) "      (let-values ([(vec iter) (iter)])" (br) "         (if vec" (br) "             (cons vec (loop iter))" (br) "             '()))))))) ; => (#(\"val2\" #f) #(\"val\" \"also\"))"))) ("control:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-immediate-continuation-mark " (var "key") " " (var "proc") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-immediate-continuation-mark " (var "key") " " (var "none-val") " " (var "proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value produced by calling " (code (var "proc")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Similar to") ((code " (continuation-marks-first (current-continuation-marks) " (var "key") " " (var "none-val") ")")) ("but only the immediate continuation's mark table is checked, and the result is delivered to " (code (var "proc")) " instead of returned. The " (code (var "proc")) ", which must be a procedure that accepts one argument, is called in tail position, so its continuation is the same as the one whose table is checked.") ((code "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (call-with-immediate-continuation-mark 'key list)) ; => (\"val\")" (br) (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (vector (call-with-immediate-continuation-mark 'key list))) ; => #((#f))" (br) "(with-continuation-mark" (br) " 'key \"val\"" (br) " (vector (call-with-immediate-continuation-mark 'key 'no list))) ; => #((no))"))) ("control:s19" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-in-continuation " (var "continuation") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-in-continuation " (var "continuation") " " (var "marks") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "continuation")) " must be a continuation, " (code (var "procedure")) " must be a procedure that accepts zero arguments, and " (code (var "marks")) " (if provided) must be a continuation mark sequence.") ("Applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to zero arguments with " (code (var "continuation")) " as the continuation of the call, escaping from the current continuation. This operation is similar to applying " (code (var "continuation")) " to values, except that the values delivered to the continuation are the ones produced by " (code (var "procedure")) " as it runs within the applied continuation.") ("If " (code (var "marks")) " is not provided, then " (code (var "procedure")) " starts with no immediate mark table. Otherwise, " (code (var "marks")) " must be consistent with the result of " (code "continuation-next-marks") " on " (code (var "continuation")) ": either the same content or one additional mark table, and the additional mark table becomes the immediate mark table when calling " (code (var "procedure")) ".")) ("control:s24" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-engine " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an engine" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("An engine is created by passing a thunk (no argument procedure) to " (code "make-engine") ". The body of the thunk is the computation to be performed by the engine. An engine itself is a procedure of three arguments:") ((dt (code (var "ticks")) ":") (dd "a positive integer that specifies the amount of " (var "fuel") " to be given to the engine. An engine executes until this fuel runs out or until its computation finishes.")) ((dt (code (var "complete")) ":") (dd "a procedure of one or more arguments that specifies what to do if the computation finishes. Its arguments are the amount of fuel left over and the values produced by the computation.")) ((dt (code (var "expire")) ":") (dd "a procedure of one argument that specifies what to do if the fuel runs out before the computation finishes. Its argument is a new engine capable of continuing the computation from the point of interruption.")) ("When an engine is applied to its arguments, it sets up a timer to fire in " (code (var "ticks")) " time units. (See " (code "set-timer") " on page " (link "./system.html#desc:set-timer" "363") ".) If the engine computation completes before the timer expires, the system invokes " (code (var "complete")) ", passing  it the number of " (code (var "ticks")) " left over and the values produced by the computation. If, on the other hand, the timer goes off before the engine computation completes, the system creates a new engine from the continuation of the interrupted computation and passes this engine to " (code (var "expire")) ". " (code (var "complete")) " and " (code (var "expire")) " are invoked in the continuation of the engine invocation.") ("An implementation of engines is given in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./examples.html#g208" "12.11") ". of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ".") ("Do not use the timer interrupt (see " (code "set-timer") ") and engines at the same time, since engines are implemented in terms of the timer.") ("The following example creates an engine from a trivial computation, 3, and gives the engine 10 ticks.") ((code "(define eng" (br) "  (make-engine" (br) "    (lambda () 3)))" (br) (br) "(eng 10" (br) "     (lambda (ticks value) value)" (br) "     (lambda (x) x)) " (math "=>") " 3")) ("It is often useful to pass " (code "list") " as the " (code (var "complete")) "  procedure to an engine, causing an engine that completes to return a list whose first element is the ticks remaining and whose remaining elements are the values returned by the computation.") ((code "(define eng" (br) "  (make-engine" (br) "    (lambda () 3)))" (br) (br) "(eng 10" (br) "     list" (br) "     (lambda (x) x)) " (math "=>") " (9 3)")) ("In the example above, the value is 3 and there are 9 ticks left over, i.e., it takes one unit of fuel to evaluate 3. (The fuel amounts given here are for illustration only. Your mileage may vary.)") ("Typically, the engine computation does not finish in one try. The following example displays the use of an engine to compute the 10th Fibonacci number in steps.") ((code "(define fibonacci" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (let fib ([i n])" (br) "      (cond" (br) "        [(= i 0) 0]" (br) "        [(= i 1) 1]" (br) "        [else (+ (fib (- i 1))" (br) "                 (fib (- i 2)))]))))" (br) (br) "(define eng" (br) "  (make-engine" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (fibonacci 10))))" (br) (br) "(eng 50" (br) "     list" (br) "     (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "       (set! eng new-eng)" (br) "       \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " \"expired\"" (br) (br) "(eng 50" (br) "     list" (br) "     (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "       (set! eng new-eng)" (br) "       \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " \"expired\"" (br) (br) "(eng 50" (br) "     list" (br) "     (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "       (set! eng new-eng)" (br) "       \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " \"expired\"" (br) (br) "(eng 50" (br) "     list" (br) "     (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "       (set! eng new-eng)" (br) "       \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " (21 55)")) ("Each time the engine's fuel runs out, the " (code (var "expire")) " procedure assigns " (code "eng") " to the new engine. The entire computation requires four blocks of 50 ticks to complete; of the last 50 it uses all but 21. Thus, the total amount of fuel used is 179 ticks. This leads to the following procedure, " (code "mileage") ", which \"times\" a computation using engines:") ((code "(define mileage" (br) "  (lambda (thunk)" (br) "    (let loop ([eng (make-engine thunk)] [total-ticks 0])" (br) "      (eng 50" (br) "           (lambda (ticks . values)" (br) "             (+ total-ticks (- 50 ticks)))" (br) "           (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "             (loop new-eng" (br) "                   (+ total-ticks 50)))))))" (br) (br) "(mileage (lambda () (fibonacci 10))) " (math "=>") " 179")) ("The choice of 50 for the number of ticks to use each time is arbitrary, of course. It might make more sense to pass a much larger number, say 10000, in order to reduce the number of times the computation is interrupted.") ("The next procedure is similar to " (code "mileage") ", but it returns a list of engines, one for each tick it takes to complete the computation. Each of the engines in the list represents a \"snapshot\" of the computation, analogous to a single frame of a moving picture. " (code "snapshot") " might be useful for \"single stepping\" a computation.") ((code "(define snapshot" (br) "  (lambda (thunk)" (br) "    (let again ([eng (make-engine thunk)])" (br) "      (cons eng" (br) "            (eng 1 (lambda (t . v) '()) again)))))")) ("The recursion embedded in this procedure is rather strange. The complete procedure performs the base case, returning the empty list, and the expire procedure performs the recursion.") ("The next procedure, " (code "round-robin") ", could be the basis for a simple time-sharing operating system. " (code "round-robin") " maintains a queue of processes (a list of engines), cycling through the queue in a " (var "round-robin") " fashion, allowing each process to run for a set amount of time. " (code "round-robin") " returns a list of the values returned by the engine computations in the order that the computations complete. Each computation is assumed to produce exactly one value.") ((code "(define round-robin" (br) "  (lambda (engs)" (br) "    (if (null? engs)" (br) "        '()" (br) "        ((car engs)" (br) "         1" (br) "         (lambda (ticks value)" (br) "           (cons value (round-robin (cdr engs))))" (br) "         (lambda (eng)" (br) "           (round-robin" (br) "             (append (cdr engs) (list eng))))))))")) ("Since the amount of fuel supplied each time, one tick, is constant, the effect of " (code "round-robin") " is to return a list of the values sorted from the quickest to complete to the slowest to complete. Thus, when we call " (code "round-robin") " on a list of engines, each computing one of the Fibonacci numbers, the output list is sorted with the earlier Fibonacci numbers first, regardless of the order of the input list.") ((code "(round-robin" (br) "  (map (lambda (x)" (br) "         (make-engine" (br) "           (lambda ()" (br) "             (fibonacci x))))" (br) "       '(4 5 2 8 3 7 6 2))) " (math "=>") " (1 1 2 3 5 8 13 21)")) ("More interesting things can happen if the amount of fuel varies each time through the loop. In this case, the computation would be nondeterministic, i.e., the results would vary from call to call.") ("The following syntactic form, " (code "por") " (parallel-or), returns the first of its expressions to complete with a true value. " (code "por") " is implemented with the procedure " (code "first-true") ", which is similar to " (code "round-robin") " but quits when any of the engines completes with a true value. If all of the engines complete, but none with a true value, " (code "first-true") " (and hence " (code "por") ") returns " (code "#f") ". Also, although " (code "first-true") " passes a fixed amount of fuel to each engine, it chooses the next engine to run at random, and is thus nondeterministic.") ((code "(define-syntax por" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ x ...)" (br) "     (first-true" (br) "       (list (make-engine (lambda () x)) ...))]))" (br) (br) "(define first-true" (br) "  (let ([pick" (br) "         (lambda (ls)" (br) "           (list-ref ls (random (length ls))))])" (br) "    (lambda (engs)" (br) "      (if (null? engs)" (br) "          #f" (br) "          (let ([eng (pick engs)])" (br) "            (eng 1" (br) "                 (lambda (ticks value)" (br) "                   (or value" (br) "                       (first-true" (br) "                         (remq eng engs))))" (br) "                 (lambda (new-eng)" (br) "                   (first-true" (br) "                     (cons new-eng" (br) "                           (remq eng engs))))))))))")) ("The list of engines is maintained with " (code "pick") ", which randomly chooses an element of the list, and " (code "remq") ", which removes the chosen engine from the list. Since " (code "por") " is nondeterministic, subsequent uses with the same expressions may not return the same values.") ((code "(por 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "(por 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(por 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "(por 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " 1")) ("Furthermore, even if one of the expressions is an infinite loop, " (code "por") " still finishes as long as one of the other expressions completes and returns a true value.") ((code "(por (let loop () (loop)) 2) " (math "=>") " 2")) ("With " (code "engine-return") " and " (code "engine-block") ", it is possible to terminate an engine explicitly. " (code "engine-return") " causes the engine to complete, as if the computation had finished. Its arguments are passed to the " (code (var "complete")) " procedure along with the number of ticks remaining. It is essentially a nonlocal exit from the engine. Similarly, " (code "engine-block") " causes the engine to expire, as if the timer had run out. A new engine is made from the continuation of the call to " (code "engine-block") "  and passed to the " (code (var "expire")) " procedure.")) ("control:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(engine-block)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This causes a running engine to stop, create a new engine capable of continuing the computation, and pass the new engine to the original engine's third argument (the expire procedure). Any remaining fuel is forfeited.") ((code "(define eng" (br) "  (make-engine" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (engine-block)" (br) "      \"completed\")))" (br) (br) "(eng 100" (br) "     (lambda (ticks value) value)" (br) "     (lambda (x)" (br) "        (set! eng x)" (br) "        \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " \"expired\"" (br) (br) "(eng 100" (br) "     (lambda (ticks value) value)" (br) "     (lambda (x)" (br) "        (set! eng x)" (br) "        \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " \"completed\""))) ("control:s36" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(engine-return " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This causes a running engine to stop and pass control to the engine's " (code (var "complete")) " argument. The first argument passed to the complete procedure is the amount of fuel remaining, as usual, and the remaining arguments are the objects " (code (var "obj") " ...") "  passed to " (code "engine-return") ".") ((code "(define eng" (br) "  (make-engine" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (reverse (engine-return 'a 'b 'c)))))" (br) (br) "(eng 100" (br) "     (lambda (ticks . values) values)" (br) "     (lambda (new-eng) \"expired\")) " (math "=>") " (a b c)"))) ("debug:s0" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-lambda " (var "name") " " (var "formals") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a traced procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "trace-lambda") " expression is equivalent to a " (code "lambda") " expression with the same formals and body except that trace information is printed to the trace output port whenever the procedure is invoked, using " (code (var "name")) " to identify the procedure. The trace information shows the value of the arguments passed to the procedure and the values returned by the procedure, with indentation to show the nesting of calls.") ("The traced procedure " (code "half") " defined below returns the integer quotient of its argument and 2.") ((code "(define half" (br) "  (trace-lambda half (x)" (br) "    (cond" (br) "      [(zero? x) 0]" (br) "      [(odd? x) (half (- x 1))]" (br) "      [(even? x) (+ (half (- x 1)) 1)])))")) ("A trace of the call " (code "(half 5)") ", which returns 2, is shown below.") ((code "|(half 5)" (br) "|(half 4)" (br) "| (half 3)" (br) "| (half 2)" (br) "| |(half 1)" (br) "| |(half 0)" (br) "| |0" (br) "| 1" (br) "|2")) ("This example highlights the proper treatment of tail and nontail calls by the trace package. Since " (code "half") " tail calls itself when its argument is odd, the call " (code "(half 4)") " appears at the same level of indentation as the call " (code "(half 5)") ". Furthermore, since the return values of " (code "(half 5)") " and " (code "(half 4)") " are necessarily the same, only one return value is shown for both calls.")) ("debug:s3" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-case-lambda " (var "name") " " (var "clause") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a traced procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "trace-case-lambda") " expression is equivalent to a " (code "case-lambda") " expression with the same clauses except that trace information is printed to the trace output port whenever the procedure is invoked, using " (code (var "name")) " to identify the procedure. The trace information shows the value of the arguments passed to the procedure and the values returned by the procedure, with indentation to show the nesting of calls.")) ("debug:s5" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-let " (var "name") " ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "trace-let") " expression is equivalent to a named " (code "let") " expression with the same name, bindings, and body except that trace information is printed to the trace output port on entry or reentry (via invocation of the procedure bound to " (code "name") ") into the " (code "trace-let") " expression.") ("A " (code "trace-let") " expression of the form") ((code "(trace-let " (var "name") " ([" (var "var") " " (var "expr") "] ...)" (br) "  " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("can be rewritten in terms of " (code "trace-lambda") " as follows:") ((code "((letrec ([" (var "name") (br) "           (trace-lambda " (var "name") " (" (var "var") " ...)" (br) "             " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)])" (br) "   " (var "name") ")" (br) " " (var "expr") " ...)")) ((code "trace-let") " may be used to trace ordinary " (code "let") " expressions as well as " (code "let") " expressions as long as the name inserted along with the " (code "trace-let") " keyword in place of " (code "let") " does not appear free within the body of the " (code "let") " expression. It is also sometimes useful to insert a " (code "trace-let") " expression into a program simply to display the value of an arbitrary expression at the current trace indentation. For example, a call to the following variant of " (code "half")) ((code "(define half" (br) "  (trace-lambda half (x)" (br) "    (cond" (br) "      [(zero? x) 0]" (br) "      [(odd? x) (half (trace-let decr-value () (- x 1)))]" (br) "      [(even? x) (+ (half (- x 1)) 1)])))")) ("with argument 5 results in the trace:") ((code "|(half 5)" (br) "| (decr-value)" (br) "| 4" (br) "|(half 4)" (br) "| (half 3)" (br) "| |(decr-value)" (br) "| |2" (br) "| (half 2)" (br) "| |(half 1)" (br) "| | (decr-value)" (br) "| | 0" (br) "| |(half 0)" (br) "| 1" (br) "|2"))) ("debug:s7" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-do ((" (var "var") " " (var "init") " " (var "update") ") ...) (" (var "test") " " (var "result") " ...) " (var "expr") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the last " (code (var "result")) " expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "trace-do") " expression is equivalent to a " (code "do") " expression with the same subforms, except that trace information is printed to the trace output port, showing the values of " (code (var "var") " ...") " and each iteration and the final value of the loop on termination. For example, the expression") ((code "(trace-do ([old '(a b c) (cdr old)]" (br) "           [new '() (cons (car old) new)])" (br) "  ((null? old) new))")) ("produces the trace") ((code "|(do (a b c) ())" (br) "|(do (b c) (a))" (br) "|(do (c) (b a))" (br) "|(do () (c b a))" (br) "|(c b a)")) ("and returns " (code "(c b a)") ".")) ("debug:s9" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace " (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of " (code (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of all currently traced top-level variables" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("In the first form, " (code "trace") " reassigns the top-level values of " (code (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...") ", whose values must be procedures, to equivalent procedures that display trace information in the manner of " (code "trace-lambda") ".") ((code "trace") " works by encapsulating the old value of each var in a traced procedure. It could be defined approximately as follows.  (The actual version records and returns information about traced variables.)") ((code "(define-syntax trace" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ var ...)" (br) "     (begin" (br) "       (set-top-level-value! 'var" (br) "         (let ([p (top-level-value 'var)])" (br) "           (trace-lambda var args (apply p args))))" (br) "       ...)]))")) ("Tracing for a procedure traced in this manner may be disabled via " (code "untrace") " (see below), an assignment of the corresponding variable to a different, untraced value, or a subsequent use of " (code "trace") " for the same variable. Because the value is traced and not the binding, however, a traced value obtained before tracing is disabled and retained after tracing is disabled will remain traced.") ((code "trace") " without subexpressions evaluates to a list of all currently traced variables. A variable is currently traced if it has been traced and not subsequently untraced or assigned to a different value.") ("The following transcript demonstrates the use of " (code "trace") " in an interactive session.") ((code "> (define half" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (cond" (br) "        [(zero? x) 0]" (br) "        [(odd? x) (half (- x 1))]" (br) "        [(even? x) (+ (half (- x 1)) 1)])))" (br) "> (half 5)" (br) "2" (br) "> (trace half)" (br) "(half)" (br) "> (half 5)" (br) "|(half 5)" (br) "|(half 4)" (br) "| (half 3)" (br) "| (half 2)" (br) "| |(half 1)" (br) "| |(half 0)" (br) "| |0" (br) "| 1" (br) "|2" (br) "2" (br) "> (define traced-half half)" (br) "> (untrace half)" (br) "(half)" (br) "> (half 2)" (br) "1" (br) "> (traced-half 2)" (br) "|(half 2)" (br) "|1" (br) "1"))) ("debug:s10" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(untrace " (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(untrace)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of untraced variables" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "untrace") " restores the original (pre-" (code "trace") ") top-level values of each currently traced variable in " (code (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...") ", effectively disabling the tracing of the values of these variables. Any variable in " (code (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ...") " that is not currently traced is ignored. If " (code "untrace") " is called without arguments, the values of all currently traced variables are restored.") ("The following transcript demonstrates the use of " (code "trace") " and " (code "untrace") " in an interactive session to debug an incorrect procedure definition.") ((code "> (define square-minus-one" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (- (* x x) 2)))" (br) "> (square-minus-one 3)" (br) "7" (br) "> (trace square-minus-one * -)" (br) "(square-minus-one * -)" (br) "> (square-minus-one 3)" (br) "|(square-minus-one 3)" (br) "| (* 3 3)" (br) "| 9" (br) "|(- 9 2)" (br) "|7" (br) "7" (br) "> (define square-minus-one" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (- (* x x) 1))) ; change the 2 to 1" (br) "> (trace)" (br) "(- *)" (br) "> (square-minus-one 3)" (br) "|(* 3 3)" (br) "|9" (br) "|(- 9 1)" (br) "|8" (br) "8" (br) "> (untrace square-minus-one)" (br) "()" (br) "> (untrace * -)" (br) "(- *)" (br) "> (square-minus-one 3)" (br) "8")) ("The first call to " (code "square-minus-one") " indicates there is an error, the second (traced) call indicates the step at which the error occurs, the third call demonstrates that the fix works, and the fourth call demonstrates that " (code "untrace") " does not wipe out the fix.")) ("debug:s11" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "trace-output-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "trace-output-port") " is a parameter that determines the output port to which tracing information is sent. When called with no arguments, " (code "trace-output-port") " returns the current trace output port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual output port, " (code "trace-output-port") " changes the value of the current trace output port.")) ("debug:s12" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "trace-print") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "trace-print") " must be a procedure of two arguments, an object and an output port. The trace package uses the value of " (code "trace-print") " to print the arguments and return values for each call to a traced procedure. " (code "trace-print") " is set to " (code "pretty-print") " by default.") ("The trace package sets " (code "pretty-initial-indent") "  to an appropriate value for the current nesting level before calling the value of " (code "trace-print") " so that multiline output can be indented properly.")) ("debug:s14" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-define " (var "var") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-define (" (var "var") " . " (var "idspec") ") " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "trace-define") " is a convenient shorthand for defining variables bound to traced procedures of the same name. The first form is equivalent to") ((code "(define " (var "var") (br) "  (let ([x " (var "expr") "])" (br) "    (trace-lambda " (var "var") " args" (br) "      (apply x args))))")) ("and the second is equivalent to") ((code "(define " (var "var") (br) "  (trace-lambda " (var "var") " " (var "idspec") (br) "    " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...))")) ("In the former case, " (code (var "expr")) " must evaluate to a procedure.") ((code "> (let ()" (br) "    (trace-define plus" (br) "      (lambda (x y) " (br) "        (+ x y)))" (br) "    (list (plus 3 4) (+ 5 6)))" (br) "|(plus 3 4)" (br) "|7" (br) "(7 11)"))) ("debug:s15" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(trace-define-syntax " (var "keyword") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "trace-define-syntax") " traces the input and output to the transformer value of " (code (var "expr")) ", stripped of the contextual information used by the expander to maintain lexical scoping.") ((code "> (trace-define-syntax let*" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ () b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (let () b1 b2 ...)]" (br) "      [(_ ((x e) m ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (let ((x e))" (br) "         (let* (m ...) b1 b2 ...))]))" (br) "> (let* ([x 3] [y (+ x x)]) (list x y))" (br) "|(let* (let* [(x 3) (y (+ x x))] [list x y]))" (br) "|(let ([x 3]) (let* ([y (+ x x)]) (list x y)))" (br) "|(let* (let* [(y (+ x x))] [list x y]))" (br) "|(let ([y (+ x x)]) (let* () (list x y)))" (br) "|(let* (let* () [list x y]))" (br) "|(let () (list x y))" (br) "(3 6)")) ("Without contextual information, the displayed forms are more readable but less precise, since different identifiers with the same name are indistinguishable, as shown in the example below.") ((code "> (let ([x 0])" (br) "    (trace-define-syntax a" (br) "      (syntax-rules ()" (br) "        [(_ y) (eq? x y)]))" (br) "    (let ([x 1])" (br) "      (a x)))" (br) "|(a (a x))" (br) "|(eq? x x)" (br) "#f"))) ("debug:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(debug)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When the default exception handler receives a serious or non-warning condition, it displays the condition and resets to the current café. Before it resets, it saves the condition in the parameter " (code "debug-condition") ". The " (code "debug") " procedure may be used to inspect the condition. Whenever one of the built-in error-reporting mechanisms is used to raise an exception, the continuation at the point where the exception was raised can be inspected as well. More generally, " (code "debug") " allows the continuation contained within any continuation condition created by " (code "make-continuation-condition") " to be inspected.") ("If the parameter " (code "debug-on-exception") " is set to " (code "#t") ", the default exception handler enters the debugger directly for all serious and non-warning conditions, delaying its reset until after the debugger exits. The " (code "--debug-on-exception") "  command-line option may be used to set " (code "debug-on-exception") " to " (code "#t") " from the command line, which is particularly useful when debugging scripts or top-level programs run via the " (code "--script") " or " (code "--program") "  command-line options.")) ("debug:s22" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(inspect " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Invokes the inspector on " (code (var "obj")) ", as described above. The commands recognized by the inspector are listed below, categorized by the type of the current object.") ((bold "Generally applicable commands")) ((code "help") " or " (code "h") " displays a brief description of how to use the inspector.") ((code "?") " displays commands applicable to the current type of object.") ((code "??") " displays the generally applicable commands.") ((code "print") " or " (code "p") " prints the current object (using " (code "pretty-print") ").") ((code "write") " or " (code "w") " writes the current object (using " (code "write") ").") ((code "size") " writes the size in bytes occupied by the current object (determined via " (code "compute-size") "), including any objects accessible from the current object except those for which the size was previously requested during the same interactive inspector session.") ((code "find") " " (code (var "expr")) " [ " (code (var "g")) " ] evaluates " (code (var "expr")) ", which should evaluate to a procedure of one argument, and searches (via " (code "make-object-finder") ") for the first occurrence of an object within the current object for which the predicate returns a true value, treating immediate values (e.g., fixnums), values in generations older than " (code (var "g")) ", and values already visited during the search as leaves. If " (code (var "g")) " is not unspecified, it defaults to the current maximum generation, i.e., the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ". If specified, " (code (var "g")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer less than or equal to the current maximum generation or the symbol " (code "static") "  representing the static generation. If such an object is found, the inspector's focus moves to that object as if through a series of steps that lead from the current object to the located object, so that the " (code "up") " command can be used to determine where the object was found relative to the original object.") ((code "find-next") " repeats the last " (code "find") ", locating an occurrence not previously found, if any.") ((code "up") " or " (code "u") " " (code (var "n")) " returns to the " (code (var "nth")) " previous level. Used to move outwards in the structure of the inspected object. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 1.") ((code "top") " or " (code "t") " returns to the outermost level of the inspected object.") ((code "forward") " or " (code "f") " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " next expression. Used to move from one element to another of an object containing a sequence of elements, such as a list, vector, record, frame, or closure. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 1.") ((code "back") " or " (code "b") " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " previous expression. Used to move from one element to another of an object containing a sequence of elements, such as a list, vector, record, frame, or closure. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 1.") ((code "=>") " " (code (var "expr")) " sends the current object to the procedure value of " (code (var "expr")) ". " (code (var "expr")) " may begin on the current or following line and may span multiple lines.") ((code "file") " " (code (var "path")) " opens the source file at the specified path for listing. The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files.") ((code "list") " " (code (var "line")) " " (code (var "count")) " lists " (code (var "count")) " lines of the current source file (see " (code "file") ") starting at " (code (var "line")) ". " (code (var "line")) " defaults to the end of the previous set of lines listed and " (code (var "count")) " defaults to ten or the number of lines previously listed. If " (code (var "line")) " is negative, listing begins " (code (var "line")) " lines before the previous set of lines listed.") ((code "files") " shows the currently open source files.") ((code "mark") " or " (code "m") " " (code (var "m")) " marks the current location with the symbolic mark " (code (var "m")) ". If " (code (var "m")) " is not specified, the current location is marked with a unique default mark.") ((code "goto") " or " (code "g") " " (code (var "m")) " returns to the location marked " (code (var "m")) ". If " (code (var "m")) " is not specified, the inspector returns to the location marked with the default mark.") ((code "new-cafe") " or " (code "n") " enters a new read-eval-print loop (café), giving access to the normal top-level environment.") ((code "quit") " or " (code "q") " exits from the inspector.") ((code "reset") " or " (code "r") " resets to the current café.") ((code "abort") " or " (code "a") " " (code (var "x")) " aborts from Scheme with exit status " (code (var "x")) ", which defaults to -1.") ((bold "Continuation commands")) ((code "show-frames") " or " (code "sf") " shows the next " (code (var "n")) " frames. If " (code (var "n")) " is not specified, all frames are displayed.") ((code "depth") " displays the number of frames in the continuation.") ((code "down") " or " (code "d") " " (code (var "n")) " move to the " (code (var "nth")) " frame down in the continuation. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 1.") ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the continuation (next frame) and, if available, the calling procedure source, the pending call source, the closure, and the frame and free-variable values. Source is available only if generation of inspector information was enabled during compilation of the corresponding lambda expression.") ((code "show-local") " or " (code "sl") " is like " (code "show") " or " (code "s") "  except that free variable values are not shown.  (If present, free variable values can be found by inspecting the closure.)") ((code "length") " or " (code "l") " displays the number of elements in the topmost frame of the continuation.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " or named frame element.  " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0. If multiple elements have the same name, only one is accessible by name, and the others must be accessed by number.") ((code "code") " or " (code "c") " moves to the source for the calling procedure.") ((code "call") " moves to the source for the pending call.") ((code "file") " opens the source file containing the pending call, if known. The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the list of source directories searched for source files identified by relative path names.") ("For absolute pathnames starting with a " (code "/") " (or " (code "\\") " or a directory specifier under Windows), the inspector tries the absolute pathname first, then looks for the last (filename) component of the path in the list of source directories.  For pathnames starting with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") " under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows), the inspector looks in " (code "\".\"") " or " (code "\"..\"") " first, as appropriate, then for the entire " (code ".") "- or " (code "..") "-prefixed pathname in the source directories, then for the last (filename) component in the source directories. For other (relative) pathnames, the inspector looks for the entire relative pathname in the list of source directories, then the last (filename) component in the list of source directories. ") ("If a file by the same name as but different contents from the original source file is found during this process, it will be skipped over. This typically happens because the file has been modified since it was compiled. Pass an explicit filename argument to force opening of a particular file (see the generally applicable commands above).") ((code "eval") " or " (code "e") " " (code (var "expr")) " evaluates the expression " (code (var "expr")) " in an environment containing bindings for the elements of the frame.  Within the evaluated expression, the value of each frame element " (code (var "n")) " is accessible via the variable " (code "%" (var "n")) ". Named elements are accessible via their names as well.  Names are available only if generation of inspector information was enabled during compilation of the corresponding lambda expression.") ((code "set!") " or " (code "!") " " (code (var "n")) " " (code (var "e")) " sets the value of the " (code (var "nth")) " frame element to " (code (var "e")) ", if the frame element corresponds to an assignable variable. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((bold "Procedure commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the source and free variables of the procedure. Source is available only if generation of inspector information was enabled during compilation of the corresponding lambda expression.") ((code "code") " or " (code "c") " moves to the source for the procedure.") ((code "file") " opens the file containing the procedure's source code, if known. See the description of the continuation " (code "file") " entry above for more information.") ((code "length") " or " (code "l") " displays the number of free variables whose values are recorded in the procedure object.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " or named free variable.  " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0. If multiple free variables have the same name, only one is accessible by name, and the others must be accessed by number.") ((code "set!") " or " (code "!") " " (code (var "n")) " " (code (var "e")) " sets the value of the " (code (var "nth")) " free variable to " (code (var "e")) ", if the variable is assignable. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((code "eval") " or " (code "e") " " (code (var "expr")) " evaluates the expression " (code (var "expr")) " in an environment containing bindings for the free variables of the procedure. Within the evaluated expression, the value of each free variable " (code (var "n")) " is accessible via the variable " (code "%" (var "n")) ". Named free variables are accessible via their names as well. Names are available only if generation of inspector information was enabled during compilation of the corresponding lambda expression.") ((bold "Pair (list) commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " " (code (var "n")) " shows the first " (code (var "n")) " elements of the list. If " (code (var "n")) " is not specified, all elements are displayed.") ((code "length") " or " (code "l") " displays the list length.") ((code "car") " moves to the object in the car of the current object.") ((code "cdr") " moves to the object in the cdr.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " element of the list. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((code "tail") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " cdr of the list. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 1.") ((bold "Vector, Bytevector, and Fxvector commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " " (code (var "n")) " shows the first " (code (var "n")) " elements of the vector. If " (code (var "n")) " is not specified, all elements are displayed.") ((code "length") " or " (code "l") " displays the vector length.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " element of the vector. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((bold "String commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " " (code (var "n")) " shows the first " (code (var "n")) " elements of the string. If " (code (var "n")) " is not specified, all elements are displayed.") ((code "length") " or " (code "l") " displays the string length.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " element of the string. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((code "unicode") " " (code (var "n")) " displays the first " (code (var "n")) " elements of the string as hexadecimal Unicode scalar values.") ((code "ascii") " " (code (var "n")) " displays the first " (code (var "n")) " elements of the string as hexadecimal ASCII values, using " (code "--") " to denote characters whose Unicode scalar values are not in the ASCII range.") ((bold "Symbol commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the fields of the symbol.") ((code "value") " or " (code "v") " moves to the top-level value of the symbol.") ((code "name") " or " (code "n") " moves to the name of the symbol.") ((code "property-list") " or " (code "pl") " moves to the property list of the symbol.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " field of the symbol. Field 0 is the top-level value of the symbol, field 1  is the symbol's name, and field 2 is its property list. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.") ((bold "Character commands")) ((code "unicode") " displays the hexadecimal Unicode scalar value for the character.") ((code "ascii") " displays the hexadecimal ASCII code for the character, using " (code "--") " to denote characters whose Unicode scalar values are not in the ASCII range.") ((bold "Box commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the contents of the box.") ((code "unbox") " or " (code "ref") " or " (code "r") " moves to the boxed object.") ((bold "Port commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the fields of the port, including the input and output size, index, and buffer fields.") ((code "name") " moves to the port's name.") ((code "handler") " moves to the port's handler.") ((code "output-buffer") " or " (code "ob") " moves to the port's output buffer.") ((code "input-buffer") " or " (code "ib") " moves to the port's input buffer.") ((bold "Record commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the contents of the record.") ((code "fields") " moves to the list of field names of the record.") ((code "name") " moves to the name of the record.") ((code "rtd") " moves to the record-type descriptor of the record.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "name")) " moves to the named field of the record, if accessible.") ((code "set!") " or " (code "!") " " (code (var "name")) " " (code (var "value")) " sets the value of the named field of the record, if mutable.") ((bold "Transport Link Cell (TLC) commands")) ((code "show") " or " (code "s") " shows the fields of the TLC.") ((code "keyval") " moves to the keyval of the TLC.") ((code "tconc") " moves to the tconc of the TLC.") ((code "next") " moves to the next link of the TLC.") ((code "ref") " or " (code "r") " " (code (var "n")) " moves to the " (code (var "nth")) " field of the symbol. Field 0 is the keyval, field 1 the tconc, and field 2 the next link. " (code (var "n")) " defaults to 0.")) ("debug:s25" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(inspect/object " (var "object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an inspector object procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "inspect/object") " is used to turn an ordinary Scheme object into an inspector object. All inspector objects accept the messages " (code "type") ", " (code "print") ", " (code "write") ", and " (code "size") ". The " (code "type") " message returns a symbolic representation of the type of the object. The " (code "print") " and " (code "write") " messages must be accompanied by a port parameter. They cause a representation of the object to be written to the port, using the Scheme procedures " (code "pretty-print") " and " (code "write") ". The " (code "size") " message returns a fixnum representing the size in bytes occupied by the object, including any objects accessible from the current object except those for which the size was already requested via an inspector object derived from the argument of the same " (code "inspect/object") " call.") ("All inspector objects except for variable inspector objects accept the message " (code "value") ", which returns the actual object encapsulated in the inspector object.") ((code "(define x (inspect/object '(1 2 3)))" (br) "(x 'type) " (math "=>") " pair" (br) "(define p (open-output-string))" (br) "(x 'write p)" (br) "(get-output-string p) " (math "=>") " \"(1 2 3)\"" (br) "(x 'length) " (math "=>") " (proper 3)" (br) "(define y (x 'car))" (br) "(y 'type) " (math "=>") " simple" (br) "(y 'value) " (math "=>") " 1")) ((bold "Pair inspector objects.") "  Pair inspector objects contain Scheme pairs.") ((code "(") (var "pair-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "pair") ".") ((code "(") (var "pair-object") " " (code "'car") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the \"car\" field of the pair.") ((code "(") (var "pair-object") " " (code "'cdr") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the \"cdr\" field of the pair.") ((code "(") (var "pair-object") " " (code "'length") (code ")") "  returns a list of the form (" (code (var "type")) " " (code (var "count")) "). The type field contains the symbol " (code "proper") ", the symbol " (code "improper") ", or the symbol " (code "circular") ", depending on the structure of the list. The count field contains the number of distinct pairs in the list.") ((bold "Box inspector objects.") "  Box inspector objects contain " (var "Chez Scheme") " boxes.") ((code "(") (var "box-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "box") ".") ((code "(") (var "box-object") " " (code "'unbox") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the contents of the box.") ((bold "TLC inspector objects.") "  TLC inspector objects contain " (var "Chez Scheme") " transport link cells.") ((code "(") (var "tlc-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "tlc") ".") ((code "(") (var "tlc-object") " " (code "'keyval") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the TLC's keyval.") ((code "(") (var "tlc-object") " " (code "'tconc") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the TLC's tconc.") ((code "(") (var "tlc-object") " " (code "'next") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the TLC's next link.") ((bold "Vector, String, Bytevector, and Fxvector inspector objects.") "  Vector (bytevector, string, fxvector) inspector objects contain Scheme vectors (bytevectors, strings, fxvectors).") ((code "(") (var "vector-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "vector") " (" (code "string") ", " (code "bytevector") ", " (code "fxvector") ").") ((code "(") (var "vector-object") " " (code "'length") (code ")") "  returns the number of elements in the vector or string.") ((code "(") (var "vector-object") " " (code "'ref") " " (code (var "n")) (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the " (code (var "nth")) " element of the vector or string.") ((bold "Simple inspector objects.") "  Simple inspector objects contain unstructured, unmodifiable objects. These include numbers, booleans, the empty list, the end-of-file object, and the void object. They may be examined directly by asking for the " (code "value") " of the object.") ((code "(") (var "simple-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "simple") ".") ((bold "Unbound inspector objects.") "  Although unbound objects are not normally accessible to Scheme programs, they may be encountered when inspecting variables.") ((code "(") (var "unbound-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "unbound") ".") ((bold "Procedure inspector objects.") "  Procedure inspector objects contain Scheme procedures.") ((code "(") (var "procedure-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "procedure") ".") ((code "(") (var "procedure-object") " " (code "'length") (code ")") "  returns the number of free variables.") ((code "(") (var "procedure-object") " " (code "'ref") " " (code (var "n")) (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the " (code (var "nth")) " free variable of the procedure. See the description below of variable inspector objects. " (code (var "n")) " must be nonnegative and less than the length of the procedure.") ((code "(") (var "procedure-object") " " (code "'eval") " " (code (var "expr")) (code ")") "  evaluates " (code (var "expr")) " and returns its value. The values of the procedure's free variables are bound within the evaluated expression to identifiers of the form %" (var "n") ", where " (var "n") " is the location number displayed by the inspector. The values of named variables are also bound to their names.") ((code "(") (var "procedure-object") " " (code "'code") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the procedure's code object. See the description below of code inspector objects.") ((bold "Continuation inspector objects.") "  Continuations created by " (code "call/cc") " are actually procedures. However, when inspecting such a procedure the underlying data structure that embodies the continuation may be exposed. A continuation structure contains the location at which computation is to resume, the variable values necessary to perform the computation, and a link to the next continuation.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "continuation") ".") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'length") (code ")") "  returns the number of free variables.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'ref") " " (code (var "n")) (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the " (code (var "nth")) " free variable of the continuation. See the description below of variable inspector objects. " (code (var "n")) " must be nonnegative and less than the length of the continuation.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'eval") " " (code (var "expr")) (code ")") "  evaluates " (code (var "expr")) " and returns its value. The values of frame locations are bound within the evaluated expression to identifiers of the form %" (var "n") ", where " (var "n") " is the location number displayed by the inspector. The values of named locations are also bound to their names.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'code") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the code object for the procedure that was active when the current continuation frame was created. See the description below of code inspector objects.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'depth") (code ")") "  returns the number of frames in the continuation.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'link") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the next continuation frame. The depth must be greater than 1.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'link*") " " (code (var "n")) (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the " (code (var "nth")) " continuation link. " (code (var "n")) " must be less than the depth.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'source") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the source information attached to the continuation (representing the source for the application that resulted in the formation of the continuation) or " (code "#f") " if no source information is attached.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'source-object") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the source object for the procedure application that resulted in the formation of the continuation or " (code "#f") " if no source object is attached.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'source-path") (code ")") "  attempts to find the pathname of the file containing the source for the procedure application that resulted in the formation of the continuation. If successful, three values are returned to identify the file and position of the application within the file: " (code (var "path")) ", " (code (var "line")) ", and " (code (var "char")) ". Two values, a file name and an absolute character position, are returned if the file name is known but the named file cannot be found. The search may be unsuccessful even if a file by the expected name is found in the path if the file has been modified since the source code was compiled. If no file name is known, no values are returned. The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files identified by relative path names.") ((bold "Code inspector objects.") "  Code inspector objects contain " (var "Chez Scheme") " code objects.") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "code") ".") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'name") (code ")") "  returns a string or " (code "#f") ". The name associated with a code inspector object is the name of the variable to which the procedure was originally bound or assigned. Since the binding of a variable can be changed, this name association may not always be accurate. " (code "#f") " is returned if the inspector cannot determine a name for the procedure.") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'realm") (code ")") "  returns a symbol or " (code "#f") ". The realm of a code object is determined by the " (code "compile-procedure-realm") " parameter at the time that the code object is compiled.") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'source") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the source information attached to the code object or " (code "#f") " if no source information is attached.") ((code "(") (var "continuation-object") " " (code "'source-object") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the source object for the code object or " (code "#f") " if no source object is attached.") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'source-path") (code ")") "  attempts to find the pathname of the file containing the source for the lambda expression that produced the code object. If successful, three values are returned to identify the file and position of the application within the file: " (code (var "path")) ", " (code (var "line")) ", and " (code (var "char")) ". Two values, a file name and an absolute character position, are returned if the file name is known but the named file cannot be found. The search may be unsuccessful even if a file by the expected name is found in the path if the file has been modified since the source code was compiled. If no file name is known, no values are returned. The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files identified by relative path names.") ((code "(") (var "code-object") " " (code "'free-count") (code ")") "  returns the number of free variables in any procedure for which this is the corresponding code.") ((bold "Variable inspector objects.") "  Variable inspector objects encapsulate variable bindings. Although the actual underlying representation varies, the variable inspector object provides a uniform interface.") ((code "(") (var "variable-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "variable") ".") ((code "(") (var "variable-object") " " (code "'name") (code ")") "  returns a symbol or " (code "#f") ". " (code "#f") " is returned if the name is not available or if the variable is a compiler-generated temporary variable. Variable names are not retained when the parameter " (code "generate-inspector-information") "  (page " (link "./system.html#g122" "12.6") ") is false during compilation.") ((code "(") (var "variable-object") " " (code "'ref") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the current value of the variable.") ((code "(") (var "variable-object") " " (code "'set!") " " (code (var "e")) (code ")") "  returns unspecified, after setting the current value of the variable to " (code (var "e")) ". An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the variable is not assignable.") ((bold "Port inspector objects.") "  Port inspector objects contain ports.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "port") ".") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'input?") (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the port is an input port, " (code "#f") " otherwise.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'output?") (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the port is an output port, " (code "#f") " otherwise.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'binary?") (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the port is a binary port, " (code "#f") " otherwise.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'closed?") (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the port is closed, " (code "#f") " if the port is open.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'name") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the port's name.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'handler") (code ")") "  returns a procedure inspector object encapsulating the port handler, such as would be returned by " (code "port-handler") ".") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'output-size") (code ")") "  returns the output buffer size as a fixnum if the port is an output port (otherwise the value is unspecified).") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'output-index") (code ")") "  returns the output buffer index as a fixnum if the port is an output port (otherwise the value is unspecified).") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'output-buffer") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the string used for buffered output.") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'input-size") (code ")") "  returns the input buffer size as a fixnum if the port is an input port (otherwise the value is unspecified).") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'input-index") (code ")") "  returns the input buffer index as a fixnum if the port is an input port (otherwise the value is unspecified).") ((code "(") (var "port-object") " " (code "'input-buffer") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the string used for buffered input.") ((bold "Symbol inspector objects.") "  Symbol inspector objects contain symbols. These include gensyms.") ((code "(") (var "symbol-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "symbol") ".") ((code "(") (var "symbol-object") " " (code "'name") (code ")") "  returns a string inspector object. The string name associated with a symbol inspector object is the print representation of a symbol, such as would be returned by the procedure " (code "symbol->string") ".") ((code "(") (var "symbol-object") " " (code "'gensym?") (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the symbol is a gensym, " (code "#f") " otherwise. Gensyms are created by " (code "gensym") ".") ((code "(") (var "symbol-object") " " (code "'top-level-value") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the global value of the symbol.") ((code "(") (var "symbol-object") " " (code "'property-list") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the property list for the symbol.") ((bold "Record inspector objects.") "  Record inspector objects contain records.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'type") (code ")") "  returns the symbol " (code "record") ".") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'name") (code ")") "  returns a string inspector object corresponding to the name of the record type.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'fields") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing a list of the field names of the record type.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'length") (code ")") "  returns the number of fields.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'rtd") (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the record-type descriptor of the record type.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'accessible?") " " (code (var "name")) (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the named field is accessible, " (code "#f") " otherwise. A field may be inaccessible if optimized away by the compiler.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'ref") " " (code (var "name")) (code ")") "  returns an inspector object containing the value of the named field. An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the named field is not accessible.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'mutable?") " " (code (var "name")) (code ")") "  returns " (code "#t") " if the named field is mutable, " (code "#f") " otherwise. A field is immutable if it is not declared mutable or if the compiler optimizes away all assignments to the field.") ((code "(") (var "record-object") " " (code "'set!") " " (code (var "name")) " " (code (var "value")) (code ")") "  sets the value of the named field to " (code (var "value")) ". An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the named field is not assignable.")) ("debug:s26" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-object-finder " (var "pred") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-object-finder " (var "pred") " " (var "g") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-object-finder " (var "pred") " " (var "x") " " (var "g") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "make-object-finder") " takes a predicate " (code (var "pred")) " and two optional arguments: a starting point " (code (var "x")) " and a maximum generation " (code (var "g")) ". The starting point defaults to the value of the procedure " (code "oblist") ", and the maximum generation defaults to the value of the parameter " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ". " (code "make-object-finder") " returns an object finder " (code (var "p")) " that can be used to search for objects satisfying " (code (var "pred")) " within the starting-point object " (code (var "x")) ". Immediate objects and objects in generations older than " (code (var "g")) " are treated as leaves. " (code (var "p")) " is a procedure accepting no arguments. If an object " (code (var "y")) " satisfying " (code (var "pred")) " can be found starting with " (code (var "x")) ", " (code (var "p")) " returns a list whose first element is " (code (var "y")) " and whose remaining elements represent the path of objects from " (code (var "x")) " to " (code (var "y")) ", listed in reverse order. " (code (var "p")) " can be invoked multiple times to find additional objects satisfying the predicate, if any. " (code (var "p")) " returns " (code "#f") " if no more objects matching the predicate can be found.") ((code (var "p")) " maintains internal state recording where it has been so it can restart at the point of the last found object and not return the same object twice. The state can be several times the size of the starting-point object " (code (var "x")) " and all that is reachable from " (code (var "x")) ".") ("The interactive inspector provides a convenient interface to the object finder in the form of " (code "find") " and " (code "find-next") " commands.") ("Relocation tables for static code objects are discarded by default, which prevents object finders from providing accurate results when static code objects are involved. That is, they will not find any objects pointed to directly from a code object that has been promoted to the static generation. If this is a problem, the command-line argument " (code "--retain-static-relocation") "  can be used to prevent the relocation tables from being discarded.")) ("debug:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-size " (var "object") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-size " (var "object") " " (var "generation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "object")) " can be any object. " (code (var "generation")) " must be a fixnum between 0 and the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ", inclusive, or the symbol " (code "static") ". If " (code (var "generation")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ".") ((code "compute-size") " returns the amount of memory, in bytes, occupied by " (code (var "object")) " and anything reachable from " (code (var "object")) " in any generation less than or equal to " (code (var "generation")) ". Immediate values such as fixnums, booleans, and characters have zero size. Size computation for a thread is limited when the thread is still active, since its full continuation cannot be inspected in that case, but the full continuation is inspected if the thread is inactive (such as during a garbage collection rendezvous when a different thread is selected by the rendezvous).") ("The following examples are valid for machines with 32-bit pointers.") ((code "(compute-size 0) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(compute-size (cons 0 0)) " (math "=>") " 8" (br) "(compute-size (cons (vector #t #f) 0)) " (math "=>") " 24" (br) (br) "(compute-size" (br) "  (let ([x (cons 0 0)])" (br) "    (set-car! x x)" (br) "    (set-cdr! x x)" (br) "    x))                  " (math "=>") " 8" (br) (br) "(define-record-type frob (fields x))" (br) "(collect 1 1) ; force rtd into generation 1" (br) "(compute-size" (br) "  (let ([x (make-frob 0)])" (br) "    (cons x x))" (br) "  0)                       " (math "=>") " 16"))) ("debug:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-composition " (var "object") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-composition " (var "object") " " (var "generation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "object")) " can be any object. " (code (var "generation")) " must be a fixnum between 0 and the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ", inclusive, or the symbol " (code "static") ". If " (code (var "generation")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ".") ((code "compute-composition") " returns an association list representing the composition of " (code (var "object")) ", including anything reachable from it in any generation less than or equal to " (code (var "generation")) ". The association list has the following structure:") ((code "((" (var "type") " " (var "count") " . " (var "bytes") ") ...)")) ((code (var "type")) " is either the name of a primitive type, represented as a symbol, e.g., " (code "pair") ", or a record-type descriptor (rtd). " (code (var "count")) " and " (code (var "bytes")) " are nonnegative fixnums.") ("Immediate values such as fixnums, booleans, and characters are not included in the composition.") ("The following examples are valid for machines with 32-bit pointers.") ((code "(compute-composition 0) " (math "=>") " ()" (br) "(compute-composition (cons 0 0)) " (math "=>") " ((pair 1 . 8))" (br) "(compute-composition" (br) "  (cons (vector #t #f) 0)) " (math "=>") " ((pair 1 . 8) (vector 1 . 16))" (br) (br) "(compute-composition" (br) "  (let ([x (cons 0 0)])" (br) "    (set-car! x x)" (br) "    (set-cdr! x x)" (br) "    x))                 " (math "=>") " ((pair 1 . 8)" (br) (br) "(define-record-type frob (fields x))" (br) "(collect 1 1) ; force rtd into generation 1" (br) "(compute-composition" (br) "  (let ([x (make-frob 0)])" (br) "    (cons x x))" (br) "  0)                       " (math "=>") " ((pair 1 . 8)" (br) "                                (#<record type frob> 1 . 8))"))) ("debug:s32" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-size-increments " (var "list") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compute-size-increments " (var "list") " " (var "generation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list as described below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "list")) " must be a list, but each element can be any object. " (code (var "generation")) " must be a fixnum between 0 and the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ", inclusive, or the symbol " (code "static") ". If " (code (var "generation")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ". In the threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ", the thread that invokes " (code "compute-size-increments") " must be the only active thread.") ((code "compute-size-increments") " is like mapping " (code "compute-size") "  over " (code (var "list")) ", except that any object reachable from an earlier element of " (code (var "list")) " is not treated as reachable by a later element of " (code (var "list")) ". In addition, each immediate element of " (code (var "list")) " is not treated as reachable by earlier elements of " (code (var "list")) "---although other values reachable from later elements of " (code (var "list")) " may be considered reachable from earlier elements.") ("Unlike " (code (var "compute-size")) ", " (code "compute-size-increments") " does not consider the " (code "car") " of a weak pair reachable from the weak pair. It also does not consider the " (code "car") " or " (code "cdr") " of an ephemeron pair to be reachable from the ephemeron pair, unless the " (code "car") " is already determined to be reachable (perhaps from an earlier element of " (code (var "list")) "); if the " (code "car") " of an ephemeron pair is discovered to be reachable later (perhaps from a later element of " (code (var "list")) "), then the " (code "cdr") " of the ephemeron pair is considered to be reachable from the " (code "car") ", which has the effect of charging the memory of the " (code "cdr") " to the same element of " (code (var "list")) " as the memory of the " (code "car") ".") ("The following examples are valid for machines with 32-bit pointers.") ((code "(compute-size-increments (list 0)) " (math "=>") " (0)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (list (cons 0 0))) " (math "=>") " (8)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (list (cons 0 0) (cons 0 0))) " (math "=>") " (8 8)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (let ([p (cons 0 0)])" (br) "                           (list p p))) " (math "=>") " (8 0)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (let ([p (cons 0 0)])" (br) "                           (list (cons 1 p) (cons 1 p)))) " (math "=>") " (16 8)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (list (ephemeron-cons 0 0))) " (math "=>") " (16)" (br) "(compute-size-increments (let* ([p (cons 0 0)]" (br) "                                [e (ephemeron-cons p (cons 0 0))])" (br) "                           (list e p))) " (math "=>") " (16 16)"))) ("expeditor:s0" ((bold "module") ": " (code "expression-editor") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "expression-editor") " module exports a set of bindings for parameters and other procedures that can be used to modify how the expression editor interacts with the user, including the particular keys used to invoke the various editing commands.") ("Basic use of the expression editor is described in Section " (link "./use.html#g7" "2.2") ".")) ("expeditor:s1" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-auto-indent")) ("The value of " (code "ee-auto-indent") " is a boolean value that determines whether the expression editor indents expressions as they are entered. Its default value is " (code "#t") ".")) ("expeditor:s2" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-standard-indent")) ("The value of " (code "ee-standard-indent") " is a nonnegative fixnum value that determines the amount (in single spaces) by which each expression is indented relative to the enclosing expression, if not aligned otherwise by one of the indenter's other heuristics, when " (code "ee-auto-indent") " is true or when one of the indentation commands is invoked explicitly. It's default value is " (code "2") ".")) ("expeditor:s3" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-auto-paren-balance")) ("The value of " (code "ee-auto-paren-balance") " is a boolean value that determines whether the expression editor automatically corrects a close parenthesis or bracket, when typed, to match the corresponding open parenthesis or bracket, if any. Its default value is " (code "#t") ".")) ("expeditor:s4" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-flash-parens")) ("The value of " (code "ee-flash-parens") " is a boolean value that determines whether the expression editor briefly moves the cursor when an open or close parenthesis or bracket is typed to the matching close or open parenthesis or bracket (if any). Its default value is " (code "#t") ".")) ("expeditor:s5" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-paren-flash-delay")) ("The value of " (code "ee-paren-flash-delay") " is a nonnegative fixnum value that determines the amount of time (in milliseconds) that the expression editor pauses when the cursor is moved to the matching parenthesis or bracket, if any, when a parenthesis or bracket is entered. The value is ignored if the " (code "ee-flash-parens") " is false. Its default value is " (code "100") ".")) ("expeditor:s6" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-default-repeat")) ("The value of " (code "ee-default-repeat") " is a nonnegative fixnum value that determines the number of times the next command is repeated after the " (code "ee-command-repeat") " editing command (bound to " (code "Esc-^U") " by default) is used and " (var "not") "  followed by a sequence of digits. It's default value is " (code "4") ".")) ("expeditor:s7" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-noisy")) ("The value of " (code "ee-noisy") " is a boolean value that determines whether the expression editor emits a beep (bell) when an error occurs, such as an attempt to find the matching delimiter for a non-delimiter character. Its default value is " (code "#f") ".")) ("expeditor:s8" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-history-limit")) ("The value of " (code "ee-history-limit") " is a nonnegative fixnum value that determines the number of history entries retained by the expression editor during and across sessions. Only the last " (code "(ee-history-limit)") " entries are retained.")) ("expeditor:s9" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "ee-common-identifiers")) ("The value of " (code "ee-common-identifiers") " is list of symbols that are considered common enough that they should appear early when one of the incremental identifier-completion editing commands is invoked. Its default value contains a few dozen entries. They are all more than a few characters long (under the theory that users will most likely type short ones out fully) and all would appear later than they likely should when incremental identifier-completion is used.")) ("expeditor:s10" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ee-bind-key " (var "key") " " (var "procedure") ")")) ((br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified") ("The " (code "ee-bind-key") " procedure is used to add to or change the set of key bindings recognized by the expression editor.") ("The " (code (var "key")) " must be a character or string; if it is a string, it must have the following form.") ("      " (table (row (cell "<key-string>") (cell (math "-->")) (cell (code "\"") "<key-char>" (sup "+") (code "\""))))) ("where") ("      " (table (row (cell "<key-char>") (cell (math "-->")) (cell (code "\\\\e") " (specifying an escape character)")) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "^" (var "x")) " (specifying control-" (code (var "x")) ")")) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "\\\\^") " (specifying caret)")) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "\\\\\\\\") " (specifying back slash)")) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "plain char") " (any character other than " (code "\\") " or " (code "^") ")")))) ("Note that each double-backslash in the syntax actually denotes just one backslash in the string.") ("For example, the " (code (var "key")) " " (code "\"\\\\eX\"") " represents the two-character sequence Escape-x, i.e., the \"escape\" key followed by the (capital) \"X\" key. Similarly, they " (code (var "key")) " " (code "\"\\\\e^X\"") " represents the two-character sequence Escape-Control-x, i.e., the \"escape\" key followed by Control-X.") ("Character keys and string keys consisting of a single plain character always represent a single keystroke.") ("The " (code (var "procedure")) " argument should normally be one of the built-in editing commands described below. It is also possible to define new editing commands with " (code "ee-string-macro") "  and " (code "ee-compose") ".")) ("expeditor:s77" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ee-string-macro " (var "string") ")")) ((br) (bold "returns: ") "a new editing command") ("The new editing command produced inserts " (code (var "string")) " before the current cursor position.") ("Two string macros are predefined:") ((table (row (cell (code "(ee-string-macro \"(define \")") " ") (cell "   escape-d")) (row (cell (code "(ee-string-macro \"(lambda \")") " ") (cell "   escape-l"))))) ("expeditor:s78" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ee-compose " (var "ecmd") " ...)")) ((br) (bold "returns: ") "a new editing command") ("Each " (code (var "ecmd")) " must be an editing command.") ("The new editing command runs each of the editing commands " (code (var "ecmd") " ...") " in sequence.") ("For example, the following expression binds " (code "^X") "-p to an editing command that behaves like " (code "ee-history-bwd-prefix") " but leaves the cursor at the end of the expression rather than at the end of the first line, causing the entire entry to be displayed.") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (import expression-editor)" (br) "  (ee-bind-key \"^Xp\"" (br) "    (ee-compose ee-history-bwd ee-end-of-entry)))")) ("A command such as " (code "ee-id-completion") " that performs a different action when run twice in succession will not recognize that it has been run twice in succession if run as part of a composite command.")) ("foreign:s4" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(system " (var "command") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "command")) " must be a string.") ("The " (code "system") " procedure creates a subprocess to perform the operation specified by " (code (var "command")) ". The subprocess may communicate with the user through the same console input and console output files used by the Scheme process. After creating the subprocess, " (code "system") " waits for the process to exit before returning.") ("When the subprocess exits, " (code "system") " returns the exit code for the subprocess, unless (on Unix-based systems) a signal caused the subprocess to terminate, in which case " (code "system") " returns the negation of the signal that caused the termination, e.g., -1 for " (code "SIGHUP") ".")) ("foreign:s5" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-process-ports " (var "command") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-process-ports " (var "command") " " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-process-ports " (var "command") " " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "command")) " must be a string. If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure creates textual ports, each of whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns binary ports. " (code (var "b-mode")) " specifies the buffer mode used by each of the ports returned by this procedure and defaults to " (code "block") ". Buffer modes are described in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./io.html#g123" "7.2") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ".") ((code "open-process-ports") " creates a subprocess to perform the operation specified by " (code (var "command")) ". Unlike " (code "system") ", " (code "process") " returns immediately after creating the subprocess, i.e., without waiting for the subprocess to terminate. It returns four values:") ((li (code (var "to-stdin")) " is an output port to which Scheme can send output to the subprocess through the subprocess's standard input file.   ") (li (code (var "from-stdout")) " is an input port from which Scheme can read input from the subprocess through the subprocess's standard output file.   ") (li (code (var "from-stderr")) " is an input port from which Scheme can read input from the subprocess through the subprocess's standard error file.   ") (li (code (var "process-id")) " is an integer identifying the created subprocess provided by the host operating system.")) ("If the process exits or closes its standard output file descriptor, any procedure that reads input from " (code (var "from-stdout")) " will return an end-of-file object. Similarly, if the process exits or closes its standard error file descriptor, any procedure that reads input from " (code (var "from-stderr")) " will return an end-of-file object.") ("The predicate " (code "input-port-ready?") "  may be used to detect whether input has been sent by the subprocess to Scheme.") ("It is sometimes necessary to force output to be sent immediately to the subprocess by invoking " (code "flush-output-port") " on " (code (var "to-stdin")) ", since " (var "Chez Scheme") " buffers the output for efficiency.") ("On UNIX systems, the " (code (var "process-id")) " is the process identifier for the shell created to execute " (code (var "command")) ". If " (code (var "command")) " is used to invoke an executable file rather than a shell command, it may be useful to prepend " (code (var "command")) " with the string " (code "\"exec \"") ", which causes the shell to load and execute the named executable directly, without forking a new process---the shell equivalent of a tail call. This will reduce by one the number of subprocesses created and cause " (code (var "process-id")) " to reflect the process identifier for the  executable once the shell has transferred control.")) ("foreign:s7" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(process " (var "command") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "command")) " must be a string.") ((code "process") " is similar to " (code "open-process-ports") ", but less general. It does not return a port from which the subprocess's standard error output can be read, and it always creates textual ports. It returns a list of three values rather than the four separate values of " (code "open-process-ports") ". The returned list contains, in order: " (code (var "from-stdout")) ", " (code (var "to-stdin")) ", and " (code (var "process-id")) ", which correspond to the second, first, and fourth return values of " (code "open-process-ports") ".")) ("foreign:s10" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(foreign-procedure " (var "conv") " ... " (var "entry-exp") " (" (var "param-type") " ...) " (var "res-type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "entry-exp")) " must evaluate to a string representing a valid foreign procedure entry point or an integer representing the address of the foreign procedure. The " (code (var "param-types")) " and " (code (var "res-type")) " must be symbols or structured forms as described below. When a " (code "foreign-procedure") " expression is evaluated, a Scheme procedure is created that will invoke the foreign procedure specified by " (code (var "entry-exp")) ". When the procedure is called each argument is checked and converted according to the specified " (code (var "param-type")) " before it is passed to the foreign procedure. The result of the foreign procedure call is converted as specified by the " (code (var "res-type")) ". Multiple procedures may be created for the same foreign entry.") ("Each " (code (var "conv")) " adjusts the calling convention to be used. A " (code "#f") " is allowed as " (code (var "conv")) " to indicate the default calling convention on the target machine (so the " (code "#f") " has no effect). Three other conventions are currently supported under Windows: " (code "__stdcall") ", " (code "__cdecl") ", and " (code "__com") " (32-bit only). Since " (code "__cdecl") " is the default, specifying " (code "__cdecl") " is equivalent to specifying " (code "#f") " or no convention. Additionally, " (code (var "conv")) " can be " (code "__collect_safe") " to indicate that garbage collection is allowed concurrently with a call of the foreign procedure, it can be " (code "__atomic") " to indicate that the foreign function never calls back to Scheme, or it can be " (code "__varargs") " or " (code "(__varargs_after " (var "n") ")") " to indicate that the procedure uses a convention that works with a variable number of arguments after the first " (code (var "n")) " (which may differ from the convention used for the fixed-argument variant on some platforms and conventions). " (code "__varargs") " is a shorthand for " (code "(__varargs_after 1)") ".") ("Use " (code "__stdcall") " to access most Windows API procedures. Use " (code "__cdecl") " for Windows API varargs procedures, for C library procedures, and for most other procedures. Use " (code "__com") " to invoke COM interface methods; COM uses the " (code "__stdcall") " convention but additionally performs the indirections necessary to obtain the correct method from a COM instance. The address of the COM instance must be passed as the first argument, which should normally be declared as " (code "iptr") ". For the " (code "__com") " interface only, " (code (var "entry-exp")) " must evaluate to the byte offset of the method in the COM vtable. For example,") ((code "(foreign-procedure __com 12 (iptr double-float) integer-32)")) ("creates an interface to a COM method at offset 12 in the vtable encapsulated within the COM instance passed as the first argument, with the second argument being a double float and the return value being an integer.") ("Use " (code "__collect_safe") " to declare that garbage collection is allowed concurrently with the foreign procedure. The " (code "__collect_safe") " declaration allows concurrent collection by deactivating the current thread (see " (code "fork-thread") ") when the foreign procedure is called, and the thread is activated again when the foreign procedure returns. The " (code "__collect_safe") " declaration is useful, for example, when calling a blocking I/O call to allow other Scheme threads to run normally. Refrain from passing collectable memory to a " (code "__collect_safe") " foreign procedure, or use " (code "lock-object") "  to lock the memory in place; see also " (code "Sdeactivate_thread") ". The " (code "__collect_safe") " declaration has no effect on a non-threaded version of the system.") ("For example, calling the C " (code "sleep") " function with the default convention will block other Scheme threads from performing a garbage collection, but adding the " (code "__collect_safe") " declaration avoids that problem:") ((code "(define c-sleep" (br) "  (foreign-procedure __collect_safe \"sleep\" (unsigned) unsigned))" (br) "(c-sleep 10) " (var "; sleeps for 10 seconds without blocking other threads"))) ("If a foreign procedure that is called with " (code "__collect_safe") " can invoke callables, then each callable should also be declared with " (code "__collect_safe") " so that the callable reactivates the thread.") ("Use " (code "__atomic") " to declare that the foreign procedure never calls back to Scheme, such as through a foreign callable, which may allow a more efficient implementation of calls to the foreign function. The " (code "__atomic") " and " (code "__collect_safe") "  declarations cannot be used at the same time.") ("Complete type checking and conversion is performed on the parameters to a foreign procedure. The types " (code "scheme-object") ", " (code "string") ", " (code "wstring") ", " (code "u8*") ", " (code "u16*") ", " (code "u32*") ", " (code "utf-8") ", " (code "utf-16") ", " (code "utf-16le") ", " (code "utf-16be") ", " (code "utf-32") ", " (code "utf-32le") ", and " (code "utf-32be") ", must be used with caution, however, since they allow allocated Scheme objects to be used in places the Scheme memory management system cannot control. No problems will arise as long as such objects are not retained in foreign variables or data structures while Scheme code is running; as long as they are not passed as arguments to a " (code "__collect_safe") " procedure, since garbage collection can occur only while Scheme code is running or when concurrent garbage collection is enabled; and as long as the foreign procedure does not reenter Scheme via a callable before returning. Other parameter types are converted to equivalent foreign representations and consequently they can be retained indefinitely in foreign variables and data structures.") ("For argument types " (code "string") ", " (code "wstring") ", " (code "utf-8") ", " (code "utf-16") ", " (code "utf-16le") ", " (code "utf-16be") ", " (code "utf-32") ", " (code "utf-32le") ", and " (code "utf-32be") ", an argument is converted to a fresh object that is passed to the foreign procedure. Since the fresh object is not accessible for locking before the call, it can never be treated correctly for a " (code "__collect_safe") " foreign procedure, so those types are disallowed as argument types for a " (code "__collect_safe") " foreign procedure. For analogous reasons, those types are disallowed as the result of a " (code "__collect_safe") "  foreign callable.") ("Following are the valid parameter types:") ((code "integer-8") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "7") " through 2" (sup "8") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range 2" (sup "7") " through 2" (sup "8") " - 1 are treated as two's complement representations of negative numbers, e.g., " (code "#xff") " is treated as -1. The argument is passed to C as an integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "signed char") ").") ((code "unsigned-8") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "7") " to 2" (sup "8") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range -2" (sup "7") " through -1 are treated as the positive equivalents of their two's complement representation, e.g., -1 is treated as " (code "#xff") ". The argument is passed to C as an unsigned integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "unsigned char") ").") ((code "integer-16") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "15") " through 2" (sup "16") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range 2" (sup "15") " through 2" (sup "16") " - 1 are treated as two's complement representations of negative numbers, e.g., " (code "#xffff") " is treated as -1. The argument is passed to C as an integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "short") ").") ((code "unsigned-16") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "15") " to 2" (sup "16") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range -2" (sup "15") " through -1 are treated as the positive equivalents of their two's complement representation, e.g., -1 is treated as " (code "#xffff") ". The argument is passed to C as an unsigned integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "unsigned short") ").") ((code "integer-32") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "31") " through 2" (sup "32") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range 2" (sup "31") " through 2" (sup "32") " - 1 are treated as two's complement representations of negative numbers, e.g., " (code "#xffffffff") " is treated as -1. The argument is passed to C as an integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "int") ").") ((code "unsigned-32") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "31") " to 2" (sup "32") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range -2" (sup "31") " through -1 are treated as the positive equivalents of their two's complement representation, e.g., -1 is treated as " (code "#xffffffff") ". The argument is passed to C as an unsigned integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "unsigned int") ").") ((code "integer-64") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "63") " through 2" (sup "64") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range 2" (sup "63") " through 2" (sup "64") " - 1 are treated as two's complement representations of negative numbers. The argument is passed to C as an integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "long long") " or, on many 64-bit platforms, " (code "long") ").") ((code "unsigned-64") ": Exact integers from -2" (sup "63") " through 2" (sup "64") " - 1 are valid. Integers in the range -2" (sup "63") " through -1 are treated as the positive equivalents of their two's complement representation, The argument is passed to C as an integer of the appropriate size (usually " (code "unsigned long long") " or, on many 64-bit platforms, " (code "long") ").") ((code "double-float") ": Only Scheme flonums are valid---other Scheme numeric types are not automatically converted. The argument is passed to C as a double float.") ((code "single-float") ": Only Scheme flonums are valid---other Scheme numeric types are not automatically converted. The argument is passed to C as a single float. Since " (var "Chez Scheme") " represents flonums in double-float format, the parameter is first converted into single-float format.") ((code "short") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "short") ".") ((code "unsigned-short") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned short") ".") ((code "int") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "int") ".") ((code "unsigned") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned") ".") ((code "unsigned-int") ": This type is an alias " (code "unsigned") ". fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned") ".") ((code "long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "long") ".") ((code "unsigned-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned long") ".") ((code "long-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of the nonstandard C type " (code "long long") ".") ((code "unsigned-long-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of the nonstandard C type " (code "unsigned long long") ".") ((code "ptrdiff_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "size_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate unsigned fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "ssize_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate signed fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "iptr") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C pointer.") ((code "uptr") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate (unsigned) fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C pointer.") ((code "void*") ": This type is an alias for " (code "uptr") ".") ((code "fixnum") ": This type is equivalent to " (code "iptr") ", except only values in the fixnum range are valid. Transmission of fixnums is slightly faster than transmission of " (code "iptr") " values, but the fixnum range is smaller, so some " (code "iptr") " values do not have a fixnum representation.") ((code "boolean") ": Any Scheme object may be passed as a boolean. " (code "#f") " is converted to 0; all other objects are converted to 1. The argument is passed to C as an " (code "int") ".") ((code "stdbool") ": Any Scheme object may be passed as a boolean. " (code "#f") " is converted to 0; all other objects are converted to 1. The argument is passed to C as a " (code "bool") " defined by the the host machine's " (code "stdbool.h") " include file.") ((code "char") ": Only Scheme characters with Unicode scalar values in the range 0 through 255 are valid " (code "char") " parameters. The character is converted to its Unicode scalar value, as with " (code "char->integer") ", and passed to C as an " (code "unsigned char") ".") ((code "wchar_t") ": Only Scheme characters are valid " (code "wchar_t") " parameters. Under Windows and any other system where " (code "wchar_t") " holds only 16-bit values rather than full Unicode scalar values, only characters with 16-bit Unicode scalar values are valid. On systems where " (code "wchar_t") " is a full 32-bit value, any Scheme character is valid. The character is converted to its Unicode scalar value, as with " (code "char->integer") ", and passed to C as a " (code "wchar_t") ".") ((code "wchar") ": This type is an alias for " (code "wchar_t") ".") ((code "double") ": This type is an alias for " (code "double-float") ".") ((code "float") ": This type is an alias for " (code "single-float") ".") ((code "scheme-object") ": The argument is passed directly to the foreign procedure; no conversion or type checking is performed. This form of parameter passing should be used with discretion. Scheme objects should not be preserved in foreign variables or data structures since the memory management system may relocate them between foreign procedure calls.") ((code "ptr") ": This type is an alias for " (code "scheme-object") ".") ((code "u8*") ": The argument must be a Scheme bytevector or " (code "#f") ". For " (code "#f") ", the null pointer (0) is passed to the foreign procedure. For a bytevector, a pointer to the first byte of the bytevector's data is passed. If the C routine to which the data is passed requires the input to be null-terminated, a null (0) byte must be included explicitly in the bytevector. The bytevector should not be retained in foreign variables or data structures, since the memory management system may relocate or discard them between foreign procedure calls, and use their storage for some other purpose.") ((code "u16*") ": Arguments of this type are treated just like arguments of type " (code "u8*") ". If the C routine to which the data is passed requires the input to be null-terminated, two null (0) bytes must be included explicitly in the bytevector, aligned on a 16-bit boundary.") ((code "u32*") ": Arguments of this type are treated just like arguments of type " (code "u8*") ". If the C routine to which the data is passed requires the input to be null-terminated, four null (0) bytes must be included explicitly in the bytevector, aligned on a 32-bit boundary.") ((code "utf-8") ": The argument must be a Scheme string or " (code "#f") ". For " (code "#f") ", the null pointer (0) is passed to the foreign procedure. A string is converted into a bytevector, as if via " (code "string->utf8") ", with an added null byte, and the address of the first byte of the bytevector is passed to C. The bytevector should not be retained in foreign variables or data structures, since the memory management system may relocate or discard them between foreign procedure calls and use their storage for some other purpose. The " (code "utf-8") " argument type is not allowed for a " (code "__collect_safe") " foreign procedure.") ((code "utf-16") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf16") " with endianness " (code "(native-endianness)") ", and they are extended by two null bytes rather than one.") ((code "utf-16le") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf16") " with endianness " (code "little") ", and they are extended by two null bytes rather than one.") ((code "utf-16be") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf16") " with endianness " (code "big") ", and they are extended by two null bytes rather than one.") ((code "utf-32") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf32") " with endianness " (code "(native-endianness)") ", and they are extended by four null bytes rather than one.") ((code "utf-32le") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf32") " with endianness " (code "little") ", and they are extended by four null bytes rather than one.") ((code "utf-32be") ": Arguments of this type are treated like arguments of type " (code "utf-8") ", except they are converted as if via " (code "string->utf32") " with endianness " (code "big") ", and they are extended by four null bytes rather than one.") ((code "string") ": This type is an alias for " (code "utf-8") ".") ((code "wstring") ": This type is an alias for " (code "utf-16") "  or " (code "utf-32") " as appropriate depending on the size of a C " (code "wchar_t") ". For example, " (code "wstring") " is equivalent to " (code "utf-16") "  under Windows running on Intel hardware.") ((code (var "ftype-name")) ": Any defined foreign type (ftype) can be used as long as it is equivalent to a type that would be allowed when written directly. See Section " (link "./foreign.html#g31" "4.5") " for a description of foreign types.") ((code "(* " (var "ftype-name") ")") ": This type allows a pointer to a foreign type (ftype) to be passed. The argument must be an ftype pointer of the type identified by " (code (var "ftype-name")) ", and the actual argument is the address encapsulated in the ftype pointer.") ((code "ftype-pointer") ": This type allows any pointer to a foreign type (ftype) to be passed, independent of the foreign type, and including generic foreign pointers and foreign pointers to Scheme objects. In other words, this type is specific to foreign pointers created with " (code "make-ftype-pointer") " and similar, but otherwise generic like " (code "void*") ".") ((code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ": This type allows a foreign pointer to a Scheme object, such as one created with " (code "make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ". The address received on the C side is like the one produced by " (code "object->reference-address") "  for a non-" (code "#f") " value; using the result of " (code "object->reference-address") " with " (code "make-ftype-pointer") "  would produce the result, but only with locked objects that cannot move, while " (code "make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer") " defers conversion to an address to a point before the call but after a garbage collection can take place (assuming that " (code "__collect_safe") " is not used). Note that " (code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") " differs from " (code "scheme-object") "  by delivering specifically a reference address to the foreign procedure, which can be useful for communicating bytevector and flvector content.") ((code "(& " (var "ftype-name") ")") ": This type allows a foreign type (ftype) to be passed as a value, but represented on the Scheme side as a pointer to the foreign-type data. That is, a " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") ")") " argument is represented on the Scheme side the same as a " (code "(* " (var "ftype-name") ")") " argument, but a " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") ")") " argument is passed to the foreign procedure as the content at the foreign pointer's address instead of as the address. For example, if " (code (var "ftype-name")) " identifies a " (code "struct") " type, then " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") ")") " passes a struct argument instead of a struct-pointer argument. The " (code (var "ftype-name")) " cannot refer to an array type. The variant " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") " ftype-pointer)") " is the same, except that the Scheme-side representation can be any foreign pointer type, not necessarily one specific to " (code (var "ftype-name")) ". The variant " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") " ftype-scheme-object-pointer)") "  is also the same, except that Scheme-side representation must be a foreign object pointer to a Scheme object like one created by " (code "make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ".") ("The result types are similar to the parameter types with the addition of a " (code "void") " type. In general, the type conversions are the inverse of the parameter type conversions. No error checking is performed on return, since the system cannot determine whether a foreign result is actually of the indicated type. Particular caution should be exercised with the result types " (code "scheme-object") ", " (code "double-float") ", " (code "double") ", " (code "single-float") ", " (code "float") ", " (code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ", and the types that result in the construction of bytevectors or strings, since invalid return values may lead to invalid memory references as well as incorrect computations. Following are the valid result types:") ((code "void") ": The result of the foreign procedure call is ignored and an unspecified Scheme object is returned. " (code "void") " should be used when foreign procedures are called for effect only.") ((code "integer-8") ": The result is interpreted as a signed 8-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme exact integer.") ((code "unsigned-8") ": The result is interpreted as an unsigned 8-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme nonnegative exact integer.") ((code "integer-16") ": The result is interpreted as a signed 16-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme exact integer.") ((code "unsigned-16") ": The result is interpreted as an unsigned 16-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme nonnegative exact integer.") ((code "integer-32") ": The result is interpreted as a signed 32-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme exact integer.") ((code "unsigned-32") ": The result is interpreted as an unsigned 32-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme nonnegative exact integer.") ((code "integer-64") ": The result is interpreted as a signed 64-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme exact integer.") ((code "unsigned-64") ": The result is interpreted as an unsigned 64-bit integer and is converted to a Scheme nonnegative exact integer.") ((code "double-float") ": The result is interpreted as a double float and is translated into a " (var "Chez Scheme") " flonum.") ((code "single-float") ": The result is interpreted as a single float and is translated into a " (var "Chez Scheme") " flonum. Since " (var "Chez Scheme") " represents flonums in double-float format, the result is first converted into double-float format.") ((code "short") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "short") ".") ((code "unsigned-short") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned short") ".") ((code "int") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "int") ".") ((code "unsigned") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned") ".") ((code "unsigned-int") ": This type is an alias " (code "unsigned") ". fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned") ".") ((code "long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "long") ".") ((code "unsigned-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C " (code "unsigned long") ".") ((code "long-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of the nonstandard C type " (code "long long") ".") ((code "unsigned-long-long") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of the nonstandard C type " (code "unsigned long long") ".") ((code "ptrdiff_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "size_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate unsigned fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "ssize_t") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate signed fixed-size type above, depending on its definition in the host machine's " (code "stddef.h") " include file.") ((code "iptr") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C pointer.") ((code "uptr") ": This type is an alias for the appropriate (unsigned) fixed-size type above, depending on the size of a C pointer.") ((code "void*") ": This type is an alias for " (code "uptr") ".") ((code "boolean") ": This type converts a C " (code "int") " return value into a Scheme boolean. 0 is converted to " (code "#f") "; all other values are converted to " (code "#t") ".") ((code "stdbool") ": This type converts a C " (code "bool") " return value into a Scheme boolean, where " (code "bool") " is from the host machine's " (code "stdbool.h") "  include file. 0 is converted to " (code "#f") "; all other values are converted to " (code "#t") ".") ((code "char") ": This type converts a C " (code "unsigned char") " return value into a Scheme character, as if via " (code "integer->char") ".") ((code "wchar_t") ": This type converts a C " (code "wchar_t") " return value into a Scheme character, as if via " (code "integer->char") ". The " (code "wchar_t") " value must be a valid Unicode scalar value.") ((code "wchar") ": This type is an alias for " (code "wchar_t") ".") ((code "double") ": This type is an alias for " (code "double-float") ".") ((code "float") ": This type is an alias for " (code "single-float") ".") ((code "scheme-object") ": The result is assumed to be a valid Scheme object, and no conversion is performed. This type is inherently dangerous, since an invalid Scheme object can corrupt the memory management system with unpredictable (but always unpleasant) results. Since Scheme objects are actually typed pointers, even integers cannot safely be returned as type " (code "scheme-object") " unless they were created by the Scheme system.") ((code "ptr") ": This type is an alias for " (code "scheme-object") ".") ((code "u8*") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 8-bit unsigned integers (bytes). If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of bytes is stored in a freshly allocated bytevector of the appropriate length, and the bytevector is returned to Scheme.") ((code "u16*") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 16-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of 16-bit integers is stored in a freshly allocated bytevector of the appropriate length, and the bytevector is returned to Scheme. The null terminator must be a properly aligned 16-bit word, i.e., two bytes of zero aligned on a 16-bit boundary.") ((code "u32*") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 32-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of 16-bit integers is stored in a freshly allocated bytevector of the appropriate length, and the bytevector is returned to Scheme. The null terminator must be a properly aligned 32-bit word, i.e., four bytes of zero aligned on a 32-bit boundary.") ((code "utf-8") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 8-bit unsigned character values. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of bytes is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf8->string") ", and the string is returned to Scheme.") ((code "utf-16") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 16-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf16->string") " with endianness " (code "(native-endianness)") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "utf-16le") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 16-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf16->string") " with endianness " (code "little") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "utf-16be") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 16-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf16->string") " with endianness " (code "big") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "utf-32") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 32-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf32->string") " with endianness " (code "(native-endianness)") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "utf-32le") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 32-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf32->string") " with endianness " (code "little") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "utf-32be") ": The result is interpreted as a pointer to a null-terminated sequence of 32-bit unsigned integers. If the result is a null pointer, " (code "#f") " is returned. Otherwise, the sequence of integers is converted into a Scheme string, as if via " (code "utf32->string") " with endianness " (code "big") ", and the string is returned to Scheme. A byte-order mark in the sequence of integers is treated as an ordinary character value and does not affect the byte ordering.") ((code "string") ": This type is an alias for " (code "utf-8") ".") ((code "wstring") ": This type is an alias for " (code "utf-16") "  or " (code "utf-32") " as appropriate depending on the size of a C " (code "wchar_t") ". For example, " (code "wstring") " is equivalent to " (code "utf-16") "  under Windows running on Intel hardware.") ((code (var "ftype-name")) ": Any defined foreign type (ftype) can be used as long as it is equivalent to a type that would be allowed when written directly. See Section " (link "./foreign.html#g31" "4.5") " for a description of foreign types.") ((code "(* " (var "ftype-name") ")") ": The result is interpreted as the address of a foreign object whose structure is described by the ftype identified by " (code (var "ftype-name")) ", and a freshly allocated ftype pointer encapsulating the address is returned.") ((code "ftype-pointer") ": The result is interpreted as the address of a foreign object of unspecified content, and a freshly allocated generic ftype pointer encapsulating the address is returned.") ((code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ": The result is interpreted as the reference address of a Scheme object, and it is returned as encapsulated within a foreign object pointer as created with " (code "make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ". Note that " (code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") " differs from " (code "scheme-object") " in that it receives a reference address from the foreign procedure, which might be the start of bytevector or flvector content. Like " (code "scheme-object") ", however, this type is inherently dangerous, and a foreign procedure would rarely be expected to produce such a value.") ((code "(& " (var "ftype-name") ")") ": The result is interpreted as a foreign object whose structure is described by the ftype identified by " (code (var "ftype-name")) ", where the foreign procedure returns a " (code (var "ftype-name")) " result, but the caller must provide an extra " (code "(* " (var "ftype-name") ")") " argument before all other arguments to receive the result. An unspecified Scheme object is returned when the foreign procedure is called, since the result is instead written into storage referenced by the extra argument. The " (code (var "ftype-name")) " cannot refer to an array type. The variant " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") " ftype-pointer)") " is the same, except that the foreign pointer passed as an extra initial argument can be any foreign pointer type, not necessarily one specific to " (code (var "ftype-name")) ". The variant " (code "(& " (var "ftype-name") " ftype-scheme-object-pointer)") "  is also the same, except that the foreign pointer passed as an extra initial argument must be a foreign object pointer to a Scheme object like one created by " (code "make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ".") ("Consider a C identity procedure:") ((code "int id(x) int x; { return x; }")) ("After a file containing this procedure has been compiled and loaded (see Section " (link "./foreign.html#g35" "4.7") ") it can be accessed as follows:") ((code "(foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "  (int) int) " (math "=>") " #<procedure>" (br) "((foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "   (int) int)" (br) " 1) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(define int-id" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "    (int) int))" (br) "(int-id 1) " (math "=>") " 1")) ("The " (code "\"id\"") " entry can also be interpreted as accepting and returning a boolean:") ((code "(define bool-id" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "    (boolean) boolean))" (br) "(bool-id #f) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(bool-id #t) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(bool-id 1) " (math "=>") " #t")) ("As the last example reveals, " (code "bool-id") " is actually a conversion procedure. When a Scheme object is passed as type " (code "boolean") " it is converted to 0 or 1, and when it is returned it is converted to " (code "#f") " or " (code "#t") ". As a result objects are converted to normalized boolean values. The " (code "\"id\"") " entry can be used to create other conversion procedures by varying the type specifications:") ((code "(define int->bool" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "    (int) boolean))" (br) "(int->bool 0) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(int->bool 5) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(map (foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "       (boolean) int)" (br) "     '(#t #f)) " (math "=>") " (1 0)" (br) "(define void" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"id\"" (br) "    (int) void))" (br) "(void 10) " (math "=>") " " (var "unspecified"))) ("There are, of course, simpler and more efficient ways of accomplishing these conversions directly in Scheme.") ("A foreign entry is resolved when a " (code "foreign-procedure") " expression is evaluated, rather than either when the code is loaded or each time the procedure is invoked. Thus, the following definition is always valid since the " (code "foreign-procedure") " expression is not immediately evaluated:") ((code "(define doit" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    ((foreign-procedure \"doit\" () void))))")) ((code "doit") " should not be invoked, however, before an entry for " (code "\"doit\"") " has been provided. Similarly, an entry for " (code "\"doit\"") " must exist before the following code is evaluated:") ((code "(define doit" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"doit\" () void))")) ("Although the second definition is more constraining on the load order of foreign files, it is more efficient since the entry resolution need be done only once.") ("It is often useful to define a template to be used in the creation of several foreign procedures with similar argument types and return values. For example, the following code creates two foreign procedures from a single foreign procedure expression, by abstracting out the foreign procedure name:") ((code "(define double->double" (br) "  (lambda (proc-name)" (br) "    (foreign-procedure proc-name" (br) "      (double)" (br) "      double)))" (br) (br) "(define log10 (double->double \"log10\"))" (br) "(define gamma (double->double \"gamma\"))")) ("Both " (code "\"log10\"") " and " (code "\"gamma\"") " must be available as foreign entries (see Section " (link "./foreign.html#g35" "4.7") ") before the corresponding definitions. The use of foreign procedure templates can simplify the coding process and reduce the amount of code generated when a large number of foreign procedures are involved, e.g., when an entire library of foreign procedures is imported into Scheme.")) ("foreign:s144" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(foreign-callable " (var "conv") " ... " (var "proc-exp") " (" (var "param-type") " ...) " (var "res-type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a code object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "proc-exp")) " must evaluate to a procedure, the Scheme procedure that is to be invoked by foreign code. The parameter and result types are as described for " (code "foreign-procedure") " in Section " (link "./foreign.html#g28" "4.2") ", except that the requirements and conversions are effectively reversed, e.g., the conversions described for " (code "foreign-procedure") "  arguments are performed for " (code "foreign-callable") " return values. A " (code "(& " (var "ftype") ")") " argument to the callable refers to an address that is valid only during the dynamic extent of the callback invocation. A " (code "(& " (var "ftype") ")") " result type for a callable causes the Scheme procedure to receive an extra " (code "(& " (var "ftype") ")") " argument before all others; the Scheme procedure should write a result into the extra argument, and the direct result of the Scheme procedure is ignored. Type checking is performed for result values but not argument values, since the parameter values are provided by the foreign code and must be assumed to be correct.") ("Each " (code (var "conv")) " adjusts the calling convention to be used. " (code "foreign-callable") " supports the same conventions as " (code "foreign-procedure") " except for " (code "__com") " and " (code "__atomic") ". The " (code "__collect_safe") " convention for a callable activates a calling thread if the thread is not already activated, and the thread's activation state is reverted when the callable returns. If a calling thread is not currently registered with the Scheme system, then reverting the thread's activation state implies destroying the thread's registration (see " (code "Sdestroy_thread") ").") ("The value produced by " (code "foreign-callable") " is a Scheme code object, which contains some header information as well as code that performs the call to the encapsulated Scheme procedure. The code object may be converted into a foreign-callable address via " (code "foreign-callable-entry-point") ", which returns an integer representing the address of the entry point within the code object. (The C-callable library function " (code "Sforeign_callable_entry_point") ", described in Section " (link "./foreign.html#g37" "4.9") ", may be used to obtain the entry point as well.)   This is an implicit pointer into an immobile Scheme object, so it will not be relocated by the storage management system, but it may be reclaimed if the code object becomes unreachable on the Scheme side; lock the code object (using " (code "lock-object") ") or otherwise retain it if the entry point is, for example, registered as a callback and retained in the \"C\" side indefinitely.") ("The following code creates a foreign-callable code object, locks the code object, and returns the entry point.") ((code "(let ([x (foreign-callable" (br) "           (lambda (x y) (pretty-print (cons x (* y 2))))" (br) "           (string integer-32)" (br) "           void)])" (br) "  (lock-object x)" (br) "  (foreign-callable-entry-point x))")) ("Unless the entry point is intended to be permanent, however, a pointer to the code object returned by " (code "foreign-callable") " should be retained (in which case locking is unnecessary, since the storage management system will not move the code object as long as it remains reachable on the Scheme side).") ("Mixed use of " (code "foreign-callable") " and " (code "foreign-procedure") "  may result in nesting of foreign and Scheme calls, and this results in some interesting considerations when continuations are involved, directly or indirectly (as via the default exception handler). See Section " (link "./foreign.html#g30" "4.4") " for a discussion of the interaction between foreign calls and continuations.") ("The following example demonstrates how the \"callback\" functions required by many windowing systems might be defined in Scheme with the use of " (code "foreign-callable") ". Assume that the following C code has been compiled and loaded (see Section " (link "./foreign.html#g35" "4.7") ").") ((code "#include <stdio.h>" (br) (br) "typedef void (*CB)(char);" (br) (br) "CB callbacks[256];" (br) (br) "void cb_init(void) {" (br) "   int i;" (br) (br) "   for (i = 0; i < 256; i += 1)" (br) "       callbacks[i] = (CB)0;" (br) "}" (br) (br) "void register_callback(char c, CB cb) {" (br) "    callbacks[c] = cb;" (br) "}" (br) (br) "void event_loop(void) {" (br) "    CB f; char c;" (br) (br) "    for (;;) {" (br) "        c = getchar();" (br) "        if (c == EOF) break;" (br) "        f = callbacks[c];" (br) "        if (f != (CB)0) f(c);" (br) "    }" (br) "}")) ("Interfaces to these functions may be defined in Scheme as follows.") ((code "(define cb-init" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"cb_init\" () void))" (br) "(define register-callback" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"register_callback\" (char void*) void))" (br) "(define event-loop" (br) "  (foreign-procedure __collect_safe \"event_loop\" () void))")) ("A callback for selected characters can then be defined.") ((code "(define callback" (br) "  (lambda (p)" (br) "    (let ([code (foreign-callable __collect_safe p (char) void)])" (br) "      (lock-object code)" (br) "      (foreign-callable-entry-point code))))" (br) "(define ouch" (br) "  (callback" (br) "    (lambda (c)" (br) "      (printf \"Ouch! Hit by '~c'~%\" c))))" (br) "(define rats" (br) "  (callback" (br) "    (lambda (c)" (br) "      (printf \"Rats! Received '~c'~%\" c))))" (br) (br) "(cb-init)" (br) "(register-callback #\\a ouch)" (br) "(register-callback #\\c rats)" (br) "(register-callback #\\e ouch)")) ("This sets up the following interaction.") ((code "> (event-loop)" (br) "a" (br) "Ouch! Hit by 'a'" (br) "b" (br) "c" (br) "Rats! Received 'c'" (br) "d" (br) "e" (br) "Ouch! Hit by 'e'")) ("The " (code "__collect_safe") " declarations in this example ensure that other threads can continue working while " (code "event-loop") "  blocks waiting for input. A more well-behaved version of the example would save each code object returned by " (code "foreign-callable") " and unlock it when it is no longer registered as a callback.")) ("foreign:s147" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-callable-entry-point " (var "code") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the address of the foreign-callable entry point in " (code (var "code")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "code")) " should be a code object produced by " (code "foreign-callable") ".")) ("foreign:s148" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-callable-code-object " (var "address") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the code object corresponding to the foreign-callable entry point " (code (var "address")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "address")) " must be an exact integer and should be the address of the entry point of a code object produced by " (code "foreign-callable") ".")) ("foreign:s149" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-alloc " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the address of a freshly allocated block of foreign data " (code (var "n")) " bytes long" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "n")) " must be a positive fixnum. The returned value is an exact integer and is guaranteed to be properly aligned for any type of value according to the requirements of the underlying hardware. An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") "  if the block of foreign data cannot be allocated.")) ("foreign:s150" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-free " (var "address") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure frees the block of storage to which " (code (var "address")) " points. " (code (var "address")) " must be an exact integer in the range -2" (sup (var "w") "-1") " through 2" (sup "w") " - 1, where " (var "w") " is the width in bits of a pointer, e.g., 64 for a 64-bit machine. It should be an address returned by an earlier call to " (code "foreign-alloc") " and not subsequently passed to " (code "foreign-free") ".")) ("foreign:s151" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-ref " (var "type") " " (var "address") " " (var "offset") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "foreign-ref") " extracts the value of type " (code (var "type")) "  from the memory location at " (code (var "offset")) " bytes offset from " (code (var "address")) ".") ((code (var "type")) " must be a symbol identifying the type of value to be extracted. The following types have machine-dependent sizes and correspond to the like-named C types:   " (ul (li (code "short") ",") (li (code "unsigned-short") ",") (li (code "int") ",") (li (code "unsigned") ",") (li (code "unsigned-int") ",") (li (code "long") ",") (li (code "unsigned-long") ",") (li (code "long-long") ",") (li (code "unsigned-long-long") ",") (li (code "ptrdiff_t") ",") (li (code "size_t") ",") (li (code "ssize_t") ",") (li (code "char") ",") (li (code "wchar_t") ",") (li (code "float") ",") (li (code "double") ", and") (li (code "void*") "."))) ("The types " (code "long-long") " and " (code "unsigned-long-long") "  correspond to the C types " (code "long long") "  and " (code "unsigned long long") ". A value of type " (code "char") " is referenced as a single byte and converted (as if via " (code "integer->char") ") into a Scheme character. A value of type " (code "wchar_t") " is converted (as if via " (code "integer->char") ") into a Scheme character. The value must be a valid Unicode scalar value.") ((code "wchar") " is an alias for " (code "wchar_t") ".") ("Several additional machine-dependent types are recognized:   " (ul (li (code "iptr") ",") (li (code "uptr") ",") (li (code "fixnum") ",") (li (code "boolean") ", and") (li (code "stdbool") "."))) ((code "uptr") " is equivalent to " (code "void*") "; both are treated as unsigned integers the size of a pointer. " (code "iptr") " is treated as a signed integer the size of a pointer. " (code "fixnum") " is treated as an " (code "iptr") ", but with a range limited to the fixnum range. " (code "boolean") " is treated as an " (code "int") ", with zero converted to the Scheme value " (code "#f") " and all other values converted to " (code "#t") ". " (code "stdbool") " is treated as an " (code "bool") " from " (code "stdbool.h") ", with zero converted to the Scheme value " (code "#f") " and all other values converted to " (code "#t") ".") ("Finally, several fixed-sized types are also supported:   " (ul (li (code "integer-8") ",") (li (code "unsigned-8") ",") (li (code "integer-16") ",") (li (code "unsigned-16") ",") (li (code "integer-32") ",") (li (code "unsigned-32") ",") (li (code "integer-64") ",") (li (code "unsigned-64") ",") (li (code "single-float") ", and") (li (code "double-float") "."))) ((code (var "address")) " must be an exact integer in the range -2" (sup (var "w") "-1") " through 2" (sup "w") " - 1, where " (var "w") " is the width in bits of a pointer, e.g., 64 for a 64-bit machine. " (code (var "offset")) " must be an exact fixnum. The sum of " (code (var "address")) " and " (code (var "offset")) " should address a readable block of memory large enough to hold a value of type " (code (var "type")) ", within a block of storage previously returned by " (code "foreign-alloc") " and not subsequently freed by " (code "foreign-free") " or within a block of storage obtained via some other mechanism, e.g., a foreign call. For multiple-byte values, the native endianness of the machine is assumed.")) ("foreign:s152" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-set! " (var "type") " " (var "address") " " (var "offset") " " (var "value") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "foreign-set!") " stores a representation of " (code (var "value")) " as type " (code (var "type")) " " (code (var "offset")) " bytes into the block of foreign data addressed by " (code (var "address")) ".") ((code (var "type")) " must be a symbol identifying the type of value to be stored, one of those listed in the description of " (code "foreign-ref") " above. Scheme characters are converted to type " (code "char") " or " (code "wchar_t") "  as if via " (code "char->integer") ". For type " (code "boolean") " or " (code "stdbool") ", Scheme " (code "#f") " is converted to the " (code "int") " 0, and any other Scheme object is converted to 1.") ((code (var "address")) " must be an exact integer in the range -2" (sup (var "w") "-1") " through 2" (sup "w") " - 1, where " (var "w") " is the width in bits of a pointer, e.g., 64 for a 64-bit machine. " (code (var "offset")) " must be an exact fixnum. The sum of " (code (var "address")) " and " (code (var "offset")) " should address a writable block of memory large enough to hold a value of type " (code (var "type")) ", within a block of storage previously returned by " (code "foreign-alloc") " and not subsequently freed by " (code "foreign-free") " or within a block of storage obtained via some other mechanism, e.g., a foreign call. " (code (var "value")) " must be an appropriate value for " (code (var "type")) ", e.g., a floating-point number for the float types or an exact integer within the appropriate range for the integer types. For multiple-byte values, the native endianness of the machine is assumed.")) ("foreign:s153" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-sizeof " (var "type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the size in bytes of " (code (var "type")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "type")) " must be one of the symbols listed in the description of " (code "foreign-ref") " above.")) ("foreign:s154" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(foreign-alignof " (var "type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the alignment in bytes of " (code (var "type")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "type")) " must be one of the symbols listed in the description of " (code "foreign-ref") " above.")) ("foreign:s155" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-ftype " (var "ftype-name") " " (var "ftype") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-ftype (" (var "ftype-name") " " (var "ftype") ") ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "define-ftype") " form is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear. It establishes one or more foreign-type (ftype) bindings for the identifier " (code (var "ftype-name")) " or identifiers " (code (var "ftype-name") " ...") "  to the foreign type represented " (code (var "ftype")) " or the foreign types represented by " (code (var "ftype") " ...") ". Each " (code (var "ftype-name")) " can be used to access foreign objects with the declared shape, and each can be used in the formation of other ftypes.") ("An " (code (var "ftype")) " must take one of the following forms:") ((code (var "ftype-name") (br) "(struct (" (var "field-name") " " (var "ftype") ") ...)" (br) "(union (" (var "field-name") " " (var "ftype") ") ...)" (br) "(array " (var "length") " " (var "ftype") ")" (br) "(* " (var "ftype") ")" (br) "(bits (" (var "field-name") " " (var "signedness") " " (var "bits") ") ...)" (br) "(function " (var "conv") " ... (" (var "ftype") " ...) " (var "ftype") ")" (br) "(packed " (var "ftype") ")" (br) "(unpacked " (var "ftype") ")" (br) "(endian " (var "endianness") " " (var "ftype") ")")) ("where " (code (var "length")) " is an exact nonnegative integer, " (code (var "bits")) " is an exact positive integer, " (code (var "field-name")) " is an identifier, " (code (var "conv")) " is " (code "#f") " or a string naming a valid convention as described on page " (link "./foreign.html#g28" "4.2") ", signedness is either " (code "signed") " or " (code "unsigned") ", and endianness is one of " (code "native") ", " (code "swapped") ", " (code "big") ", or " (code "little") ".") ("A restriction not reflected above is that  " (code "function") " ftypes cannot be used as the types of field names or array elements. That is, function ftypes are valid only at the top level of an ftype, e.g,:") ((code "(define-ftype bvcopy_t (function (u8* u8* size_t) void))")) ("or as the immediate sub-type of a pointer (" (code "*") ") ftype, as in the following definitions, which are equivalent assuming the definition of " (code "bvcopy_t") " above.") ((code "(define-ftype A" (br) "  (struct" (br) "    [x int]" (br) "    [f (* (function (u8* u8* size_t) void))]))" (br) (br) "(define-ftype A" (br) "  (struct" (br) "    [x int]" (br) "    [f (* bvcopy_t)]))")) ("That is, a function cannot be embedded within a struct, union, or array, but a pointer to a function can be so embedded.") ("The following definitions establish ftype bindings for " (code "F") ", " (code "A") ", and " (code "E") ".") ((code "(define-ftype F (function (wchar_t int) int))" (br) (br) "(define-ftype A (array 10 wchar_t))" (br) (br) "(define-ftype E" (br) "  (struct" (br) "    [a int]" (br) "    [b double]" (br) "    [c (array 25" (br) "         (struct" (br) "           [a short]" (br) "           [_ long]" (br) "           [b A]))]" (br) "    [d (endian big" (br) "         (union" (br) "           [v1 unsigned-32]" (br) "           [v2 (bits" (br) "                 [hi unsigned 12]" (br) "                 [lo unsigned 20])]))]" (br) "    [e (* A)]" (br) "    [f (* F)]))")) ("The ftype " (code "F") " describes the type of a foreign function that takes two arguments, a wide character and an integer, and returns an integer. The ftype " (code "A") " is simply an array of 10 " (code "wchar_t") " values, and its size will be 10 times the size of a single " (code "wchar_t") ". The ftype " (code "E") " is a structure with six fields: an integer " (code "a") ", a double-float " (code "b") ", an array " (code "c") ", a union " (code "d") ", a pointer " (code "e") ", and a pointer " (code "f") ". The array " (code "c") " is an array of 25 structs, each of which contains a short integer, a long integer, and a " (code "A") " array. The size of the " (code "c") " array will be 25 times the size of a single " (code "A") " array, plus 25 times the space needed to store each of the short and long integers. The union " (code "d") " is either a 32-bit unsigned integer or a 32-bit unsigned integer split into high (12 bits) and low (20 bits) components. The fields of a union overlap so that writing to one effectively overlaps the other. Thus, one can use the " (code "d") " union type to split apart an unsigned integer by writing the integer into " (code "v1") " and reading the pieces from " (code "hi") " and " (code "lo") ". The pointer " (code "e") " points to an " (code "A") " array; it is not itself an array, and its size is just the size of a single pointer. Similarly, " (code "f") " points to a function, and its size is also that of a single pointer.") ("An underscore ( " (code "_") " ) can be used as the field name for one or more fields of a " (code "struct") ", " (code "union") ", or " (code "bits") " ftype. Such fields are included in the layout but are considered unnamed and cannot be accessed via the ftype operators described below. Thus, in the example above, the " (code "long") " field within the " (code "c") " array is inaccessible.") ("Non-underscore field names are handled symbolically, i.e., they are treated as symbols rather than identifiers. Each symbol must be unique (as a symbol) with respect to the other field names within a single " (code "struct") ", " (code "union") ", or " (code "bits") " ftype but need not be unique with respect to field names in other " (code "struct") ", " (code "union") ", or " (code "bits") " ftypes within the same ftype.") ("Each " (code (var "ftype-name")) " in an " (code (var "ftype")) " must either (a) have been defined previously by " (code "define-ftype") ", (b) be defined by the current " (code "define-ftype") ", (c) be a base-type name, i.e., one of the type names supported by " (code "foreign-ref") " and " (code "foreign-set!") ", or (d) " (code "ftype-pointer") " or " (code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer") ", which indicate a generic ftype pointer or an ftype pointer for a Scheme object, respectively. In case (b), any reference within one " (code (var "ftype")) " to the " (code (var "ftype-name")) " of one of the earlier bindings is permissible, but a reference to the " (code (var "ftype-name")) " of the current or a subsequent binding can appear only within a pointer field.") ("For example, in:") ((code "(define-ftype" (br) "  [Qlist (struct" (br) "           [head int]" (br) "           [tail (* Qlist)])])")) ("the reference to " (code "Qlist") " is permissible since it appears within a pointer field. Similarly, in:") ((code "(define-ftype" (br) "  [Qfrob (struct" (br) "           [head int]" (br) "           [tail (* Qsnark)])]" (br) "  [Qsnark (struct" (br) "            [head int]" (br) "            [xtra Qfrob]" (br) "            [tail (* Qfrob)])])")) ("the mutually recursive references to " (code "Qsnark") " and " (code "Qfrob") "  are permissible. In the following, however:") ((code "(define-ftype" (br) "  [Qfrob (struct" (br) "           [head int]" (br) "           [xtra Qfrob]" (br) "           [tail (* Qsnark)])]" (br) "  [Qsnark (struct" (br) "            [head int]" (br) "            [tail (* Qfrob)])])")) ("the reference to " (code "Qfrob") " within the " (code (var "ftype")) " for " (code "Qfrob") "  is invalid, and in:") ((code "(define-ftype" (br) "  [Qfrob (struct" (br) "           [head int]" (br) "           [xtra Qsnark]" (br) "           [tail (* Qsnark)])]" (br) "  [Qsnark (struct" (br) "            [head int]" (br) "            [tail (* Qfrob)])])")) ("the reference to " (code "Qsnark") " is similarly invalid.") ("By default, padding is inserted where appropriate to maintain proper alignment of multiple-byte scalar values in an attempt to mirror the target machine's C struct layout conventions, where such layouts are adequately documented. For packed ftypes (ftypes wrapped in a " (code "packed") " form with no closer enclosing " (code "unpacked") " form), this padding is not inserted.") ("Multiple-byte scalar values are stored in memory using the target machine's native \"endianness,\" e.g., " (code "little") "  on X86 and X86_64-based platforms and " (code "big") " on Sparc-based platforms. Big-endian or little-endian representation can be forced via the " (code "endian") " ftype with a " (code "big") " or " (code "little") "  " (code (var "endianness")) " specifier. The " (code "native") " specifier can be used to force a return back to " (code "native") " representation. The " (code "swapped") " specifier can be used to swap the representation relative to the default or enclosing representation. Each " (code "endian") " form affects only ftypes nested syntactically within it and not nested within a closer " (code "endian") " form. The endianness of an ftype is fixed once it is defined.") ("The total size " (var "n") " of the fields within an ftype bits form must be 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, or 64.  Padding must be added manually, if needed. In little-endian representation, the first field occupies the low-order bits of the containing 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, or 64-bit word, with each subsequent field just above the preceding field. In big-endian representation, the first field occupies the high-order bits, with each subsequent field just below the preceding field. For a machine type where endianness is not known at compile time (such as the portable bytecode virtual machine), a bit field must be specified explicitly as " (code "big") " or " (code "little") " endian by an enclosing declaration.") ("Two ftypes are considered equivalent only if defined by the same " (code "ftype") " binding. If two ftype definitions look identical but appear in two parts of the same program, the ftypes are not identical, and attempts to access one using the name of the other via the operators described below will fail with a run-time exception.") ("Array bounds must always be constant. If an array's length cannot be known until run time, the array can be placed at the end of the ftype (and any containing ftype) and declared to have size zero, as illustrated by the example below.") ((code "(define-ftype Vec" (br) "  (struct" (br) "    [len int]" (br) "    [data (array 0 double)]))" (br) "(define make-Vec" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (let ([fptr (make-ftype-pointer Vec" (br) "                  (foreign-alloc" (br) "                    (+ (ftype-sizeof Vec)" (br) "                       (* (ftype-sizeof double) n))))])" (br) "      (ftype-set! Vec (len) fptr n)" (br) "      fptr)))" (br) "(define x (make-Vec 100))" (br) "(/ (- (ftype-pointer-address (ftype-&ref Vec (data 10) x))" (br) "      (ftype-pointer-address x)                            " (math "=>") " 10" (br) "      (ftype-sizeof int))" (br) "   (ftype-sizeof double))" (br) "(foreign-free (ftype-pointer-address x))")) ("No array bounds checks are performed for zero-length arrays. Only one variable-sized array can appear in a single foreign object, but one can work around this by treating the object as multiple individual objects.") ("To avoid specifying the constant length of an array in more than one place, a macro that binds both a variable to the size as well as an ftype name to the ftype can be used. For example,") ((code "(define-syntax define-array" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ array-name type size-name size)" (br) "     (begin" (br) "       (define size-name size)" (br) "       (define-ftype array-name" (br) "         (array size type)))]))" (br) "(define-array A int A-size 100)" (br) "A-size " (math "=>") " 100" (br) "(ftype-pointer-ftype" (br) "  (make-ftype-pointer A" (br) "    (foreign-alloc (ftype-sizeof A)))) " (math "=>") " (array 100 int)")) ("This technique can be used to define arbitrary ftypes with arbitrary numbers of array fields.") ("A struct ftype is an implicit subtype of the type of the first field of the struct. Similarly, an array ftype is an implicit subtype of the type of its elements. Thus, the struct or array extends the type of first field or element with additional fields or elements. This allows an instance of the struct or array to be treated as an instance of the type of its first field or element, without the need to use " (code "ftype-&ref") " to allocate a new pointer to the field or element.")) ("foreign:s160" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-sizeof " (var "ftype-name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the size in bytes of the ftype identified by " (code (var "ftype-name")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The size includes the sizes of any ftypes directly embedded within the identified ftype but excludes those indirectly embedded via a pointer ftype. In the latter case, the size of the pointer is included.") ((code (var "ftype-name")) " must not be defined as a function ftype, since the size of a function cannot generally be determined.") ((code "(define-ftype B" (br) "  (struct" (br) "    [b1 integer-32]" (br) "    [b2 (array 10 integer-32)]))" (br) "(ftype-sizeof B) " (math "=>") " 44" (br) (br) "(define-ftype C (* B))" (br) "(ftype-sizeof C) " (math "=>") " 4  " (var "; on 32-bit machines") (br) "(ftype-sizeof C) " (math "=>") " 8  " (var "; on