s a string. For example, with the following definition of " (code "bvcopy_t") ",") ((code "(define-ftype bvcopy_t (function (u8* u8* size_t) void))")) ("the two definitions of " (code "bvcopy-ftpr") " below are equivalent.") ((code "(define bvcopy-fptr (make-ftype-pointer bvcopy_t \"memcpy\"))" (br) "(define bvcopy-fptr (make-ftype-pointer bvcopy_t (foreign-entry \"memcpy\")))")) ("A library that defines " (code (var "memcpy")) " must be loaded first via " (code "load-shared-object") ", or " (code "memcpy") " must be registered via one of the methods described in Section  " (link "./foreign.html#g35" "4.7") ".")) ("foreign:s165" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(make-ftype-scheme-object-pointer " (var "expr") " " (var "offset") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an ftype-pointer object that refers to a Scheme object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Returns an ftype pointer object that can be used in the same places as a generic ftype pointer, and where the address encapsulated by the pointer is a reference address in the sense of " (code "object->reference-address") ". The reference address is for the result of " (code (var "expr")) ", without special treatment of " (code "#f") ". If an " (code (var "offset")) " expression is present, then it must produce a " (code "iptr") "-compatible integer, and the address encapsulated by the pointer is shifted by that amount.") ("The resulting ftype pointer is recognized by " (code "ftype-scheme-object-pointer?") " as well as " (code "ftype-pointer?") " without an " (code (var "ftype-name")) ".")) ("foreign:s166" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-pointer? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is an ftype pointer, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-pointer? " (var "ftype-name") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj"))