 from it and also from any shared object dynamically linked to it.") ("Because " (var "Chez Scheme") " is usually dynamically linked to the operating system's C library, all built-in C library functions are also accessible after evaluation of " (code "(load-shared-object #f)") ". This provides a simple way to gain access to standard C functions (such as " (code (var "memcpy")) " or " (code (var "getenv")) "), which may be very convenient for Scheme programs intended to be portable across different systems. If " (var "Chez Scheme") " is statically linked however, the standard C functions may only be accessible in this manner if they are present in the executable and exported, otherwise the shared object containing the C library must be explicitly named; see below for examples for some platforms.") ("On most Unix systems, " (code "load-shared-object") " is based on the system routine " (code "dlopen") ". Under Windows, " (code "load-shared-object") " is based on " (code "LoadLibrary") ". Refer to the documentation for these routines and for the C compiler and loader for precise rules for locating and building shared objects.") ((code "load-shared-object") " can be used to access built-in C library functions, such as " (code "getenv") ". The name of the shared object varies from one system to another.") ("On Linux systems:") ((code "(load-shared-object \"libc.so.6\")")) ("On Solaris, OpenSolaris, FreeBSD, NetBSD, and OpenBSD systems:") ((code "(load-shared-object \"libc.so\")")) ("On MacOS X systems:") ((code "(load-shared-object \"libc.dylib\")")) ("On Windows:") ((code "(load-shared-object \"msvcrt.dll\")")) ("Once the C library has been loaded, " (code "getenv") " should be available as a foreign entry.") ((code "(foreign-entry? \"getenv\") " (math "=>") " #t")) ("An equivalent Scheme procedure may be defined and invoked as follows.") ((code "(define getenv" (br) "  (foreign-procedure \"getenv\"" (br) "    (string)" (br) "    string))" (br) "(getenv \"HOME\") " (math "=>") " \"/home/elmer/fudd\"" (br) "(getenv \"home\") " (math "=>") " #f")) ((code "load-shared-object") " can be used to access user-created libraries as well. Suppose the C file " (code "\"even.c\"") "  contains") ((code "int even(n) int n; { return n == 0 || odd(n - 1); }")) ("and the C file " (code "\"odd.c\"") " contains") ((code "int odd(n) int n; { return n != 0 && even(n - 1); }")) ("The files must be compiled and linked into a shared object before they can be loaded. How this is done depends upon the host system. On Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, and OpenSolaris systems:") ((code "(system \"cc -fPIC -shared -o evenodd.so even.c odd.c\")")) ("Depending on the host configuration, the " (code "-m32") " or " (code "-m64") " option might be needed to specify 32-bit or 64-bit compilation as appropriate.") ("On MacOS X (Intel or PowerPC) systems:") ((code "(system \"cc -dynamiclib -o evenodd.so even.c odd.c\")")) ("Depending on the host configuration, the " (code "-m32") " or " (code "-m64") " option might be needed to specify 32-bit or 64-bit compilation as appropriate.") ("On 32-bit Sparc Solaris:") ((code "(system \"cc -KPIC -G -o evenodd.so even.c odd.c\")")) ("On 64-bit Sparc Solaris:") ((code "(system \"cc -xarch=v9 -KPIC -G -o evenodd.so even.c odd.c\")")) ("On Windows, we build a DLL (dynamic link library) file. In order to make the compiler generate the appropriate entry points, we alter " (code "even.c") " to read") ((code "#ifdef WIN32" (br) "#define EXPORT extern __declspec (dllexport)" (br) "#else" (br) "#define EXPORT extern" (br) "#endif" (br) (br) "EXPORT int even(n) int n; { return n == 0 || odd(n - 1); }")) ("and " (code "odd.c") " to read") ((code "#ifdef WIN32" (br) "#define EXPORT extern __declspec (dllexport)" (br) "#else" (br) "#define EXPORT extern" (br) "#endif" (br) (br) "EXPORT int odd(n) int n; { return n != 0 && even(n - 1); }")) ("We can then build the DLL as follows, giving it the extension \".so\" rather than \".dll\" for consistency with the other systems.") ((code "(system \"cl -c -DWIN32 even.c\")" (br) "(system \"cl -c -DWIN32 odd.c\")" (br) "(system \"link -dll -out:evenodd.so even.obj odd.obj\")")) ("The resulting \".so\" file can be loaded into Scheme and " (code "even") " and " (code "odd") " made available as foreign procedures:") ((code "(load-shared-object \"./evenodd.so\")" (br) "(let ([odd (foreign-procedure \"odd\"" (br) "             (integer-32) boolean)]" (br) "      [even (foreign-procedure \"even\"" (br) "              (integer-32) boolean)])" (br) "  (list (even 100) (odd 100))) " (math "=>") " (#t #f)")) ("The filename is given as " (code "\"./evenodd.so\"") " rather than simply " (code "\"evenodd.so\"") ", because some systems look for shared libraries in a standard set of system directories that does not include the current directory.")) ("foreign:s192" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(remove-foreign-entry " (var "entry-name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "remove-foreign-entry") " blocks further access to the entry specified by the string " (code (var "entry-name")) ". An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&assertion") "  if the entry does not exist. Since access previously established by " (code "foreign-procedure") " is not affected, " (code "remove-foreign-entry") " may be used to clean up after the desired interface to a group of foreign procedures has been established.") ((code "remove-foreign-entry") " can be used to remove entries registered using " (code "Sforeign_symbol") " and " (code "Sregister_symbol") " but not entries created as a result of a call to " (code "load-shared-object") ".")) ("io:s9" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(utf-16-codec)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(utf-16-codec " (var "endianness") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(utf-16le-codec)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(utf-16be-codec)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a codec" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "endianness")) " must be the symbol " (code "big") " or the symbol " (code "little") ".") ("The codec returned by " (code "utf-16-codec") " can be used to create and process data written UTF-16 format. When called without the " (code (var "endianness")) " argument or with " (code (var "endianness")) "  " (code "big") ", " (code "utf-16-codec") " returns a codec for standard UTF-16 data, i.e., one that defaults to big-endian format if no byte-order mark (BOM) is found.") ("When output is transcoded with a transcoder based on this codec, a BOM is emitted just before the first character written, and each character is written as a UTF-16 character in big-endian format. For input, a BOM is looked for at the start of the input and, if present, controls the byte order of the remaining UTF-16 characters. If no BOM is present, big-endian order is assumed. For input-output ports, the BOM is not emitted if the file is read before written, and a BOM is not looked for if the file is written before read.") ("For textual ports created via " (code "transcoded-port") ", a BOM written or read via the transcoder appears at the beginning of the underlying data stream or file only if the binary port passed to " (code "transcoded-port") "  is positioned at the start of the data stream or file. When the transcoder can determine this is the case, it sets a flag that causes " (code "set-port-position!") " to position the port beyond the BOM if an attempt is made to reposition the port to the start of the data stream or file, so that the BOM is preserved.") ("When called with " (code (var "endianness")) " " (code "little") ", " (code "utf-16-codec") "  returns a codec that defaults to the little-endian format both for reading and for writing. For output-only streams or input/output streams that are written before read, the result is standard UTF-16, with a BOM that specifies little-endian format followed by characters in little-endian byte order. For input-only streams or input/output streams that are read before written, this codec allows programs to read from input streams that either begin with a BOM or are encoded in UTF-16LE format. This is particularly useful for handling files that might have been produced by older Windows applications that claim to produce UTF-16 files but actually produce UTF-16LE files.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "utf-16-codec") " lacks the optional " (code (var "endianness")) " argument.") ("The codecs returned by " (code "utf-16le-codec") " and " (code "utf-16be-codec") "  are used to read and write data in the UTF-16LE and UTF-16BE formats, i.e., UTF-16 with little-endian or big-endian byte order and no BOM. For output, these codecs are useful for controlling whether and where the BOM is emitted, since no BOM is emitted implicitly and a BOM can be emitted explicitly as an ordinary character. For input, these codecs are useful for processing files known to be in little-endian or big-endian format with no BOM.")) ("io:s10" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(iconv-codec " (var "code-page") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a codec" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "code-page")) " must be a string and should identify a codec accepted by the " (code "iconv") " library installed on the target machine. The codec returned by this procedure can be used to convert from the non-Unicode single- and multiple-byte character sets supported by " (code "iconv") ". When used in the input direction, the codec converts byte sequences into Scheme strings, and when used in the output direction, it converts Scheme strings to byte sequences.") ("The set of supported code pages depends on the version of " (code "iconv") " available; consult the " (code "iconv") " documentation or use the shell command " (code "iconv --list") " to obtain a list of supported code pages.") ("While the Windows operating system does not supply an " (code "iconv") "  library, it is possible to use " (code "iconv-codec") " on Windows systems by supplying an " (code "iconv") " dynamic-link library (named " (code "iconv.dll") ", " (code "libiconv.dll") ", or " (code "libiconv-2.dll") ") that provides Posix-conformant " (code "iconv_open") ", " (code "iconv") ", and " (code "iconv_close") " entry points either under those names or under the alternative names " (code "libiconv_open") ", " (code "libiconv") ", and " (code "libiconv_close") ". The dll must be located in a standard location for dlls or in the current directory of the process the first time " (code "iconv-codec") "  is called.")) ("io:s11" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-transcoder") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The transcoder value of the " (code "current-transcoder") " parameter is used whenever a textual file is opened with an implicit transcoder, e.g., by " (code "open-input-file") " and other convenience I/O procedures, " (code "compile-file") " " (code "include") ", " (code "load") ", and " (code "pretty-file") ". Its initial value is the value of the " (code "native-transcoder") " procedure.")) ("io:s12" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(transcoder? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a transcoder, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("io:s13" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "transcoded-port-buffer-size") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "transcoded-port-buffer-size") " is a parameter that specifies the size of the string buffer that is allocated when creating a new transcoded port. The value of this parameter must be a positive fixnum. When transcoding a binary input port that implements " (code "port-position") ", a transcoded input port maintains an fxvector the same size as its string buffer to support " (code "port-position") " on the transcoded port.")) ("io:s14" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "make-codec-buffer") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "make-codec-buffer") " must be a procedure. The procedure is invoked with the binary port " (code (var "bp")) " when " (code "transcoded-port") " is called with a buffered port " (code (var "bp")) ". The procedure must return a mutable bytevector of length at least four. The behavior of " (code "transcoded-port") " and its callers is unspecified if a continuation captured within the " (code "make-codec-buffer") " procedure is invoked multiple times.")) ("io:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-input-port " (var "handler") " " (var "input-buffer") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-output-port " (var "handler") " " (var "output-buffer") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-input/output-port " (var "handler") " " (var "input-buffer") " " (var "output-buffer") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new textual port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "handler")) " must be a procedure, and " (code (var "input-buffer")) " and " (code (var "output-buffer")) " must be strings. Each procedure creates a generic port. The handler associated with the port is " (code (var "handler")) ", the input buffer is " (code (var "input-buffer")) ", and the output buffer is " (code (var "output-buffer")) ". For " (code "make-input-port") ", the output buffer is undefined, and for " (code "make-output-port") ", the input buffer is undefined.") ("The input size of an input or input/output port is initialized to the string length of the input buffer, and the input index is set to 0. The output size and index of an output or input/output port are initialized similarly.") ("The length of an input or output buffer may be zero, in which case buffering is effectively disabled.")) ("io:s17" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-handler " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("For generic ports, " (code "port-handler") " returns the handler passed to one of the generic port creation procedures described above. For ports created by " (code "open-input-file") " and similar procedures, " (code "port-handler") " returns an internal handler that may be invoked in the same manner as any other handler.")) ("io:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-input-buffer " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-input-size " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-input-index " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-input-buffer " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-input-size " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-input-index " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-input-buffer " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-input-size " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-input-index " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures return the input buffer, size, or index of the input port. The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterparts.")) ("io:s19" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-input-index! " (var "input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-input-size! " (var "input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-input-buffer! " (var "input-port") " " (var "x") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-input-index! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-input-size! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-input-buffer! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-input-index! " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-input-size! " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-input-buffer! " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "bytevector") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "set-port-input-index!") " sets the input index field of " (code (var "input-port")) " to " (var "n") ", which must be a nonnegative integer less than or equal to the port's input size.") ("The procedure " (code "set-port-input-size!") " sets the input size field of " (code (var "input-port")) " to " (var "n") ", which must be a nonnegative integer less than or equal to the string length of the port's input buffer. It also sets the input index to 0.") ("The procedure " (code "set-port-input-buffer!") " sets the input buffer field of " (code (var "input-port")) " to " (var "x") ", which must be a string for textual ports and a bytevector for binary ports. It also sets the input size to the length of the string or bytevector and the input index to 0.") ("The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterparts.")) ("io:s20" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-input-count " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-input-count " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-input-count " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures return an exact integer representing the number of characters or bytes left to be read from the port's input buffer, i.e., the difference between the buffer size and index.") ("The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterpart.")) ("io:s21" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-input-empty? " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port's input buffer contains no more data, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure determines whether the port's input count is zero without computing or returning the actual count.")) ("io:s22" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-output-buffer " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-output-size " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-output-index " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-output-buffer " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-output-size " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-output-index " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-output-buffer " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-output-size " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-output-index " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures return the output buffer, size, or index of the output port. The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterparts.")) ("io:s23" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-output-index! " (var "output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-output-size! " (var "output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-output-buffer! " (var "output-port") " " (var "x") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-output-index! " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-output-size! " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-textual-port-output-buffer! " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-output-index! " (var "output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-output-size! " (var "output-port") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-binary-port-output-buffer! " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "bytevector") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "set-port-output-index!") " sets the output index field of the output port to " (var "n") ", which must be a nonnegative integer less than or equal to the port's output size.") ("The procedure " (code "set-port-output-size!") " sets the output size field of the output port to " (var "n") ", which must be a nonnegative integer less than or equal to the string length of the port's output buffer. It also sets the output index to 0.") ("The procedure " (code "set-port-output-buffer!") " sets the output buffer field of " (code (var "output-port")) " to " (var "x") ", which must be a string for textual ports and a bytevector for binary ports. It also sets the output size to the length of the string or bytevector and the output index to 0.") ("The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterparts.")) ("io:s24" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-output-count " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(textual-port-output-count " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(binary-port-output-count " (var "binary-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures return an exact integer representing the amount of space in characters or bytes available to be written in the port's output buffer, i.e., the difference between the buffer size and index.") ("The variants specialized to textual or binary ports are slightly more efficient than their generic counterpart.")) ("io:s25" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-output-full? " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port's input buffer has no more room, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure determines whether the port's output count is zero without computing or returning the actual count.")) ("io:s26" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mark-port-closed! " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure directly marks the port closed so that no further input or output operations are allowed on it. It is typically used by handlers upon receipt of a " (code "close-port") "  message.")) ("io:s27" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-closed? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "port")) " is closed, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "  (port-closed? p)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "  (close-port p)" (br) "  (port-closed? p)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("io:s28" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-bol! " (var "output-port") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code (var "obj")) " is " (code "#f") ", the port's beginning-of-line (BOL) flag is cleared; otherwise, the port's BOL flag is set.") ("The BOL flag is consulted by " (code "fresh-line") "  (page " (link "./io.html#desc:fresh-line" "274") ") to determine if it needs to emit a newline. This flag is maintained automatically for file output ports, string output ports, and transcript ports. The flag is set for newly created file and string output ports, except for file output ports created with the " (code "append") " option, for which the flag is reset. The BOL flag is clear for newly created generic ports and never set automatically, but may be set explicitly using " (code "set-port-bol!") ". The port is always flushed immediately before the flag is consulted, so it need not be maintained on a per-character basis for buffered ports.")) ("io:s29" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-bol? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "port")) "'s BOL flag is set, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("io:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-eof! " (var "input-port") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code (var "obj")) " is not " (code "#f") ", " (code "set-port-eof!") " marks " (code (var "input-port")) "  so that, once its buffer is empty, the port is treated as if it were at eof even if more data is available in the underlying byte or character stream. Once this artificial eof has been read, the eof mark is cleared, making any additional data in the stream available beyond the eof. This feature can be used by a generic port to simulate a stream consisting of multiple input files.") ("When " (code (var "obj")) " is " (code "#f") ", the eof mark is cleared.") ("The following example assumes /dev/zero provides an infinite stream of zero bytes.") ((code "(define p" (br) "  (parameterize ([file-buffer-size 3])" (br) "    (open-file-input-port \"/dev/zero\")))" (br) "(set-port-eof! p #t)" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " #!eof" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(set-port-eof! p #t)" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " #!eof" (br) "(get-u8 p) " (math "=>") " 0"))) ("io:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-name " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the name associated with " (code (var "port")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The name may be any object but is usually a string or " (code "#f") "  (denoting no name). For file ports, the name is typically a string naming the file.") ((code "(let ([p (open-input-file \"myfile.ss\")])" (br) "  (port-name p)) " (math "=>") " \"myfile.ss\"" (br) (br) "(let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "  (port-name p)) " (math "=>") " \"string\""))) ("io:s32" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-name! " (var "port") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure sets " (code (var "port")) "'s name to " (code (var "obj")) ", which should be a string or " (code "#f") " (denoting no name).")) ("io:s33" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-length " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-length " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the length of the file or other object to which " (code (var "port")) " refers" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-has-port-length? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port supports " (code "port-length") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A port may allow the length of the underlying stream of characters or bytes to be determined. If so, the procedure " (code "port-has-port-length?") " returns " (code "#t") " and " (code "port-length") " returns the current length. For binary ports, the length is always an exact nonnegative integer byte count. For textual ports, the representation of a length is unspecified; it may not be an exact nonnegative integer and, even if it is, it may not represent either a byte or character count. The length may be used at some later time to reset the length if the port supports " (code "set-port-length!") ". If " (code "port-length") " is called on a port that does not support it, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("File lengths beyond 2" (sup "32") " might not be reported property for compressed files on 32-bit versions of the system.") ((code "file-length") " is identical to " (code "port-length") ".")) ("io:s34" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-length! " (var "port") " " (var "len") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-has-set-port-length!? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port supports " (code "set-port-length!") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A port may allow the length of the underlying stream of characters or bytes to be set, i.e., extended or truncated. If so, the procedure " (code "port-has-set-port-length!?") " returns " (code "#t") " and " (code "set-port-length!") " changes the length. For binary ports, the length " (code (var "len")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer byte count. For textual ports, the representation of a length is unspecified, as described in the entry for " (code "port-length") " above, but " (code (var "len")) " must be an appropriate length for the textual port, which is usually guaranteed to be the case only if it was obtained from a call to " (code "port-length") "  on the same port. If " (code "set-port-length!") " is called on a port that does not support it, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("It is not possible to set the length of a port opened with compression to an arbitrary position, and the result of an attempt to set the length of a compressed file beyond 2" (sup "32") " on 32-bit versions of the system is undefined.")) ("io:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-nonblocking? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port is in nonblocking mode, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-has-port-nonblocking?? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port supports " (code "port-nonblocking?") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A port may allow the nonblocking status of the port to be determined. If so, the procedure " (code "port-has-port-nonblocking??") " returns " (code "#t") " and " (code "port-nonblocking?") " returns a boolean value reflecting whether the port is in nonblocking mode.")) ("io:s36" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-port-nonblocking! " (var "port") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-has-set-port-nonblocking!? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the port supports " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A port may allow reads or writes to be performed in a \"nonblocking\" fashion. If so, the procedure " (code "port-has-set-port-nonblocking!?") " returns " (code "#t") " and " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") " sets the port to nonblocking mode (if " (code (var "obj")) " is a true value) or blocking mode (if " (code (var "obj")) " is " (code "#f") "). If " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") " is called on a port that does not support it, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("Ports created by the standard Revised" (sup "6") " port opening procedures are initially set in blocking mode by default. The same is true for most of the procedures described in this document. A generic port based on a nonblocking source may be nonblocking initially. A port returned by " (code "open-fd-input-port") ", " (code "open-fd-output-port") ", or " (code "open-fd-input/output-port") "  is initially in nonblocking mode if the file-descriptor passed in is in nonblocking mode. Similarly, a port returned by " (code "standard-input-port") ", " (code "standard-output-port") ", or " (code "standard-error-port") " is initially in nonblocking mode if the underlying stdin, stdout, or stderr file descriptor is in nonblocking mode.") ("Although " (code "get-bytevector-some") " and " (code "get-string-some") " normally cannot return an empty bytevector or empty string, they can if the port is in nonblocking mode and no input is available. Also, " (code "get-bytevector-some!") " and " (code "get-string-some!") "  may not read any data if the port is in nonblocking mode and no data is available. Similarly, " (code "put-bytevector-some") " and " (code "put-string-some") "  may not write any data if the port is in nonblocking mode and no room is available.") ("Nonblocking mode is not supported under Windows.")) ("io:s37" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-position " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-position " (var "port") " " (var "pos") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When the second argument is omitted, this procedure behaves like the R6RS " (code "port-position") " procedure, and when present, like the R6RS " (code "set-port-position!") " procedure.") ("For compressed files opened with the " (code "compressed") " flag, " (code "file-position") " returns the position in the uncompressed stream of data. Changing the position of a compressed input file opened with the " (code "compressed") " flag generally requires rewinding and rereading the file and might thus be slow. The position of a compressed output file opened with the " (code "compressed") " flag can be moved forward only; this is accomplished by writing a (compressed) sequence of zeros. File positions beyond 2" (sup "32") " might not be reported property for compressed files on 32-bit versions of the system.")) ("io:s38" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(clear-input-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(clear-input-port " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "input-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current input port. This procedure discards any data in the buffer associated with " (code (var "input-port")) ". This may be necessary, for example, to clear any type-ahead from the keyboard in preparation for an urgent query.")) ("io:s39" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(clear-output-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(clear-output-port " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port. This procedure discards any data in the buffer associated with " (code (var "output-port")) ". This may be necessary, for example, to clear any pending output on an interactive port in preparation for an urgent message.")) ("io:s40" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(flush-output-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(flush-output-port " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port. This procedure  forces any data in the buffer associated with " (code (var "output-port")) " to be printed immediately. The console output port is automatically flushed after a newline and before input from the console input port; all ports are automatically flushed when they are closed. " (code "flush-output-port") " may be necessary, however, to force a message without a newline to be sent to the console output port or to force output to appear on a file without delay.")) ("io:s41" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-file-compressed! " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "port")) " must be an input or an output port, but not an input/output port. It must be a file port pointing to a regular file, i.e., a file on disk rather than, e.g., a socket. The port can be a binary or textual port. If the port is an output port, subsequent output sent to the port will be compressed. If the port is an input port, subsequent input will be decompressed if and only if the port is currently pointing at compressed data. The compression format for output is determined by the " (code "compress-format") "  parameter, while the compression format on input is inferred. The compression level, which is relevant only for output, is determined by the " (code "compress-level") " parameter. This procedure has no effect if the port is already set for compression.")) ("io:s44" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compress-format") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "compress-format") " determines the compression algorithm and format used for output. Currently, the possible values of the parameter are the symbols " (code "lz4") " (the default) and " (code "gzip") ".") ("The " (code "lz4") " format uses the LZ4 compression library developed by Yann Collet. It is therefore compatible with the " (code "lz4") " program, which means that " (code "lz4") " may be used to uncompress files produced by " (var "Chez Scheme") " and visa versa.") ("The " (code "gzip") " format uses the zlib compression library developed by Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler. It is therefore compatible with the " (code "gzip") " program, which means that " (code "gzip") " may be used to uncompress files produced by " (var "Chez Scheme") " and visa versa.") ("Reading " (code "lz4") "-compressed data tends to be much faster than reading " (code "gzip") "-compressed data, while " (code "gzip") "-compressed data tends to  be significantly smaller.")) ("io:s45" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compress-level") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "compress-level") " determines the amount of effort spent on compression and is thus relevant only for output. It can be set to one of the symbols " (code "minimum") ", " (code "low") ", " (code "medium") ", " (code "high") ", or " (code "maximum") ", which are listed in order from shortest to longest expected compression time and least to greatest expected effectiveness. Its default value is " (code "medium") ".")) ("io:s46" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-input-string " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new string input port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A string input port is similar to a file input port, except that characters and objects drawn from the port come from " (code (var "string")) "  rather than from a file.") ("A string port is at \"end of file\" when the port reaches the end of the string. It is not necessary to close a string port, although it is okay to do so.") ((code "(let ([p (open-input-string \"hi mom!\")])" (br) "  (let ([x (read p)])" (br) "    (list x (read p)))) " (math "=>") " (hi mom!)"))) ("io:s48" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-input-from-string " (var "string") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values returned by " (code (var "thunk")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments. " (code "with-input-from-string") " parameterizes the current input port to be the result of opening " (code (var "string")) " for input during the application of " (code (var "thunk")) ".") ((code "(with-input-from-string \"(cons 3 4)\"" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (eval (read)))) " (math "=>") " (3 . 4)"))) ("io:s49" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-output-string)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new string output port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A string output port is similar to a file output port, except that characters and objects written to the port are placed in a string (which grows as needed) rather than to a file. The string built by writing to a string output port may be obtained with " (code "get-output-string") ". See the example given for " (code "get-output-string") " below. It is not necessary to close a string port, although it is okay to do so.")) ("io:s51" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-output-string " (var "string-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the string associated with " (code (var "string-output-port")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "string-output-port")) " must be an port returned by " (code "open-output-string") ".") ("As a side effect, " (code "get-output-string") " resets " (code (var "string-output-port")) "  so that subsequent output to " (code (var "string-output-port")) " is placed into a fresh string.") ((code "(let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "  (write 'hi p)" (br) "  (write-char #\\space p)" (br) "  (write 'mom! p)" (br) "  (get-output-string p)) " (math "=>") " \"hi mom!\"")) ("An implementation of " (code "format") " (Section " (link "./io.html#g90" "9.13") ") might be written using string-output ports to produce string output.")) ("io:s52" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-output-to-string " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a string containing the output" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments. " (code "with-output-to-string") " parameterizes the current output port to a new string output port during the application of " (code (var "thunk")) ". If " (code (var "thunk")) " returns, the string associated with the new string output port is returned, as with " (code "get-output-string") ".") ((code "(with-output-to-string" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (display \"Once upon a time ...\")" (br) "    (newline))) " (math "=>") " \"Once upon a time ...\\n\""))) ("io:s53" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "file-buffer-size") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "file-buffer-size") " is a parameter that determines the size of each buffer created when the buffer mode is not " (code "none") " for a port created by one of the file open operations, e.g., " (code "open-input-file") "  or " (code "open-file-output-port") ". The value of this parameter must be a positive fixnum.")) ("io:s54" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-port? " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "port")) " is a file port, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A file port is any port based directly on an O/S file descriptor, e.g., one created by " (code "open-file-input-port") ", " (code "open-output-port") ", " (code "open-fd-input-port") ", etc., but not a string, bytevector, or custom  port.")) ("io:s55" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(port-file-descriptor " (var "port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the file descriptor associated with " (code (var "port")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "port")) " must be a file port, i.e., a port for which " (code (var "file-port?")) "  returns " (code "#t") ".")) ("io:s56" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "custom-port-buffer-size") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "custom-port-buffer-size") " is a parameter that determines the sizes of the buffers associated with newly created custom ports. The value of this parameter must be a positive fixnum.")) ("io:s57" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "console-input-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "console-input-port") " is a parameter that determines the input port used by the waiter and interactive debugger. When called with no arguments, it returns the console input port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual input port, it changes the value of the console input port. The initial value of this parameter is a port tied to the standard input (stdin) stream of the Scheme process.")) ("io:s58" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-input-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-input-port") " is a parameter that determines the default port argument for most input procedures, including " (code "read-char") ", " (code "peek-char") ", and " (code "read") ", When called with no arguments, " (code "current-input-port") " returns the current input port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual input port, it changes the value of the current input port. The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "current-input-port") " accepts only zero arguments, i.e., it cannot be used to change the current input port. The initial value of this parameter is the same port as the initial value of " (code "console-input-port") ".")) ("io:s59" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-input-file " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-input-file " (var "path") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new input port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "open-input-file") " opens a textual input port for the file named by " (code (var "path")) ". An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") " if the file does not exist or cannot be opened for input.") ((code (var "options")) ", if present, is a symbolic option name or option list. Possible symbolic option names are  " (code "compressed") ", " (code "uncompressed") ", " (code "buffered") ", and " (code "unbuffered") ". An option list is a list containing zero or more symbolic option names.") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "compressed") " and " (code "uncompressed") " options determine whether the input file should be decompressed if it is compressed (where the compression format is inferred). (See " (code "open-output-file") ".) The default is " (code "uncompressed") ", so the " (code "uncompressed") "  option is useful only as documentation.") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "buffered") " and " (code "unbuffered") "  options determine whether input is buffered. When input is buffered, it is read in large blocks and buffered internally for efficiency to reduce the number of operating system requests. When the " (code "unbuffered") " option is specified, input is unbuffered, but not fully, since one character of buffering is required to support " (code "peek-char") " and " (code "unread-char") ". Input is buffered by default, so the " (code "buffered") " option is useful only as documentation.") ("For example, the call") ((code "(open-input-file \"frob\" '(compressed))")) ("opens the file frob with decompression enabled.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "open-input-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.")) ("io:s60" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-input-file " (var "path") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-input-file " (var "path") " " (var "procedure") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values returned by " (code (var "procedure")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one argument.") ((code "call-with-input-file") " creates a new input port for the file named by " (code (var "path")) ", as if with " (code "open-input-file") ", and passes this port to " (code (var "procedure")) ". If " (code (var "procedure")) " returns normally, " (code "call-with-input-file") " closes the input port and returns the values returned by " (code (var "procedure")) ".") ((code "call-with-input-file") " does not automatically close the input port if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "procedure")) " is invoked, since it is possible that another continuation created inside of " (code (var "procedure")) " will be invoked at a later time, returning control to " (code (var "procedure")) ". If " (code (var "procedure")) " does not return, an implementation is free to close the input port only if it can prove that the input port is no longer accessible. As shown in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./control.html#g102" "5.6") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ", " (code "dynamic-wind") " may be used to ensure that the port is closed if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "procedure")) " is invoked.") ("See " (code "open-input-file") " above for a description of the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "call-with-input-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "input")) " argument.")) ("io:s61" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-input-from-file " (var "path") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-input-from-file " (var "path") " " (var "thunk") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values returned by " (code (var "thunk")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments.") ((code "with-input-from-file") " temporarily changes the current input port to be the result of opening the file named by " (code (var "path")) ", as if with " (code "open-input-file") ", during the application of " (code (var "thunk")) ". If " (code (var "thunk")) " returns, the port is closed and the current input port is restored to its old value.") ("The behavior of " (code "with-input-from-file") " is unspecified if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "thunk")) " is invoked before " (code (var "thunk")) " returns. An implementation may close the port and restore the current input port to its old value---but it may not.") ("See " (code "open-input-file") " above for a description of the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "with-input-from-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.")) ("io:s62" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input-port " (var "fd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new input port for the file descriptor " (code (var "fd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "fd")) " must be a nonnegative exact integer and should be a valid open file descriptor. If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual input port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary input port. See the lead-in to Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./io.html#g123" "7.2") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "  for a description of the constraints on and effects of the other arguments.") ("The file descriptor is closed when the port is closed.")) ("io:s63" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-input-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-input-port " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-input-port " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new input port connected to the process's standard input" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual input port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary input port. The buffer mode " (code (var "b-mode")) " defaults to " (code "block") ".") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this procedure does not accept the optional " (code (var "b-mode")) " and " (code (var "?transcoder")) " arguments, which limits it to an implementation-dependent buffering mode (" (code "block") " in " (var "Chez Scheme") ") and binary output.")) ("io:s64" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-string-some " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a nonempty string or the eof object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is at end of file, the eof object is returned. Otherwise, " (code "get-string-some") " reads (as if with " (code "get-u8") ") at least one character and possibly more, and returns a string containing these characters. The port's position is advanced past the characters read. The maximum number of characters read by this operation is implementation-dependent.") ("An exception to the \"at least one character\" guarantee occurs if the port is in nonblocking mode (see " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") ") and no input is ready. In this case, an empty string is returned.")) ("io:s65" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-string-some! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "string") " " (var "start") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the count of characters read, as an exact nonnegative integer, or the eof object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must be exact nonnegative integers, and the sum of " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must not exceed the length of " (code (var "string")) ".") ("If " (code (var "n")) " is 0, this procedure returns zero without attempting to read from " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " and without modifying " (code (var "string")) ".") ("Otherwise, if " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is at end of file, this procedure returns the eof object, except it returns zero when the port is in nonblocking mode (see " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") ") and the port cannot be determined to be at end of file without blocking. In either case, " (code (var "string")) " is not modified.") ("Otherwise, this procedure reads (as if with " (code "get-char") ") up to " (code (var "n")) " characters from the port, stores the characters in consecutive locations of " (code (var "string")) "  starting at " (code (var "start")) ", advances the port's position just past the characters read, and returns the count of characters read.") ("If the port is in nonblocking mode, this procedure reads no more than it can without blocking and thus might read zero characters; otherwise, it reads at least one character but no more than are available when the first character becomes available.")) ("io:s66" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-bytevector-some! " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "bytevector") " " (var "start") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the count of bytes read, as an exact nonnegative integer, or the eof object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must be exact nonnegative integers, and the sum of " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must not exceed the length of " (code (var "bytevector")) ".") ("If " (code (var "n")) " is 0, this procedure returns zero without attempting to read from " (code (var "binary-input-port")) " and without modifying " (code (var "bytevector")) ".") ("Otherwise, if " (code (var "binary-input-port")) " is at end of file, this procedure returns the eof object, except it returns zero when the port is in nonblocking mode (see " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") ") and the port cannot be determined to be at end of file without blocking. In either case, " (code (var "bytevector")) " is not modified.") ("Otherwise, this procedure reads (as if with " (code "get-u8") ") up to " (code (var "n")) " bytes from the port, stores the bytes in consecutive locations of " (code (var "bytevector")) "  starting at " (code (var "start")) ", advances the port's position just past the bytes read, and returns the count of bytes read.") ("If the port is in nonblocking mode, this procedure reads no more than it can without blocking and thus might read zero bytes; otherwise, it reads at least one byte but no more than are available when the first byte becomes available.")) ("io:s67" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unread-char " (var "char") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unread-char " (var "char") " " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unget-char " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "char") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("For " (code "unread-char") ", if " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current input port. These procedures \"unread\" the last character read from " (code "textual-input-port") ". " (code (var "char")) " may or may not be ignored, depending upon the implementation. In any case, " (code (var "char")) " should be last character read from the port. A character should not be unread twice on the same port without an intervening call to " (code "read-char") " or " (code "get-char") ".") ((code "unread-char") " and " (code "unget-char") " are provided for applications requiring one character of lookahead and may be used in place of, or even in combination with, " (code "peek-char") " or " (code "lookahead-char") ". One character of lookahead is required in the procedure " (code "read-word") ", which is defined below in terms of " (code "unread-char") ". " (code "read-word") " returns the next word from a textual input port as a string, where a word is defined to be a sequence of alphabetic characters. Since it does not know until it reads one character too many that it has read the entire word, " (code "read-word") " uses " (code "unread-char") " to return the character to the input port.") ((code "(define read-word" (br) "  (lambda (p)" (br) "    (list->string" (br) "      (let f ([c (read-char p)])" (br) "        (cond" (br) "          [(eof-object? c) '()]" (br) "          [(char-alphabetic? c)" (br) "           (cons c (f (read-char p)))]" (br) "          [else" (br) "           (unread-char c p)" (br) "           '()])))))")) ("In the alternate version below, " (code "peek-char") " is used instead of " (code "unread-char") ".") ((code "(define read-word" (br) "  (lambda (p)" (br) "    (list->string" (br) "      (let f ([c (peek-char p)])" (br) "        (cond" (br) "          [(eof-object? c) '()]" (br) "          [(char-alphabetic? c)" (br) "           (read-char p)" (br) "           (cons c (f (peek-char p)))]" (br) "          [else '()])))))")) ("The advantage of " (code "unread-char") " in this situation is that only one call to " (code "unread-char") " per word is required, whereas one call to " (code "peek-char") " is required for each character in the word plus the first character beyond. In many cases, " (code "unread-char") " and " (code "unget-char") " do not enjoy this advantage, and " (code "peek-char") " or " (code "lookahead-char") "  should be used instead.")) ("io:s68" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unget-u8 " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "octet") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedures \"unreads\" the last byte read from " (code "binary-input-port") ". " (code (var "octet")) " may or may not be ignored, depending upon the implementation. In any case, " (code (var "octet")) " should be last byte read from the port. A byte should not be unread twice on the same port without an intervening call to " (code "get-u8") ".")) ("io:s69" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(input-port-ready? " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if data is available on " (code (var "input-port")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "input-port-ready?") " allows a program to check to see if input is available on a textual or binary input port without hanging. If input is available or the port is at end of file, " (code "input-port-ready?") " returns " (code "#t") ". If it cannot determine from the port whether input is ready,  " (code "input-port-ready?") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&i/o-read-error") ". Otherwise, it returns " (code "#f") ".")) ("io:s70" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(char-ready?)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(char-ready? " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if a character is available on " (code (var "textual-input-port")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current input port. " (code "char-ready?") " is like " (code "input-port-ready?") " except it is restricted to textual input ports.")) ("io:s71" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(block-read " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(block-read " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "string") " " (var "count") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "count")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum less than or equal to the length of " (code (var "string")) ". If not provided, it defaults to the length of " (code (var "string")) ".") ("If " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is at end-of-file, an eof object is returned. Otherwise, " (code (var "string")) " is filled with as many characters as are available for reading from " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " up to " (code (var "count")) ", and the number of characters placed in the string is returned.") ("If " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is buffered and the buffer is nonempty, the buffered input or a portion thereof is returned; otherwise " (code "block-read") " bypasses the buffer entirely.")) ("io:s72" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(read-token)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(read-token " (var "textual-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(read-token " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "bfp") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. " (code (var "bfp")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer and should be the character position of the next character to be read from " (code (var "textual-input-port")) ".") ("Parsing of a Scheme datum is conceptually performed in two steps. First, the sequence of characters that form the datum are grouped into " (code "tokens") ", such as symbols, numbers, left parentheses, and double quotes. During this first step, whitespace and comments are discarded. Second, these tokens are grouped into data.") ((code "read") " performs both of these steps and creates an internal representation of each datum it parses. " (code "read-token") " may be used to perform the first step only, one token at a time. " (code "read-token") " is intended to be used by editors and program formatters that must be able to parse a program or datum without actually reading it.") ("If " (code (var "textual-input-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current input port. One token is read from the input port and returned as four values:") ((dt (code (var "type")) ":") (dd " a symbol describing the type of token read,")) ((dt (code (var "value")) ":") (dd " the token value,")) ((dt (code (var "start")) ":") (dd " the position of the first character of the token, relative to the starting position of the input port (or " (code "#f") ", if the position cannot be determined), and")) ((dt (code (var "end")) ":") (dd " the first position beyond the token, relative to the starting position of the input port (or " (code "#f") ", if the position cannot be determined).")) ("The input port is left pointing to the first character position beyond the token.") ("When the token type fully specifies the token, " (code "read-token") " returns " (code "#f") " for the value. The token types are listed below with the corresponding " (code (var "value")) "  in parentheses.") ((dt (code "atomic")) (dd " (" (code (var "atom")) ") an atomic value, i.e.,  a symbol, boolean, number, character, " (code "#!eof") ", or " (code "#!bwp")) (dt (code "box")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") box prefix, i.e.,  " (code "#&")) (dt (code "dot")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") dotted pair separator, i.e., " (code ".")) (dt (code "eof")) (dd " (" (code "#!eof") ") end of file") (dt (code "fasl")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") fasl prefix, i.e., " (code "#@")) (dt (code "insert")) (dd " (" (code (var "n")) ") graph reference, i.e., " (code "#" (var "n") "#")) (dt (code "lbrack")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") open square bracket") (dt (code "lparen")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") open parenthesis") (dt (code "mark")) (dd " (" (code (var "n")) ") graph mark, i.e., " (code "#" (var "n") "=")) (dt (code "quote")) (dd " (" (code "quote") ", " (code "quasiquote") ",  " (code "syntax") ", " (code "unquote") ", " (code "unquote-splicing") ", or " (code "datum-comment") ") an abbreviation mark, e.g., " (code "'") " or " (code ",@") " or datum-comment prefix") (dt (code "rbrack")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") close square bracket") (dt (code "record-brack")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") record open bracket, i.e., " (code "#[")) (dt (code "rparen")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") close parenthesis") (dt (code "vfxnparen")) (dd " (" (code (var "n")) ") fxvector prefix, i.e., " (code "#" (var "n") "vfx(")) (dt (code "vfxparen")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") fxvector prefix, i.e., " (code "#vfx(")) (dt (code "vnparen")) (dd " (" (code (var "n")) ") vector prefix, i.e., " (code "#" (var "n") "(")) (dt (code "vparen")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") vector prefix, i.e., " (code "#(")) (dt (code "vu8nparen")) (dd " (" (code (var "n")) ") bytevector prefix, i.e., " (code "#" (var "n") "vu8(")) (dt (code "vu8paren")) (dd " (" (code "#f") ") bytevector prefix, i.e., " (code "#vu8("))) ("The set of token types is likely to change in future releases of the system; check the release notes for details on such changes.") ("Specifying " (code (var "sfd")) " and " (code (var "bfp")) " improves the quality of error messages, guarantees " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "end")) " can be determined, and eliminates the overhead of asking for a file position on each call to " (code "read-token") ". In most cases, " (code (var "bfp")) " should be 0 for the first call to " (code "read-token") " at the start of a file, and it should be the fourth return value (" (code (var "end")) ") of the preceding call to " (code "read-token") " for each subsequent call. This protocol is necessary to handle files containing multiple-byte characters, since file positions do not necessarily correspond to character positions.") ((code "(define s (open-input-string \"(a b c)\"))" (br) "(read-token s) " (math "=>") " lparen" (br) "                   #f" (br) "                   0" (br) "                   1" (br) "(define s (open-input-string \"abc 123\"))" (br) "(read-token s) " (math "=>") " atomic" (br) "                   abc" (br) "                   0" (br) "                   3" (br) "(define s (open-input-string \"\"))" (br) "(read-token s) " (math "=>") " eof" (br) "                   #!eof" (br) "                   0" (br) "                   0" (br) "(define s (open-input-string \"#7=#7#\"))" (br) "(read-token s) " (math "=>") " mark" (br) "                   7" (br) "                   0" (br) "                   3" (br) "(read-token s) " (math "=>") " insert" (br) "                   7" (br) "                   3" (br) "                   6")) ("The information " (code "read-token") " returns is not always sufficient for reconstituting the exact sequence of characters that make up a token. For example, " (code "1.0") " and " (code "1e0") " both return " (code (var "type")) " " (code "atomic") " with " (code (var "value")) " " (code "1.0") ". The exact sequence of characters may be obtained only by repositioning the port and reading a block of characters of the appropriate length, using the relative positions given by " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "end")) ".")) ("io:s73" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "console-output-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "console-output-port") " is a parameter that determines the output port used by the waiter and interactive debugger. When called with no arguments, it returns the console output port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual output port, it changes the value of the console output port. The initial value of this parameter is a port tied to the standard output (stdout) stream of the Scheme process.")) ("io:s74" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-output-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-output-port") " is a parameter that determines the default port argument for most output procedures, including " (code "write-char") ", " (code "newline") ", " (code "write") ", " (code "display") ", and " (code "pretty-print") ". When called with no arguments, " (code "current-output-port") " returns the current output port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual output port, it changes the value of the current output port. The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "current-output-port") " accepts only zero arguments, i.e., it cannot be used to change the current output port. The initial value of this parameter is the same port as the initial value of " (code "console-output-port") ".")) ("io:s75" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "console-error-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "console-error-port") " is a parameter that can be used to set or obtain the console error port, which determines the port to which conditions and other messages are printed by the default exception handler. When called with no arguments, " (code "console-error-port") " returns the console error port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual output port, it changes the value of the console error port.") ("If the system determines that the standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) streams refer to the same file, socket, pipe, virtual terminal, device, etc., this parameter is initially set to the same value as the parameter " (code "console-output-port") ". Otherwise, this parameter is initially set to a different port tied to the standard error (stderr) stream of the Scheme process.")) ("io:s76" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-error-port") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-error-port") " is a parameter that can be used to set or obtain the current error port. When called with no arguments, " (code "current-error-port") " returns the current error port. When called with one argument, which must be a textual output port, it changes the value of the current error port. The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "current-error-port") " accepts only zero arguments, i.e., it cannot be used to change the current error port. The initial value of this parameter is the same port as the initial value of " (code "console-error-port") ".")) ("io:s77" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-output-file " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-output-file " (var "path") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new output port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "open-output-file") " opens a textual output port for the file named by " (code (var "path")) ".") ((code (var "options")) ", if present, is a symbolic option name or option list. Possible symbolic option names are  " (code "error") ", " (code "truncate") ", " (code "replace") ", " (code "append") ", " (code "compressed") ", " (code "uncompressed") ", " (code "buffered") ", " (code "unbuffered") ", " (code "exclusive") ", and " (code "nonexclusive") ". An option list is a list containing zero or more symbolic option names and possibly the two-element option " (code "mode " (var "mode")) ".") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "error") ", " (code "truncate") ", " (code "replace") ", and " (code "append") " options are used to direct what happens when the file to be opened already exists. " (dt (code "error") ":") (dd " An exception is raised with condition-type " (code "&i/o-filename") ".") (dt (code "replace") ":") (dd " The existing file is deleted before the new file is opened.") (dt (code "truncate") ":") (dd " The existing file is opened and truncated to zero length.") (dt (code "append") ":") (dd "  The existing file is opened and the output port is positioned at the end of the file before each write so that output to the port is always appended to the file.") "  The default behavior is to raise an exception.") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "compressed") " and " (code "uncompressed") " options determine whether the output file is to be compressed. The compression format and level are determined by the " (code "compress-format") "  and " (code "compress-level") "  parameters. Files are uncompressed by default, so the " (code "uncompressed") "  option is useful only as documentation.") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "buffered") " and " (code "unbuffered") "  options determine whether output is buffered. Unbuffered output is sent immediately to the file, whereas buffered output not written until the port's output buffer is filled or the port is flushed (via " (code "flush-output-port") ") or closed (via " (code "flush-output-port") " or by the storage management system when the port becomes inaccessible). Output is buffered by default for efficiency, so the " (code "buffered") " option is useful only as documentation.") ("The mutually exclusive " (code "exclusive") " and " (code "nonexclusive") "  options determine whether access to the file is \"exclusive.\" When the exclusive option is specified, the file is locked until the port is closed to prevent access by other processes. On some systems the lock is advisory, i.e., it inhibits access by  other processes only if they also attempt to open exclusively. Nonexclusive access is the default, so the " (code "nonexclusive") " option is useful only as documentation.") ("The " (code "mode") " option determines the permission bits on Unix systems when the file is created by the operation, subject to the process umask. The subsequent element in the options list must be an exact integer specifying the permissions in the manner of the Unix " (code "open") "  function. The mode option is ignored under Windows.") ("For example, the call") ((code "(open-output-file \"frob\" '(compressed truncate mode #o644))")) ("opens the file frob with compression enabled. If frob already exists it is truncated. On Unix-based systems, if frob does not already exist, the permission bits on the newly created file are set to logical and of " (code "#o644") " and the process's umask.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "open-output-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.")) ("io:s80" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-output-file " (var "path") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-output-file " (var "path") " " (var "procedure") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values returned by " (code (var "procedure")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one argument.") ((code "call-with-output-file") " creates a new output port for the file named by " (code (var "path")) ", as if with " (code "open-output-file") ", and passes this port to " (code (var "procedure")) ". If " (code (var "procedure")) " returns, " (code "call-with-output-file") " closes the output port and returns the values returned by " (code (var "procedure")) ".") ((code "call-with-output-file") " does not automatically close the output port if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "procedure")) " is invoked, since it is possible that another continuation created inside of " (code (var "procedure")) " will be invoked at a later time, returning control to " (code (var "procedure")) ". If " (code (var "procedure")) " does not return, an implementation is free to close the output port only if it can prove that the output port is no longer accessible. As shown in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./control.html#g102" "5.6") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ", " (code "dynamic-wind") " may be used to ensure that the port is closed if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "procedure")) " is invoked.") ("See " (code "open-output-file") " above for a description of the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "call-with-output-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.")) ("io:s81" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-output-to-file " (var "path") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-output-to-file " (var "path") " " (var "thunk") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value returned by " (code (var "thunk")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments.") ((code "with-output-to-file") " temporarily rebinds the current output port to be the result of opening the file named by " (code (var "path")) ", as if with " (code "open-output-file") ", during the application of " (code (var "thunk")) ". If " (code (var "thunk")) " returns, the port is closed and the current output port is restored to its old value.") ("The behavior of " (code "with-output-to-file") " is unspecified if a continuation created outside of " (code (var "thunk")) " is invoked before " (code (var "thunk")) " returns. An implementation may close the port and restore the current output port to its old value---but it may not.") ("See " (code "open-output-file") " above for a description of the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of " (code "with-output-to-file") " does not support the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument.")) ("io:s82" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-output-port " (var "fd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-output-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-output-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new output port for the file descriptor " (code (var "fd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "fd")) " must be a nonnegative exact integer and should be a valid open file descriptor. If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual output port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary output port. See the lead-in to Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./io.html#g123" "7.2") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "  for a description of the constraints on and effects of the other arguments.") ("The file descriptor is closed when the port is closed.")) ("io:s83" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-output-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-output-port " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-output-port " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new output port connected to the process's standard output" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual output port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary output port. The buffer mode " (code (var "b-mode")) " defaults to " (code "line") ", which differs from " (code "block") " in " (var "Chez Scheme") " only for textual output ports.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this procedure does not accept the optional " (code (var "b-mode")) " and " (code (var "?transcoder")) " arguments, which limits it to an implementation-dependent buffering mode (" (code "line") " in " (var "Chez Scheme") ") and binary output.")) ("io:s84" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-error-port)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-error-port " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(standard-error-port " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new output port connected to the process's standard error" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual output port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary output port. The buffer mode " (code (var "b-mode")) " defaults to " (code "none") ". See the lead-in to Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./io.html#g123" "7.2") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "  for a description of the constraints on and effects of the other arguments.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this procedure does not accept the optional " (code (var "b-mode")) " and " (code (var "?transcoder")) " arguments, which limits it to an implementation-dependent buffering mode (" (code "none") " in " (var "Chez Scheme") ") and binary output.")) ("io:s85" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-bytevector-some " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "bytevector") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-bytevector-some " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "bytevector") " " (var "start") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-bytevector-some " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "bytevector") " " (var "start") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of bytes written" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must be nonnegative exact integers, and the sum of " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must not exceed the length of " (code (var "bytevector")) ". If not supplied, " (code (var "start")) " defaults to zero and " (code (var "n")) " defaults to the difference between the length of " (code (var "bytevector")) " and " (code (var "start")) ".") ("This procedure normally writes the " (code (var "n")) " bytes of " (code (var "bytevector")) "  starting at " (code (var "start")) " to the port and advances the its position past the end of the bytes written. If the port is in nonblocking mode (see " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") "), however, the number of bytes written may be less than " (code (var "n")) ", if the system would have to block to write more bytes.")) ("io:s86" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-string-some " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-string-some " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") " " (var "start") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-string-some " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") " " (var "start") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of characters written" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must be nonnegative exact integers, and the sum of " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "n")) " must not exceed the length of " (code (var "string")) ". If not supplied, " (code (var "start")) " defaults to zero and " (code (var "n")) " defaults to the difference between the length of " (code (var "string")) " and " (code (var "start")) ".") ("This procedure normally writes the " (code (var "n")) " characters of " (code (var "string")) "  starting at " (code (var "start")) " to the port and advances the its position past the end of the characters written. If the port is in nonblocking mode (see " (code "set-port-nonblocking!") "), however, the number of characters written may be less than " (code (var "n")) ", if the system would have to block to write more characters.")) ("io:s87" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-string " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-string " (var "string") " " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "display-string") " writes the characters contained within " (code (var "string")) " to " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " or to the current-output port  if " (code "textual-output-port") " is not specified. The enclosing string quotes are not printed, and special characters within the string are not escaped. " (code "display-string") " is a more efficient alternative to " (code "display") " for displaying strings.")) ("io:s88" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(block-write " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(block-write " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "string") " " (var "count") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "count")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum less than or equal to the length of " (code (var "string")) ". If not provided, it defaults to the length of " (code (var "string")) ".") ((code "block-write") " writes the first " (code (var "count")) " characters of " (code (var "string")) "  to " (code (var "textual-output-port")) ". If the port is buffered and the buffer is nonempty, the buffer is flushed before the contents of " (code (var "string")) " are written. In any case, the contents of " (code (var "string")) " are written immediately, without passing through the buffer.")) ("io:s89" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(truncate-port " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(truncate-port " (var "output-port") " " (var "pos") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(truncate-file " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(truncate-file " (var "output-port") " " (var "pos") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "truncate-port") " and " (code "truncate-file") " are identical.") ((code (var "pos")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer.  It defaults to 0.") ("These procedures truncate the file or other object associated with " (code (var "output-port")) " to " (code (var "pos")) " and repositions the port to that position, i.e., it combines the functionality of " (code "set-port-length!") " and " (code "set-port-position!") " and can be called on a port only if " (code "port-has-set-port-length!?") " and " (code "port-has-set-port-position!?") " are both true of the port.")) ("io:s90" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fresh-line)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fresh-line " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port.") ("This procedure behaves like " (code "newline") ", i.e., sends a newline character to " (code (var "textual-output-port")) ", unless it can determine that the port is already positioned at the start of a line. It does this by flushing the port and consulting the \"beginning-of-line\" (BOL) flag associated with the port. (See page " (link "./io.html#desc:set-port-bol" "253") ".)")) ("io:s91" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-input-output-file " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-input-output-file " (var "path") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new input-output port" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "open-input-output-file") " opens a textual input-output port for the file named by " (code (var "path")) ".") ("The port may be used to read from or write to the named file. The file is created if it does not already exist.") ((code (var "options")) ", if present, is a symbolic option name or option list. Possible symbolic option names are  " (code "buffered") ", " (code "unbuffered") ", " (code "exclusive") ", and " (code "nonexclusive") ". An option list is a list containing zero or more symbolic option names and possibly the two-element option " (code "mode " (var "mode")) ". See the description of " (code "open-output-file") " for an explanation of these options.") ("Input/output files are usually closed using " (code "close-port") "  but may also be closed with either " (code "close-input-port") " or " (code "close-output-port") ".")) ("io:s92" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input/output-port " (var "fd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input/output-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-fd-input/output-port " (var "fd") " " (var "b-mode") " " (var "?transcoder") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new input/output port for the file descriptor " (code (var "fd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "fd")) " must be a nonnegative exact integer and should be a valid open file descriptor. If " (code (var "?transcoder")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it must be a transcoder, and this procedure returns a textual input/output port whose transcoder is " (code (var "?transcoder")) ". Otherwise, this procedure returns a binary input/output port. See the lead-in to Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./io.html#g123" "7.2") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "  for a description of the constraints on and effects of the other arguments.") ("The file descriptor is closed when the port is closed.")) ("io:s94" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(multibyte->string " (var "code-page") " " (var "bytevector") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a string containing the characters encoded in " (code (var "bytevector")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(string->multibyte " (var "code-page") " " (var "string") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a bytevector containing the encodings of the characters in " (code (var "string")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are available only under Windows. The procedure " (code "multibyte->string") " is a wrapper for the Windows API " (code "MultiByteToWideChar") " function, and " (code "string->multibyte") " is a wrapper for the Windows API " (code "WideCharToMultiByte") " function.") ((code (var "code-page")) " declares the encoding of the byte sequences in the input or output bytevectors. It must be an exact nonnegative integer identifying a code page or one of the symbols " (code "cp-acp") ", " (code "cp-maccp") ", " (code "cp-oemcp") ", " (code "cp-symbol") ", " (code "cp-thread-acp") ", " (code "cp-utf7") ", or " (code "cp-utf8") ", which have the same meanings as the API function meanings for the like-named constants.")) ("io:s95" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pretty-print " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pretty-print " (var "obj") " " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port.") ((code "pretty-print") " is similar to " (code "write") " except that it uses any number of spaces and newlines in order to print " (code (var "obj")) " in a style that is pleasing to look at and which shows the nesting level via indentation. For example,") ((code "(pretty-print '(define factorial (lambda (n) (let fact ((i n) (a 1))" (br) "  (if (= i 0) a (fact (- i 1) (* a i)))))))")) ("might produce") ((code "(define factorial" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (let fact ([i n] [a 1])" (br) "      (if (= i 0) a (fact (- i 1) (* a i))))))"))) ("io:s96" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pretty-file " (var "ifn") " " (var "ofn") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "ifn")) " and " (code (var "ofn")) " must be strings. " (code "pretty-file") " reads each object in turn from the file named by " (code (var "ifn")) " and pretty prints the object to the file named by " (code (var "ofn")) ". Comments present in the input are discarded by the reader and so do not appear in the output file. If the file named by " (code (var "ofn")) " already exists, it is replaced.")) ("io:s97" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pretty-format " (var "sym") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pretty-format " (var "sym") " " (var "fmt") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("By default, the pretty printer uses a generic algorithm for printing each form. This procedure is used to override this default and guide the pretty-printers treatment of specific forms. The symbol " (code (var "sym")) " names a syntactic form or procedure. With just one argument, " (code "pretty-format") " returns the current format associated with " (code (var "sym")) ", or " (code "#f") " if no format is associated with " (code (var "sym")) ".") ("In the two-argument case, the format " (code (var "fmt")) " is associated with " (code (var "sym")) " for future invocations of the pretty printer. " (code (var "fmt")) " must be in the formatting language described below.") ("      " (table (row (cell "<fmt>") (cell (math "-->")) (cell (code "(quote " (var "symbol") ")"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "var"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code (var "symbol")))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(read-macro " (var "string") " " (var "symbol") ")"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(meta)"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(bracket . " (var "fmt-tail") ")"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(alt " (var "fmt") " " (var "fmt") "*)"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code (var "fmt-tail")))) (row (cell (code (var "fmt-tail"))) (cell (math "-->")) (cell (code "()"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(" (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " ...)"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(" (var "fmt") " " (var "tab") " ...)"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(" (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " . " (var "fmt-tail") ")"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(" (var "fmt") " ...)"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(" (var "fmt") " . " (var "fmt-tail") ")"))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "(fill " (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " ...)"))) (row (cell (code (var "tab"))) (cell (math "-->")) (cell (code (var "int")))) (row (cell "  ") (cell "|") (cell (code "#f"))))) ("Some of the format forms are used for matching when there are multiple alternatives, while others are used for matching and control indentation or printing. A description of each " (code (var "fmt")) " is given below.") ((dt (code "(quote " (var "symbol") ")") ":") (dd "This matches only the symbol " (code (var "symbol")) ".")) ((dt (code "var") ":") (dd "This matches any symbol.")) ((dt (code (var "symbol")) ":") (dd "This matches any input.")) ((dt (code "(read-macro " (var "string") " " (var "symbol") ")") ":") (dd "This is used for read macros like " (code "quote") " and " (code (var "syntax")) ". It matches any input of the form (" (code (var "symbol")) " " (code (var "subform")) "). For forms that match, the pretty printer prints " (code (var "string")) " immediately followed by " (code (var "subform")) ".")) ((dt (code "(meta)") ":") (dd "This is a special case used for the " (code "meta") " keyword (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g111" "11.8") ") which is used as a keyword prefix of another form.")) ((dt (code "(alt " (var "fmt") " " (var "fmt") "*)") ":") (dd "This compares the input against the specified formats and uses the one that is the closest match. Most often, one of the formats will match exactly, but in other cases, as when input is malformed or appears in abstract form in the template of a syntactic abstraction, none of the formats will match exactly.")) ((dt (code "(bracket . " (var "fmt-tail") ")") ":") (dd "This matches any list-structured input and prints the input enclosed in square brackets, i.e., " (code "[") " and " (code "]") ", rather than parentheses.")) ((dt (code (var "fmt-tail")) ":") (dd "This matches any list-structured input.")) ("Indentation of list-structured forms is determined via the " (code (var "fmt-tail")) " specifier used to the last two cases above. A description of each " (code (var "fmt-tail")) " is given below.") ((dt (code "()") ":") (dd "This matches an empty list tail.")) ((dt (code "(" (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " ...)") ":") (dd "This matches the tail of any proper list; if the tail is nonempty and the list does not fit entirely on the current line, a line break is inserted before the first subform of the tail and " (code (var "tab")) " (see below) determines the amount by which this and all subsequent subforms are indented.")) ((dt (code "(" (var "fmt") " " (var "tab") " ...)") ":") (dd "This matches the tail of any proper list; if the tail is nonempty and the list does not fit entirely on the current line, a line break is inserted after the first subform of the tail and " (code (var "tab")) " (see below) determines the amount by which all subsequent subforms are indented.")) ((dt (code "(" (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " . " (var "fmt-tail") ")") ":") (dd "This matches a nonempty tail if the tail of the tail matches " (code (var "fmt-tail")) ". If the list does not fit entirely on the current line, a line break is inserted before the first subform of the tail and " (code (var "tab")) " (see below) determines the amount by which the subform is indented.")) ((dt (code "(" (var "fmt") " ...)") ":") (dd "This matches the tail of any proper list and specified that no line breaks are to be inserted before or after the current or subsequent subforms.")) ((dt (code "(" (var "fmt") " . " (var "fmt-tail") ")") ":") (dd "This matches a nonempty tail if the tail of the tail matches " (code (var "fmt-tail")) "  and specifies that no line break is to be inserted before or after the current subform.")) ((dt (code "(fill " (var "tab") " " (var "fmt") " ...)") ":") (dd "This matches the tail of any proper list and invokes a fill mode in which the forms are packed with as many as will fit on each line.")) ("A " (code (var "tab")) " determines the amount by which a list subform is indented. If " (code (var "tab")) " is a nonnegative exact integer " (code (var "int")) ", the subform is indented " (code (var "int")) " spaces in from the character position just after the opening parenthesis or bracket of the parent form. If " (code (var "tab")) " is " (code "#f") ", the standard indentation is used. The standard indentation can be determined or changed via the parameter " (code "pretty-standard-indent") ", which is described later in this section.") ("In cases where a format is given that doesn't quite match, the pretty printer tries to use the given format as far as it can. For example, if a format matches a list-structured form with a specific number of subforms, but more or fewer subform are given, the pretty printer will discard or replicate subform formats as necessary.") ("Here is an example showing the formatting of " (code (var "let")) " might be specified.") ((code "(pretty-format 'let" (br) "  '(alt (let ([bracket var x] 0 ...) #f e #f e ...)" (br) "        (let var ([bracket var x] 0 ...) #f e #f e ...)))")) ("Since " (code "let") " comes in two forms, named and unnamed, two alternatives are specified. In either case, the " (code "bracket") " " (code (var "fmt")) " is used to enclose the bindings in square brackets, with all bindings after the first appearing just below the first (and just after the enclosing opening parenthesis), if they don't all fit on one line. Each body form is indented by the standard indentation.")) ("io:s100" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "pretty-line-length") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "pretty-one-line-limit") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of each of these parameters must be a positive fixnum.") ("The parameters " (code "pretty-line-length") " and " (code "pretty-one-line-limit") " control the output produced by " (code "pretty-print") ". " (code "pretty-line-length") " determines after which character position (starting from the first) on a line the pretty printer attempts to cut off output. This is a soft limit only; if necessary, the pretty-printer will go beyond " (code "pretty-line-length") ".") ((code "pretty-one-line-limit") " is similar to " (code "pretty-line-length") ", except that it is relative to the first nonblank position on each line of output. It is also a soft limit.")) ("io:s102" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "pretty-initial-indent") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a nonnegative fixnum.") ("The parameter " (code "pretty-initial-indent") " is used to tell " (code "pretty-print") " where on an output line it has been called. If " (code "pretty-initial-indent") " is zero (the default), " (code "pretty-print") "  assumes that the first line of output it produces will start at the beginning of the line. If set to a nonzero value " (code (var "n")) ", " (code "pretty-print") " assumes that the first line will appear at character position " (code (var "n")) " and will adjust its printing of subsequent lines.")) ("io:s104" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "pretty-standard-indent") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a nonnegative fixnum.") ("This determines the amount by which " (code "pretty-print") " indents subexpressions of most forms, such as " (code "let") "  expressions, from the form's keyword or first subexpression.")) ("io:s105" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "pretty-maximum-lines") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The parameter " (code "pretty-maximum-lines") " controls how many lines " (code "pretty-print") " prints when it is called. If set to " (code "#f") " (the default), no limit is imposed; if set to a nonnegative fixnum " (code (var "n")) ", at most " (code (var "n")) " lines are printed.")) ("io:s106" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(format " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(format #f " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(format #t " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(format " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When the first argument to format is a string or " (code "#f") " (first and second forms above), " (code "format") " constructs an output string from " (code (var "format-string")) " and the objects " (code (var "obj") " ...") ". Characters are copied from " (code (var "format-string")) " to the output string from left to right, until " (code (var "format-string")) " is exhausted. The format string may contain one or more " (code (var "format directives")) ", which are multi-character sequences prefixed by a tilde ( " (code "~") " ). Each directive is replaced by some other text, often involving one or more of the " (code (var "obj") " ...") " arguments, as determined by the semantics of the directive.") ("When the first argument is " (code "#t") ", output is sent to the current output port instead, as with " (code "printf") ". When the first argument is a port, output is sent to that port, as with " (code "fprintf") ". " (code "printf") " and " (code "fprintf") " are described later in this section.") ((var "Chez Scheme") "'s implementation of " (code "format") " supports all of the Common Lisp [" (link "./bibliography.html#g190" "30") "] format directives except for those specific to the Common Lisp pretty printer. Please consult a Common Lisp reference or the " (link "http://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Front/index.htm" "Common Lisp Hyperspec") ", for complete documentation. A few of the most useful directives are described below.") ("Absent any format directives, " (code "format") " simply displays its string argument.") ((code "(format \"hi there\") " (math "=>") " \"hi there\"")) ("The " (code "~s") " directive is replaced by the printed representation of the next " (code (var "obj")) ", which may be any object, in machine-readable format, as with " (code "write") ".") ((code "(format \"hi ~s\" 'mom) " (math "=>") " \"hi mom\"" (br) "(format \"hi ~s\" \"mom\") " (math "=>") " \"hi \\\"mom\\\"\"" (br) "(format \"hi ~s~s\" 'mom #\\!) " (math "=>") " \"hi mom#\\\\!\"")) ("The general form of a " (code "~s") " directive is actually " (code "~" (var "mincol") "," (var "colinc") "," (var "minpad") "," (var "padchar") "s") ", and the " (code "s") " can be preceded by an at sign ( " (code "@") " ) modifier. These additional parameters are used to control padding in the output, with at least " (code (var "minpad")) " copies of " (code (var "padchar")) "  plus an integer multiple of " (code (var "colinc")) " copies of " (code (var "padchar")) "  to make the total width, including the written object, " (code (var "mincol")) " characters wide. The padding is placed on the left if the " (code (var "@")) " modifier is present, otherwise on the right. " (code (var "mincol")) " and " (code (var "minpad")) " default to 0, " (code (var "colinc")) " defaults to 1, and " (code (var "padchar")) " defaults to space. If specified, " (code (var "padchar")) " is prefixed by a single quote mark.") ((code "(format \"~10s\" 'hello) " (math "=>") " \"hello     \"" (br) "(format \"~10@s\" 'hello) " (math "=>") " \"     hello\"" (br) "(format \"~10,,,'*@s\" 'hello) " (math "=>") " \"*****hello\"")) ("The " (code "~a") " directive is similar, but prints the object as with " (code "display") ".") ((code "(format \"hi ~s~s\" \"mom\" #\\!) " (math "=>") " \"hi \\\"mom\\\"#\\\\!\"" (br) "(format \"hi ~a~a\" \"mom\" #\\!) " (math "=>") " \"hi mom!\"")) ("A tilde may be inserted into the output with " (code "~~") ", and a newline may be inserted with " (code "~%") " (or embedded in the string with " (code "\\n") ").") ((code "(format \"~~line one,~%line two.~~\") " (math "=>") " \"~line one,\\nline two.~\"" (br) "(format \"~~line one,\\nline two.~~\") " (math "=>") " \"~line one,\\nline two.~\"")) ("Real numbers may be printed in floating-point notation with " (code "~f") ".") ((code "(format \"~f\" 3.14159) " (math "=>") " 3.14159")) ("Exact numbers may printed as well as inexact numbers in this manner; they are simply converted to inexact first as if with " (code "exact->inexact") ".") ((code "(format \"~f\" 1/3) " (math "=>") " \"0.3333333333333333\"")) ("The general form is actually " (code "~" (var "w") "," (var "d") "," (var "k") "," (var "overflowchar") "," (var "padchar") "f") ". If specified, " (code (var "w")) " determines the overall width of the output, and " (code (var "d")) " the number of digits to the right of the decimal point. " (code (var "padchar")) ", which defaults to space, is the pad character used if padding is needed. Padding is always inserted on the left. The number is scaled by 10" (sup "k") " when printed; " (code (var "k")) " defaults to zero. The entire " (code (var "w")) "-character field is filled with copies of " (code (var "overflowchar")) " if " (code (var "overflowchar")) " is specified and the number cannot be printed in " (code (var "w")) " characters. " (code (var "k")) " defaults to 1 If an " (code "@") " modifier is present, a plus sign is printed before the number for nonnegative inputs; otherwise, a sign is printed only if the number is negative.") ((code "(format \"~,3f\" 3.14159) " (math "=>") " \"3.142\"" (br) "(format \"~10f\" 3.14159) " (math "=>") " \"   3.14159\"" (br) "(format \"~10,,,'#f\" 1e20) " (math "=>") " \"##########\"")) ("Real numbers may also be printed with " (code "~e") " for scientific notation or with " (code "~g") ", which uses either floating-point or scientific notation based on the size of the input.") ((code "(format \"~e\" 1e23) " (math "=>") " \"1.0e+23\"" (br) "(format \"~g\" 1e23) " (math "=>") " \"1.0e+23\"")) ("A real number may also be printed with " (code "~$") ", which uses monetary notation defaulting to two digits to the right of the decimal point.") ((code "(format \"$~$\" (* 39.95 1.06)) " (math "=>") " \"$42.35\"" (br) "(format \"~$USD\" 1/3) " (math "=>") " \"0.33USD\"")) ("Words can be pluralized automatically using " (code "p") ".") ((code "(format \"~s bear~:p in ~s den~:p\" 10 1) " (math "=>") " \"10 bears in 1 den\"")) ("Numbers may be printed out in words or roman numerals using variations on " (code "~r") ".") ((code "(format \"~r\" 2599) " (math "=>") "  \"two thousand five hundred ninety-nine\"" (br) "(format \"~:r\" 99) " (math "=>") "  \"ninety-ninth\"" (br) "(format \"~@r\" 2599) " (math "=>") " \"MMDXCIX\"")) ("Case conversions can be performed by bracketing a portion of the format string with the " (code "~@(") " and " (code "~)") " directives.") ((code "(format \"~@(~r~)\" 2599) " (math "=>") "  \"Two thousand five hundred ninety-nine\"" (br) "(format \"~@:(~a~)\" \"Ouch!\") " (math "=>") "  \"OUCH!\"")) ("Some of the directives shown above have more options and parameters, and  there are other directives as well, including directives for conditionals, iteration, indirection, and justification. Again, please consult a Common Lisp reference for complete documentation.") ("An implementation of a greatly simplified version of " (code "format") "  appears in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./examples.html#g180" "12.6") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ".")) ("io:s108" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(printf " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fprintf " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "format-string") " " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are simple wrappers for " (code "format") ". " (code "printf") " prints the formatted output to the current output, as with a first-argument of " (code "#t") " to " (code "format") ", and " (code "fprintf") " prints the formatted output to the " (code (var "textual-output-port")) ", as when the first argument to " (code "format") " is a port.")) ("io:s109" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(char-name " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(char-name " (var "name") " " (var "char") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "char-name") " is used to associate names (symbols) with characters or to retrieve the most recently associated name or character for a  given character or name. A name can map to only one character, but more than one name can map to the same character. The name most recently associated with a character determines how that character prints, and each name associated with a character may be used after the " (code "#\\") " character prefix to name that character on input.") ("Character associations created by " (code "char-name") " are ignored by the printer unless the parameter " (code "print-char-name") " is set to a true value. The reader recognizes character names established by " (code "char-name") "  except after " (code "#!r6rs") ", which is implied within a library or R6RS top-level program.") ("In the one-argument form, " (code (var "obj")) " must be a symbol or character. If it is a symbol and a character is associated with the symbol, " (code "char-name") " returns that character. If it is a symbol and no character is associated with the symbol, " (code "char-name") " returns " (code "#f") ". Similarly, if " (code (var "obj")) " is a character, " (code "char-name") " returns the most recently associated symbol for the character or " (code "#f") " if no name is associated with the character. For example, with the default set of character names:") ((code "(char-name #\\space) " (math "=>") " space" (br) "(char-name 'space) " (math "=>") " #\\space" (br) "(char-name 'nochar) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(char-name #\\a) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("When passed two arguments, " (code (var "name")) " is added to the set of names associated with " (code (var "char")) ", and any other association for " (code (var "name")) "  is dropped. " (code (var "char")) " may be " (code "#f") ", in which case any other association for " (code (var "name")) " is dropped and no new association is formed. In either case, any other names associated with " (code (var "char")) " remain associated with " (code (var "char")) ".") ("The following interactive session demonstrates the use of " (code "char-name") " to establish and remove associations between characters and names, including the association of more than one name with a character.") ((code "(print-char-name #t)" (br) "(char-name 'etx) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(char-name 'etx #\\x3)" (br) "(char-name 'etx) " (math "=>") " #\\etx" (br) "(char-name #\\x3) " (math "=>") " etx" (br) "#\\etx " (math "=>") " #\\etx" (br) "(eq? #\\etx #\\x3) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "#!r6rs #\\etx " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid character name etx") (br) "#!chezscheme #\\etx " (math "=>") " #\\etx" (br) "(char-name 'etx #\\space)" (br) "(char-name #\\x3) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(char-name 'etx) " (math "=>") " #\\etx" (br) "#\\space " (math "=>") " #\\etx" (br) "(char-name 'etx #f)" (br) "#\\etx " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid character name etx") (br) "#\\space " (math "=>") " #\\space")) ("(When using the expression editor, it is necessary to type Control-J to force the editor to read the erroneous " (code "#\\etx") " input on the two inputs above that result in read errors, since typing Enter causes the expression editor to read the input only if the input is well-formed.)") ("The reader does not recognize hex scalar value escapes in character names, as it does in symbols, so " (code "#\\new\\x6c;ine") " is not equivalent to " (code "#\\newline") ". In general, programmers should avoid the use of character name symbols that cannot be entered without the use of hex scalar value escapes or other symbol-name escape mechanisms, since such character names will not be readable.")) ("io:s110" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-char-name") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-char-name") " is set to " (code "#f") " (the default), associations created by " (code "char-name") " are ignored by " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", " (code "pretty-print") ", and the " (code "format") "  \"" (code "~s") "\" directive. Otherwise, these procedures use the names established by " (code "char-name") " when printing character objects.") ((code "(char-name 'etx #\\x3)" (br) "(format \"~s\" #\\x3) " (math "=>") " \"#\\\\x3\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-char-name #t])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" #\\x3)) " (math "=>") " \"#\\\\etx\""))) ("io:s111" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "case-sensitive") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "case-sensitive") " parameter determines whether the reader is case-sensitive with respect to symbol and character names. When set to true (the default, as required by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report) the case of alphabetic characters within symbol names is significant. When set to " (code "#f") ", case is insignificant. More precisely, when set to " (code "#f") ", symbol and character names are folded (as if by " (code "string-foldcase") "); otherwise, they are left as they appear in the input.") ("The value of the " (code "case-sensitive") " matters only if neither " (code "#!fold-case") " nor " (code "#!no-fold-case") " has appeared previously in the same input stream. That is, symbol and character name are folded if " (code "#!fold-case") " has been seen. They are not folded if " (code "#!no-fold-case") " has been seen. If neither has been seen, they are folded if and only if " (code "(case-sensitive)") " is " (code "#f") ".") ((code "(case-sensitive) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(eq? 'abc 'ABC) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "'ABC " (math "=>") " ABC" (br) "(case-sensitive #f)" (br) "'ABC " (math "=>") " abc" (br) "(eq? 'abc 'ABC) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("io:s112" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-graph") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-graph") " is set to a true value, " (code "write") " and " (code "pretty-print") "  locate and print objects with shared structure, including cycles, in a notation that may be read subsequently with " (code "read") ". This notation employs the syntax \"" (code "#" (var "n") "=" (var "obj")) ",\" where " (code (var "n")) "  is a nonnegative integer and " (code (var "obj")) " is the printed representation of an object, to label the first occurrence of " (code (var "obj")) " in the output. The syntax \"" (code "#" (var "n") "#") "\" is used to refer to the object labeled by " (code (var "n")) " thereafter in the output. " (code "print-graph") " is set to " (code "#f") " by default.") ("If graph printing is not enabled, the settings of " (code "print-length") " and " (code "print-level") "  are insufficient to force finite output, and " (code "write") " or " (code "pretty-print") " detects a cycle in an object it is given to print, a warning is issued (an exception with condition type " (code "&warning") " is raised) and the object is printed as if " (code "print-graph") " were enabled.") ("Since objects printed through the " (code "~s") " option in the format control strings of " (code "format") ", " (code "printf") ", and " (code "fprintf") " are printed as with " (code "write") ", the printing of such objects is also affected by " (code "print-graph") ".") ((code "(parameterize ([print-graph #t])" (br) "  (let ([x (list 'a 'b)])" (br) "    (format \"~s\" (list x x)))) " (math "=>") " \"(#0=(a b) #0#)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-graph #t])" (br) "  (let ([x (list 'a 'b)])" (br) "    (set-car! x x)" (br) "    (set-cdr! x x)" (br) "    (format \"~s\" x))) " (math "=>") " \"#0=(#0# . #0#)\"")) ("The graph syntax is understood by the procedure " (code "read") ", allowing graph structures to be printed and read consistently.")) ("io:s116" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-level") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-length") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These parameters can be used to limit the extent to which nested or multiple-element structures are printed. When called without arguments, " (code "print-level") " returns the current print level and " (code "print-length") " returns the current print length. When called with one argument, which must be a nonnegative fixnum or " (code "#f") ", " (code "print-level") " sets the current print level and " (code "print-length") " sets the current print length to the argument.") ("When " (code "print-level") " is set to a nonnegative integer " (code (var "n")) ", " (code "write") " and " (code "pretty-print") "  traverse only " (code (var "n")) " levels deep into nested structures. If a structure being printed exceeds " (code (var "n")) " levels of nesting, the substructure beyond that point is replaced in the output by an ellipsis ( " (code "...") " ). " (code "print-level") " is set to " (code "#f") " by default, which places no limit on the number of levels printed.") ("When " (code "print-length") " is set to a nonnegative integer " (code (var "n")) ", the procedures " (code "write") " and " (code "pretty-print") "  print only " (code (var "n")) " elements of a list or vector, replacing the remainder of the list or vector with an ellipsis ( " (code "...") " ). " (code "print-length") " is set to " (code "#f") " by default, which places no limit on the number of elements printed.") ("Since objects printed through the " (code "~s") " option in the format control strings of " (code "format") ", " (code "printf") ", and " (code "fprintf") " are printed as with " (code "write") ", the printing of such objects is also affected by " (code "print-level") "  and " (code "print-length") ".") ("The parameters " (code "print-level") " and " (code "print-length") " are useful for controlling the volume of output in contexts where only a small portion of the output is needed to identify the object being printed. They are also useful in situations where circular structures may be printed (see also " (code "print-graph") ").") ((code "(format \"~s\" '((((a) b) c) d e f g)) " (math "=>") " \"((((a) b) c) d e f g)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-level 2])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" '((((a) b) c) d e f g))) " (math "=>") " \"(((...) c) d e f g)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-length 3])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" '((((a) b) c) d e f g))) " (math "=>") " \"((((a) b) c) d e ...)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-level 2]" (br) "               [print-length 3])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" '((((a) b) c) d e f g))) " (math "=>") " \"(((...) c) d e ...)\""))) ("io:s121" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-radix") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "print-radix") " parameter determines the radix in which numbers are printed by " (code "write") ", " (code "pretty-print") ", and " (code "display") ". Its value should be an integer between 2 and 36, inclusive. Its default value is 10.") ("When the value of " (code "print-radix") " is not 10, " (code "write") " and " (code "pretty-print") " print a radix prefix before the number (" (code "#b") " for radix 2, " (code "#o") " for radix 8, " (code "#x") " for radix 16, and " (code "#" (var "n") "r") " for any other radix " (code (var "n")) ").") ("Since objects printed through the " (code "~s") " and " (code "~a") " options in the format control strings of " (code "format") ", " (code "printf") ", and " (code "fprintf") " are printed as with " (code "write") " and " (code "display") ", the printing of such objects is also affected by " (code "print-radix") ".") ((code "(format \"~s\" 11242957) " (math "=>") " \"11242957\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-radix 16])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" 11242957)) " (math "=>") " \"#xAB8DCD\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-radix 16])" (br) "  (format \"~a\" 11242957)) " (math "=>") " \"AB8DCD\""))) ("io:s122" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-gensym") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-gensym") " is set to " (code "#t") " (the default) gensyms are printed with an extended symbol syntax that includes both the pretty name and the unique name of the gensym:  " (code "#{" (var "pretty-name") " " (var "unique-name") "}") ". When set to " (code "pretty") ", the pretty name only is shown, with the prefix " (code "#:") ". When set to " (code "pretty/suffix") ", the printer prints the gensym's \"pretty\" name along with a suffix based on the gensym's \"unique\" name, separated by a dot ( \".\" ). If the gensym's unique name is generated automatically during the current session, the suffix is that portion of the unique name that is not common to all gensyms created during the current session. Otherwise, the suffix is the entire unique name. When set to " (code "#f") ", the pretty name only is shown, with no prefix.") ("Since objects printed through the " (code "~s") " option in the format control strings of " (code "format") ", " (code "printf") ", " (code "errorf") ", etc., are printed as with " (code "write") ", the printing of such objects is also affected by " (code "print-gensym") ".") ("When printing an object that may contain more than one occurrence of a gensym and " (code "print-graph") " is set to " (code "pretty") " or " (code "#f") ", it is useful to set " (code "print-graph") " to " (code "#t") " so that multiple occurrences of the same gensym are marked as identical in the output.") ((code "(let ([g (gensym)])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" g)) " (math "=>") " \"#{g0 bdids2xl6v49vgwe-a}\"" (br) (br) "(let ([g (gensym)])" (br) "  (parameterize ([print-gensym 'pretty])" (br) "    (format \"~s\" g))) " (math "=>") " \"#:g1" (br) (br) "(let ([g (gensym)])" (br) "  (parameterize ([print-gensym #f])" (br) "    (format \"~s\" g))) " (math "=>") " \"g2\"" (br) (br) "(let ([g (gensym)])" (br) "  (parameterize ([print-graph #t] [print-gensym 'pretty])" (br) "    (format \"~s\" (list g g)))) " (math "=>") " \"(#0=#:g3 #0#)\"" (br) (br) "(let ([g1 (gensym \"x\")]" (br) "      [g2 (gensym \"x\")]" (br) "      [g3 (gensym \"y\")])" (br) "  (parameterize ([print-gensym 'pretty/suffix])" (br) "    (format \"~s ~s ~s\" g1 g2 g3))) " (math "=>") " \"x.1 x.2 y.3\""))) ("io:s127" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-brackets") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-brackets") " is set to a true value, the pretty printer (see " (code "pretty-print") ") uses square brackets rather than parentheses around certain subexpressions of common control structures, e.g., around " (code "let") " bindings and " (code "cond") " clauses. " (code "print-brackets") " is set to " (code "#t") " by default.") ((code "(let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "  (pretty-print '(let ([x 3]) x) p) " (math "=>") " \"(let ([x 3]) x)" (br) "  (get-output-string p))            " (math "  ") " \"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-brackets #f])" (br) "  (let ([p (open-output-string)])" (br) "    (pretty-print '(let ([x 3]) x) p) " (math "=>") " \"(let ((x 3)) x)" (br) "    (get-output-string p)))           " (math "  ") " \""))) ("io:s129" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-extended-identifiers") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Chez Scheme extends the syntax of identifiers as described in Section " (link "./intro.html#g1" "1.1") ", except within a set of forms prefixed by " (code "#!r6rs") " (which is implied in a library or top-level program).") ("When this parameter is set to false (the default), identifiers in the extended set are printed with hex scalar value escapes as necessary to conform to the R6RS syntax for identifiers. When this parameter is set to a true value, identifiers in the extended set are printed without the escapes. Identifiers whose names fall outside of both syntaxes are printed with the escapes regardless of the setting of this parameter.") ("For example:") ((code "(parameterize ([print-extended-identifiers #f])" (br) "  (printf \"~s\\n~s\\n\"" (br) "    '(1+ --- { } .xyz)" (br) "    (string->symbol \"123\")))")) ("prints") ((code "(\\x31;+ \\x2D;-- \\x7B; \\x7D; \\x2E;xyz)" (br) "\\x31;23")) ("while") ((code "(parameterize ([print-extended-identifiers #t])" (br) "  (printf \"~s\\n~s\\n\"" (br) "    '(1+ --- { } .xyz)" (br) "    (string->symbol \"123\")))")) ("prints") ((code "(1+ --- { } .xyz)" (br) "\\x31;23"))) ("io:s130" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-vector-length") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-vector-length") " is set to a true value, " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", and " (code "pretty-print") " include the length for all vectors, bytevectors, and fxvectors immediately after the \"" (code "#") "\". This parameter is set to " (code "#f") " by default.") ("When " (code "print-vector-length") " is set to a true value, " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", and " (code "pretty-print") "  also suppress duplicated trailing elements in the vector to reduce the amount of output. This form is also recognized by the reader.") ("Since objects printed through the " (code "~s") " option in the format control strings of " (code "format") ", " (code "printf") ", and " (code "fprintf") " are printed as with " (code "write") ", the printing of such objects is also affected by the setting of " (code "print-vector-length") ".") ((code "(format \"~s\" (vector 'a 'b 'c 'c 'c)) " (math "=>") " \"#(a b c c c)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-vector-length #t])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" (vector 'a 'b 'c 'c 'c))) " (math "=>") " \"#5(a b c)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-vector-length #t])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" (bytevector 1 2 3 4 4 4))) " (math "=>") " \"#6vu8(1 2 3 4)\"" (br) (br) "(parameterize ([print-vector-length #t])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" (fxvector 1 2 3 4 4 4))) " (math "=>") " \"#6vfx(1 2 3 4)\""))) ("io:s132" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-precision") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-precision") " is set to " (code "#f") " (the default), " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", " (code "pretty-print") ", and the " (code "format") " \"" (code "~s") "\" directive do not include the vertical-bar \"mantissa-width\" syntax after each floating-point number---except for subnormal floating-point numbers, in which case precision printing is controlled by " (code "print-subnormal-precision") ". When set to a nonnegative exact integer, the mantissa width is included, as per the precision argument to " (code "number->string") ".") ((code "(format \"~s\" 1e-100) " (math "=>") " \"1e-100\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-precision 32])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" 1e-100)) ; => \"1e-100|53\"" (br) "(format \"~s\" 1e-310) " (math "=>") " \"1e-310|45\""))) ("io:s133" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-subnormal-precision") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-subnormal-precision") " is set to a true value (the default), " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", " (code "pretty-print") ", and the " (code "format") " \"" (code "~s") "\" directive include the vertical-bar \"mantissa-width\" syntax after each floating-point number whose value is subnormal (roughly 4.94e-324 to 2.225e-308), regardless of the value of " (code "print-precision") ". When " (code "print-subnormal-precision") " and " (code "print-precision") " are both set to " (code "#f") ", precision is not printed for a subnormal value.") ((code "(format \"~s\" 1e-310) " (math "=>") " \"1e-310|45\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-subnormal-precision #f])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" 1e-310)) ; => \"1e-310\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-precision 32]" (br) "               [print-subnormal-precision #f])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" 1e-310)) ; => \"1e-310|45\""))) ("io:s134" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-positive-exponent-sign") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-positive-exponent-sign") " is set to a true value, then when a flonum is printed in exponential form and the exponent is positive, a plus sign is written before the exponent. When " (code "print-positive-exponent-sign") " is set " (code "#f") " (the default), no plus sign is written before a positive exponent.")) ("io:s135" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-select-flonum-exponential-format") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "print-select-flonum-exponential-format") " must be a procedure that accepts three arguments: a radix as an integer between 2 and 36 (inclusive), an exponent as an integer, and the number of non-zero significant digits in the flonum's printed form using the matissa (where the first digit is before the \"decimal\" point). If the procedure returns a true value, a number is printed in expotential form, otherwise it is printed in nonexponential form.") ((code "(format \"~s ~s\" 1e2 1e8) " (math "=>") " \"100.0 100000000.0\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-select-flonum-exponential-format" (br) "                (lambda (radix exponent digits)" (br) "                  (> exponent 3))])" (br) "  (format \"~s ~s\" 1e2 1e8)) " (math "=>") " \"100.0 1e8\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-select-flonum-exponential-format" (br) "                (lambda (radix exponent digits)" (br) "                  (< digits 3))])" (br) "  (format \"~s ~s ~s\" 1e2 1.5e8 3.14159e-8)) " (math "=>") " \"1e2 1.5e8 0.0000000314159\""))) ("io:s136" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-unicode") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "print-unicode") " is set to " (code "#f") ", " (code "write") ", " (code "put-datum") ", " (code "pretty-print") ", and the " (code "format") " \"" (code "~s") "\" directive display Unicode characters with encodings 80" (sub "16") " (128) and above that appear  within character objects, symbols, and strings using hexadecimal character escapes. When set to a true value (the default), they are displayed like other printing characters, as if by " (code "put-char") ".") ((code "(format \"~s\" #\\x3bb) " (math "=>") " \"#\\\\" (math "λ") "\"" (br) "(parameterize ([print-unicode #f])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" #\\x3bb)) " (math "=>") " \"#\\\\x3BB\""))) ("io:s138" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-write " (var "obj") " " (var "binary-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-write " (var "obj") " " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "external-pred") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-write " (var "obj") " " (var "binary-output-port") " " (var "external-pred") " " (var "omit-rtds?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "fasl-write") " writes the fasl representation of " (code (var "obj")) " to " (code (var "binary-output-port")) ". An exception is raised with condition-type " (code "&assertion") " if " (code (var "obj")) " or any portion of " (code (var "obj")) " has no external fasl representation, e.g., if " (code (var "obj")) " is or contains a procedure.") ("If " (code (var "externals-pred")) " is provided and not " (code "#f") ", then it must be a procedure that accepts one argument.  It is applied to each distinct object  encountered in " (code (var "obj")) ", including symbols, but not necessarily values of other primitive datatypes for which " (code "equal?") " implies " (code "eq?") ". If " (code (var "externals-pred")) " returns true for an object, that object is not written to the fasl representation. Instead, a placeholder is written containing a position as the number of preceding calls to " (code (var "externals-pred")) " that had returned true. Typically, for each object where it returns true, " (code (var "externals-pred")) " saves the object through its closure. When the fasl representation is read with " (code "fasl-read") ", a vector with as many items as generated positions must be provided, and each placeholder is replaced with the corresponding vector element. Beware that replacing values used for system-internal data structures (which are always instances of primitive datatypes) with non-equal values at load time results in undefined behavior.") ("If " (code (var "omit-rtds?")) " is present and true, then any record types relevant to " (code (var "obj")) " are not written to the fasl representation. When the fasl representation is read, these record types must already be declared in the loading context, and the loading context is assumed to have compatible record-type registrations using the same unique ID.  Behavior if the loading context has an " (var "incompatible") " record-type registration using the same (ostensibly unique) ID is undefined.") ("The fasl representation of " (code (var "obj")) " is compressed if the parameter " (code "fasl-compressed") ", described below, is set to " (code "#t") ", its default value. For this reason, " (code (var "binary-output-port")) " generally should not be opened with the compressed option. A warning is issued (an exception with condition type " (code "&warning") "  is raised) on the first attempt to write fasl objects to or read fasl objects from a compressed file.") ((code "(define bop (open-file-output-port \"tmp.fsl\"))" (br) "(fasl-write '(a b c) bop)" (br) "(close-port bop)" (br) (br) "(define bip (open-file-input-port \"tmp.fsl\"))" (br) "(fasl-read bip) " (math "=>") " (a b c)" (br) "(fasl-read bip) " (math "=>") " #!eof" (br) "(close-port bip)"))) ("io:s139" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-read " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-read " (var "binary-input-port " (var "situation")) ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-read " (var "binary-input-port " (var "situation") " " (var "externals")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If present, " (code (var "situation")) " must be one of the symbols " (code "load") ", " (code "visit") ", or " (code "revisit") "; it defaults to " (code "load") ". If present, " (code (var "exterrnals")) " must be a vector; it defaults to " (code "'#()") ".") ((code "fasl-read") " reads one object from " (code (var "binary-input-port")) ", which must be positioned at the front of an object written in fasl format. " (code "fasl-read") " returns the eof object if the file is positioned at the end of file. If the situation is " (code "visit") ", " (code "fasl-read") " skips over  any revisit (run-time-only) objects, and if the situation is " (code "revisit") ", " (code "fasl-read") " skips over  any visit (compile-time-only) objects. It doesn't skip any if the situation is " (code "load") ". Similarly, objects marked as both visit and revisit (e.g., object code corresponding to source code within an " (code "eval-when") " form with situation " (code "load") " or situations " (code "visit") " and " (code "revisit") ") are never skipped.") ("The " (code (var "externals")) " vector must have the same length as the number of true-returning calls to " (code (var "external-pred")) " during the " (code "fasl-write") " call that produced the fasl representation.") ((code "fasl-read") " automatically decompresses the representation of each fasl object written in compressed format by " (code "fasl-write") ". Thus, " (code (var "binary-input-port")) " generally should not be opened with the compressed option. A warning is issued (an exception with condition type " (code "&warning") "  is raised) on the first attempt to write fasl objects to or read fasl objects from a compressed file.") ((code "(define bop (open-file-output-port \"tmp.fsl\"))" (br) "(fasl-write '(a b c) bop)" (br) "(close-port bop)" (br) (br) "(define bip (open-file-input-port \"tmp.fsl\"))" (br) "(fasl-read bip) " (math "=>") " (a b c)" (br) "(fasl-read bip) " (math "=>") " #!eof" (br) "(close-port bip)"))) ("io:s140" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "fasl-compressed") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to its default value, " (code "#t") ", " (code "fasl-write") " compresses the representation of each object as it writes it, often resulting in substantially smaller output but possibly taking more time to write and read. The compression format and level are determined by the " (code "compress-format") "  and " (code "compress-level") "  parameters.")) ("io:s143" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fasl-file " (var "ifn") " " (var "ofn") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "ifn")) " and " (code (var "ofn")) " must be strings. " (code "fasl-file") " may be used to convert a file in human-readable format into an equivalent file written in fasl format. " (code "fasl-file") " reads each object in turn from the file named by " (code (var "ifn")) " and writes the fasl format for the object onto the file named by " (code (var "ofn")) ". If the file named by " (code (var "ofn")) " already exists, it is replaced.")) ("io:s144" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "current-directory") (br) (bold "global parameter") ": " (code "cd") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When invoked without arguments, " (code "current-directory") " returns a string representing the current working directory. Otherwise, the current working directory is changed to the directory specified by the argument, which must be a string representing a valid directory pathname.") ((code "cd") " is bound to the same parameter.")) ("io:s145" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(directory-list " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of file names" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. The return value is a list of strings representing the names of files found in the directory named by " (code (var "path")) ". " (code "directory-list") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") " if " (code (var "path")) " does not name a directory or if the process cannot list the directory.")) ("io:s146" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-exists? " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-exists? " (var "path") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the file named by " (code (var "path")) " exists, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. If the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument is true (the default), " (code "file-exists?") " follows symbolic links; otherwise it does not. Thus, " (code "file-exists?") " will return " (code "#f") " when handed the pathname of a broken symbolic link unless " (code (var "follow?")) " is provided and is " (code "#f") ".") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "file-exists?") " does not accept the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument. Whether it follows symbolic links is unspecified.")) ("io:s147" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-regular? " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-regular? " (var "path") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the file named by " (code (var "path")) " is a regular file, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. If the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument is true (the default), " (code "file-regular?") " follows symbolic links; otherwise it does not.")) ("io:s148" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-directory? " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-directory? " (var "path") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the file named by " (code (var "path")) " is a directory, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. If the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument is true (the default), this procedure follows symbolic links; otherwise it does not.")) ("io:s149" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-symbolic-link? " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the file named by " (code (var "path")) " is a symbolic link, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "file-symbolic-link?") " never follows symbolic links in making its determination.")) ("io:s150" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-access-time " (var "path/port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-access-time " (var "path/port") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the access time of the specified file" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-change-time " (var "path/port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-change-time " (var "path/port") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the change time of the specified file" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-modification-time " (var "path/port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(file-modification-time " (var "path/port") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the modification time of the specified file" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path/port")) " must be a string or port. If " (code (var "path/port")) " is a string, the time returned is for the file named by the string, and the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument determines whether symbolic links are followed. If " (code (var "follow?")) " is true (the default), this procedure follows symbolic links; otherwise it does not. If " (code (var "path/port")) " is a port, it must be a file port, and the time returned is for the associated file. In this case, " (code (var "follow?")) " is ignored.") ("The returned times are represented as time objects (Section " (link "./system.html#g126" "12.10") ").")) ("io:s151" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mkdir " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mkdir " (var "path") " " (var "mode") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "mode")) " must be a fixnum.") ((code "mkdir") " creates a directory with the name given by " (code (var "path")) ". All " (code (var "path")) " path components leading up to the last must already exist. If the optional " (code (var "mode")) " argument is present, it overrides the default permissions for the new directory. Under Windows, the " (code (var "mode")) " argument is ignored.") ((code "mkdir") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") " if the directory cannot be created.")) ("io:s152" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(delete-file " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(delete-file " (var "path") " " (var "error?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "delete-file") " removes the file named by " (code (var "path")) ". If the optional " (code (var "error?")) " argument is " (code "#f") " (the default), " (code "delete-file") " returns a boolean value: " (code "#t") " if the operation is successful and " (code "#f") " if it is not. Otherwise, " (code "delete-file") " returns an unspecified value if the operation is successful and raises an exception with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") " if it is not.") ("The Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "delete-file") " does not accept the optional " (code (var "error?")) " argument but behaves as if " (code (var "error?")) "  is true.")) ("io:s153" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(delete-directory " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(delete-directory " (var "path") " " (var "error?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "delete-directory") " removes the directory named by " (code (var "path")) ". If the optional " (code (var "error?")) " argument is " (code "#f") " (the default), " (code "delete-directory") " returns a boolean value: " (code "#t") " if the operation is successful and " (code "#f") " if it is not. Otherwise, " (code "delete-directory") " returns an unspecified value if the operation is successful and raises an exception with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") " if it is not. The behavior is unspecified if the directory is not empty, but on most systems the operations will not succeed.")) ("io:s154" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(rename-file " (var "old-pathname") " " (var "new-pathname") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "old-pathname")) " and " (code (var "new-pathname")) " must be strings. " (code "rename-file") " changes the name of the file named by " (code (var "old-pathname")) "  to " (code (var "new-pathname")) ". If the file does not exist or cannot be renamed, an exception is raised with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") ".")) ("io:s155" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(chmod " (var "path") " " (var "mode") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code (var "mode")) " must be a fixnum.") ((code "chmod") " sets the permissions on the file named by " (code (var "path")) " to " (code (var "mode")) ". Bits 0, 1, and 2 of " (code (var "mode")) " are the execute, write, and read permission bits for users other than the file's owner who are not in the file's group. Bits 3-5 are the execute, write, and read permission bits for users other than the file's owner but in the file's group. Bits 6-8 are the execute, write, and read permission bits for the file's owner. Bits 7-9 are the Unix sticky, set-group-id, and set-user-id bits. Under Windows, all but the user \"write\" bit are ignored. If the file does not exist or the permissions cannot be changed, an exception is raised with condition type " (code "&i/o-filename") ".")) ("io:s156" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-mode " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-mode " (var "path") " " (var "follow?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the current permissions mode for " (code (var "path")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "get-mode") " retrieves the permissions on the file named by " (code (var "path")) " and returns them as a fixnum in the same form as the " (code (var "mode")) "  argument to " (code "chmod") ". If the optional " (code (var "follow?")) " argument is true (the default), this procedure follows symbolic links; otherwise it does not.")) ("io:s157" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(directory-separator? " (var "char") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "char")) " is a directory separator, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The character " (code "#\\/") " is a directory separator on all current machine types, and " (code "#\\\\") " is a directory separator under Windows.")) ("io:s158" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(directory-separator)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the preferred directory separator" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The preferred directory separator is " (code "#\\\\") " for Windows and " (code "#\\/") " for other systems.")) ("io:s159" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-build " (var "dir-path") " " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a combined path" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Combines " (code (var "dir-path")) " and " (code (var "path")) ", adding a directory separator between them if needed. If " (code (var "dir-path")) " is the empty string, " (code (var "path")) " is returned.")) ("io:s160" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-first " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-rest " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-last " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-parent " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-extension " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-root " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the specified component of " (code (var "path")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(path-absolute? " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "path")) " is absolute, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. The return value is also a (possibly empty) string.") ("The path first component is the first directory in the path, or the empty string if the path consists only of a single filename. The path rest component is the portion of the path that does not include the path first component or the directory separator (if any) that separates it from the rest of the path. The path last component is the last (filename) portion of " (code (var "path")) ". The path parent component is the portion of " (code (var "path")) " that does not include the path last component, if any, or the directory separator that separates it from the rest of the path.") ("If the first component of the path names a root directory (including drives and shares under Windows), home directory (e.g., " (code "~/abc") " or " (code "~user/abc") "),  the current directory (" (code ".") "), or the parent directory (" (code "..") "), " (code "path-first") " returns that component. For paths that consist only of such a directory, both " (code "path-first") " and " (code "path-parent") " act as identity procedures, while " (code "path-rest") " and " (code "path-last") "  return the empty string.") ("The path extension component is the portion of " (code (var "path")) " that follows the last dot (period) in the last component of a path name. The path root component is the portion of " (code (var "path")) " that does not include the extension, if any, or the dot that precedes it.") ("If the first component names a root directory (including drives and shares under Windows) or home directory, " (code "path-absolute?") " returns " (code "#t") ". Otherwise, " (code "path-absolute?") " returns " (code "#f") ". ") ("The tables below identify the components for several example paths, with underscores representing empty strings.") ((table (row (cell "path               ") (cell " abs         ") (cell " first              ") (cell " rest               ") (cell " parent             ") (cell " last               ") (cell " root               ") (cell " ext        ")) (row (cell (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "a/") "        ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "a/") "        ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "a/b") "       ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "b") "         ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "b") "         ") (cell " " (code "a/b") "       ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "a/b.c") "     ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "a") "         ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "a/b") "       ") (cell " " (code "c") " ")) (row (cell (code "/") "         ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "/") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "/") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "/") "         ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "/a/b.c") "    ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "/") "         ") (cell " " (code "a/b.c") "     ") (cell " " (code "/a") "        ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "/a/b") "      ") (cell " " (code "c") " ")) (row (cell (code "~/a/b.c") "   ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "~") "         ") (cell " " (code "a/b.c") "     ") (cell " " (code "~/a") "       ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "~/a/b") "     ") (cell " " (code "c") " ")) (row (cell (code "~u/a/b.c") "  ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "~u") "        ") (cell " " (code "a/b.c") "     ") (cell " " (code "~u/a") "      ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "~u/a/b") "    ") (cell " " (code "c") " ")) (row (cell (code "../..") "     ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "..") "        ") (cell " " (code "..") "        ") (cell " " (code "..") "        ") (cell " " (code "..") "        ") (cell " " (code "../..") "     ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell)))) ("The second table shows the components when Windows drives and shares are involved.") ((table (row (cell "path               ") (cell " abs         ") (cell " first              ") (cell " rest               ") (cell " parent             ") (cell " last               ") (cell " root               ") (cell " ext       ")) (row (cell (code "c:") "        ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "c:") "        ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "c:") "        ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "c:") "        ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "c:/") "       ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "c:/") "       ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "c:/") "       ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "c:/") "       ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "c:a/b") "     ") (cell " " (code "#f") " ") (cell " " (code "c:") "        ") (cell " " (code "a/b") "       ") (cell " " (code "c:a") "       ") (cell " " (code "b") "         ") (cell " " (code "c:a/b") "     ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell (code "//s/a/b.c") " ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "//s") "       ") (cell " " (code "a/b.c") "     ") (cell " " (code "//s/a") "     ") (cell " " (code "b.c") "       ") (cell " " (code "//s/a/b") "   ") (cell " " (code "c") " ")) (row (cell (code "//s.com") "   ") (cell " " (code "#t") " ") (cell " " (code "//s.com") "   ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "//s.com") "   ") (cell " " (code "_") "         ") (cell " " (code "//s.com") "   ") (cell " " (code "_") " ")) (row (cell)))) ("The following procedure can be used to reproduce the tables above.") ((code "(define print-table" (br) "  (lambda path*" (br) "    (define print-row" (br) "      (lambda (abs? path first rest parent last root extension)" (br) "        (printf \"~a~11t~a~17t~a~28t~a~39t~a~50t~a~61t~a~73t~a\\n\"" (br) "          abs? path first rest parent last root extension)))" (br) "    (print-row \"path\" \"abs\" \"first\" \"rest\" \"parent\" \"last\" \"root\" \"ext\")" (br) "    (for-each" (br) "      (lambda (path)" (br) "        (define uscore (lambda (s) (if (eqv? s \"\") \"_\" s)))" (br) "        (apply print-row path" (br) "          (map (lambda (s) (if (eqv? s \"\") \"_\" s))" (br) "               (list (path-absolute? path) (path-first path)" (br) "                 (path-rest path) (path-parent path) (path-last path)" (br) "                 (path-root path) (path-extension path)))))" (br) "      path*)))")) ("For example, the first table can be produced with:") ((code "(print-table \"a\" \"a/\" \"a/b\" \"a/b.c\" \"/\" \"/a/b.c\" \"~/a/b.c\"" (br) "  \"~u/a/b.c\" \"../..\")")) ("while the second can be produced (under Windows) with:") ((code "(print-table \"c:\" \"c:/\" \"c:a/b\" \"//s/a/b.c\" \"//s.com\")"))) ("libraries:s13" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(library " (var "name") " " (var "exports") " " (var "imports") " " (var "library-body") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "library") " form defines a new library with the specified name, exports, imports, and body. Details on the syntax and semantics of the library form are given in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g145" "10.3") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") " and in the Revised" (sup "6") "  Report.") ("Only one version of a library can be loaded at any given time, and an exception is raised if a library is implicitly loaded via " (code "import") "  when another version of the library has already been loaded. " (var "Chez Scheme") " permits a different version of the library, or a new instance of the same version, to be entered explicitly into the REPL or loaded explicitly from a file, to facilitate interactive testing and debugging. The programmer should take care to make sure that any code that uses the library is also reentered or reloaded, to make sure that code accesses the bindings of the new instance of the library.") ((code "(library (test (1)) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 3))" (br) "(import (test))" (br) "(define f (lambda () x))" (br) "(f) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) (br) "(library (test (1)) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 4))" (br) "(import (test))" (br) "(f) " (math "=>") " 3    ; oops---forgot to redefine f" (br) "(define f (lambda () x))" (br) "(f) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) (br) "(library (test (2)) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 5))" (br) "(import (test))" (br) "(define f (lambda () x))" (br) "(f) " (math "=>") " 5")) ("As with module imports (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g108" "11.5") "), a library " (code "import") " may appear anywhere a definition may appear, including at top level in the REPL, in a file to be loaded by " (code "load") ", or within a " (code "lambda") ", " (code "let") ", " (code "letrec") ", " (code "letrec*") ", etc., body. The same " (code "import") " form may be used to import from both libraries and modules.") ((code "(library (foo) (export a) (import (rnrs)) (define a 'a-from-foo))" (br) "(module bar (b) (define b 'b-from-bar))" (br) "(let () (import (foo) bar) (list a b)) " (math "=>") " (a-from-foo b-from-bar)")) ("The " (code "import") " keyword is not visible within a library body unless the library imports it from the " (code "(chezscheme)") " library.")) ("libraries:s14" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(top-level-program " (var "imports") " " (var "body") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "top-level-program") " form may be entered into the REPL or placed in a file to be loaded via " (code "load") ", where it behaves as if its subforms were placed in a file and loaded via " (code "load-program") ". Details on the syntax and semantics of a top-level program are given in Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g145" "10.3") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") " and in the Revised" (sup "6") "  Report.") ("The following transcript illustrates a " (code "top-level-program") " being tested in the REPL.") ((code "> (top-level-program (import (rnrs))" (br) "    (display \"hello!\\n\"))" (br) "hello!"))) ("libraries:s16" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(import " (var "import-spec") " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(import-only " (var "import-spec") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("An " (code "import") " or " (code "import-only") " form is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear, including at the top level of a program, nested within the bodies of " (code "lambda") " expressions, and nested within modules and libraries.") ("Each " (code (var "import-spec")) " must take one of the following forms.") ((code (var "import-set") (br) "(for " (var "import-set") " " (var "import-level") " ...)")) ("The " (code "for") " wrapper and " (code (var "import-level")) " are described in  Chapter " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g142" "10") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") ". They are ignored by " (var "Chez Scheme") ", which determines automatically the levels at which identifiers must be imported, as permitted by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report. This frees the programmer from the obligation to do so and results in more generality as well as more precision in the set of libraries actually imported at compile and run time [" (link "./bibliography.html#g181" "21") "," (link "./bibliography.html#g179" "19") "].") ("An " (code (var "import-set")) " must take one of the following forms:") ((code (var "library-spec") (br) (var "module-name") (br) "(only " (var "import-set") " " (var "identifier") " ...)" (br) "(except " (var "import-set") " " (var "identifier") " ...)" (br) "(prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")" (br) "(add-prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")" (br) "(drop-prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")" (br) "(rename " (var "import-set") " (" (var "import-name") " " (var "internal-name") ") ...)" (br) "(alias " (var "import-set") " (" (var "import-name") " " (var "internal-name") ") ...)")) ("Several of these are specified by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report; the remainder are " (var "Chez Scheme") " extensions, including " (code (var "module-name")) " and the " (code "add-prefix") ", " (code "drop-prefix") ", and " (code "alias") " forms.") ("An " (code "import") " or " (code "import-only") " form makes the specified bindings visible in the scope in which they appear. Except at top level, they differ in that " (code "import") " leaves all bindings except for those shadowed by the imported names visible, whereas " (code "import-only") "  hides all existing bindings, i.e., makes only the imported names visible. At top level, " (code "import-only") " behaves like " (code "import") ".") ("Each " (code (var "import-set")) " identifies a set of names to make visible as follows.") ((dt (code (var "library-spec")) ":") (dd "all exports of the library identified by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code (var "library-spec")) "  (Chapter " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g142" "10") ").")) ((dt (code (var "module-name")) ":") (dd "all exports of module named by the identifier " (code (var "module-name")))) ((dt (code "(only " (var "import-set") " " (var "identifier") " ...)") ":") (dd "of those specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", just " (code (var "identifier") " ..."))) ((dt (code "(except " (var "import-set") " " (var "identifier") " ...)") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) " except " (code (var "identifier") " ..."))) ((dt (code "(prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", each prefixed by " (code (var "prefix")))) ((dt (code "(add-prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", each prefixed by " (code (var "prefix")) "  (just like " (code "prefix") ")")) ((dt (code "(drop-prefix " (var "import-set") " " (var "prefix") ")") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", with prefix " (code (var "prefix")) " removed")) ((dt (code "(rename " (var "import-set") " (" (var "import-name") " " (var "internal-name") ") ...)") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", with each identifier " (code (var "import-name")) "  renamed to the corresponding identifier " (code (var "internal-name")))) ((dt (code "(alias " (var "import-set") " (" (var "import-name") " " (var "internal-name") ") ...)") ":") (dd "all specified by " (code (var "import-set")) ", with each " (code (var "internal-name")) " as an alias for " (code (var "import-name")))) ("The " (code "alias") " form differs from the " (code "rename") " form in that both " (code (var "import-name")) " and " (code (var "internal-name")) " are in the resulting set, rather than just " (code (var "internal-name")) ".") ("It is a syntax violation if the given selection or transformation cannot be made because of a missing export or prefix.") ("An identifier made visible via an import of a module or library is scoped as if its definition appears where the import occurs. The following example illustrates these scoping rules, using a local module " (code "m") ".") ((code "(library (A) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 0))" (br) "(let ([x 1])" (br) "  (module m (x setter)" (br) "    (define-syntax x (identifier-syntax z))" (br) "    (define setter (lambda (x) (set! z x)))" (br) "    (define z 2))" (br) "  (let ([y x] [z 3])" (br) "    (import m (prefix (A) a:))" (br) "    (setter 4)" (br) "    (list x a:x y z))) " (math "=>") " (4 0 1 3)")) ("The inner " (code "let") " expression binds " (code "y") " to the value of the " (code "x") " bound by the outer " (code "let") ". The import of " (code "m") " makes the definitions of " (code "x") "  and " (code "setter") " visible within the inner " (code "let") ". The import of " (code "(A)") " makes the variable " (code "x") " exported from " (code "(A)") " visible as " (code "a:x") " within the body of the inner " (code "let") ". Thus, in the expression " (code "(list x a:x y z)") ", " (code "x") " refers to the identifier macro exported from " (code "m") " while " (code "a:x") " refers to the variable " (code "x") " exported from " (code "(A)") " and " (code "y") " and " (code "z") "  refer to the bindings established by the inner " (code "let") ". The identifier macro " (code "x") " expands into a reference to the variable " (code "z") " defined within the module.") ("With local import forms, it is rarely necessary to use the extended import specifiers. For example, an abstraction that encapsulates the import and reference can easily be defined and used as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax from" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ m id) (let () (import-only m) id)]))" (br) (br) "(library (A) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 1))" (br) "(let ([x 10])" (br) "  (module M (x) (define x 2))" (br) "  (cons (from (A) x) (from M x))) " (math "=>") " (1 . 2)")) ("The definition of " (code "from") " could use " (code "import") " rather than " (code "import-only") ", but by using " (code "import-only") " we get feedback if an attempt is made to import an identifier from a library or module that does not export the identifier. With " (code "import") " instead of " (code "import-only") ", the current binding, if any, would be visible if the library or module does not export the specified name.") ((code "(define-syntax lax-from" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ m id) (let () (import m) id)]))" (br) (br) "(library (A) (export x) (import (rnrs)) (define x 1))" (br) (br) "(let ([x 10])" (br) "  (module M (x) (define x 2))" (br) "  (+ (from (A) x) (from M y))) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: unbound identifier y") (br) " " (br) "(let ([x 10] [y 20])" (br) "  (module M (x) (define x 2))" (br) "  (+ (lax-from (A) x) (lax-from M y))) " (math "=>") " 21")) ("Import visibility interacts with hygienic macro expansion in such a way that, as one might expect, an identifier " (code (var "x")) " imported from a module " (code (var "M")) " is treated in the importing context as if the corresponding export identifier had been present in the import form along with " (code (var "M")) ".") ("The " (code "from") " abstraction above works because both " (code (var "M")) " and " (code (var "id")) "  appear in the input to the abstraction, so the imported " (code (var "id")) " captures the reference to " (code (var "id")) ".") ("The following variant of " (code (var "from")) " also works, because both names are introduced into the output by the transformer.") ((code "(module M (x) (define x 'x-of-M))" (br) "(define-syntax x-from-M" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_) (let () (import M) x)]))" (br) (br) "(let ([x 'local-x]) (x-from-M)) " (math "=>") " x-of-M")) ("On the other hand, imports of introduced module names do not capture free references.") ((code "(let ([x 'local-x])" (br) "  (define-syntax alpha" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ var) (let () (import M) (list x var))]))" (br) " " (br) "  (alpha x)) " (math "=>") " (x-of-M local-x)")) ("Similarly, imports from free module names do not capture references to introduced variables.") ((code "(let ([x 'local-x])" (br) "  (define-syntax beta" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ m var) (let () (import m) (list x var))]))" (br) (br) "  (beta M x)) " (math "=>") " (local-x x-of-M)")) ("This semantics extends to prefixed, renamed, and aliased bindings created by the extended " (code "import") " specifiers " (code "prefix") ", " (code "rename") ", and " (code "alias") ".") ("The " (code "from") " abstraction works for variables but not for exported keywords, record names, or module names, since the output is an expression and may thus appear only where expressions may appear. A generalization of this technique is used in the following definition of " (code "import*") ", which supports renaming of imported bindings and selective import of specific bindings---without the use of the built-in " (code "import") " subforms for selecting and renaming identifiers") ((code "(define-syntax import*" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ m) (begin)]" (br) "    [(_ m (new old))" (br) "     (module (new)" (br) "       (module (tmp)" (br) "         (import m)" (br) "         (alias tmp old))" (br) "       (alias new tmp))]" (br) "    [(_ m id) (module (id) (import m))]" (br) "    [(_ m spec0 spec1 ...)" (br) "     (begin (import* m spec0) (import* m spec1 ...))]))")) ("To selectively import an identifier from module or library " (code "m") ", the " (code "import*") " form expands into an anonymous module that first imports all exports of " (code "m") " then re-exports only the selected identifier. To rename on import the macro expands into an anonymous module that instead exports an alias (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g113" "11.10") ") bound to the new name.") ("If the output placed the definition of " (code "new") " in the same scope as the import of " (code "m") ", a naming conflict would arise whenever " (code "new") " is also present in the interface of " (code "m") ". To prevent this, the output instead places the import within a nested anonymous module and links " (code "old") " and " (code "new") "  by means of an alias for the introduced identifier " (code "tmp") ".") ("The macro expands recursively to handle multiple import specifications. Each of the following examples imports " (code "cons") " as " (code "+") " and " (code "+") " as " (code "cons") ", which is probably not a very good idea.") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (import* scheme (+ cons) (cons +))" (br) "  (+ (cons 1 2) (cons 3 4))) " (math "=>") " (3 . 7)" (br) (br) "(let ()" (br) "  (import* (rnrs) (+ cons) (cons +))" (br) "  (+ (cons 1 2) (cons 3 4))) " (math "=>") " (3 . 7)"))) ("libraries:s17" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(export " (var "export-spec") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("An " (code "export") " form is a definition and can appear with other definitions at the front of a " (code "library") " or " (code "module") ". It is a syntax error for an " (code "export") " form to appear in other contexts, including at top level or among the definitions of a top-level program or " (code "lambda") " body.") ("Each " (code (var "export-spec")) " must take one of the following forms.") ((code (var "identifier") (br) "(rename (" (var "internal-name") " " (var "export-name") ") ...)" (br) "(import " (var "import-spec") " ...)")) ("where each " (code (var "internal-name")) " and " (code (var "export-name")) " is an identifier. The first two are syntactically identical to " (code "library") "  " (code (var "export-spec")) "s, while the third is syntactically identical to a " (var "Chez Scheme") " " (code "import") " form, which is an extension of the R6RS library " (code "import") " subform. The first form names a single export, " (code (var "identifier")) ", whose export name is the same as its internal name. The second names a set of exports, each of whose export name is given explicitly and may differ from its internal name.") ("For the third, the identifiers identified by the " (code "import") " form become exports, with aliasing, renaming, prefixing, etc., as specified by the " (code (var "import-spec")) "s. The module or library whose bindings are exported by an " (code "import") "  form appearing within an " (code "export") " form can be defined within or outside the exporting module or library and need not be imported elsewhere within the exporting module or library.") ("The following library exports a two-armed-only variant of " (code "if") "  along with all remaining bindings of the " (code "(rnrs)") " library.") ((code "(library (rnrs-no-one-armed-if) (export) (import (except (chezscheme) if))" (br) "  (export if (import (except (rnrs) if)))" (br) "  (define-syntax if" (br) "    (let ()" (br) "      (import (only (rnrs) if))" (br) "      (syntax-rules ()" (br) "        [(_ tst thn els) (if tst thn els)]))))" (br) (br) "(import (rnrs-no-one-armed-if))" (br) "(if #t 3 4) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(if #t 3) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid syntax"))) ("Another way to define the same library would be to define the two-armed-only " (code "if") " with a different internal name and use " (code "rename") " to export it under the name " (code "if") ":") ((code "(library (rnrs-no-one-armed-if) (export) (import (chezscheme))" (br) "  (export (rename (two-armed-if if)) (import (except (rnrs) if)))" (br) "  (define-syntax two-armed-if" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ tst thn els) (if tst thn els)])))" (br) (br) "(import (rnrs-no-one-armed-if))" (br) "(if #t 3 4) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(if #t 3) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid syntax"))) ("The placement of the " (code "export") " form in the library body is irrelevant, e.g., the " (code "export") " form can appear after the definition in the examples above.")) ("libraries:s18" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(indirect-export " (var "id") " " (var "indirect-id") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This form is a definition and can appear wherever any other definition can appear.") ("An " (code "indirect-export") " form declares that the named " (code (var "indirect-id")) "s are indirectly exported to top level if " (code (var "id")) "  is exported to top level.") ("In general, if an identifier is not directly exported by a library or module, it can be referenced outside of the library or module only in the expansion of a macro defined within and exported from the library or module. Even this cannot occur for libraries or modules defined at top level (or nested within other libraries or modules), unless either (1) the library or module has been set up to implicitly export all identifiers as indirect exports, or (2) each indirectly exported identifier is explicitly declared as an indirect export of some other identifier that is exported, either directly or indirectly, from the library or module, via an " (code "indirect-export") " or the built-in indirect export feature of a " (code "module") " export subform. By default, (1) is true for a library and false for a module, but the default can be overridden via the " (code "implicit-exports") "  form, which is described below.") ("This form is meaningful only within a top-level library, top-level module, or module enclosed within a library or top-level module, although it has no effect if the library or module already implicitly exports all bindings. It is allowed anywhere else definitions can appear, however, so macros that expand into indirect export forms can be used in any definition context.") ("Indirect exports are listed so the compiler can determine the exact set of bindings (direct and indirect) that must be inserted into the top-level environment, and conversely, the set of bindings that may be treated more efficiently as local bindings (and perhaps discarded, if they are not used).") ("In the example below, " (code "indirect-export") " is used to indirectly export " (code "count") " to top level when " (code "current-count") " is exported to top level.") ((code "(module M (bump-count current-count)" (br) "  (define-syntax current-count (identifier-syntax count))" (br) "  (indirect-export current-count count)" (br) "  (define count 0)" (br) "  (define bump-count" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (set! count (+ count 1)))))" (br) (br) "(import M)" (br) "(bump-count)" (br) "current-count " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "count " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: unbound identifier count"))) ("An " (code "indirect-export") " form is not required to make " (code "count") "  visible for " (code "bump-count") ", since it is a procedure whose code is contained within the module rather than a macro that might expand into a reference to " (code "count") " somewhere outside the module.") ("It is often useful to use " (code "indirect-export") " in the output of a macro that expands into another macro named " (code (var "a")) " if " (code (var "a")) " expands into references to identifiers that might not be directly exported, as illustrated by the alternative definition of module " (code "M") " above.") ((code "(define-syntax define-counter" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ getter bumper init incr)" (br) "     (begin" (br) "       (define count init)" (br) "       (define-syntax getter (identifier-syntax count))" (br) "       (indirect-export getter count)" (br) "       (define bumper" (br) "         (lambda ()" (br) "           (set! count (incr count)))))]))" (br) (br) "(module M (bump-count current-count)" (br) "  (define-counter current-count bump-count 0 add1))"))) ("libraries:s19" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(implicit-exports #t)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(implicit-exports #f)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("An " (code "implicit-exports") " form is a definition and can appear with other definitions at the front of a " (code "library") " or " (code "module") ". It is a syntax error for an " (code "implicit-exports") " form to appear in other contexts, including at top level or among the definitions of a top-level program or " (code "lambda") " body.") ("The " (code "implicit-exports") " form determines whether identifiers not directly exported from a module or library are automatically indirectly exported to the top level if any meta-binding (keyword, meta definition, or property definition) is directly exported to top level from the library or module. The default for libraries is " (code "#t") ", to match the behavior required by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report, while the default for modules is " (code "#f") ". The " (code "implicit-exports") " form is meaningful only within a library, top-level module, or module enclosed within a library or top-level module. It is allowed in a module enclosed within a " (code "lambda") ", " (code "let") ", or similar body, but ignored there because none of that module's bindings can be exported to top level.") ("The advantage of " (code "(implicit-exports #t)") " is that indirect exports need not be listed explicitly, which is convenient. A disadvantage is that it often results in more bindings than necessary being elevated to top level where they cannot be discarded as useless by the optimizer. For modules, another disadvantage is such bindings cannot be proven immutable, which inhibits important optimizations such as procedure inlining. This can result in significantly lower run-time performance.")) ("libraries:s20" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(invoke-library " (var "libref") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "libref")) " must be an s-expression in the form of a library reference. The syntax for library references is given in Chapter " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g142" "10") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") " and in the Revised" (sup "6") "  Report.") ("A library is implicitly invoked when or before some expression outside the library (e.g., in another library or in a top-level program) evaluates a reference to one of the library's exported variables. When the library is invoked, its body expressions (the right-hand-sides of the library's variable definitions and its initialization expressions) are evaluated. Once invoked, the library is not invoked again within the same process, unless it is first explicitly redefined or reloaded.") ((code "invoke-library") " explicitly invokes the library specified by " (code (var "libref")) " if it has not already been invoked or has since been redefined or reloaded. If the library has not yet been loaded, " (code "invoke-library") "  first loads the library via the process described in Section " (link "./use.html#g9" "2.4") ".") ((code "invoke-library") " is typically only useful for libraries whose body expressions have side effects. It is useful to control when the side effects occur and to force invocation of a library that has no exported variables. Invoking a library does not force the compile-time code (macro transformer expressions and meta definitions) to be loaded or evaluated, nor does it cause the library's bindings to become visible.") ("It is good practice to avoid externally visible side effects in library bodies so the library can be used equally well at compile time and run time. When feasible, consider moving the side effects of a library body to an initialization routine and adding a top-level program that imports the library and calls the initialization routine. With this structure, calls to " (code "invoke-library") " on the library can be replaced by calls to  " (code "load-program") " on the top-level program.")) ("libraries:s23" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "library-directories") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "library-extensions") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The parameter " (code "library-directories") " determines where the files containing library source and object code are located in the file system, and the parameter " (code "library-extensions") " determines the filename extensions for the files holding the code, as described in section " (link "./use.html#g9" "2.4") ". The values of both parameters are lists of pairs of strings. The first string in each " (code "library-directories") " pair identifies a source-file root directory, and the second identifies the corresponding object-file root directory. Similarly, the first string in each " (code "library-extensions") " pair identifies a source-file extension, and the second identifies the corresponding object-file extension. The full path of a library source or object file consists of the source or object root followed by the components of the library name prefixed by slashes, with the library extension added on the end. For example, for root " (code "/usr/lib/scheme") ", library name " (code "(app lib1)") ", and extension " (code ".sls") ", the full path is " (code "/usr/lib/scheme/app/lib1.sls") ". If the library name portion forms an absolute pathname, e.g., " (code "~/.myappinit") ", the " (code "library-directories") " parameter is ignored and no prefix is added.") ("The initial values of these parameters are shown below.") ((code "(library-directories) " (math "=>") " ((\".\" . \".\"))" (br) (br) "(library-extensions) " (math "=>") " ((\".chezscheme.sls\" . \".chezscheme.so\")" (br) "                     " (math "  ") "  (\".ss\" . \".so\")" (br) "                     " (math "  ") "  (\".sls\" . \".so\")" (br) "                     " (math "  ") "  (\".scm\" . \".so\")" (br) "                     " (math "  ") "  (\".sch\" . \".so\"))")) ("As a convenience, when either of these parameters is set, any element of the list can be specified as a single source string, in which case the object string is determined automatically. For " (code "library-directories") ", the object string is the same as the source string, effectively naming the same directory as a source- and object-code root. For " (code "library-extensions") ", the object string is the result of removing the last (or only) extension from the string and appending " (code "\".so\"") ". The " (code "library-directories") " and " (code "library-extensions") "  parameters also accept as input strings in the format described in Section " (link "./use.html#g10" "2.5") "  for the " (code "--libdirs") " and " (code "--libexts") " command-line options.")) ("libraries:s26" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-imported-libraries") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When the value of this parameter is " (code "#t") ", " (code "import") "  automatically calls the value of the " (code "compile-library-handler") " parameter (which defaults to a procedure that simply calls " (code "compile-library") ") on any imported library if the object file is missing, older than the corresponding source file, older than any source files included (via " (code "include") ") when the object file was created, or itself requires a library that has been or must be recompiled, as described in Section " (link "./use.html#g9" "2.4") ". The " (code "library-timestamp-mode") " parameter controls the meaning of \"older.\" The default initial value of this parameter is " (code "#f") ". It can be set to " (code "#t") " via the command-line option " (code "--compile-imported-libraries") ".") ("When " (code "import") " compiles a library via this mechanism, it does not also load the compiled library, because this would cause portions of library to be reevaluated. Because of this, run-time expressions in the file outside of a " (code "library") " form will not be evaluated. If such expressions are present and should be evaluated, the library should be loaded explicitly.")) ("libraries:s29" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "import-notify") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When the new parameter " (code "import-notify") " is set to a true value, " (code "import") " displays messages to the console-output port as it searches for the file containing each library it needs to load. The default value of this parameter is " (code "#f") ".")) ("libraries:s30" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "library-search-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of parameter must be a procedure that follows the protocol described below for " (code "default-library-search-handler") ", which is the default value of this parameter.") ("The value of this parameter is invoked to locate the source or object code for a library during " (code "import") ", " (code "compile-whole-program") ", or " (code "compile-whole-library") ".")) ("libraries:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(default-library-search-handler " (var "who") " " (var "library") " " (var "directories") " " (var "extensions") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure is the default value of the " (code "library-search-handler") ", which is called to locate the source or object code for a library during " (code "import") ", " (code "compile-whole-program") ", or " (code "compile-whole-library") ". " (code (var "who")) " is a symbol that provides context in " (code "import-notify") " messages. " (code (var "library")) " is the name of the desired library. " (code (var "directories")) " is a list of source and object directory pairs in the form returned by " (code "library-directories") ". " (code (var "extensions")) " is a list of source and object extension pairs in the form returned by " (code "library-extensions") ".") ("This procedure searches the specified directories until it finds a library source or object file with one of the specified extensions. If it finds the source file first, it constructs the corresponding object file path and checks whether the file exists. If it finds the object file first, the procedure looks for a corresponding source file with one of the given source extensions in a source directory paired with that object directory. The procedure returns three values: the file-system path of the library source file or " (code "#f") " if not found, the file-system path of the corresponding object file, which may be " (code "#f") ", and a boolean that is true if the object file exists.")) ("libraries:s32" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "library-timestamp-mode") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of parameter must be either " (code "'modification-time") " (the default) or " (code "'exists") ". If it is " (code "'modification-time") ", the timestamp of library source and object files is obtained with " (code "file-modification-time") " to determine whether a file is older than the other. If the parameter's value is " (code "'exists") ", then all files that exist are considered to have the same age. This parameter's value can be set to " (code "'exists") " via the command-line option " (code "--disable-library-timestamps") ".")) ("libraries:s34" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-list)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of the libraries currently defined" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The set of libraries initially defined includes those listed in Section " (link "./libraries.html#g96" "10.1") " above.")) ("libraries:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-version " (var "libref") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the version of the specified library" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-exports " (var "libref") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of the exports of the specified library" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-requirements " (var "libref") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of libraries required by the specified library" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-requirements " (var "libref") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of libraries required by the specified library, filtered by " (code (var "options")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(library-object-filename " (var "libref") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the name of the object file holding the specified library, if any" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Information can be obtained only for built-in libraries or libraries previously loaded into the system. " (code (var "libref")) " must be an s-expression in the form of a library reference. The syntax for library references is given in Chapter " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g142" "10") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") " and in the Revised" (sup "6") "  Report.") ("The " (code "library-version") " return value is a list of numbers (possibly empty) representing the library's version.") ("The list of exports returned by " (code "library-exports") " is a list of symbols, each identifying one of the library's exports. The order in which the elements appear is unspecified.") ("When the optional " (code (var "options")) " argument is supplied, it must be an enumeration set over the symbols constituting valid library-requirements options, as described in the " (code "library-requirements-options") " entry below. It defaults to a set containing all of the options. Each element of the list of libraries returned by " (code "library-requirements") " is an s-expression form of a library reference. The library reference includes the actual version of the library that is present in the system (if nonempty), even if a version was not specified when it was imported. The order in which the libraries appear in the list returned by " (code "library-requirements") " is unspecified.") ((code "library-object-filename") " returns a string naming the object file if the specified library was loaded from or compiled to an object file. Otherwise, it returns " (code "#f") ".") ((code "(with-output-to-file \"A.ss\"" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (pretty-print" (br) "      '(library (A (1 2)) (export x z)" (br) "         (import (rnrs))" (br) "         (define x 'ex)" (br) "         (define y 23)" (br) "         (define-syntax z" (br) "           (syntax-rules ()" (br) "             [(_ e) (+ y e)])))))" (br) "  'replace)" (br) "(with-output-to-file \"B.ss\"" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (pretty-print" (br) "      '(library (B) (export x w)" (br) "         (import (rnrs) (A))" (br) "         (define w (cons (z 12) x)))))" (br) "  'replace)" (br) "(compile-imported-libraries #t)" (br) "(import (B))" (br) "(library-exports '(A)) " (math "=>") " (x z) ; or (z x)" (br) "(library-exports '(A (1 2))) " (math "=>") " (x z) ; or (z x)" (br) "(library-exports '(B)) " (math "=>") " (x w) ; or (w x)" (br) "(library-version '(A)) " (math "=>") " (1 2)" (br) "(library-version '(B)) " (math "=>") " ()" (br) "(library-requirements '(A)) " (math "=>") " ((rnrs (6)))" (br) "(library-requirements '(B)) " (math "=>") " ((rnrs (6)) (A (1 2)))" (br) "(library-object-filename '(A)) " (math "=>") " \"A.so\"" (br) "(library-object-filename '(B)) " (math "=>") " \"B.so\""))) ("libraries:s36" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(library-requirements-options " (var "symbol") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a library-requirements-options enumeration set" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Library-requirements-options enumeration sets are passed to " (code "library-requirements") " to determine the library requirements to be listed.  The available options are described below.") ((dt (code "import") ":") (dd "Include the libraries that must be imported when the specified library is imported.")) ((dt (code "visit@visit") ":") (dd "Includes the libraries that must be visited when the specified library is visited.")) ((dt (code "invoke@visit") ":") (dd "Include the libraries that must be invoked when the specified library is visited.")) ((dt (code "invoke") ":") (dd "Includes the libraries that must be invoked when the specified library is invoked."))) ("numeric:s11" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bignum? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a bignum, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(bignum? 0) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(bignum? (most-positive-fixnum)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(bignum? (most-negative-fixnum)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(bignum? (* (most-positive-fixnum) 2)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(bignum? 3/4) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(bignum? 'a) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("numeric:s12" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ratnum? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a ratnum, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(ratnum? 0) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(ratnum? (* (most-positive-fixnum) 2)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(ratnum? 3/4) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(ratnum? -10/2) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(ratnum? -11/2) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(ratnum? 'a) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("numeric:s13" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cflonum? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is an inexact complexnum or flonum, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(cflonum? 0) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(cflonum? 0.0) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(cflonum? 3+4i) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(cflonum? 3.0+4i) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(cflonum? +i) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(cflonum? +1.0i) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("numeric:s14" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(most-positive-fixnum)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the most positive fixnum supported by the system" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(most-negative-fixnum)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the most negative fixnum supported by the system" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "greatest-fixnum") " and " (code "least-fixnum") " procedures.")) ("numeric:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx= " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx< " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx> " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx<= " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx>= " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the relation holds, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The predicate " (code "fx=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are equal. The predicate " (code "fx<") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically increasing, i.e., each argument is greater than the preceding ones, while " (code "fx>") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically decreasing. The predicate " (code "fx<=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically nondecreasing, i.e., each argument is not less than the preceding ones, while " (code "fx>=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically nonincreasing. When passed only one argument, each of these predicates returns " (code "#t") ".") ("These procedures are similar to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report procedures " (code "fx=?") ", " (code "fx<?") ", " (code "fx>?") ", " (code "fx<=?") ", and " (code "fx>=?") " except that the Revised" (sup "6") " Report procedures require two or more arguments, and their names have the \"" (code "?") "\" suffix.") ((code "(fx= 0) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fx= 0 0) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fx< (most-negative-fixnum) 0 (most-positive-fixnum)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([x 3]) (fx<= 0 x 9)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fx<= 0 3 3) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fx>= 0 0 (most-negative-fixnum)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("numeric:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxnonpositive? " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "fixnum")) " is not greater than zero, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxnonnegative? " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "fixnum")) " is not less than zero, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "fxnonpositive?") " is equivalent to " (code "(lambda (x) (fx<= x 0))") ", and " (code "fxnonnegative?") " is equivalent to " (code "(lambda (x) (fx>= x 0))") ".") ((code "(fxnonpositive? 128) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(fxnonpositive? 0) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fxnonpositive? -1) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(fxnonnegative? -65) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(fxnonnegative? 0) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(fxnonnegative? 1) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("numeric:s17" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx+ " (var "fixnum") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the sum of the arguments " (code (var "fixnum") " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When called with no arguments, " (code "fx+") " returns " (code "0") ".") ((code "(fx+) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(fx+ 1 2) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(fx+ 3 4 5) " (math "=>") " 12" (br) "(apply fx+ '(1 2 3 4 5)) " (math "=>") " 15"))) ("numeric:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx- " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a fixnum" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When called with one argument, " (code "fx-") " returns the negative of " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) ". Thus, " (code "(fx- " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) ")") " is an idiom for " (code "(fx- 0 " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) ")") ".") ("When called with two or more arguments, " (code "fx-") " returns the result of subtracting the sum of the numbers " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...") " from " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) ".") ((code "(fx- 3) " (math "=>") " -3" (br) "(fx- 4 3) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fx- 4 3 2 1) " (math "=>") " -2"))) ("numeric:s19" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx* " (var "fixnum") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the product of the arguments " (code (var "fixnum") " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When called with no arguments, " (code "fx*") " returns " (code "1") ".") ((code "(fx*) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fx* 1 2) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "(fx* 3 -4 5) " (math "=>") " -60" (br) "(apply fx* '(1 -2 3 -4 5)) " (math "=>") " 120"))) ("numeric:s20" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx/ " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When called with one argument, " (code "fx/") " returns the reciprocal of " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) ". That is, " (code "(fx/ " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) ")") " is an idiom for " (code "(fx/ 1 " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) ")") ".") ("When called with two or more arguments, " (code "fx/") " returns the result of dividing " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) " by the product of the remaining arguments " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...") ".") ((code "(fx/ 1) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fx/ -17) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(fx/ 8 -2) " (math "=>") " -4" (br) "(fx/ -9 2) " (math "=>") " -4" (br) "(fx/ 60 5 3 2) " (math "=>") " 2"))) ("numeric:s21" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx+/wraparound " (var "fixnum") " " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx-/wraparound " (var "fixnum") " " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx-/wraparound " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx*/wraparound " (var "fixnum") " " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxsll/wraparound " (var "fixnum") " " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the arithmetic result, wrapping on overflow" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These functions are like " (code "fx+") ", " (code "fx-") ", " (code "fx*") ", and " (code "fxsll") ", but when the result is too large to fit in a fixnum, as many high bits of the result as necessary are discarded to make the result representable as a fixnum.")) ("numeric:s22" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx1+ " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fx1- " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code (var "fixnum")) " plus 1 or " (code (var "fixnum")) " minus 1" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(define fxplus" (br) "  (lambda (x y)" (br) "    (if (fxzero? x)" (br) "        y" (br) "        (fxplus (fx1- x) (fx1+ y)))))" (br) (br) "(fxplus 7 8) " (math "=>") " 15")) ((code "fx1+") " and " (code "fx1-") " can be defined as follows:") ((code "(define fx1+ (lambda (x) (fx+ x 1)))" (br) "(define fx1- (lambda (x) (fx- x 1)))"))) ("numeric:s23" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxquotient " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "fxquotient") " is identical to " (code "fx/") ". See the description of " (code "fx/") " above.")) ("numeric:s24" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxremainder " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the fixnum remainder of " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) " divided by " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "2"))) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The result of " (code "fxremainder") " has the same sign as " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) ".") ((code "(fxremainder 16 4) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(fxremainder 5 2) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fxremainder -45 7) " (math "=>") " -3" (br) "(fxremainder 10 -3) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fxremainder -17 -9) " (math "=>") " -8"))) ("numeric:s25" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxmodulo " (var "fixnum" (sub "1")) " " (var "fixnum" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the fixnum modulus of " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "2"))) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The result of " (code "fxmodulo") " has the same sign as " (code (var "fixnum" (sub "2"))) ".") ((code "(fxmodulo 16 4) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(fxmodulo 5 2) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fxmodulo -45 7) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) "(fxmodulo 10 -3) " (math "=>") " -2" (br) "(fxmodulo -17 -9) " (math "=>") " -8"))) ("numeric:s26" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fxabs " (var "fixnum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the absolute value of " (code (var "fixnum")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(fxabs 1) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fxabs -1) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(fxabs 0) " (math "=>") " 0"))) ("numeric:s27" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(flonum->fixnum " (var "flonum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the fixnum representation of " (code (var "flonum")) ", truncated" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The truncated value of " (code (var "flonum")) " must fall within the fixnum range. " (code "flonum->fixnum") " is a restricted version of " (code "exact") ", which converts any numeric representation to its exact equivalent.") ((code "(flonum->fixnum 0.0) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(flonum->fixnum 3.9) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(flonum->fixnum -2.2) " (math "=>") " -2"))) ("numeric:s29" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fl= " (var "flonum" (sub "1")) " " (var "flonum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fl< " (var "flonum" (sub "1")) " " (var "flonum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fl> " (var "flonum" (sub "1")) " " (var "flonum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fl<= " (var "flonum" (sub "1")) " " (var "flonum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fl>= " (var "flonum" (sub "1")) " " (var "flonum" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the relation holds, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The predicate " (code "fl=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are equal. The predicate " (code "fl<") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically increasing, i.e., each argument is greater than the preceding ones, while " (code "fl>") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically decreasing. The predicate " (code "fl<=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically nondecreasing, i.e., each argument is not less than the preceding ones, while " (code "fl>=") " returns " (code "#t") " if its arguments are monotonically nonincreasing. When passed only one argument, each of these predicates returns " (code "#t") ".") ("IEEE NANs are not comparable, i.e., comparisons involving NANs always return " (code "#f") ".") ("These procedures are similar to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report procedures " (code "fl=?") ", " (code "fl<?") ", " (code "fl>?") ", " (code "fl<=?") ", and " (code "fl>=?") " exc