-proc")) " is a procedure, a new association between " (code (var "rtd")) " and " (code (var "equal-proc")) " is established, replacing any existing such association. If " (code (var "equal-proc")) " is " (code "#f") ", any existing association between " (code (var "rtd")) " and an equality procedure is dropped.") ("In the second form, " (code "record-type-equal-procedure") " returns the equality procedure associated with " (code (var "rtd")) ", if any, otherwise " (code "#f") ".") ("When changing a record type's equality procedure, the record type's hash procedure, if any, should be updated if necessary to maintain the property that it produces the same hash value for any two instances the equality procedure considers equal.")) ("objects:s201" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-equal-procedure " (var "record" (sub "1")) " " (var "record" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the shared equality procedure for " (code (var "record" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "record" (sub "2"))) ", if there is one, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "record-equal-procedure") " traverses the inheritance chains for both record instances in an attempt to find the most specific type for each that is associated with an equality procedure, if any. If such type is found and is the same for both instances, the equality procedure associated with the type is returned. Otherwise, " (code "#f") " is returned.")) ("objects:s202" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-hash-procedure " (var "rtd") " " (var "hash-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-hash-procedure " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "hash procedure associated with " (code (var "rtd")) ", if any, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("In the first form, " (code (var "hash-proc")) " must be a procedure or " (code "#f") ". If " (code (var "hash-proc")) " is a procedure, a new association between " (code (var "rtd")) " and " (code (var "hash-proc")) " is established, replacing any existing such association. If " (code (var "hash-proc")) " is " (code "#f") ", any existing association between " (code (var "rtd")) " and a hash procedure is dropped.") ("In the second form, " (code "record-type-hash-procedure") " returns the hash procedure associated with " (code (var "rtd")) ", if any, otherwise " (code "#f") ".") ("The procedure " (code (var "hash-proc")) " should accept two arguments, the instance for which it should compute a hash value and a hash procedure to use to compute hash values for arbitrary fields of the instance, and it returns a nonnegative exact integer. A record type's hash procedure should produce the same hash value for any two instances the record type's equality procedure considers equal.")) ("objects:s203" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-hash-procedure " (var "record") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the hash procedure for " (code (var "record")) ", if there is one, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "record-hash-procedure") " traverses the inheritance chain for the record instance in an attempt to find the most specific type that is associated with a hash procedure, if any. If such type is found, the hash procedure associated with the type is returned. Otherwise, " (code "#f") " is returned.")) ("objects:s204" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "default-record-equal-procedure") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines how two record instances are compared by " (code "equal?") " if neither has a type-specific equality procedure. When the parameter has the value " (code "#f") " (the default), " (code "equal?") "  compares the instances with " (code "eq?") ", i.e., there is no attempt at determining structural equivalence. Otherwise, the parameter's value must be a procedure, and " (code "equal?") "  invokes that procedure to compare the instances, passing it three arguments: the two instances and a procedure that should be used to recursively compare arbitrary values within the instances.")) ("objects:s205" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "default-record-hash-procedure") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines the hash procedure used when " (code "equal-hash") "  is called on a record instance and the instance does not have a type-specific hash procedure. When the parameter has the value " (code "#f") " (the default), " (code "equal-hash") "  returns a value that is independent of the record type and contents of the instance. Otherwise, the parameter's value must be a procedure, and " (code "equal-hash") "  invokes the procedure to compute the instance's hash value, passing it the record instance and a procedure to invoke to recursively compute hash values for arbitrary values contained within the record. The procedure should return a nonnegative exact integer, and the return value should be the same for any two instances the default equal procedure considers equivalent.")) ("objects:s216" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-record " (var "name") " (" (var "fld" (sub "1")) " ...) ((" (var "fld" (sub "2")) " " (var "init") ") ...) (" (var "opt") " ...))") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-record " (var "name") " " (var "parent") " (" (var "fld" (sub "1")) " ...) ((" (var "fld" (sub "2")) " " (var "init") ") ...) (" (var "opt") " ...))") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "define-record") " form is a definition and may appear anywhere and only where other definitions may appear.") ((code "define-record") " creates a new record type containing a specified set of named fields and defines a set of procedures for creating and manipulating instances of the record type.") ((code (var "name")) " must be an identifier. If " (code (var "name")) " is a generated symbol (gensym), the record definition is " (var "nongenerative") ", otherwise it is " (var "expand-time generative") ". (See the discussion of generativity earlier in this section.)") ("Each " (code (var "fld")) " must be an identifier " (code (var "field-name")) ", or it must take the form") ((code "(" (var "class") " " (var "type") " " (var "field-name") ")")) ("where " (code (var "class")) " and " (code (var "type")) " are optional and " (code (var "field-name")) " is an identifier. " (code (var "class")) ", if present, must be the keyword " (code "immutable") "  or the keyword " (code "mutable") ". If the " (code "immutable") " class specifier is present, the field is immutable; otherwise, the field is mutable. " (code (var "type")) ", if present, specifies how the field is represented, as described below. ") ((table (row (cell (code "ptr") " ") (cell "            any Scheme object")) (row (cell (code "scheme-object") " ") (cell "  same as " (code "ptr"))) (row (cell (code "int") " ") (cell "            a C " (code "int"))) (row (cell (code "unsigned") " ") (cell "       a C " (code "unsigned int"))) (row (cell (code "short") " ") (cell "          a C " (code "short"))) (row (cell (code "unsigned-short") " ") (cell " a C " (code "unsigned short"))) (row (cell (code "long") " ") (cell "           a C " (code "long"))) (row (cell (code "unsigned-long") " ") (cell "  a C " (code "unsigned long"))) (row (cell (code "iptr") " ") (cell "           a signed integer the size of a " (code "ptr"))) (row (cell (code "uptr") " ") (cell "           an unsigned integer the size of a " (code "ptr"))) (row (cell (code "float") " ") (cell "          a C " (code "float"))) (row (cell (code "double") " ") (cell "         a C " (code "double"))) (row (cell (code "integer-8") " ") (cell "      an eight-bit signed integer")) (row (cell (code "unsigned-8") " ") (cell "     an eight-bit unsigned integer")) (row (cell (code "integer-16") " ") (cell "     a 16-bit signed integer")) (row (cell (code "unsigned-16") " ") (cell "    a 16-bit unsigned integer")) (row (cell (code "integer-32") " ") (cell "     a 32-bit signed integer")) (row (cell (code "unsigned-32") " ") (cell "    a 32-bit unsigned integer")) (row (cell (code "integer-64") " ") (cell "     a 64-bit signed integer")) (row (cell (code "unsigned-64") " ") (cell "    a 64-bit unsigned integer")) (row (cell (code "single-float") " ") (cell "   a 32-bit single floating point number")) (row (cell (code "double-float") " ") (cell "   a 64-bit double floating point number")))) ("If a type is specified, the field can contain objects only of the specified type. If no type is specified, the field is of type " (code "ptr") ", meaning that it can contain any Scheme object.") ("The field identifiers name the fields of the record. The values of the " (var "n") " fields described by " (code (var "fld" (sub "1")) " ...") " are specified by the " (var "n") " arguments to the generated constructor procedure. The values of the remaining fields, " (code (var "fld" (sub "2")) " ...") ", are given by the corresponding expressions, " (code (var "init") " ...") ". Each " (code (var "init")) " is evaluated within the scope of the set of field names given by " (code (var "fld" (sub "1")) " ...") " and each field in " (code (var "fld" (sub "2")) " ...") " that precedes it, as if within a " (code "let*") " expression. Each of these field names is bound to the value of the corresponding field during initialization.") ("If " (code (var "parent")) " is present, the record type named by " (code (var "parent")) "  is the parent of the record. The new record type inherits each of the parent record's fields, and records of the new type are considered records of the parent type. If " (code (var "parent")) " is not present, the parent record type is a base record type with no fields.") ("The following procedures are defined by " (code "define-record") ":   " (ul (li "a constructor procedure whose name is " (code "make-" (var "name")) ",") (li "a type predicate whose name is " (code (var "name") "?") ",") (li "an access procedure whose name is " (code (var "name") "-" (var "fieldname")) "  for each noninherited field, and") (li "an assignment procedure whose name is " (code "set-" (var "name") "-" (var "fieldname") "!") "  for each noninherited mutable field."))) ("If no parent record type is specified, the constructor behaves as if defined as") ((code "(define make-" (var "name") (br) "  (lambda (" (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...)" (br) "    (let* ([" (var "id" (sub "2")) " " (var "init") "] ...)" (br) "      " (var "body") ")))")) ("where " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " are the names of the fields defined by " (code (var "fld" (sub "1")) " ...") ", " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") " are the names of the fields defined by " (code (var "fld" (sub "2")) " ...") ", and " (code (var "body")) " builds the record from the values of the identifiers " (code (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...") " and " (code (var "id" (sub "2")) " ...") ".") ("If a parent record type is specified, the parent arguments appear first, and the parent fields are inserted into the record before the child fields.") ("The options " (code (var "opt") " ...") " control the selection of names of the generated constructor, predicate, accessors, and mutators.") ((code "(constructor " (var "id") ")" (br) "(predicate " (var "id") ")" (br) "(prefix " (var "string") ")")) ("The option " (code "(constructor " (var "id") ")") " causes the generated constructor's name to be " (code (var "id")) " rather than " (code "make-" (var "name")) ". The option " (code "(predicate " (var "id") ")") " likewise causes the generated predicate's name to be " (code (var "id")) " rather than " (code (var "name") "?") ". The option " (code "(prefix " (var "string") ")") " determines the prefix to be used in the generated accessor and mutator names in place of " (code (var "name") "-") ".") ("If no options are needed, the third subexpression, " (code "(" (var "opt") " ...)") ", may be omitted. If no options and no fields other than those initialized by the arguments to the constructor procedure are needed, both the second and third subexpressions may be omitted. If options are specified, the second subexpression must be present, even if it contains no field specifiers.") ("Here is a simple example with no inheritance and no options.") ((code "(define-record marble (color quality))" (br) "(define x (make-marble 'blue 'medium))" (br) "(marble? x) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(pair? x) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(vector? x) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(marble-color x) " (math "=>") " blue" (br) "(marble-quality x) " (math "=>") " medium" (br) "(set-marble-quality! x 'low)" (br) "(marble-quality x) " (math "=>") " low" (br) (br) "(define-record marble ((immutable color) (mutable quality))" (br) "  (((mutable shape) (if (eq? quality 'high) 'round 'unknown))))" (br) "(marble-shape (make-marble 'blue 'high)) " (math "=>") " round" (br) "(marble-shape (make-marble 'blue 'low)) " (math "=>") " unknown" (br) "(define x (make-marble 'blue 'high))" (br) "(set-marble-quality! x 'low)" (br) "(marble-shape x) " (math "=>") " round" (br) "(set-marble-shape! x 'half-round)" (br) "(marble-shape x) " (math "=>") " half-round")) ("The following example illustrates inheritance.") ((code "(define-record shape (x y))" (br) "(define-record point shape ())" (br) "(define-record circle shape (radius))" (br) (br) "(define a (make-point 7 -3))" (br) "(shape? a) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(point? a) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(circle? a) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(shape-x a) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) "(set-shape-y! a (- (shape-y a) 1))" (br) "(shape-y a) " (math "=>") " -4" (br) (br) "(define b (make-circle 7 -3 1))" (br) "(shape? b) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(point? b) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(circle? b) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(circle-radius b) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(circle-radius a) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: not of type circle") (br) (br) "(define c (make-shape 0 0))" (br) "(shape? c) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(point? c) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(circle? c) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("This example demonstrates the use of options:") ((code "(define-record pair (car cdr)" (br) "  ()" (br) "  ((constructor cons)" (br) "   (prefix \"\")))" (br) (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(car x) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "(cdr x) " (math "=>") " b" (br) "(pair? x) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(pair? '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "x " (math "=>") " #[#{pair bdhavk1bwafxyss1-a} a b]")) ("This example illustrates the use a specified reader name, immutable fields, and the graph mark and reference syntax.") ((code "(define-record triple ((immutable x1) (mutable x2) (immutable x3)))" (br) "(record-reader 'triple (type-descriptor triple))" (br) (br) "(let ([t '#[triple #1=(1 2) (3 4) #1#]])" (br) "  (eq? (triple-x1 t) (triple-x3 t))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([x '(#1=(1 2) . #[triple #1# b c])])" (br) "  (eq? (car x) (triple-x1 (cdr x)))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([t #[triple #1# (3 4) #1=(1 2)]])" (br) "  (eq? (triple-x1 t) (triple-x3 t))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([t '#1=#[triple a #1# c]])" (br) "  (eq? t (triple-x2 t))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([t '#1=(#[triple #1# b #1#])])" (br) "  (and (eq? t (triple-x1 (car t)))" (br) "       (eq? t (triple-x1 (car t))))) " (math "=>") " #t")) ("Cycles established with the mark and reference syntax can be resolved only if a mutable record field or mutable location of some other object is involved the cycle, as in the last two examples above. An exception is raised with condition type " (code "&lexical") " if only immutable fields are involved.") ((code "'#1=#[triple #1# (3 4) #1#] " (math "=>") " " (var "exception"))) ("The following example demonstrates the use of nongenerative record definitions.") ((code "(module A (point-disp)" (br) "  (define-record #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b} (x y))" (br) "  (define square (lambda (x) (* x x)))" (br) "  (define point-disp" (br) "    (lambda (p1 p2)" (br) "      (sqrt (+ (square (- (point-x p1) (point-x p2)))" (br) "               (square (- (point-y p1) (point-y p2))))))))" (br) (br) "(module B (base-disp)" (br) "  (define-record #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b} (x y))" (br) "  (import A)" (br) "  (define base-disp" (br) "    (lambda (p)" (br) "      (point-disp (make-point 0 0) p))))" (br) (br) "(let ()" (br) "  (import B)" (br) "  (define-record #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b} (x y))" (br) "  (base-disp (make-point 3 4))) " (math "=>") " 5")) ("This works even if the different program components are loaded from different source files or are compiled separately and loaded from different object files.")) ("objects:s220" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "predicate") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "prefix") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "constructor") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These identifiers are auxiliary keywords for " (code "define-record") ". It is a syntax violation to reference these identifiers except in contexts where they are recognized as auxiliary keywords. " (code "mutable") " and " (code "immutable") " are also auxiliary keywords for " (code "define-record") ", shared with the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "define-record-type") ".")) ("objects:s223" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(type-descriptor " (var "name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the record-type descriptor associated with " (code (var "name")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "name")) " must name a record type defined by " (code "define-record") "  or " (code "define-record-type") ".") ("This form is equivalent to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "record-type-descriptor") " form.") ("The record-type descriptor is useful for overriding the default read and write syntax using " (code "record-reader") " and " (code "record-writer") " and may also be used with the procedural interface routines described later in this section.") ((code "(define-record frob ())" (br) "(type-descriptor frob) " (math "=>") " #<record type frob>"))) ("objects:s224" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-reader " (var "name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the record-type descriptor associated with " (code (var "name")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-reader " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the first name associated with " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-reader " (var "name") " " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-reader " (var "name") " #f)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-reader " (var "rtd") " #f)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "name")) " must be a symbol, and " (code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ("With one argument, " (code "record-reader") " is used to retrieve the record type associated with a name or name associated with a record type. If no association has been created, " (code "record-reader") " returns " (code "#f")) ("With arguments " (code (var "name")) " and " (code (var "rtd")) ", " (code "record-reader") " registers " (code (var "rtd")) " as the record-type descriptor to be used whenever the " (code "read") " procedure encounters a record named by " (code (var "name")) " and printed in the default record syntax.") ("With arguments " (code (var "name")) " and " (code "#f") ", " (code "record-reader") " removes any association for " (code (var "name")) " to a record-type descriptor. Similarly, with arguments " (code (var "rtd")) " and " (code "#f") ", " (code "record-reader") "  removes any association for " (code (var "rtd")) " to a name.") ((code "(define-record marble (color quality))" (br) "(define m (make-marble 'blue 'perfect))" (br) "m " (math "=>") " #[#{marble bdhavk1bwafxyss1-c} blue perfect]" (br) (br) "(record-reader (type-descriptor marble)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(record-reader 'marble) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(record-reader 'marble (type-descriptor marble))" (br) "(marble-color '#[marble red miserable]) " (math "=>") " red" (br) (br) "(record-reader (type-descriptor marble)) " (math "=>") " marble" (br) "(record-reader 'marble) " (math "=>") " #<record type marble>" (br) (br) "(record-reader (type-descriptor marble) #f)" (br) "(record-reader (type-descriptor marble)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(record-reader 'marble) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(record-reader 'marble (type-descriptor marble))" (br) "(record-reader 'marble #f)" (br) "(record-reader (type-descriptor marble)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(record-reader 'marble) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("The introduction of a record reader also changes the default printing of records. The printer always chooses the reader name first assigned to the record, if any, in place of the unique record name, as this continuation of the example above demonstrates.") ((code "(record-reader 'marble (type-descriptor marble))" (br) "(make-marble 'pink 'splendid) " (math "=>") " #[marble pink splendid]"))) ("objects:s225" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-writer " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the record writer associated with " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-writer " (var "rtd") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor, and " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept three arguments, as described below.") ("When passed only one argument, " (code "record-writer") " returns the record writer associated with " (code (var "rtd")) ", which is initially the default record writer for all records. The default print method prints all records in a uniform syntax that includes the generated name for the record  and the values of each of the fields, as described in the introduction to this section.") ("When passed two arguments, " (code "record-writer") " establishes a new association between " (code (var "rtd")) " and " (code (var "procedure")) " so that " (code (var "procedure")) " will be used by the printer in place of the default printer for records of the given type. The printer passes " (code (var "procedure")) " three arguments: the record " (code (var "r")) ", a port " (code (var "p")) ", and a procedure " (code (var "wr")) " that should be used to write out the values of arbitrary Scheme objects that the print method chooses to include in the printed representation of the record, e.g., values of the record's fields.") ((code "(define-record marble (color quality))" (br) "(define m (make-marble 'blue 'medium))" (br) (br) "m " (math "=>") " #[#{marble bdhavk1bwafxyss1-d} blue medium]" (br) (br) "(record-writer (type-descriptor marble)" (br) "  (lambda (r p wr)" (br) "    (display \"#<\" p)" (br) "    (wr (marble-quality r) p)" (br) "    (display \" quality \" p)" (br) "    (wr (marble-color r) p)" (br) "    (display \" marble>\" p)))" (br) (br) "m " (math "=>") " #<medium quality blue marble>")) ("The record writer is used only when " (code "print-record") " is true (the default). When the parameter " (code "print-record") " is set to " (code "#f") ", records are printed using a compressed syntax that identifies only the type of record.") ((code "(parameterize ([print-record #f])" (br) "  (format \"~s\" m)) " (math "=>") " \"#<record of type marble>\"")) ("A print method may be called more than once during the printing of a single record to support cycle detection and graph printing (see " (code "print-graph") "), so print methods that perform side effects other than printing to the given port are discouraged. Whenever a print method is called more than once during the printing of a single record, in all but one call, a generic \"bit sink\" port is used to suppress output automatically so that only one copy of the object appears on the actual port. In order to avoid confusing the cycle detection and graph printing algorithms, a print method should always produce the same printed representation for each object. Furthermore, a print method should normally use the supplied procedure " (code (var "wr")) " to print subobjects, though atomic values, such as strings or numbers, may be printed by direct calls to " (code "display") " or " (code "write") " or by other means.") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (define-record ref () ((contents 'nothing)))" (br) "  (record-writer (type-descriptor ref)" (br) "    (lambda (r p wr)" (br) "      (display \"<\" p)" (br) "      (wr (ref-contents r) p)" (br) "      (display \">\" p)))" (br) "  (let ([ref-lexive (make-ref)])" (br) "    (set-ref-contents! ref-lexive ref-lexive)" (br) "    ref-lexive)) " (math "=>") " #0=<#0#>")) ("Print methods need not be concerned with handling nonfalse values of the parameters " (code "print-level") ". The printer handles " (code "print-level") " automatically even when user-defined print procedures are used. Since records typically contain a small, fixed number of fields, it is usually possible to ignore nonfalse values of " (code "print-length") " as well.") ((code "(print-level 3)" (br) "(let ()" (br) "  (define-record ref () ((contents 'nothing)))" (br) "  (record-writer (type-descriptor ref)" (br) "    (lambda (r p wr)" (br) "      (display \"<\" p)" (br) "      (wr (ref-contents r) p)" (br) "      (display \">\" p)))" (br) "  (let ([ref-lexive (make-ref)])" (br) "    (set-ref-contents! ref-lexive ref-lexive)" (br) "    ref-lexive)) " (math "=>") " <<<<#[...]>>>>"))) ("objects:s229" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "print-record") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls the printing of records. If set to true (the default) the record writer associated with a record type is used to print records of that type. If set to false, all records are printed with the syntax " (code "#<record of type " (var "name") ">") ", where " (code (var "name")) " is the name of the record type as returned by " (code "record-type-name") ".")) ("objects:s230" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-record-type " (var "type-name") " " (var "fields") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-record-type " (var "parent-rtd") " " (var "type-name") " " (var "fields") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a record-type descriptor for a new record type" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "make-record-type") " creates a new data type and returns a record-type descriptor, a value representing the new data type. The new type is disjoint from all others.") ("If present, " (code (var "parent-rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ((code (var "type-name")) " must be a string or gensym. If " (code (var "type-name")) " is a string, a new record type is generated. If " (code (var "type-name")) " is a gensym, a new record type is generated only if one with the same gensym has not already been defined. If one has already been defined, the parent and fields must be identical to those of the existing record type, and the existing record type is used. If the parent and fields are not identical, an exception is raised with condition-type " (code "&assertion") ".") ((code (var "fields")) " must be a list of field descriptors, each of which describes one field of instances of the new record type. A field descriptor is either a symbol or a list in the following form:") ((code "(" (var "class") " " (var "type") " " (var "field-name") ")")) ("where " (code (var "class")) " and " (code (var "type")) " are optional. " (code (var "field-name")) " must be a symbol. " (code (var "class")) ", if present, must be the symbol " (code "immutable") " or the symbol " (code "mutable") ". If the " (code "immutable") " class-specifier is present, the field is immutable; otherwise, the field is mutable. " (code (var "type")) ", if present, specifies how the field is represented. The types are the same as those given in the description of " (code "define-record") " on page " (link "./objects.html#record-field-types" "200") ".") ("If a type is specified, the field can contain objects only of the specified type. If no type is specified, the field is of type " (code "ptr") ", meaning that it can contain any Scheme object.") ("The behavior of a program that modifies the string " (code (var "type-name")) "  or the list " (code (var "fields")) " or any of its sublists is unspecified.") ("The record-type descriptor may be passed as an argument to any of the Revised" (sup "6") " Report procedures   " (ul (li (code "record-constructor") ",") (li (code "record-predicate") ",") (li (code "record-accessor") ", and") (li (code "record-mutator") ","))) ("or to the " (var "Chez Scheme") " variants   " (ul (li (code "record-constructor") ",") (li (code "record-field-accessor") ", and") (li (code "record-field-mutator")))) ("to obtain procedures for creating and manipulating records of the new type.") ((code "(define marble" (br) "  (make-record-type \"marble\"" (br) "    '(color quality)" (br) "    (lambda (r p wr)" (br) "      (display \"#<\" p)" (br) "      (wr (marble-quality r) p)" (br) "      (display \" quality \" p)" (br) "      (wr (marble-color r) p)" (br) "      (display \" marble>\" p))))" (br) "(define make-marble" (br) "  (record-constructor marble))" (br) "(define marble?" (br) "  (record-predicate marble))" (br) "(define marble-color" (br) "  (record-field-accessor marble 'color))" (br) "(define marble-quality" (br) "  (record-field-accessor marble 'quality))" (br) "(define set-marble-quality!" (br) "  (record-field-mutator marble 'quality))" (br) "(define x (make-marble 'blue 'high))" (br) "(marble? x) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(marble-quality x) " (math "=>") " high" (br) "(set-marble-quality! x 'low)" (br) "(marble-quality x) " (math "=>") " low" (br) "x " (math "=>") " #<low quality blue marble>")) ("The order in which the fields appear in " (code (var "fields")) " is important. While field names are generally distinct, it is permissible for one field name to be the same as another in the list of fields or the same as an inherited name. In this case, field ordinals must be used to select fields in calls to " (code "record-field-accessor") "  and " (code "record-field-mutator") ". Ordinals range from zero through one less than the number of fields. Parent fields come first, if any, followed by the fields in " (code (var "fields")) ", in the order given.") ((code "(define r1 (make-record-type \"r1\" '(t t)))" (br) "(define r2 (make-record-type r1 \"r2\" '(t)))" (br) "(define r3 (make-record-type r2 \"r3\" '(t t t)))" (br) (br) "(define x ((record-constructor r3) 'a 'b 'c 'd 'e 'f))" (br) "((record-field-accessor r3 0) x) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "((record-field-accessor r3 2) x) " (math "=>") " c" (br) "((record-field-accessor r3 4) x) " (math "=>") " e" (br) "((record-field-accessor r3 't) x) " (math "=>") " " (var "unspecified")))) ("objects:s233" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-constructor " (var "rcd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-constructor " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a constructor for records of the type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Like the Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this procedure, this procedure may be passed a record-constructor descriptor, " (code (var "rcd")) ", which determines the behavior of the constructor. It may also be passed a record-type descriptor, " (code (var "rtd")) ", in which case the constructor accepts as many arguments as there are fields in the record; these arguments are the initial values of the fields in the order given when the record-type descriptor was created.")) ("objects:s234" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-field-accessor " (var "rtd") " " (var "field-id") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an accessor for the identified field" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor, " (code (var "field-id")) " must be a symbol or field ordinal, i.e., a nonnegative exact integer less than the number of fields of the given record type. The specified field must be accessible.") ("The generated accessor expects one argument, which must be a record of the type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) ". It returns the contents of the specified field of the record.")) ("objects:s235" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-field-accessible? " (var "rtd") " " (var "field-id") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the specified field is accessible, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor, " (code (var "field-id")) " must be a symbol or field ordinal, i.e., a nonnegative exact integer less than the number of fields of the given record type.") ("The compiler is free to eliminate a record field if it can prove that the field is not accessed. In making this determination, the compiler is free to ignore the possibility that an accessor might be created from a record-type descriptor obtained by calling " (code "record-type-descriptor") " on an instance of the record type.")) ("objects:s236" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-field-mutator " (var "rtd") " " (var "field-id") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a mutator for the identified field" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor, " (code (var "field-id")) " must be a symbol or field ordinal, i.e., a nonnegative exact integer less than the number of fields of the given record type. The specified field must be mutable.") ("The mutator expects two arguments, " (code (var "r")) " and " (code (var "obj")) ". " (code (var "r")) " must be a record of the type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) ". " (code (var "obj")) " must be a value that is compatible with the type declared for the specified field when the record-type descriptor was created. " (code (var "obj")) " is stored in the specified field of the record.")) ("objects:s237" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-field-mutable? " (var "rtd") " " (var "field-id") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the specified field is mutable, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor, " (code (var "field-id")) " must be a symbol or field ordinal, i.e., a nonnegative exact integer less than the number of fields of the given record type.") ("Any field declared immutable is immutable. In addition, the compiler is free to treat a field as immutable if it can prove that the field is never assigned. In making this determination, the compiler is free to ignore the possibility that a mutator might be created from a record-type descriptor obtained by calling " (code "record-type-descriptor") " on an instance of the record type.")) ("objects:s238" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-name " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the name of the record-type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ("The name is a always a string. If a gensym is provided as the record-type name in a " (code "define-record") " form or " (code "make-record-type") " call, the result is the \"pretty\" name of the gensym (see " (link "./objects.html#g61" "7.11") ").") ((code "(record-type-name (make-record-type \"empty\" '())) " (math "=>") " \"empty\"" (br) (br) "(define-record #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b} (x y))" (br) "(define p (type-descriptor #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b}))" (br) "(record-type-name p) " (math "=>") " \"point\""))) ("objects:s239" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-symbol " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the generated symbol associated with " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ((code "(define e (make-record-type \"empty\" '()))" (br) "(record-type-symbol e) " (math "=>") " #{empty bdhavk1bwafxyss1-e}" (br) (br) "(define-record #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b} (x y))" (br) "(define p (type-descriptor #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b}))" (br) "(record-type-symbol p) " (math "=>") " #{point bdhavk1bwafxyss1-b}"))) ("objects:s240" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-field-names " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of field names of the type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor. The field names are symbols.") ("See also the Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this prodecure (exported from the " (code "(chezscheme)") " library) which returns a vector.") ((code "(define-record triple ((immutable x1) (mutable x2) (immutable x3)))" (br) "(record-type-field-names (type-descriptor triple)) " (math "=>") " (x1 x2 x3)"))) ("objects:s241" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-field-decls " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of field declarations of the type represented by " (code (var "rtd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor. Each field declaration has the following form:") ((code "(" (var "class") " " (var "type") " " (var "field-name") ")")) ("where " (code (var "class")) ", " (code (var "type")) ", and " (code (var "field-name")) " are as described under " (code "make-record-type") ".") ((code "(define-record shape (x y))" (br) "(define-record circle shape (radius))" (br) (br) "(record-type-field-decls" (br) "  (type-descriptor circle)) " (math "=>") " ((mutable ptr x)" (br) "                            " (math "  ") "  (mutable ptr y)" (br) "                            " (math "  ") "  (mutable ptr radius))"))) ("objects:s242" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a record, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record? " (var "obj") " " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a record of the given type, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If present, " (code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ("A record is \"of the given type\" if it is an instance of the record type or one of its ancestors. The predicate generated by " (code "record-predicate") " for a record-type descriptor " (code (var "rtd")) " is equivalent to the following.") ((code "(lambda (x) (record? x " (var "rtd") "))"))) ("objects:s243" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-instance? " (var "obj") " " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a record of the given type, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "obj")) " must be a record, and " (code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.") ("The result is the same as for a two-argument " (code "record?") " call, but " (code (var "obj")) " is constrained to be a record. In unsafe mode, " (code "record-instance?") " might be faster than " (code "record?") ".")) ("objects:s244" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-descriptor " (var "rec") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the record-type descriptor of " (code (var "rec")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme csv7)")) ((code (var "rec")) " must be a record. This procedure is intended for use in the definition of portable printers and debuggers. For records created with " (code "make-record-type") ", it may not be the same as the descriptor returned by " (code "make-record-type") ". See the comments about field accessibility and mutability under " (code "record-field-accessible?") " and " (code "record-field-mutable?") " above.") ("This procedure is equivalent to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "record-rtd") "  procedure.") ((code "(define rtd (make-record-type \"frob\" '(blit blat)))" (br) "rtd " (math "=>") " #<record type frob>" (br) "(define x ((record-constructor rtd) 1 2))" (br) "(record-type-descriptor x) " (math "=>") " #<record type frob>" (br) "(eq? (record-type-descriptor x) rtd) " (math "=>") " " (var "unspecified")))) ("objects:s245" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-record-type-descriptor " (var "name") " " (var "parent") " " (var "uid") " " (var "s?") " " (var "o?") " " (var "fields") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a record-type descriptor for a new record type" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Like the Revised" (sup "6") " Report version of this procedure, but " (code (var "fields")) "  is also allowed to be a pair of non-negative integers, in which case the new record type has anonymous fields. The " (code "car") " of " (code (var "fields")) "  is a field count, and it is added to any fields present in " (code (var "parent")) " to determine the record type's total number of fields. The " (code "cdr") " of " (code (var "fields")) " must be an exact non-negative integer that is treated as a bit array; a " (code "1") " bit indicates the the corresponding field among new fields is mutable. The total number of fields in a record type must be a fixnum.") ("If " (code (var "parent")) " is a record type descriptor, it must also have anonymous fields. The resulting anonymous-field record type can only be the parent of a record type with anonymous fields.") ("When a function like " (code "record-type-field-names") " is applied to an anonymous-field record type, the field names are all reported as " (code "field") ".")) ("objects:s246" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(record-type-has-named-fields? " (var "rtd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a boolean indicating whether " (code (var "rtd")) " has named fields" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "rtd")) " must be a record-type descriptor.")) ("objects:s247" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(procedure-arity-mask " (var "proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an exact integer bitmask identifying the accepted argument counts of " (code (var "proc")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The bitmask is represented as two's complement number with the bit at each index " (code (var "n")) " set if and only if " (code (var "proc")) " accepts " (code (var "n")) "  arguments.") ("The two's complement encoding implies that if " (code (var "proc")) " accepts " (code (var "n")) " or more arguments, the encoding is a negative number, since all the bits from " (code (var "n")) " and up are set. For example, if " (code (var "proc")) " accepts any number of arguments, the two's complement encoding of all bits set is " (code "-1") ".") ((code "(procedure-arity-mask (lambda () 'none)) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask car) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask (case-lambda [() 'none] [(x) x])) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask (lambda x x)) " (math "=>") " -1" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask (case-lambda [() 'none] [(x y . z) x])) " (math "=>") " -3" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask (case-lambda)) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(logbit? 1 (procedure-arity-mask pair?)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(logbit? 2 (procedure-arity-mask pair?)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(logbit? 2 (procedure-arity-mask cons)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("objects:s248" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-wrapper-procedure " (var "proc") " " (var "arity-mask") " " (var "data") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-arity-wrapper-procedure " (var "proc") " " (var "arity-mask") " " (var "data") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure that behaves like " (code (var "proc")) ", but with the given " (code (var "arity-mask")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "proc")) " must be a procedure, and " (code (var "arity-mask")) " must be an exact integer representing an arity mask in the sense of " (code "procedure-arity-mask") ".") ("The resulting procedure behaves the same as " (code (var "proc")) ", except that " (code "procedure-arity-mask") " on the result procedure returns " (code (var "arity-mask")) ". When the result of " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") "  is called, the given arguments are compared to the given arity mask, and an error is reported if the argument count does not match. The result of " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") ", in contrast, does not enforce the given arity mask.") ("The " (code (var "data")) " argument can be any value, and it can be retrieved from the result procedure with " (code "wrapper-procedure-data") ".") ((code "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 #f))" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask vector) ; => -1" (br) "(procedure-arity-mask vector3) ; => 8" (br) "(vector3 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " #(1 2 3)" (br) "(vector3 1 2) " (math "=>") " #(1 2)" (br) "(define vector3/check (make-arity-wrapper-procedure vector 8 #f))" (br) "(vector3/check 1 2 3) " (math "=>") "  #(1 2 3)" (br) "(vector3/check 1 2) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("objects:s249" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(wrapper-procedure? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a wrapper procedure, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Determines whether " (code (var "obj")) " is a wrapper procedure produced by either " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") " or " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") ".") ((code "(wrapper-procedure? vector) ; => #f" (br) "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 #f))" (br) "(wrapper-procedure? vector3) ; => #t"))) ("objects:s250" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(wrapper-procedure-procedure " (var "w-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the procedure wrapped by the wrapper procedure " (code (var "proc")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "w-proc")) " must be a wrapper procedure produced by either " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") " or " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") ".") ((code "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 'my-data))" (br) "(wrapper-procedure-procedure vector3) ; => #<procedure vector>"))) ("objects:s251" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(wrapper-procedure-data " (var "w-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the data stored with the wrapper procedure " (code (var "proc")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "w-proc")) " must be a wrapper procedure produced by either " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") " or " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") ".") ((code "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 'my-data))" (br) "(wrapper-procedure-data vector3) ; => 'my-data"))) ("objects:s252" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-wrapper-procedure-data! " (var "w-proc") " " (var "data") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "w-proc")) " must be a wrapper procedure produced by either " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") " or " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") ".") ("Changes the data stored in " (code (var "w-proc")) " to " (code (var "data")) ".") ((code "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 'my-data))" (br) "(wrapper-procedure-data vector3) ; => 'my-data" (br) "(set-wrapper-procedure-data! vector3 'my-new-data)" (br) "(wrapper-procedure-data vector3) ; => 'my-new-data"))) ("objects:s253" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-wrapper-procedure-procedure! " (var "w-proc") " " (var "proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "w-proc")) " must be a wrapper procedure produced by either " (code "make-wrapper-procedure") " or " (code "make-arity-wrapper-procedure") ", and " (code (var "proc")) " must be a procedure.") ("Changes " (code (var "w-proc")) " so that it behaves the same as " (code (var "proc")) ", leaving the results of " (br) " " (code "(procedure-arity-mask " (var "w-proc") ")") " and " (code "(wrapper-procedure-data " (var "w-proc") ")") " unchanged.") ((code "(define vector3 (make-wrapper-procedure vector 8 'my-data))" (br) "(vector3 1 2 3) ; => #(1 2 3)" (br) "(set-wrapper-procedure-procedure! vector3 list)" (br) "(vector3 1 2 3) ; => (1 2 3)"))) ("objects:s254" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(procedure-known-single-valued? " (var "proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a boolean indicating whether " (code (var "proc")) " is known to always produce a single value" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Returns an approximate classification of " (code (var "proc")) " as always having a single result value or not. The result may be " (code "#f") " for a procedure that always returns a single value, but with an implementation that was too complex for the compiler to prove that fact. The result is " (code "#t") " only for a procedure that always produces a single result value.         ")) ("smgmt:s10" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect " (var "cg") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect " (var "cg") " " (var "max-tg") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect " (var "cg") " " (var "min-tg") " " (var "max-tg") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect " (var "cg") " " (var "min-tg") " " (var "max-tg") " " (var "objs") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list if " (code (var "objs")) " is a list, unspecified otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure causes the storage manager to perform a garbage collection. " (code "collect") " is invoked periodically without arguments by the default collect-request handler, but it may also be called explicitly, e.g., from a custom collect-request handler, between phases of a computation when collection is most likely to be successful, or before timing a computation. In the threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ", the thread that invokes " (code "collect") " must be the only active thread.") ("When called without arguments, the system determines automatically which generations to collect and the target generation for each collected generation as described in the lead-in to this section.") ("When called with arguments, the system collects all and only objects in generations less than or equal to " (code (var "cg")) " (the maximum collected generation) into the target generation or generations determined by " (code (var "min-tg")) " (the minimum target generation) and " (code (var "max-tg")) "  (the maximum target generation). Specifically, the target generation for any object in a collected generation " (code (var "g")) " is " (math "min(max(g+1, min-tg), max-tg)") ", where " (code "static") " is taken to have the value one greater than the maximum nonstatic generation.") ("If present, " (code (var "cg")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum no greater than the maximum nonstatic generation, i.e., the current value of the parameter " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ".") ("If present, " (code (var "max-tg")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum or the symbol " (code "static") " and either equal to " (code (var "cg")) " or one greater than " (code (var "cg")) ", again treating " (code "static") " as having the value one greater than the maximum nonstatic generation. If " (code (var "max-tg")) " is not present (but " (code (var "cg")) " is), it defaults to " (code (var "cg")) " if " (code (var "cg")) " is equal to the maximum target generation and to one more than " (code (var "cg")) " otherwise.") ("If present, " (code (var "min-tg")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum or the symbol " (code "static") " and no greater than " (code (var "max-tg")) ", again treating " (code "static") " as having the value one greater than the maximum nonstatic generation. Unless " (code (var "max-cg")) " is the same as " (code (var "cg")) ", " (code (var "min-tg")) " must also be greater than " (code (var "cg")) ". If " (code (var "min-tg")) " is not present (but " (code (var "cg")) " is), it defaults to the same value as " (code (var "max-tg")) ".") ("If present, " (code (var "objs")) " must be either " (code "#f") " or a list. If " (code (var "objs")) " is a list, the collection is combined with counting as in " (code "compute-size-increments") ". Counting looks through all generations, as when " (code "'static") " is the second argument to " (code "compute-size-increments") ", but the returned sizes from " (code "collect") " do not include any objects in a generation older than " (code (var "g")) ". Another difference is that an object later in " (code (var "objs")) " is treated as unreachable by earlier objects in " (code (var "objs")) " only when the later object is a record, thread, or procedure (including continuations). Finally, if an object is included in " (code (var "objs")) " using a weak pair, then the object's result size is 0 unless it is reachable from earlier objects; if the object is not reachable at all, it can be collected.")) ("smgmt:s11" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collect-rendezvous)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Requests a garbage collection in the same way as when the system determines that a collection should occur. All running threads are coordinated so that one of them calls the collect-request handler, while the other threads pause until the handler returns. If the initial thread is active at the time of the rendezvous, it is used to call the collect-request handler.") ("Note that if the collect-request handler (see " (code "collect-request-handler") ") does not call " (code "collect") ", then " (code "collect-rendezvous") " does not actually perform a garbage collection.")) ("smgmt:s12" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-notify") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code "collect-notify") " is set to a true value, the collector prints a message whenever a collection is run. " (code "collect-notify") " is set to " (code "#f") " by default.")) ("smgmt:s13" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-trip-bytes") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines the approximate amount of storage that is allowed to be allocated between garbage collections. Its value must be a positive fixnum.") ((var "Chez Scheme") " allocates memory internally in large chunks and subdivides these chunks via inline operations for efficiency. The storage manager determines whether to request a collection only once per large chunk allocated. Furthermore, some time may elapse between when a collection is requested by the storage manager and when the collect request is honored, especially if interrupts are temporarily disabled via " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") "  or " (code "disable-interrupts") ". Thus, " (code "collect-trip-bytes") " is an approximate measure only.")) ("smgmt:s16" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-generation-radix") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines how often each generation is collected when " (code "collect") " is invoked without arguments, as by the default collect-request handler. Its value must be a positive fixnum. Generations are collected once every " (var "r") (sup "g") " times a collection occurs, where " (var "r") " is the value of " (code "collect-generation-radix") " and " (var "g") " is the generation number.") ("Setting " (code "collect-generation-radix") " to one forces all generations to be collected each time a collection occurs. Setting " (code "collect-generation-radix") " to a very large number effectively delays collection of older generations indefinitely.")) ("smgmt:s17" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-maximum-generation-threshold-factor") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines how often the maximum generation is collected when " (code "collect") " is invoked without arguments, as by the default collect-request handler. Its value must be a nonnegative real number. The maximum generation is collected only when the total amount of allocated memory before a collection is " (var "k") " times the amount of memory allocated after the most recent collection of the maximum generation, where " (var "k") "  is the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation-threshold-factor") ".") ("Setting " (code "collect-maximum-generation-threshold-factor") " to zero causes every regularly scheduled collection of the maximum generation to take place. Setting the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation-threshold-factor") " to a large number can increase peak memory use by delaying collection of the maximum generation.")) ("smgmt:s18" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-maximum-generation") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines the maximum nonstatic generation, hence the total number of generations, currently in use. Its value is an exact integer in the range 1 through 6. When set to 1, only two nonstatic generations are used; when set to 2, three nonstatic generations are used, and so on. When set to 6, 7 nonstatic generations are used, plus the single static generation for a total of 8 generations. Increasing the number of generations effectively decreases how often old objects are collected, potentially decreasing collection overhead but potentially increasing the number of inaccessible objects retained in the system and thus the total amount of memory required.")) ("smgmt:s19" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "collect-request-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "collect-request-handler") " must be a procedure. The procedure is invoked without arguments whenever the system determines that a collection should occur, i.e., some time after an amount of storage determined by the parameter " (code "collect-trip-bytes") " has been allocated since the last collection.") ("By default, " (code "collect-request-handler") " simply invokes " (code "collect") " without arguments.") ("Automatic collection may be disabled by setting " (code "collect-request-handler") " to a procedure that does nothing, e.g.:") ((code "(collect-request-handler void)")) ("Collection can also be temporarily disabled using " (code "critical-section") ", which prevents any interrupts from being handled.") ("In the threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ", the collect-request handler is invoked by a single thread with all other threads temporarily suspended.")) ("smgmt:s20" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "release-minimum-generation") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter's value must be between 0 and the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ", inclusive, and defaults to the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ".") ("As new data is allocated and collections occur, the storage-management system automatically requests additional virtual memory address space from the operating system. Correspondingly, in the event the heap shrinks significantly, the system attempts to return some of the virtual-memory previously obtained from the operating system back to the operating system. By default, the system attempts to do so only after a collection that targets the maximum nonstatic generation. The system can be asked to do so after collections targeting younger generations as well by altering the value " (code "release-minimum-generation") " to something less than the value of " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ". When the generation to which the parameter is set, or any older generation, is the target generation of a collection, the storage management system attempts to return unneeded virtual memory to the operating system following the collection.") ("When " (code "collect-maximum-generation") " is set to a new value " (code (var "g")) ", " (code "release-minimum-generation") " is implicitly set to " (code (var "g")) " as well if (a) the two parameters have the same value before the change, or (b) " (code "release-minimum-generation") " has a value greater than " (code (var "g")) ".")) ("smgmt:s21" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "in-place-minimum-generation") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines when the storage-management system attempts to trade long-term space usage for the benefit of collection time and short-term space usage. When performing a collection at a generation at least as large as this parameter's value, objects already residing at the generation are kept in place---unless the objects are in a memory region where previously keeping them in place resulted in too much fragmentation.") ("Typically, the value of " (code "in-place-minimum-generation") " should match the value of the " (code "collect-maximum-generation") " parameter, but the value of this parameter can be lower to move objects even less frequently, or it can be higher to disable attempts to keep otherwise mobile objects in place.")) ("smgmt:s22" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "heap-reserve-ratio") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter determines the approximate amount of memory reserved (not returned to the O/S as described in the entry for " (code "release-minimum-generation") ") in proportion to the amount currently occupied, excluding areas of memory that have been made static. Its value must be an inexact nonnegative flonum value; if set to an exact real value, the exact value is converted to an inexact value. The default value, 1.0, reserves one page of memory for each currently occupied nonstatic page. Setting it to a smaller value may result in a smaller average virtual memory footprint, while setting it to a larger value may result in fewer calls into the operating system to request and free memory space.")) ("smgmt:s23" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(keep-live " (var "v") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Ensures that the value produced by " (code (var "v")) " is retained by the store manager until the " (code "keep-live") " call is performed. This function can be particularly useful for ensuring that an immobile object remains in place.")) ("smgmt:s28" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(weak-cons " (var "obj" (sub "1")) " " (var "obj" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new weak pair" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "obj" (sub "1"))) " becomes the car and " (code (var "obj" (sub "2"))) " becomes the cdr of the new pair. Weak pairs are indistinguishable from ordinary pairs in all but two ways:   " (ul (li "weak pairs can be distinguished from pairs using the " (code "weak-pair?") " predicate, and") (li "weak pairs maintain a weak pointer to the object in the car of the pair."))) ("The weak pointer in the car of a weak pair is just like a normal pointer as long as the object to which it points is accessible through a normal (nonweak) pointer somewhere in the system. If at some point the garbage collector recognizes that there are no nonweak pointers to the object, however, it replaces each weak pointer to the object with the \"broken weak-pointer\" object, " (code "#!bwp") ", and discards the object.") ("The cdr field of a weak pair is " (var "not") " a weak pointer, so weak pairs may be used to form lists of weakly held objects. These lists may be manipulated using ordinary list-processing operations such as " (code "length") ", " (code "map") ", and " (code "assv") ". (Procedures like " (code "map") " that produce list structure always produce lists formed from nonweak pairs, however, even when their input lists are formed from weak pairs.) Weak pairs may be altered using " (code "set-car!") " and " (code "set-cdr!") "; after a " (code "set-car!") " the car field contains a weak pointer to the new object in place of the old object. Weak pairs are especially useful for building association pairs in association lists or hash tables.") ("Weak pairs are printed in the same manner as ordinary pairs; there is no reader syntax for weak pairs. As a result, weak pairs become normal pairs when they are written and then read.") ((code "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " (a . b)" (br) (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(set! x '*)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp")) ("The latter example above may in fact return " (code "(a . b)") " if a garbage collection promoting the pair into an older generation occurs prior to the assignment of " (code "x") " to " (code "*") ". It may be necessary to force an older generation collection to allow the object to be reclaimed. The storage management system guarantees only that the object will be reclaimed eventually once all nonweak pointers to it are dropped, but makes no guarantees about when this will occur.")) ("smgmt:s29" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(weak-pair? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if obj is a weak pair, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(weak-pair? (weak-cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(weak-pair? (cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(weak-pair? \"oops\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("smgmt:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ephemeron-cons " (var "obj" (sub "1")) " " (var "obj" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new ephemeron pair" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "obj" (sub "1"))) " becomes the car and " (code (var "obj" (sub "2"))) " becomes the cdr of the new pair. Ephemeron pairs are indistinguishable from ordinary pairs in all but two ways:   " (ul (li "ephemeron pairs can be distinguished from pairs using the " (code "ephemeron-pair?") " predicate, and") (li "ephemeron pairs maintain a weak pointer to the object in the car of the pair, and the cdr of the pair is preserved only as long as the car of the pair is preserved."))) ("An ephemeron pair behaves like a weak pair, but the cdr is treated specially in addition to the car: the cdr of an ephemeron is set to " (code "#!bwp") " at the same time that the car is set to " (code "#!bwp") ". Since the car and cdr fields are set to " (code "#!bwp") " at the same time, then the fact that the car object may be referenced through the cdr object does not by itself imply that car must be preserved (unlike a weak pair); instead, the car must be saved for some reason independent of the cdr object.") ("Like weak pairs and other pairs, ephemeron pairs may be altered using " (code "set-car!") " and " (code "set-cdr!") ", and ephemeron pairs are printed in the same manner as ordinary pairs; there is no reader syntax for ephemeron pairs.") ((code "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (ephemeron-cons x x))" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " (a . b)" (br) "(cdr p) " (math "=>") " (a . b)" (br) (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (ephemeron-cons x x))" (br) "(set! x '*)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp" (br) "(cdr p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp" (br) (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x x)) ; " (var "not an ephemeron pair") (br) "(set! x '*)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " (a . b)" (br) "(cdr p) " (math "=>") " (a . b)")) ("As with weak pairs, the last two expressions of the middle example above may in fact return " (code "(a . b)") " if a garbage collection promoting the pair into an older generation occurs prior to the assignment of " (code "x") " to " (code "*") ". In the last example above, however, the results of the last two expressions will always be " (code "(a . b)") ", because the cdr of a weak pair holds a non-weak reference, and that non-weak reference prevents the car field from becoming " (code "#!bwp") ".")) ("smgmt:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ephemeron-pair? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if obj is a ephemeron pair, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(ephemeron-pair? (ephemeron-cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(ephemeron-pair? (cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(ephemeron-pair? (weak-cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(ephemeron-pair? \"oops\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("smgmt:s32" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bwp-object? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if obj is the broken weak-pair object, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(bwp-object? #!bwp) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(bwp-object? 'bwp) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(set! x '*)" (br) "(collect (collect-maximum-generation))" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp" (br) "(bwp-object? (car p)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("smgmt:s33" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-guardian)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-guardian " (var "ordered?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new guardian that is unordered unless " (code (var "ordered?")) " is true" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Guardians are represented by procedures that encapsulate groups of objects registered for preservation. When a guardian is created, the group of registered objects is empty. An object is registered with a guardian by passing the object as an argument to the guardian:") ((code "(define G (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'aaa 'bbb))" (br) "x " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)" (br) "(G x)")) ("It is also possible to specify a \"representative\" object when registering an object. Continuing the above example:") ((code "(define y (cons 'ccc 'ddd))" (br) "y " (math "=>") " (ccc . ddd)" (br) "(G y 'rep)")) ("The group of registered objects associated with a guardian is logically subdivided into two disjoint subgroups: a subgroup referred to as \"accessible\" objects, and one referred to \"inaccessible\" objects. Inaccessible objects are objects that have been proven to be inaccessible (except through the guardian mechanism itself or through the car field of a weak or ephemeron pair), and accessible objects are objects that have not been proven so. The word \"proven\" is important here: it may be that some objects in the accessible group are indeed inaccessible but that this has not yet been proven. This proof may not be made in some cases until long after the object actually becomes inaccessible (in the current implementation, until a garbage collection of the generation containing the object occurs).") ("Objects registered with a guardian are initially placed in the accessible group and are moved into the inaccessible group at some point after they become inaccessible. Objects in the inaccessible group are retrieved by invoking the guardian without arguments. If there are no objects in the inaccessible group, the guardian returns " (code "#f") ". Continuing the above example:") ((code "(G) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(set! x #f)" (br) "(set! y #f)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)   ; " (var "this might come out second") (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " rep           ; " (var "and this first") (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("The initial call to " (code "G") " returns " (code "#f") ", since the pairs bound to " (code "x") " and " (code "y") " are the only object registered with " (code "G") ", and the pairs are still accessible through those bindings. When " (code "collect") " is called, the objects shift into the inaccessible group. The two calls to " (code "G") " therefore return the pair previously bound to " (code "x") " and the representative of the pair previously bound to " (code "y") ", though perhaps in the other order from the one shown. (As noted above for weak pairs, the call to collect may not actually be sufficient to prove the object inaccessible, if the object has migrated into an older generation.)") ("Although an object registered without a representative and returned from a guardian has been proven otherwise inaccessible (except possibly via the car field of a weak or ephemeron pair), it has not yet been reclaimed by the storage management system and will not be reclaimed until after the last nonweak pointer to it within or outside of the guardian system has been dropped. In fact, objects that have been retrieved from a guardian have no special status in this or in any other regard. This feature circumvents the problems that might otherwise arise with shared or cyclic structure. A shared or cyclic structure consisting of inaccessible objects is preserved in its entirety, and each piece registered for preservation with any unordered guardian is placed in the inaccessible set for that guardian. The programmer then has complete control over the order in which pieces of the structure are processed.") ("An ordered guardian, as created by providing a true value for " (code (var "ordered?")) ", treats an object as inaccessible only when it is not accessible from any representative of an object that is in any usable guardian's inaccessible group and that is distinct from the object itself. Cycles among objects registered with ordered guardians can never become inaccessible unless the cycle is broken or some of the relevant guardians are dropped by the program, and each registered object's representative (if different from the object) can contribute to such cycles. If an object is registered to an ordered guardian with a representative that is different from the object but that references the object, then the object is in a cycle and will not become inaccessible unless the reference from the representative to the object is destroyed. Weak references do not count, so objects that form a cycle only when counting weak references may still become inaccessible.") ("An object may be registered with a guardian more than once, in which case it will be retrievable more than once:") ((code "(define G (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'aaa 'bbb))" (br) "(G x)" (br) "(G x)" (br) "(set! x #f)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)" (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)")) ("It may also be registered with more than one guardian, and guardians themselves can be registered with other guardians. If an object is registered to both an unordered guardian and an ordered guardian and neither guardian is dropped, the object can become inaccessible for the ordered guardian only after it has been determined inaccessible for the unordered guardian and then retrieved and dropped again by the program.") ("An object that has been registered with a guardian without a representative and placed in the car field of a weak or ephemeron pair remains in the car field of the weak or ephemeron pair until after it has been returned from the guardian and dropped by the program or until the guardian itself is dropped.") ((code "(define G (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'aaa 'bbb))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(G x)" (br) "(set! x #f)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(set! y (G))" (br) "y " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " (aaa . bbb)" (br) "(set! y #f)" (br) "(collect 1)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp")) ("(The first collector call above would promote the object at least into generation 1, requiring the second collector call to be a generation 1 collection. This can also be forced by invoking " (code "collect") " several times.)") ("On the other hand, if a representative (other than the object itself) is specified, the guarded object is dropped from the car field of the weak or ephemeron pair at the same time as the representative becomes available from the guardian.") ((code "(define G (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'aaa 'bbb))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(G x 'rep)" (br) "(set! x #f)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(G) " (math "=>") " rep" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp")) ("The following example illustrates that the object is deallocated and the car field of the weak pair set to " (code "#!bwp") " when the guardian itself is dropped:") ((code "(define G (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'aaa 'bbb))" (br) "(define p (weak-cons x '()))" (br) "(G x)" (br) "(set! x #f)" (br) "(set! G #f)" (br) "(collect)" (br) "(car p) " (math "=>") " #!bwp")) ("The example below demonstrates how guardians might be used to deallocate external storage, such as storage managed by the C library \"malloc\" and \"free\" operations.") ((code "(define malloc" (br) "  (let ([malloc-guardian (make-guardian)])" (br) "    (lambda (size)" (br) "      ; first free any storage that has been dropped.  to avoid long" (br) "      ; delays, it might be better to deallocate no more than, say," (br) "      ; ten objects for each one allocated" (br) "      (let f ()" (br) "        (let ([x (malloc-guardian)])" (br) "          (when x" (br) "            (do-free x)" (br) "            (f))))" (br) "      ; then allocate and register the new storage" (br) "      (let ([x (do-malloc size)])" (br) "        (malloc-guardian x)" (br) "        x))))")) ((code "do-malloc") " must return a Scheme object \"header\" encapsulating a pointer to the external storage (perhaps as an unsigned integer), and all access to the external storage must be made through this header. In particular, care must be taken that no pointers to the external storage exist outside of Scheme after the corresponding header has been dropped. " (code "do-free") " must deallocate the external storage using the encapsulated pointer. Both primitives can be defined in terms of " (code "foreign-alloc") "  and " (code "foreign-free") " or the C-library \"malloc\" and \"free\" operators, imported as foreign procedures.  (See Chapter " (link "./foreign.html#g22" "4") ".)") ("If it is undesirable to wait until " (code "malloc") " is called to free dropped storage previously allocated by " (code "malloc") ", a collect-request handler can be used instead to check for and free dropped storage, as shown below.") ((code "(define malloc)" (br) "(let ([malloc-guardian (make-guardian)])" (br) "  (set! malloc" (br) "    (lambda (size)" (br) "      ; allocate and register the new storage" (br) "      (let ([x (do-malloc size)])" (br) "        (malloc-guardian x)" (br) "        x)))" (br) "  (collect-request-handler" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      ; first, invoke the collector" (br) "      (collect)" (br) "      ; then free any storage that has been dropped" (br) "      (let f ()" (br) "        (let ([x (malloc-guardian)])" (br) "          (when x" (br) "            (do-free x)" (br) "            (f)))))))")) ("With a bit of refactoring, it would be possible to register the encapsulated foreign address as a representative with each header, in which " (code "do-free") " would take just the foreign address as an argument. This would allow the header to be dropped from the Scheme heap as soon as it becomes inaccessible.") ("Guardians can also be created via " (code "ftype-guardian") ", which supports reference counting of foreign objects.")) ("smgmt:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(guardian? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if obj is a guardian, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(guardian? (make-guardian)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(guardian? (ftype-guardian iptr)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(guardian? (lambda x x)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(guardian? \"oops\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("smgmt:s36" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unregister-guardian " (var "guardian") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "unregister-guardian") " unregisters the as-yet unresurrected objects currently registered with the guardian, with one caveat.") ("The caveat, which applies only to threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ", is that objects registered with the guardian by other threads since the last garbage collection might not be unregistered. To ensure that all objects are unregistered in a multithreaded application, a single thread can be used both to register and unregister objects. Alternatively, an application can arrange to define a collect-request handler that calls " (code "unregister-guardian") " after it calls " (code "collect") ".") ("In any case, " (code "unregister-guardian") " returns a list containing each object (or its representative, if specified) that it unregisters, with duplicates as appropriate if the same object is registered more than once with the guardian. Objects already resurrected but not yet retrieved from the guardian are not included in the list but remain retrievable from the guardian.") ("In the current implementation, " (code "unregister-guardian") " takes time proportional to the number of unresurrected objects currently registered with all guardians rather than those registered just with the corresponding guardian.") ("The example below assumes no collections occur except for those resulting from explicit calls to " (code "collect") ".") ((code "(define g (make-guardian))" (br) "(define x (cons 'a 'b))" (br) "(define y (cons 'c 'd))" (br) "(g x)" (br) "(g x)" (br) "(g y)" (br) "(g y)" (br) "(set! y #f)" (br) "(collect 0 0)" (br) "(unregister-guardian g) " (math "=>") " ((a . b) (a . b))" (br) "(g) " (math "=>") " (c . d)" (br) "(g) " (math "=>") " (c . d)" (br) "(g) " (math "=>") " #f")) ((code "unregister-guardian") " can also be used to unregister ftype pointers registered with guardians created by " (code "ftype-guardian") "  (Section " (link "./threads.html#g151" "15.6") ").")) ("smgmt:s40" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(lock-object " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Locking an object prevents the storage manager from reclaiming or relocating the object. Locking should be used sparingly, as it introduces memory fragmentation and increases storage management overhead.") ("Locking can also lead to accidental retention of storage if objects are not unlocked. Objects may be unlocked via " (code "unlock-object") " or the equivalent C library procedure " (code "Sunlock_object") ".") ("Locking immediate values, such as fixnums, booleans, and characters, or objects that have been made static is unnecessary but harmless.")) ("smgmt:s42" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(unlock-object " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("An object may be locked more than once by successive calls to " (code "lock-object") ", " (code "Slock_object") ", or both, in which case it must be unlocked by an equal number of calls to " (code "unlock-object") " or " (code "Sunlock_object") " before it is truly unlocked.") ("An object contained within a locked object, such as an object in the car of a locked pair, need not also be locked unless a separate C pointer to the object exists. That is, if the inner object is accessed only via an indirection of the outer object, it should be left unlocked so that the collector is free to relocate it during collection.") ("Unlocking immediate values, such as fixnums, booleans, and characters, or objects that have been made static is unnecessary and ineffective but harmless.")) ("smgmt:s43" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(locked-object? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is locked, immediate, or static" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This predicate returns true if " (code (var "obj")) " cannot be relocated or reclaimed by the collector, including immediate values, such as fixnums, booleans, and characters, and objects that have been made static.")) ("smgmt:s44" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(box-immobile " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a box" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Like " (code "box") ", but creates a box that will not be relocated in memory by the storage management system until it is reclaimed.")) ("smgmt:s45" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-immobile-vector " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-immobile-vector " (var "n") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a vector" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Like " (code "make-vector") ", but creates a vector that will not be relocated in memory by the storage management system until it is reclaimed.")) ("smgmt:s46" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-immobile-bytevector " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-immobile-bytevector " (var "n") " " (var "byte") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a vector" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Like " (code "make-bytevector") ", but creates a bytevector that will not be relocated in memory by the storage management system until it is reclaimed.")) ("smgmt:s48" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-phantom-bytevector " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a phantom bytevector" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "n")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer.") ("Creates a phantom bytevector that reflects " (code (var "n")) " bytes of external allocation.") ("The value " (code (var "n")) " must reflect actual allocation in the sense of consuming a portion of the process's address space. Claiming significantly more bytes than are actually allocated introduces the possibility of overflow within the storage management system's calculations.")) ("smgmt:s49" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(phantom-bytevector? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if obj is a phantom bytevector, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(phantom-bytevector? (make-phantom-bytevector 1024)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("smgmt:s50" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(phantom-bytevector-length " (var "pbv") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the length of the phantom bytevector" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "pbv")) " must be a phantom bytevector.") ("Returns the number of bytes of external allocation that " (code (var "pbv")) "  represents.") ((code "(phantom-bytevector-length (make-phantom-bytevector 1024)) " (math "=>") " 1024"))) ("smgmt:s51" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-phantom-bytevector-length! " (var "pbv") " " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "pbv")) " must be a phantom bytevector, and " (code (var "n")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer.") ("Adjusts the allocation represented by " (code (var "pbv")) " to " (code (var "n")) " bytes. For example, when an externally allocated object represented by " (code (var "pbv")) "  is deallocated, then " (code (var "pbv")) "'s length should be set to " (code "0") ".") ((code "(define pbv (make-phantom-bytevector 1024))" (br) "(phantom-bytevector-length pbv) " (math "=>") " 1024" (br) "(set-phantom-bytevector-length! pbv 1)" (br) "(phantom-bytevector-length pbv) " (math "=>") " 1"))) ("syntax:s0" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(fluid-let-syntax ((" (var "keyword") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see explanation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each " (code (var "expr")) " must evaluate to a transformer. " (code "fluid-let-syntax") " is similar to the standard " (code "let-syntax") ", except that instead of introducing new bindings for the keywords " (code (var "keyword") " ...") ", " (code "fluid-let-syntax") " temporarily alters the existing bindings for the keywords during the expansion of its body. That is, during the expansion of " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") ", the visible lexical (or top-level) binding for each " (code "keyword") " is temporarily replaced by a new association between the keyword and the corresponding transformer. This affects any references to the keyword that resolve to the same lexical (or top-level) binding whether the references occur in the text of the body or are introduced during its expansion. In contrast, " (code "let-syntax") " captures only those references that occur within the text of its body.") ("The following example shows how " (code "fluid-let-syntax") "  differs from " (code "let-syntax") ".") ((code "(let ([f (lambda (x) (+ x 1))])" (br) "  (let-syntax ([g (syntax-rules ()" (br) "                    [(_ x) (f x)])])" (br) "    (let-syntax ([f (syntax-rules ()" (br) "                      [(_ x) x])])" (br) "      (g 1)))) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) (br) "(let ([f (lambda (x) (+ x 1))])" (br) "  (let-syntax ([g (syntax-rules ()" (br) "                    [(_ x) (f x)])])" (br) "    (fluid-let-syntax ([f (syntax-rules ()" (br) "                            [(_ x) x])])" (br) "      (g 1)))) " (math "=>") " 1")) ("The two expressions are identical except that the inner " (code "let-syntax") " form in the first expression is a " (code "fluid-let-syntax") " form in the second. In the first expression, the " (code "f") " occurring in the expansion of " (code "(g 1)") " refers to the " (code "let") "-bound variable " (code "f") ", whereas in the second it refers to the keyword " (code "f") " by virtue of the fluid syntax binding for " (code "f") ".") ("The following code employs " (code "fluid-let-syntax") " in the definition of a " (code "define-integrable") " form that is similar to " (code "define") " for procedure definitions except that it causes the code for the procedure to be " (var "integrated") ", or inserted, wherever a direct call to the procedure is found. No semantic difference is visible between procedures defined with " (code "define-integrable") " and those defined with " (code "define") ", except that a top-level " (code "define-integrable") " form must appear before the first reference to the defined identifier. Lexical scoping is preserved, the actual parameters in an integrated call are evaluated once and at the proper time, integrable procedures may be used as first-class values, and recursive procedures do not cause indefinite recursive expansion.") ((code "(define-syntax define-integrable" (br) "  (syntax-rules (lambda)" (br) "    [(_ name (lambda formals form1 form2 ...))" (br) "     (begin" (br) "       (define xname" (br) "         (fluid-let-syntax ([name (identifier-syntax xname)])" (br) "           (lambda formals form1 form2 ...)))" (br) "       (define-syntax name" (br) "         (lambda (x)" (br) "           (syntax-case x ()" (br) "             [_ (identifier? x) #'xname]" (br) "             [(_ arg (... ...))" (br) "              #'((fluid-let-syntax ([name (identifier-syntax xname)])" (br) "                   (lambda formals form1 form2 ...))" (br) "                  arg" (br) "                  (... ...))]))))]))")) ("A " (code "define-integrable") " has the following form.") ((code "(define-integrable " (var "name") " " (var "lambda-expression") ")")) ("A " (code "define-integrable") " form expands into a pair of definitions: a syntax definition of " (code (var "name")) " and a variable definition of " (code "xname") ". The transformer for " (code (var "name")) " converts apparent calls to " (code (var "name")) " into direct calls to " (code (var "lambda-expression")) ". Since the resulting forms are merely direct " (code "lambda") " applications (the equivalent of " (code "let") " expressions), the actual parameters are evaluated exactly once and before evaluation of the procedure's body, as required. All other references to " (code (var "name")) " are replaced with references to " (code "xname") ". The definition of " (code "xname") " binds it to the value of " (code (var "lambda-expression")) ". This allows the procedure to be used as a first-class value. Because " (code "xname") " is introduced by the transformer, the binding for " (code "xname") " is not visible anywhere except where references to it are introduced by the transformer for " (code (var "name")) ".") ("Within " (code (var "lambda-expression")) ", wherever it appears, " (code (var "name")) "  is rebound to a transformer that expands all references into references to " (code "xname") ". The use of " (code "fluid-let-syntax") "  for this purpose prevents indefinite expansion from indirect recursion among integrable procedures. This allows the procedure to be recursive without causing indefinite expansion. Nothing special is done by " (code "define-integrable") " to maintain lexical scoping, since lexical scoping is maintained automatically by the expander.") ((var "Chez Scheme") " integrates locally defined procedures automatically when it is appropriate to do so. It cannot integrate procedures defined at top-level, however, since code that assigns top-level variables can be introduced into the system (via " (code "eval") " or " (code "load") ") at any time. " (code "define-integrable") " can be used to force the integration of procedures bound at top-level, even if the integration of locally bound procedures is left to the compiler. It can also be used to force the integration of large procedures that the compiler would not normally integrate. (The " (code "expand/optimize") " procedure is useful for determining when integration does or does not take place.)")) ("syntax:s4" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(syntax-rules (" (var "literal") " ...) " (var "clause") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a transformer" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each " (code (var "literal")) " must be an identifier other than an underscore ( " (code "_") " ) or ellipsis ( " (code "...") " ). Each clause must take the form below.") ((code "(" (var "pattern") " " (var "template") ")" (br) "(" (var "pattern") " " (var "fender") " " (var "template") ")")) ("The first form is the only form supported by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report.")) ("syntax:s6" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax->list " (var "syntax-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of syntax objects" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure takes a syntax object representing a list-structured form and returns a list of syntax objects, each representing the corresponding subform of the input form.") ((code "syntax->list") " may be defined as follows.") ((code "(define syntax->list" (br) "  (lambda (ls)" (br) "    (syntax-case ls ()" (br) "      [() '()]" (br) "      [(x . r) (cons #'x (syntax->list #'r))])))" (br) (br) "#'(a b c) " (math "=>") " #<syntax (a b c)>" (br) "(syntax->list #'(a b c)) " (math "=>") " (#<syntax a> #<syntax b> #<syntax c>)")) ((code "syntax->list") " is not required for list structures constructed from individual pattern variable values or sequences of pattern-variable values, since such structures are already lists. For example:") ((code "(list? (with-syntax ([x #'a] [y #'b] [z #'c]) #'(x y z)))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(list? (with-syntax ([(x ...) #'(a b c)]) #'(x ...))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("syntax:s7" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax->vector " (var "syntax-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a vector of syntax objects" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure takes a syntax object representing a vector-structured form and returns a vector of syntax objects, each representing the corresponding subform of the input form.") ((code "syntax->vector") " may be defined as follows.") ((code "(define syntax->vector" (br) "  (lambda (v)" (br) "    (syntax-case v ()" (br) "      [#(x ...) (apply vector (syntax->list #'(x ...)))])))" (br) (br) "#'#(a b c) " (math "=>") " #<syntax #(a b c)>" (br) "(syntax->vector #'#(a b c)) " (math "=>") " #(#<syntax a> #<syntax b> #<syntax c>)")) ((code "syntax->vector") " is not required for vector structures constructed from individual pattern variable values or sequences of pattern-variable values, since such structures are already vectors. For example:") ((code "(vector? (with-syntax ([x #'a] [y #'b] [z #'c]) #'#(x y z)))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(vector? (with-syntax ([(x ...) #'(a b c)]) #'#(x ...))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("syntax:s8" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax-object->datum " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code (var "obj")) " stripped of syntactic information" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "syntax-object->datum") " is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "syntax->datum") ".")) ("syntax:s9" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(datum " (var "template") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(datum " (var "template") ")") " is a convenient shorthand syntax for") ((code "(syntax->datum (syntax " (var "template") "))")) ((code (var "datum")) " may be defined simply as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax datum" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ t) (syntax->datum #'t)]))" (br) (br) "(with-syntax ((a #'(a b c))) (datum a)) " (math "=>") " (a b c)"))) ("syntax:s10" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(datum->syntax-object " (var "template-identifier") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a syntax object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "datum->syntax-object") " is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "datum->syntax") ".")) ("syntax:s11" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(quote-syntax " (var "datum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a syntax object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "quote-syntax") " expression is like a " (code "quote") "  expression, except that contextual information associated with the datum is retained.") ("Unlike " (code "syntax") ", " (code "quote-syntax") " does not substitute pattern variables bound by " (code "with-syntax") " or " (code "syntax-case") ".") ((code "(let-syntax ([m (lambda (stx)" (br) "                  (syntax-case stx ()" (br) "                    [(_ x)" (br) "                     #`(list 'x '#,(quote-syntax x))]))])" (br) "  (m y)) " (math "=>") " (y x)"))) ("syntax:s12" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(with-implicit (" (var "id" (sub "0")) " " (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This form abstracts over the common usage of " (code "datum->syntax") "  for creating implicit identifiers (see above). The form") ((code "(with-implicit (" (var "id" (sub "0")) " " (var "id" (sub "1")) " ...)" (br) "  " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("is equivalent to") ((code "(with-syntax ([" (var "id" (sub "1")) " (datum->syntax #'" (var "id" (sub "0")) " '" (var "id" (sub "1")) ")] ...)" (br) "  " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ((code "with-implicit") " can be defined simply as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax with-implicit" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ (tid id ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "     (with-syntax ([id (datum->syntax #'tid 'id)] ...)" (br) "       b1 b2 ...)]))")) ("We can use " (code "with-implicit") " to simplify the (correct version of) " (code "loop") " above.") ((code "(define-syntax loop" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(k e ...)" (br) "       (with-implicit (k break)" (br) "         #'(call-with-current-continuation" (br) "             (lambda (break)" (br) "               (let f () e ... (f)))))])))"))) ("syntax:s13" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(include " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "include") " expands into a " (code "begin") " expression containing the forms found in the file named by " (code (var "path")) ". For example, if the file " (code "f-def.ss") " contains " (code "(define f (lambda () x))") ", the expression") ((code "(let ([x \"okay\"])" (br) "  (include \"f-def.ss\")" (br) "  (f))")) ("evaluates to " (code "\"okay\"") ". An include form is treated as a definition if it appears within a sequence of definitions and the forms on the file named by " (code (var "path")) " are all definitions, as in the above example. If the file contains expressions instead, the " (code "include") " form is treated as an expression.") ((code "include") " may be defined portably as follows, although " (var "Chez Scheme") " uses an implementation-dependent definition that allows it to capture and maintain source information for included code.") ((code "(define-syntax include" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (define read-file" (br) "      (lambda (fn k)" (br) "        (let ([p (open-input-file fn)])" (br) "          (let f ([x (read p)])" (br) "            (if (eof-object? x)" (br) "                (begin (close-input-port p) '())" (br) "                (cons (datum->syntax k x)" (br) "                      (f (read p))))))))" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(k filename)" (br) "       (let ([fn (datum filename)])" (br) "         (with-syntax ([(exp ...) (read-file fn #'k)])" (br) "           #'(begin exp ...)))])))")) ("The definition of " (code "include") " uses " (code "datum->syntax") " to convert the objects read from the file into syntax objects in the proper lexical context, so that identifier references and definitions within those expressions are scoped where the " (code "include") " form appears.") ("In " (var "Chez Scheme") "'s implementation of " (code "include") ", the parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files not identified by absolute path names.")) ("syntax:s14" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax-error " (var "obj") " " (var "string") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Syntax errors may be reported with " (code "syntax-error") ", which produces a message by concatenating " (code (var "string") " ...") " and a printed representation of " (code (var "obj")) ". If no string arguments are provided, the string " (code "\"invalid syntax\"") "  is used instead. When " (code (var "obj")) " is a syntax object, the syntax-object wrapper is stripped (as with " (code "syntax->datum") ") before the printed representation is created. If source file information is present in the syntax-object wrapper, " (code "syntax-error") " incorporates this information into the error message.") ((code "syntax-case") " and " (code "syntax-rules") " call " (code "syntax-error") "  automatically if the input fails to match one of the clauses.") ("We can use " (code "syntax-error") " to precisely report the cause of the errors detected in the following definition of (unnamed) " (code "let") ".") ((code "(define-syntax let" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (define check-ids!" (br) "      (lambda (ls)" (br) "        (unless (null? ls)" (br) "          (unless (identifier? (car ls))" (br) "            (syntax-error (car ls) \"let cannot bind non-identifier\"))" (br) "          (check-ids! (cdr ls)))))" (br) "    (define check-unique!" (br) "      (lambda (ls)" (br) "        (unless (null? ls)" (br) "          (let ([x (car ls)])" (br) "            (when (let mem? ([ls (cdr ls)])" (br) "                    (and (not (null? ls))" (br) "                         (or (bound-identifier=? x (car ls))" (br) "                             (mem? (cdr ls)))))" (br) "              (syntax-error x \"let cannot bind two occurrences of\")))" (br) "          (check-unique! (cdr ls)))))" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(_ ((i e) ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (begin" (br) "         (check-ids! #'(i ...))" (br) "         (check-unique! #'(i ...))" (br) "         #'((lambda (i ...) b1 b2 ...) e ...))])))")) ("With this change, the expression") ((code "(let ([a 3] [a 4]) (+ a a))")) ("produces the error message \"let cannot bind two occurrences of " (code "a") ".\"")) ("syntax:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(literal-identifier=? " (var "identifier" (sub "1")) " " (var "identifier" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure is identical to the Revised" (sup "6") " Report " (code "free-identifier=?") ", and is provided for backward compatibility only.")) ("syntax:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-compile-time-value " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a compile-time value" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A compile time value is a kind of transformer with which a keyword may be associated by any of the keyword binding constructs, e.g., " (code "define-syntax") "  or " (code "let-syntax") ". The transformer encapsulates the supplied " (code (var "obj")) ". The encapsulated object may be retrieved as described above.") ("The following example illustrates how this feature might be used to define a simple syntactic record-definition mechanism where the record type descriptor is generated at expansion time.") ((code "(define-syntax drt" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (define construct-name" (br) "      (lambda (template-identifier . args)" (br) "        (datum->syntax template-identifier" (br) "          (string->symbol" (br) "            (apply string-append" (br) "              (map (lambda (x)" (br) "                     (if (string? x)" (br) "                         x" (br) "                         (symbol->string (syntax->datum x))))" (br) "                   args))))))" (br) "    (define do-drt" (br) "      (lambda (rname fname* prtd)" (br) "        (with-syntax ([rname rname]" (br) "                      [rtd (make-record-type-descriptor" (br) "                             (syntax->datum rname) prtd #f #f #f" (br) "                             (list->vector" (br) "                               (map (lambda (fname)" (br) "                                      `(immutable ,(syntax->datum fname)))" (br) "                                    fname*)))]" (br) "                      [make-rname (construct-name rname \"make-\" rname)]" (br) "                      [rname? (construct-name rname rname \"?\")]" (br) "                      [(rname-fname ...)" (br) "                       (map (lambda (fname)" (br) "                              (construct-name fname rname \"-\" fname))" (br) "                            fname*)]" (br) "                      [(i ...) (enumerate fname*)])" (br) "          #'(begin" (br) "              (define-syntax rname (make-compile-time-value 'rtd))" (br) "              (define rcd (make-record-constructor-descriptor 'rtd #f #f))" (br) "              (define make-rname (record-constructor rcd))" (br) "              (define rname? (record-predicate 'rtd))" (br) "              (define rname-fname (record-accessor 'rtd i))" (br) "              ...))))" (br) "    (syntax-case x (parent)" (br) "      [(_ rname (fname ...))" (br) "       (for-all identifier? #'(rname fname ...))" (br) "       (do-drt #'rname #'(fname ...) #f)]" (br) "      [(_ rname pname (fname ...))" (br) "       (for-all identifier? #'(rname pname fname ...))" (br) "       (lambda (lookup)" (br) "         (let ([prtd (lookup #'pname)])" (br) "           (unless (record-type-descriptor? prtd)" (br) "             (syntax-error #'pname \"unrecognized parent record type\"))" (br) "           (do-drt #'rname #'(fname ...) prtd)))])))")) ((code "(drt prec (x y))" (br) "(drt crec prec (z))" (br) "(define r (make-crec 1 2 3))" (br) "(prec? r) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(prec-x r) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(crec-z r) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "prec " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid syntax prec")))) ("syntax:s17" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-time-value? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a compile-time value; " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(define-syntax x (make-compile-time-value \"eggs\"))" (br) "(compile-time-value? (top-level-syntax 'x)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("syntax:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-time-value-value " (var "ctv") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value of a compile-time value" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(define-syntax x (make-compile-time-value \"eggs\"))" (br) "(compile-time-value-value (top-level-syntax 'x)) " (math "=>") " \"eggs\""))) ("syntax:s19" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-property " (var "id") " " (var "key") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "define-property") " form attaches a property to an existing identifier binding without disturbing the existing meaning of the identifier in the scope of that binding. It is typically used by one macro to record information about a binding for use by another macro. Both " (code (var "id")) " and " (code (var "key")) " must be identifiers. The expression " (code (var "expr")) " is evaluated when the " (code "define-property") "  form is expanded, and a new property associating " (code (var "key")) " with the value of " (code (var "expr")) " is attached to the existing binding of " (code (var "id")) ", which must have a visible local or top-level binding.") ((code "define-property") " is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear. The scope of a property introduced by " (code "define-property") " is the entire body in which the " (code "define-property") " form appears or global if it appears at top level, except where it is replaced by a property for the same " (code (var "id")) " and " (code (var "key")) " or where the binding to which it is attached is shadowed. Any number of properties can be attached to the same binding with different keys. Attaching a new property with the same name as an property already attached to a binding shadows the existing property with the new property.") ("The following example defines a macro, " (code "get-info") ", that retrieves the " (code "info") " property of a binding, defines the variable " (code "x") ", attaches an " (code "info") " property to the binding of " (code "x") ", retrieves the property via " (code "get-info") ", references " (code "x") " to show that its normal binding is still intact, and uses " (code "get-info") " again within the scope of a different binding of " (code "x") " to show that the properties are shadowed as well as the outer binding of " (code "x") ".") ((code "(define info)" (br) "(define-syntax get-info" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (lambda (lookup)" (br) "      (syntax-case x ()" (br) "        [(_ q)" (br) "         (let ([info-value (lookup #'q #'info)])" (br) "           #`'#,(datum->syntax #'* info-value))]))))" (br) "(define x \"x-value\")" (br) "(define-property x info \"x-info\")" (br) "(get-info x) " (math "=>") " \"x-info\"" (br) "x " (math "=>") " \"x-value\"" (br) "(let ([x \"inner-x-value\"]) (get-info x)) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("For debugging, it is often useful to have a form that retrieves an arbitrary property, given an identifier and a key. The " (code "get-property") " macro below does just that.") ((code "(define-syntax get-property" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (lambda (r)" (br) "      (syntax-case x ()" (br) "        [(_ id key)" (br) "         #`'#,(datum->syntax #'* (r #'id #'key))]))))" (br) "(get-property x info) " (math "=>") " \"x-info\"")) ("The bindings for both identifiers must be visible where " (code "get-property") " is used.") ("The version of " (code "drt") " defined below is like the one defined using " (code "make-compile-time-value") " above, except that it defines the record name as a macro that raises an exception with a more descriptive message, while attaching the record type descriptor to the binding as a separate property. The variable " (code "drt-key") " defined along with " (code "drt") " is used only as the key for the property that " (code "drt") " attaches to a record name. Both " (code "drt-key") " and " (code "drt") " are defined within a module that exports only the latter, ensuring that the properties used by " (code "drt") "  cannot be accessed or forged.") ((code "(library (drt) (export drt) (import (chezscheme))" (br) "  (define drt-key)" (br) "  (define-syntax drt" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (define construct-name" (br) "        (lambda (template-identifier . args)" (br) "          (datum->syntax template-identifier" (br) "            (string->symbol" (br) "              (apply string-append" (br) "                (map (lambda (x)" (br) "                       (if (string? x)" (br) "                           x" (br) "                           (symbol->string (syntax->datum x))))" (br) "                     args))))))" (br) "      (define do-drt" (br) "        (lambda (rname fname* prtd)" (br) "          (with-syntax ([rname rname]" (br) "                        [rtd (make-record-type-descriptor" (br) "                               (syntax->datum rname) prtd #f #f #f" (br) "                               (list->vector" (br) "                                 (map (lambda (fname)" (br) "                                        `(immutable ,(syntax->datum fname)))" (br) "                                      fname*)))]" (br) "                        [make-rname (construct-name rname \"make-\" rname)]" (br) "                        [rname? (construct-name rname rname \"?\")]" (br) "                        [(rname-fname ...)" (br) "                         (map (lambda (fname)" (br) "                                (construct-name fname rname \"-\" fname))" (br) "                              fname*)]" (br) "                        [(i ...) (enumerate fname*)])" (br) "            #'(begin" (br) "                (define-syntax rname" (br) "                  (lambda (x)" (br) "                    (syntax-error x \"invalid use of record name\")))" (br) "                (define rcd (make-record-constructor-descriptor 'rtd #f #f))" (br) "                (define-property rname drt-key 'rtd)" (br) "                (define make-rname (record-constructor rcd))" (br) "                (define rname? (record-predicate 'rtd))" (br) "                (define rname-fname (record-accessor 'rtd i))" (br) "                ...))))" (br) "      (syntax-case x (parent)" (br) "        [(_ rname (fname ...))" (br) "         (for-all identifier? #'(rname fname ...))" (br) "         (do-drt #'rname #'(fname ...) #f)]" (br) "        [(_ rname pname (fname ...))" (br) "         (for-all identifier? #'(rname pname fname ...))" (br) "         (lambda (lookup)" (br) "           (let ([prtd (lookup #'pname #'drt-key)])" (br) "             (unless prtd" (br) "               (syntax-error #'pname \"unrecognized parent record type\"))" (br) "             (do-drt #'rname #'(fname ...) prtd)))]))))")) ((code "(import (drt))" (br) "(drt prec (x y))" (br) "(drt crec prec (z))" (br) "(define r (make-crec 1 2 3))" (br) "(prec? r) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(prec-x r) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(crec-z r) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "prec " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid use of record name prec")))) ("syntax:s22" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(module " (var "name") " " (var "interface") " " (var "defn") " ... " (var "init") " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(module " (var "interface") " " (var "defn") " ... " (var "init") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "name")) " is an identifier, " (code (var "defn") " ...") "  are definitions, and " (code (var "init") " ...") " are expressions. " (code (var "interface")) " is a list of exports " (code "(" (var "export") " ...)") ", where each " (code (var "export")) " is either an identifier " (code (var "identifier")) "  or of the form " (code "(" (var "identifier") " " (var "export") " ...)") ".") ("The first syntax for " (code "module") " establishes a named scope that encapsulates a set of identifier bindings. The exported bindings may be made visible via " (code "import") " or " (code "import-only") " (Section " (link "./libraries.html#g99" "10.4") ") anywhere the module name is visible. The second syntax for " (code "module") " introduces an anonymous module whose bindings are implicitly imported (as if by " (code "import") " of a hidden module name) where the module form appears.") ("A module consists of a (possibly empty) set of definitions and a (possibly empty) sequence of initialization expressions. The identifiers defined within a module are visible within the body of the module and, if exported, within the scope of an import for the module. Each identifier listed in a module's interface must be defined within or imported into that module. A " (code "module") " form is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear, including at the top level of a program, nested within the bodies of " (code "lambda") " expressions, nested within " (code "library") " and top-level program forms, and nested within other modules. Also, because module names are scoped like other identifiers, modules and libraries may export module names as well as variables and keywords.") ("When an interface contains an export of the form " (code "(" (var "identifier") " " (var "export") " ...)") ", only " (code (var "identifier")) " is visible in the importing context. The identifiers within " (code (var "export") " ...") " are " (var "indirect imports") ", as if declared via an " (code "indirect-export") " form (Section " (link "./libraries.html#g99" "10.4") ").") ("Module names occupy the same namespace as other identifiers and follow the same scoping rules. Unless exported, identifiers defined within a module are visible only within that module.") ("Expressions within a module can reference identifiers bound outside of the module.") ((code "(let ([x 3])" (br) "  (module m (plusx)" (br) "    (define plusx (lambda (y) (+ x y))))" (br) "  (import m)" (br) "  (let ([x 4])" (br) "    (plusx 5))) " (math "=>") " 8")) ("Similarly, " (code "import") " does not prevent access to identifiers that are visible where the import form appears, except for those variables shadowed by the imported identifiers.") ((code "(module m (y) (define y 'm-y))" (br) "(let ([x 'local-x] [y 'local-y])" (br) "  (import m)" (br) "  (list x y)) " (math "=>") " (local-x m-y)")) ("On the other hand, use of " (code "import-only") " within a module establishes an isolated scope in which the only visible identifiers are those exported by the imported module.") ((code "(module m (y) (define y 'm-y))" (br) "(let ([x 'local-x] [y 'local-y])" (br) "  (import-only m)" (br) "  x) " (math "=>") " Error: x is not visible")) ("This is sometimes desirable for static verification that no identifiers are used except those explicitly imported into a module or local scope.") ("Unless a module imported via " (code "import-only") " exports " (code "import") " or " (code "import-only") " and the name of at least one module, subsequent imports within the scope of the " (code "import-only") " form are not possible. To create an isolated scope containing the exports of more than one module without making " (code "import") " or " (code "import-only") "  visible, all of the modules to be imported must be listed in the same " (code "import-only") " form.") ("Another solution is to create a single module that contains the exports of each of the other modules.") ((code "(module m2 (y) (define y 'y))" (br) "(module m1 (x) (define x 'x))" (br) "(module mega-module (cons x y)" (br) "  (import m1)" (br) "  (import m2)" (br) "  (import scheme))" (br) "(let ([y 3])" (br) "  (import-only mega-module)" (br) "  (cons x y)) " (math "=>") " (x . y)")) ("Before it is compiled, a source program is translated into a core language program containing no syntactic abstractions, syntactic definitions, library definitions, module definitions, or import forms. Translation is performed by a " (var "syntax expander") " that processes the forms in the source program via recursive descent.") ("A " (code "define-syntax") " form associates a keyword with a transformer in a translation-time environment. When the expander encounters a keyword, it invokes the associated transformer and reprocesses the resulting form. A " (code "module") " form associates a module name with an interface. When the expander encounters an " (code "import") " form, it extracts the corresponding module interface from the translation-time environment and makes the exported bindings visible in the scope where the " (code "import") " form appears.") ("Internal definitions and definitions within a " (code "module") "  body are processed from left to right so that a module's definition and import may appear within the same sequence of definitions. Expressions appearing within a body and the right-hand sides of variable definitions, however, are translated only after the entire set of definitions has been processed, allowing full mutual recursion among variable and syntactic definitions.") ("Module and import forms affect only the visibility of identifiers in the source program, not their meanings. In particular, variables are bound to locations whether defined within or outside of a module, and " (code "import") " does not introduce new locations. Local variables are renamed as necessary to preserve the scoping relationships established by both modules and syntactic abstractions. Thus, the expression:") ((code "(let ([x 1])" (br) "  (module m (x setter)" (br) "    (define-syntax x (identifier-syntax z))" (br) "    (define setter (lambda (x) (set! z x)))" (br) "    (define z 5))" (br) "  (let ([y x] [z 0])" (br) "    (import m)" (br) "    (setter 3)" (br) "    (+ x y z))) " (math "=>") " 4")) ("is equivalent to the following program in which identifiers have been consistently renamed as indicated by subscripts.") ((code "(let ([x" (var (sub "0")) " 1])" (br) "  (define-syntax x" (var (sub "1")) " (identifier-syntax z" (var (sub "1")) "))" (br) "  (define setter" (var (sub "1")) " (lambda (x" (var (sub "2")) ") (set! z" (var (sub "1")) " x" (var (sub "2")) ")))" (br) "  (define z" (var (sub "1")) " 5)" (br) "  (let ([y" (var (sub "3")) " x" (var (sub "0")) "] [z" (var (sub "3")) " 0])" (br) "    (setter" (var (sub "1")) " 3)" (br) "    (+ x" (var (sub "1")) " y" (var (sub "3")) " z" (var (sub "3")) ")))")) ("Definitions within a top-level " (code "begin") ", " (code "lambda") ", top-level program, " (code "library") ", or " (code "module") " body are processed from left to right by the expander at expand time, and the variable definitions are evaluated from left-to-right at run time. Initialization expressions appearing within a " (code "module") " body are evaluated in sequence after the evaluation of the variable definitions.") ("Mutually recursive modules can be defined in several ways. In the following program, " (code "a") " and " (code "b") " are mutually recursive modules exported by an anonymous module whose local scope is used to statically link the two. For example, the free variable " (code "y") " within module " (code "a") " refers to the binding for " (code "y") ", provided by importing " (code "b") ", in the enclosing module.") ((code "(module (a b)" (br) "  (module a (x) (define x (lambda () y)))" (br) "  (module b (y) (define y (lambda () x)))" (br) "  (import a)" (br) "  (import b))")) ("The following syntactic abstraction generalizes this pattern to permit the definition of multiple mutually recursive modules.") ((code "(define-syntax rec-modules" (br) "  (syntax-rules (module)" (br) "    [(_ (module m (id ...) form ...) ...)" (br) "     (module (m ...)" (br) "       (module m (id ...) form ...) ..." (br) "       (import m) ...)]))")) ("Because a module can re-export imported bindings, it is quite easy to provide multiple views on a single module, as " (code "s") " and " (code "t") " provide for " (code "r") "  below, or to combine several modules into a compound, as " (code "r") " does.") ((code "(module p (x y)" (br) "  (define x 1) (define y 2))" (br) "(module q (y z)" (br) "  (define y 3) (define z 4))" (br) "(module r (a b c d)" (br) "  (import* p (a x) (b y))" (br) "  (import* q (c y) (d z)))" (br) "(module s (a c) (import r))" (br) "(module t (b d) (import r))")) ("To allow interfaces to be separated from implementations, the following syntactic abstractions support the definition and use of named interfaces.") ((code "(define-syntax define-interface" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ name (export ...))" (br) "     (define-syntax name" (br) "       (lambda (x)" (br) "         (syntax-case x ()" (br) "           [(_ n defs)" (br) "            (with-implicit (n export ...)" (br) "              #'(module n (export ...) ." (br) "                  defs))])))]))" (br) (br) "(define-syntax define-module" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ name interface defn ...)" (br) "     (interface name (defn ...))]))")) ((code "define-interface") " creates an interface macro that, given a module name and a list of definitions, expands into a module definition with a concrete interface.") ((code "with-implicit") " is used to ensure that the introduced " (code "export") " identifiers are visible in the same scope as the name of the module in the " (code "define-module") " form.") ((code "define-interface") " and " (code "define-module") " can be used as follows.") ((code "(define-interface simple (a b))" (br) "(define-module m simple" (br) "  (define-syntax a (identifier-syntax 1))" (br) "  (define b (lambda () c))" (br) "  (define c 2))" (br) "(let () (import m) (+ a (b))) " (math "=>") " 3")) ("The abstract module facility defined below allows a module interface to be satisfied incrementally when module forms are evaluated. This permits flexibility in the separation between the interface and implementation, supports separate compilation of mutually recursive modules, and permits redefinition of module implementations.") ((code "(define-syntax abstract-module" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ name (ex ...) (kwd ...) defn ...)" (br) "     (module name (ex ... kwd ...)" (br) "       (declare ex) ..." (br) "       defn ...)]))" (br) (br) "(define-syntax implement" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ name form ...)" (br) "     (module () (import name) form ...)]))")) ("Within an " (code "abstract-module") " form, each of the exports in the list " (code (var "ex") " ...") " must be variables. The values of these variables are supplied by one or more separate " (code "implement") " forms. Since keyword bindings must be present at compile time, they cannot be satisfied incrementally and are instead listed as separate exports and defined within the abstract module.") ("Within an " (code "implement") " form, the sequence of forms " (code (var "form") " ...") " is a sequence of zero or more definitions followed by a sequence of zero or more expressions. Since the module used in the expansion of " (code "implement") " does not export anything, the definitions are all local to the " (code "implement") " form. The expressions may be arbitrary expressions, but should include one " (code "satisfy") " form for each variable whose definition is supplied by the " (code "implement") " form. A " (code "satisfy") " form has the syntax") ((code "(satisfy " (var "variable") " " (var "expr") ")")) ((code "declare") " and " (code "satisfy") " may simply be the equivalents of " (code "define") " and " (code "set!") ".") ((code "(define-syntax declare (identifier-syntax define))" (br) "(define-syntax satisfy (identifier-syntax set!))")) ("Alternatively, " (code "declare") " can initialize the declared variable to the value of a flag known only to " (code "declare") " and " (code "satisfy") ", and " (code "satisfy") " can verify that this flag is still present to insure that only one attempt to satisfy the value of a given identifier is made.") ((code "(module ((declare cookie) (satisfy cookie))" (br) "  (define cookie \"chocolate chip\")" (br) "  (define-syntax declare" (br) "    (syntax-rules () [(_ var) (define var cookie)]))" (br) "  (define-syntax satisfy" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ var exp)" (br) "       (if (eq? var cookie)" (br) "           (set! var exp)" (br) "           (assertion-violationf 'satisfy" (br) "             \"value of variable ~s has already been satisfied\"" (br) "             'var))])))")) ("Using " (code "abstract-module") " and " (code "implement") ", we can define mutually recursive and separately compilable modules as follows.") ((code "(abstract-module e (even?) (pred)" (br) "  (define-syntax pred" (br) "    (syntax-rules () [(_ exp) (- exp 1)])))" (br) (br) "(abstract-module o (odd?) ())" (br) (br) "(implement e" (br) "  (import o)" (br) "  (satisfy even?" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (or (zero? x) (odd? (pred x))))))" (br) (br) "(implement o" (br) "  (import e)" (br) "  (satisfy odd?" (br) "    (lambda (x) (not (even? x)))))" (br) (br) "(let () (import-only e) (even? 38)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("syntax:s23" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "only") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "except") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "add-prefix") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "drop-prefix") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "rename") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "alias") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These identifiers are auxiliary keywords for " (code "import") "  and " (code "import-only") ". It is a syntax violation to reference these identifiers except in contexts where they are recognized as auxiliary keywords.")) ("syntax:s29" ((bold "module") ": " (code "scheme") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "scheme") " contains all user-visible top-level bindings (variables, keywords, and module names) built into " (var "Chez Scheme") ".")) ("syntax:s30" ((bold "module") ": " (code "r5rs") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "r5rs") " contains all top-level bindings (variables and keywords) defined in the Revised" (sup "5") " Report on Scheme. The bindings exported from " (code "r5rs") " are precisely those that are available within an expression evaluated via " (code "eval") " with the environment specifier returned by " (code "scheme-report-environment") ".")) ("syntax:s32" ((bold "module") ": " (code "r5rs-syntax") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "r5rs-syntax") " contains all top-level keyword bindings defined in the Revised" (sup "5") " Report on Scheme. The bindings exported from " (code "r5rs-syntax") " are precisely those that are available within an expression evaluated via " (code "eval") " with the environment specifier returned by " (code "null-environment") ".")) ("syntax:s34" ((bold "module") ": " (code "ieee") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "ieee") " contains all top-level bindings (variables and keywords) defined in the ANSI/IEEE standard for Scheme. The bindings exported from " (code "ieee") " are precisely those that are available within an expression evaluated via " (code "eval") " with the environment specifier returned by " (code "ieee-environment") ".")) ("syntax:s36" ((bold "module") ": " (code "$system") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "$system") " contains all user-visible top-level bindings built into " (var "Chez Scheme") " along with various undocumented system bindings.")) ("syntax:s37" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(meta . " (var "definition") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "meta") " keyword is actually a prefix that can be placed in front of any definition keyword, e.g.,") ((code "(meta define x 3)")) ("It tells the expander that any variable definition resulting from the definition is to be an expand-time definition available only to the right-hand sides of other meta definitions and, most importantly, transformer expressions. It is used to define expand-time helpers and other information for use by one or more " (code "syntax-case") " transformers.") ((code "(module M (helper1 a b)" (br) "  (meta define helper1" (br) "    (lambda (---)" (br) "      ---))" (br) "  (meta define helper2" (br) "    (lambda (---)" (br) "      --- (helper2 ---) ---))" (br) "  (define-syntax a" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      --- (helper1 ---) ---))" (br) "  (define-syntax b" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      --- (helper1 ---) ---" (br) "      --- (helper2 ---) ---)))")) ("The right-hand-side expressions of a syntax definition or meta definition can refer only to identifiers whose values are already available in the compile-time environment. Because of the left-to-right expansion order for " (code "library") ", " (code "module") ", " (code "lambda") ", and similar bodies, this implies a semantics similar to " (code "let*") " for a sequence of meta definitions, in which each right-hand side can refer only to the variables defined earlier in the sequence. An exception is that the right-hand side of a meta definition can refer to its own name as long as the reference is not evaluated until after the value of the expression has been computed. This permits meta definitions to be self-recursive but not mutually recursive. The right-hand side of a meta definition can, however, build syntax objects containing occurrences of any identifiers defined in the body in which the meta definition appears.") ("Meta definitions propagate through macro expansion, so one can write, for example:") ((code "(module (a)" (br) "  (meta define-record foo (x))" (br) "  (define-syntax a" (br) "    (let ([q (make-foo #''q)])" (br) "      (lambda (x) (foo-x q)))))" (br) "a " (math "=>") " q")) ("where define-record is a macro that expands into a set of defines.") ("It is also sometimes convenient to write") ((code "(meta begin defn ...)")) ("or") ((code "(meta module {exports} defn ...)")) ("or") ((code "(meta include \"" (var "path") "\")")) ("to create groups of meta bindings.")) ("syntax:s38" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(meta-cond " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each " (code (var "clause")) " but the last must take the form:") ((code "(" (var "test") " " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("The last may take the same form or be an " (code "else") " clause of the form:") ((code "(" (var "else") " " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("During expansion, the " (code (var "test")) " expressions are evaluated in order until one evaluates to a true value or until all of the tests have been evaluated. If a " (code (var "test")) " evaluates to a true value, the " (code "meta-cond") " form expands to a " (code "begin") " form containing the corresponding expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") ". If no " (code (var "test")) " evaluates to a true value and an " (code "else") " clause is present, the " (code "meta-cond") " form expands to a " (code "begin") " form containing the expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") " from the " (code "else") " clause. Otherwise the " (code "meta-cond") " expression expands into a call to the " (code "void") " procedure.") ((code "meta-cond") " might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax meta-cond" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ [a0 a1 a2 ...] [b0 b1 b2 ...] ...)" (br) "     (let-syntax ([expr (cond" (br) "                          [a0 (identifier-syntax (begin a1 a2 ...))]" (br) "                          [b0 (identifier-syntax (begin b1 b2 ...))]" (br) "                          ...)])" (br) "       expr)]))")) ((code "meta-cond") " is used to choose, at expansion time, from among a set of possible forms. For example, one might have safe (error-checking) and unsafe (non-error-checking) versions of a procedure and decide which to call based on the compile-time optimization level, as shown below.") ((code "(meta-cond" (br) "  [(= (optimize-level) 3) (unsafe-frob x)]" (br) "  [else (safe-frob x)])"))) ("syntax:s39" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(alias " (var "id" (sub "1")) " " (var "id" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "alias") " is a definition and can appear anywhere other definitions can appear. It is used to transfer the binding from one identifier to another.") ((code "(let ([x 3]) (alias y x) (set! y 4) (list x y)) " (math "=>") " (4 4)" (br) (br) "(module lisp (if)" (br) "  (module (scheme:if)" (br) "    (import scheme)" (br) "    (alias scheme:if if))" (br) "  (define-syntax if" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_ e_1 e_2 e_3)" (br) "       (scheme:if (not (memq e_1 '(#f ()))) e_2 e_3)])))" (br) "(define (length ls)" (br) "  (import lisp)" (br) "  (if ls (+ (length (cdr ls)) 1) 0))" (br) "(length '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " 3")) ("Because of left-to-right expansion order, aliases should appear after the definition of the right-hand-side identifier, e.g.:") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (import-only (chezscheme))" (br) "  (define y 3)" (br) "  (alias x y)" (br) "  x) " (math "=>") " 3")) ("rather than:") ((code "(let ()" (br) "  (import-only (chezscheme))" (br) "  (alias x y)" (br) "  (define y 3)" (br) "  x) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: unbound identifier")))) ("syntax:s50" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-annotation " (var "obj") " " (var "source-object") " " (var "stripped-obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-annotation " (var "obj") " " (var "source-object") " " (var "stripped-obj") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an annotation" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The annotation is formed with " (code (var "obj")) " as its expression component, " (code (var "source-object")) " as its source-object component, and " (code (var "stripped-obj")) "  as its stripped component. " (code (var "obj")) " should represent an expression, possibly with embedded annotations. " (code (var "stripped-obj")) " should be a stripped version of " (code (var "obj")) ", i.e., equivalent to " (code (var "obj")) " with each annotation replaced by its expression component. " (code (var "options")) ", if present must be an enumeration set over the symbols " (code "debug") " and " (code "profile") ", and defaults to an enumeration set containing both " (code "debug") " and " (code "profile") ". Annotations marked " (code "debug") " are used for compile-time error reporting and run-time error reporting and inspection; annotations marked " (code "profile") " are used for profiling.")) ("syntax:s51" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(annotation? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is an annotation, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s52" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(annotation-expression " (var "annotation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the expression component of " (code (var "annotation")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s53" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(annotation-source " (var "annotation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the source-object component of " (code (var "annotation")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s54" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(annotation-stripped " (var "annotation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the stripped component of " (code (var "annotation")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s55" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(annotation-option-set " (var "annotation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the options enumeration set of " (code (var "annotation")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s56" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-object " (var "sfd") " " (var "bfp") " " (var "efp") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-object " (var "sfd") " " (var "bfp") " " (var "efp") " " (var "line") " " (var "column") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a source object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. " (code (var "bfp")) " and " (code (var "efp")) " must be exact nonnegative integers, and " (code (var "bfp")) "  should not be greater than " (code (var "efp")) ". " (code (var "line")) " and " (code (var "column")) " must be exact positive integers.")) ("syntax:s57" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a source object, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s58" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object-sfd " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the sfd component of " (code (var "source-object")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s59" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object-bfp " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the bfp component of " (code (var "source-object")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s60" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object-efp " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the efp component of " (code (var "source-object")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s61" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object-line " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the line component of " (code (var "source-object")) " if present, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s62" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-object-column " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the column component of " (code (var "source-object")) " if present, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s63" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-make-source-object") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-make-source-object") " is used by the reader to construct a source object for an annotation. " (code "current-make-source-object") "  is initially bound to " (code "make-source-object") ", and the reader always calls the function bound to the parameter with three arguments.") ("Adjust this parameter to, for example, eagerly convert a position integer to a file-position object, instead of delaying the conversion to " (code "locate-source") ".")) ("syntax:s64" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-file-descriptor " (var "obj") " " (var "binary-input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-file-descriptor " (var "obj") " " (var "binary-input-port") " " (var "reset?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a source-file descriptor" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("To compute the checksum encapsulated in the source-file descriptor, this procedure must read all of the data from " (code (var "binary-input-port")) ". If " (code (var "reset?")) " is present and " (code "#t") ", the port is reset to its original position, as if via " (code "port-position") ". Otherwise, it is left pointing at end-of-file.")) ("syntax:s65" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-file-descriptor? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a source-file descriptor, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s66" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-file-descriptor-checksum " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the checksum component of " (code (var "sfd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s67" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-file-descriptor-path " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the path component of " (code (var "sfd")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. The result is typically a string, but a source file descriptor can have any value representing a path.")) ("syntax:s68" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-file-descriptor " (var "path") " " (var "checksum") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new source-file-descriptor" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "checksum")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer.  " (code (var "path")) " is usually a string, but may be any object. This procedure can be used to construct custom source-file descriptors or to reconstitute source-file descriptors from the " (code (var "path")) " and " (code (var "checksum")) " components.")) ("syntax:s69" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(annotation-options " (var "symbol") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an annotation-options enumeration set" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Annotation-options enumeration sets may be passed to " (code "make-annotation") " to control whether the annotation is used for debugging, profiling, both, or neither. Accordingly, each " (code (var "symbol")) " must be either " (code "debug") " or " (code "profile") ".")) ("syntax:s70" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax->annotation " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an annotation or " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "obj")) " is an annotation or syntax-object encapsulating an annotation, the annotation is returned.")) ("syntax:s71" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-datum/annotations " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "bfp") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. " (code (var "bfp")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer and should be the character position of the next character to be read from " (code (var "textual-input-port")) ".") ("This procedure returns two values: an annotated object and an ending file position. In most cases, " (code (var "bfp")) " should be 0 for the first call to " (code "get-datum/annotation") " at the start of a file, and it should be the second return value of the preceding call to " (code "get-datum/annotation") " for each subsequent call. This protocol is necessary to handle files containing multiple-byte characters, since file positions do not necessarily correspond to character positions.")) ("syntax:s72" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(open-source-file " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a port or " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. This procedure attempts to locate and open the source file identified by " (code (var "sfd")) ". It returns a textual input port, positioned at the beginning of the file, if successful, and " (code "#f") " otherwise. It can fail even if a file with the correct name exists in one of the source directories when the file's checksum does not match the checksum recorded in " (code (var "sfd")) ".")) ("syntax:s73" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(locate-source " (var "sfd") " " (var "pos") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(locate-source " (var "sfd") " " (var "pos") " " (var "use-cache?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor, and " (code (var "pos")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer.") ("This procedure either uses cached information from a previous request for " (code (var "sfd")) " (only when " (code (var "use-cache?")) " is provided as true) or attempts to locate and open the source file identified by " (code (var "sfd")) " (which can only work when its path is a string). If successful, it returns three values: a string " (code (var "path")) ", an exact nonnegative integer " (code (var "line")) ", and an exact nonnegative integer " (code (var "char")) "  representing the absolute pathname, line, and character position within the line represented by the specified source-file descriptor and file position. If unsuccessful, it returns zero values. It can fail even if a file with the correct name exists in one of the source directories when the file's checksum does not match the checksum recorded in " (code (var "sfd")) ".")) ("syntax:s74" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(locate-source-object-source " (var "source-object") " " (var "get-start?") " " (var "use-cache?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure is similar to " (code "locate-source") ", but instead of taking an sfd and a position, it takes a source object plus a request for either the start or end location.") ("If " (code (var "get-start?")) " is true and " (code (var "source-object")) " has a line and column, this procedure returns the path in " (code (var "source-objects")) "'s sfd, " (code (var "source-object")) "'s line, and " (code (var "source-objects")) "'s column.") ("Otherwise, this procedure calls " (code "locate-source") " on " (code (var "source-object")) "'s sfd, either " (code (var "source-object")) "'s bfp or efp depending on " (code (var "get-start?")) ", and " (code (var "use-cache?")) ".")) ("syntax:s75" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-locate-source-object-source") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-locate-source-object-source") " determines the source-location lookup function that is used by the system to report errors based on source objects. This parameter is initially bound to " (code "locate-source-object-object") ".") ("Adjust this parameter to control the way that source locations are extracted from source objects, possibly using recorded information, caches, and the filesystem in a way different from " (code "locate-source-object-object") ".")) ("syntax:s82" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-table)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a source table" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A source table contains associations between source objects and arbitrary values.  For purposes of the source-table operations described below, two source objects are the same if they have the same source-file descriptor, equal beginning file positions and equal ending file positions. Two source-file descriptors are the same if they have the same path and checksum.")) ("syntax:s83" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a source-table; " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s84" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-set! " (var "source-table") " " (var "source-object") " " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "source-table-set!") " associates " (code (var "source-object")) "  with " (code (var "obj")) " in " (code (var "source-table")) ", replacing the existing association, if any.")) ("syntax:s85" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-ref " (var "source-table") " " (var "source-object") " " (var "default") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "default")) " may be any Scheme value.") ((code "source-table-ref") " returns the value associated with " (code (var "source-object")) " in " (code (var "source-table")) ". If no value is associated with " (code (var "source-object")) " in " (code (var "source-table")) ", " (code "source-table-ref") " returns " (code (var "default")) ".")) ("syntax:s86" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-contains? " (var "source-table") " " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if an association for " (code (var "source-object")) " exists in " (code (var "source-table")) ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s87" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-cell " (var "source-table") " " (var "source-object") " " (var "default") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a pair (see below)" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "default")) " may be any Scheme value.") ("If no value is associated with " (code (var "source-object")) " in " (code (var "source-table")) ", " (code "source-table-cell") " modifies " (code (var "source-table")) " to associate " (code (var "source-object")) " with " (code (var "default")) ". Regardless, it returns a pair whose car is " (code (var "source-object")) " and whose cdr is the associated value. Changing the cdr of this pair effectively updates the table to associate " (code (var "source-object")) " with a new value. The car field of the pair should not be modified.")) ("syntax:s88" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-delete! " (var "source-table") " " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "source-table-delete!") " drops the association for " (code (var "source-object")) " from " (code (var "source-table")) ", if one exists.")) ("syntax:s89" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-size " (var "source-table") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of entries in " (code (var "source-table")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("syntax:s90" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-source-table " (var "textual-output-port") " " (var "source-table") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure writes a representation of the information stored in " (code (var "source-table")) " to the port.")) ("syntax:s91" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-source-table! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "source-table") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-source-table! " (var "textual-input-port") " " (var "source-table") " " (var "combine") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The port must be positioned at a representation of source-table information written by some previous call to " (code "put-source-table") ", which reads the information and merges it into " (code "source-table") ".") ("If present and non-false, " (code (var "combine")) " must be a procedure and should accept two arguments. It is called whenever associations for the same source object are present both in " (code (var "source-table")) " and in the information read from the port. In this case, " (code (var "combine")) " is passed two arguments: the associated value from " (code (var "source-table")) " and the associated value from the port (in that order) and must return one value, which is recorded as the new associated value for the source object in " (code (var "source-table")) ".") ("If " (code (var "combine")) " is not present, " (code (var "combine")) " is " (code "#f") ", or no association for a source object read from the port already exists in " (code (var "source-table")) ", the value read from the port is recorded as the associated value of the source object in " (code (var "source-table")) ".") ((code "(define st (make-source-table))" (br) "(call-with-port (open-input-file \"profile.out1\")" (br) "  (lambda (ip) (get-source-table! ip st)))" (br) "(call-with-port (open-input-file \"profile.out2\")" (br) "  (lambda (ip) (get-source-table! ip st +)))"))) ("system:s1" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(warning " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "warning") " raises a continuable exception with condition type " (code "&warning") " and should be used to describe situations for which the " (code "&warning") " condition type is appropriate, typically a situation that should not prevent the program from continuing but might result in a more serious problem at some later point.") ("The continuation object with which the exception is raised also includes a " (code "&who") " condition whose who field is " (code (var "who")) " if " (code (var "who")) " is not " (code "#f") ", a " (code "&message") " condition whose message field is " (code (var "msg")) ", and an " (code "&irritants") " condition whose irritants field is " (code "(" (var "irritant") " ...)") ".") ((code (var "who")) " must be a string, a symbol, or " (code "#f") " identifying the procedure or syntactic form reporting the warning. It is usually best to identify a procedure the programmer has called rather than some other procedure the programmer may not be aware is involved in carrying out the operation. " (code (var "msg")) " must be a string and should describe the exceptional situation. The irritants may be any Scheme objects and should include values that may have caused or been materially involved in the exceptional situation.")) ("system:s2" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(assertion-violationf " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(errorf " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(warningf " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are like " (code "assertion-violation") ", " (code "error") ", and " (code "warning") " except that " (code (var "msg")) " is assumed to be a format string, as if in a call to " (code "format") " (Section " (link "./io.html#g90" "9.13") "), with " (code (var "irritant") " ...") " treated as the additional arguments to " (code "format") ". This allows programs to control the appearance of the error message, at least when the default exception handler is in place.") ("For each of these procedures, the continuation object with which the exception is raised includes a " (code "&format") " condition to signify that the string contained in the condition object's " (code "&message") " condition is a " (code "format") " string and the objects contained in the condition object's " (code "&irritants") " condition should be treated as the additional " (code "format") " arguments.")) ("system:s4" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "&format") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-format-condition)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a condition of type " (code "&format") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(format-condition? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition of type " (code "&format") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Presence of this condition type within a compound condition indicates that the string provided by the " (code "&message") " condition, if present, is a " (code "format") " string and the list of objects provided by the " (code "&irritants") " condition, if present, should be treated as additional " (code "format") " arguments. This condition type might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define-condition-type &format &condition" (br) "  make-format-condition format-condition?)"))) ("system:s5" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "&source") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-source-condition " (var "form") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a condition of type " (code "&source") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-condition? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition of type " (code "&source") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-condition-form " (var "condition") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the contents of " (code (var "condition")) "'s " (code "form") " field" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This condition type can be included within a compound condition when a source expression can be identified in situations in which a " (code "&syntax") " condition would be inappropriate, such as when a run-time assertion violation is detected. The " (code "form") " argument should be an s-expression or syntax object representing the source expression. This condition type might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define-condition-type &source &condition" (br) "  make-source-condition source-condition?" (br) "  (form source-condition-form))"))) ("system:s6" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "&continuation") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-continuation-condition " (var "continuation") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a condition of type " (code "&continuation") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(continuation-condition? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition of type " (code "&continuation") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-continuation " (var "condition") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the contents of " (code (var "condition")) "'s " (code "continuation") " field" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This condition type can be included within a compound condition to indicate the current continuation at the point where the exception described by the condition occurred. The continuation of a failed " (code "assert") " or a call to " (code "assertion-violation") ", " (code "assertion-violationf") ", " (code "error") ", " (code "errorf") ", or " (code "syntax-error") " is now included via this condition type in the conditions passed to " (code "raise") ". The " (code "continuation") " argument should be a continuation. This condition type might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define-condition-type &continuation &condition" (br) "  make-continuation-condition continuation-condition?" (br) "  (continuation condition-continuation))"))) ("system:s7" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-condition " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-condition " (var "obj") " " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port. This procedure displays a message to the effect that an exception has occurred with value " (code (var "obj")) ". If " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition (Chapter " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./exceptions.html#g147" "11") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "), it displays information encapsulated within the condition, handling messages, " (code (var "who")) " conditions, irritants, source information, etc., as appropriate.")) ("system:s8" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(default-exception-handler " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure is the default value of the " (code "base-exception-handler") "  parameter called on a condition when no other exception handler has been defined or when all dynamically established exception handlers have chosen not to handle the condition. It first displays " (code (var "obj")) ", as if with " (code "display-condition") ", to the console error port. For non-serious warning conditions, it returns immediately after displaying the condition.") ("For serious or other non-warning conditions, it saves the condition in the parameter " (code "debug-condition") ", where " (code "debug") " (Section " (link "./debug.html#g17" "3.2") ") can retrieve it and allow it to be inspected. If the " (code "debug-on-exception") " parameter is set to " (code "#f") " (the default unless the " (code "--debug-on-exception") " command-line option is provided), the handler prints a message instructing the user to type " (code "(debug)") " to enter the debugger, then resets to the current café. Otherwise, the handler invokes " (code "debug") " directly and resets if " (code "debug") " returns.") ("If an I/O exception occurs while attempting to display the condition, the default exception handler resets (as if by calling " (code "reset") "). The intent is to avoid an infinite regression (ultimately ending in exhaustion of memory) in which the process repeatedly recurs back to the default exception handler trying to write to a console-error port (typically stderr) that is no longer writable, e.g., due to the other end of a pipe or socket having been closed.")) ("system:s10" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "debug-on-exception") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter determines whether the default exception handler immediately enters the debugger immediately when it receives a serious or non-warning condition. If the " (code "--debug-on-exception") "  command-line option (Section " (link "./use.html#g6" "2.1") ") has been provided, the initial value of this parameter is " (code "#t") ". Otherwise, the initial value is " (code "#f") ".")) ("system:s12" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "base-exception-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure, and the procedure should accept one argument. The default value of " (code "base-exception-handler") " is the procedure " (code "default-exception-handler") ".") ("The value of this parameter is invoked whenever no exception handler established by a program has chosen to handle an exception.")) ("system:s13" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "debug-condition") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter is used by the default exception handler to hold the last serious or non-warning condition received by the handler, where it can be inspected via the " (code "debug") " procedure (Section " (link "./debug.html#g17" "3.2") "). It can also be invoked by user code to store or retrieve a condition.")) ("system:s14" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-exception-state") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-exception-state") " may be used to get or set the current exception state. When called without arguments, " (code "current-exception-state") " returns an " (var "exception state") " comprising the current stack of handlers established by " (code "with-exception-handler") " and " (code "guard") ". When called with a single argument, which must be an exception state, " (code "current-exception-state") " sets the exception state. When a new thread is created, it starts with a default exception state equivalent to " (code "(create-exception-state)") ".")) ("system:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(create-exception-state)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(create-exception-state " (var "handler") ")") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "create-exception-state") " creates an exception state whose stack of exception handlers is empty except for, in effect, an infinite number of occurrences of " (var "handler") " at its base. " (code (var "handler")) " must be a procedure, and should accept one argument. If not provided, " (code (var "handler")) " defaults to a procedure equivalent to the value of the following expression.") ((code "(lambda (x) ((base-exception-handler) x))"))) ("system:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(assert-unreachable)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "assert-unreachable") " is an assertion with the special property that at optimize-level 3 the optimizer can assume that the any application of " (code "assert-unreachable") " is, in fact, not reachable.") ("In code expanded or compiled at optimize-levels less than 3, invoking " (code "assert-unreachable") " raises a non-continuable exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") ".") ("In code expanded or compiled at optimize-level 3, the behavior of invoking " (code "assert-unreachable") " is undefined, which gives the compiler liberty to pick convenient or efficient behavior. For example:") ((code "(print-gensym #f)" (br) "(optimize-level 3)" (br) "(expand/optimize" (br) "  '(lambda (x)" (br) "     (if (pair? x)" (br) "         (car x)" (br) "         (assert-unreachable)))))" (br) (math "=>") " (lambda (x) (#3%car x))")) ("because choosing to make " (code "(assert-unreachable)") " behave the same as " (code "(#3%car x)") " makes both branches of the " (code "if") " the same, and then " (code "pair?") " test can be eliminated (since it has no side effects).")) ("system:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(break " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(break " (var "who") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(break)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The arguments to " (code "break") " follow the protocol described above for " (code "errorf") ". The default break handler (see " (code "break-handler") ") displays a message and invokes the debugger. The format string and objects may be omitted, in which case the message issued by the default break handler identifies the break using the " (code (var "who")) " argument but provides no more information about the break. If the " (code (var "who")) " argument is omitted as well, no message is generated. The default break handler returns normally if the debugger exits normally.")) ("system:s20" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "break-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure. The current break handler is called by " (code "break") ", which passes along its arguments. See " (code "break") " for a description of the default break handler. The example below shows how to disable breaks.") ((code "(break-handler (lambda args (void)))"))) ("system:s21" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "keyboard-interrupt-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure. The keyboard-interrupt handler is called (with no arguments) when a keyboard interrupt occurs. The default keyboard-interrupt handler invokes the interactive debugger. If the debugger exits normally the interrupted computation is resumed. The example below shows how to install a keyboard-interrupt handler that resets without invoking the debugger.") ((code "(keyboard-interrupt-handler" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (newline (console-output-port))" (br) "    (reset)))"))) ("system:s23" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-timer " (var "n") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "previous current timer value" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "n")) " must be a nonnegative integer. When " (code (var "n")) " is nonzero, " (code "set-timer") " starts an internal timer with an initial value of " (code (var "n")) ". When " (code (var "n")) " ticks elapse, a timer interrupt occurs, resulting in invocation of the timer interrupt handler. Each tick corresponds roughly to one nonleaf procedure call (see the introduction to this section); thus, ticks are not uniform time units but instead depend heavily on how much work is done by each procedure call.") ("When " (code (var "n")) " is zero, " (code "set-timer") " turns the timer off.") ("The value returned in either case is the value of the timer before the call to " (code "set-timer") ". A return value of 0 should not be taken to imply that the timer was not on; the return value may also be 0 if the timer was just about to fire when the call to " (code "set-timer") " occurred.") ("The engine mechanism (Section " (link "./control.html#g49" "6.4") ") is built on top of the timer interrupt so timer interrupts should not be used with engines.")) ("system:s25" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "timer-interrupt-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure. The timer interrupt handler is called by the system when the internal timer (set by " (code "set-timer") ") expires. The default handler raises an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") "  to say that the handler has not been defined; any program that uses the timer should redefine the handler before setting the timer.")) ("system:s27" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(disable-interrupts)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(enable-interrupts)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "disable count" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "disable-interrupts") " disables the handling of interrupts, including timer, keyboard, and collect request interrupts. " (code "enable-interrupts") " re-enables these interrupts. The system maintains a disable count that starts at zero; when zero, interrupts are enabled. Each call to " (code "disable-interrupts") " increments the count, effectively disabling interrupts. Each call to " (code "enable-interrupts") " decrements the count, if not already zero, effectively enabling interrupts. For example, two calls to " (code "disable-interrupts") " followed by one call to " (code "enable-interrupts") " leaves interrupts disabled. Calls to " (code "enable-interrupts") " when the count is already zero (and interrupts are enabled) have no effect. The value returned by either procedure is the number of calls to " (code "enable-interrupts") " required to enable interrupts.") ("Great care should be exercised when using these procedures, since disabling interrupts inhibits the normal processing of keyboard interrupts, timer interrupts, and, perhaps most importantly, collect request interrupts. Since garbage collection does not happen automatically when interrupts are disabled, it is possible for the storage allocator to run out of space unnecessarily should interrupts be disabled for a long period of time.") ("The " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") " syntactic form should be used instead of these more primitive procedures whenever possible, since " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") " ensures that interrupts are re-enabled whenever a nonlocal exit occurs, such as when an exception is handled by the default exception handler.")) ("system:s28" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(with-interrupts-disabled " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(critical-section " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "with-interrupts-disabled") " evaluates the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") " with interrupts disabled. That is, upon entry, interrupts are disabled, and upon exit, interrupts are re-enabled. Thus, " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") " allows the implementation of indivisible operations in nonthreaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") " or within a single thread in threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ". " (code "critical-section") " is the same as " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") " and is provided for backward compatibility.") ((code "with-interrupts-disabled") " can be defined as follows.") ((code "(define-syntax with-interrupts-disabled" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ b1 b2 ...)" (br) "     (dynamic-wind" (br) "       disable-interrupts" (br) "       (lambda () b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       enable-interrupts)]))")) ("The use of " (code "dynamic-wind") " ensures that interrupts are disabled whenever the body of the " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") " expression is active and re-enabled whenever it is not. Since calls to " (code "disable-interrupts") " are counted (see the discussion under " (code "disable-interrupts") " and " (code "enable-interrupts") " above), " (code "with-interrupts-disabled") "  expressions may be nested with the desired effect.")) ("system:s29" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(register-signal-handler " (var "sig") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "register-signal-handler") " is used to establish a signal handler for a given low-level signal. " (code (var "sig")) " must be an exact integer identifying a valid signal, and " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one argument. See your host system's " (code "<signal.h>") " or documentation for a list of valid signals and their numbers. After a signal handler for a given signal has been registered, receipt of the specified signal results in a call to the handler. The handler is passed the signal number, allowing the same handler to be used for different signals while differentiating among them. In a threaded version of the system, signals are always delivered to the main thread.") ("Signals handled in this fashion are treated like keyboard interrupts in that the handler is not called immediately when the signal is delivered to the process, but rather at some procedure call boundary after the signal is delivered. It is generally not a good idea, therefore, to establish handlers for memory faults, illegal instructions, and the like, since the code that causes the fault or illegal instruction will continue to execute (presumably erroneously) for some time before the handler is invoked. A finite amount of storage is used to buffer as-yet unhandled signals, after which additional signals are dropped.") ((code "register-signal-handler") " is supported only on Unix-based systems.")) ("system:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(environment? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is an environment, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(environment? (interaction-environment)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(environment? 'interaction-environment) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(environment? (copy-environment (scheme-environment))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(environment? (environment '(prefix (rnrs) $rnrs-))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("system:s31" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(environment-mutable? " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "env")) " is mutable, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(environment-mutable? (interaction-environment)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(environment-mutable? (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(environment-mutable? (copy-environment (scheme-environment))) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(environment-mutable? (environment '(prefix (rnrs) $rnrs-))) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("system:s32" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-environment)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an environment" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "scheme-environment") " returns an environment containing the initial top-level bindings. This environment corresponds to the " (code "scheme") " module.") ("The environment returned by this procedure is immutable.") ((code "(define cons 3)" (br) "(top-level-value 'cons (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " #<procedure cons>" (br) "(set-top-level-value! 'cons 3 (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("system:s33" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(ieee-environment)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an IEEE/ANSI standard compatibility environment" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "ieee-environment") " returns an environment containing bindings for the keywords and variables whose meanings are defined by the IEEE/ANSI Standard for Scheme [" (link "./bibliography.html#g186" "26") "].") ("The bindings for each of the identifiers in the IEEE environment are those of the corresponding Revised" (sup "6") " Report library, so this does not provide full backward compatibility.") ("The environment returned by this procedure is immutable.") ((code "(define cons 3)" (br) "(top-level-value 'cons (ieee-environment)) " (math "=>") " #<procedure cons>" (br) "(set-top-level-value! 'cons 3 (ieee-environment)) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("system:s34" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "interaction-environment") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The original value of " (code "interaction-environment") " is the default top-level environment. It is initially set to a mutable copy of " (code "(scheme-environment)") " and which may be extended or otherwise altered by top-level definitions and assignments. It may be set to any environment, mutable or not, to change the default top-level evaluation environment.") ("An expression's top-level bindings resolve to the environment that is in effect when the expression is expanded, and changing the value of this parameter has no effect on running code. Changes affect only code that is subsequently expanded, e.g., as the result of a call to " (code "eval") ", " (code "load") ", or " (code "compile-file") ".") ((code "(define cons 3)" (br) "cons " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(top-level-value 'cons (interaction-environment)) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) (br) "(interaction-environment (scheme-environment))" (br) "cons " (math "=>") " #<procedure cons>" (br) "(set! cons 3) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: attempt to assign immutable variable") (br) "(define cons 3) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: invalid definition in immutable environment")))) ("system:s35" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(copy-environment " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(copy-environment " (var "env") " " (var "mutable?") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(copy-environment " (var "env") " " (var "mutable?") " " (var "syms") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new environment" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "copy-environment") " returns a copy of " (code (var "env")) ", i.e., a new environment that contains the same bindings as " (code (var "env")) ".") ("The environment is mutable if " (code (var "mutable?")) " is omitted or true; if " (code (var "mutable?")) " is false, the environment is immutable.") ("The set of bindings copied from " (code (var "env")) " to the new environment is determined by " (code (var "syms")) ", which defaults to the value of " (code "(environment-symbols " (var "env") ")") ". The binding, if any, for each element of " (code (var "syms")) " is copied to the new environment, and no other bindings are present in the new environment.") ("In the current implementation, the storage space used by an environment is never collected, so repeated use of " (code "copy-environment") " will eventually cause the system to run out of memory.") ((code "(define e (copy-environment (scheme-environment)))" (br) "(eval '(define cons +) e)" (br) "(eval '(cons 3 4) e)                    " (math "=>") " 7" (br) "(eval '(cons 3 4) (scheme-environment)) " (math "=>") " (3 . 4)"))) ("system:s36" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(environment-symbols " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of symbols" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure returns a list of symbols representing the identifiers bound in environment " (code (var "env")) ". It is primarily useful in building the list of symbols to be copied from one environment to another.") ((code "(define listless-environment" (br) "  (copy-environment" (br) "    (scheme-environment)" (br) "    #t" (br) "    (remq 'list (environment-symbols (scheme-environment)))))" (br) "(eval '(let ([x (cons 3 4)]) x) listless-environment) " (math "=>") " (3 . 4)" (br) "(eval '(list 3 4) listless-environment) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("system:s37" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(apropos-list " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(apropos-list " (var "s") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure returns a selected list of symbols and pairs. Each symbol in the list represents an identifier bound in " (code (var "env")) ". Each pair represents a set of identifiers exported by a predefined library or a library previously defined or loaded into the system. The car of the pair is the library name, and the cdr is a list of symbols. If " (code (var "s")) " is a string, only entries whose names have " (code (var "s")) " as a substring are included, and if " (code (var "s")) " is a symbol, only those whose names have the name of " (code (var "s")) " as a substring are selected. If no environment is provided, it defaults to the value of " (code "interaction-environment") ".") ((code "(library (a) (export a-vector-sortof) (import (rnrs))" (br) "  (define a-vector-sortof '(vector 1 2 3)))" (br) "(apropos-list 'vector-sort) " (math "=>") (br) "  (vector-sort vector-sort!" (br) "   ((a) a-vector-sortof)" (br) "   ((chezscheme) vector-sort vector-sort!)" (br) "   ((rnrs) vector-sort vector-sort!)" (br) "   ((rnrs sorting) vector-sort vector-sort!)" (br) "   ((scheme) vector-sort vector-sort!))"))) ("system:s38" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(apropos " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(apropos " (var "s") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "apropos") " is like " (code "apropos-list") " except the information is displayed to the current output port, as shown in the following transcript.") ((code "> (library (a) (export a-vector-sortof) (import (rnrs))" (br) "    (define a-vector-sortof '(vector 1 2 3)))" (br) "> (apropos 'vector-sort)" (br) "interaction environment:" (br) "  vector-sort, vector-sort!" (br) "(a):" (br) "  a-vector-sortof" (br) "(chezscheme):" (br) "  vector-sort, vector-sort!" (br) "(rnrs):" (br) "  vector-sort, vector-sort!" (br) "(rnrs sorting):" (br) "  vector-sort, vector-sort!" (br) "(scheme):" (br) "  vector-sort, vector-sort!"))) ("system:s39" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(eval " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(eval " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "value of the Scheme form represented by " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "eval") " treats " (code (var "obj")) " as the representation of an expression. It evaluates the expression in environment " (code (var "env")) " and returns its value. If no environment is provided, it defaults to the environment returned by " (code "interaction-environment") ".") ("Single-argument " (code "eval") " is a " (var "Chez Scheme") " extension. " (var "Chez Scheme") " also permits " (code (var "obj")) " to be the representation of a nonexpression form, i.e., a definition, whenever the environment is mutable. " (var "Chez Scheme") " further allows " (code (var "obj")) " to be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") "), and the default evaluators make use of annotations to incorporate source-file information in error messages and associate source-file information with compiled code.") ("In " (var "Chez Scheme") ", " (code "eval") " is actually a wrapper that simply passes its arguments to the current evaluator. (See " (code "current-eval") ".) The default evaluator is " (code "compile") ", which expands the expression via the current expander (see " (code "current-expand") "), compiles it, executes the resulting code, and returns its value. If the environment argument, " (code (var "env")) ", is present, " (code "compile") " passes it along to the current expander, which is " (code "sc-expand") " by default.")) ("system:s40" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-eval") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-eval") " determines the evaluation procedure used by the procedures " (code "eval") ", " (code "load") ", and " (code "new-cafe") ". " (code "current-eval") " is initially bound to the value of " (code "compile") ". (In " (var "Petite Chez Scheme") ", it is initially bound to the value of " (code "interpret") ".) The evaluation procedure should expect one or two arguments: an object to evaluate and an optional environment. The second argument might be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ").") ((code "(current-eval interpret)" (br) "(+ 1 1) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) (br) "(current-eval (lambda (x . ignore) x))" (br) "(+ 1 1) " (math "=>") " (+ 1 1)"))) ("system:s44" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "value of the Scheme form represented by " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "obj")) ", which can be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or unannotated value, is treated as a Scheme expression, expanded with the current expander (the value of " (code "current-expand") ") in the specified environment (or the interaction environment, if no environment is provided), compiled to machine code, and executed. " (code "compile") " is the default value of the " (code "current-eval") "  parameter.")) ("system:s45" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(interpret " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(interpret " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "value of the Scheme form represented by " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "interpret") " is like " (code "compile") ", except that the expression is interpreted rather than compiled. " (code "interpret") " may be used as a replacement for " (code "compile") ", with the following caveats:   " (ul (li "Interpreted code runs significantly slower.") (li "Inspector information is not generated for interpreted code, so the inspector is not as useful for interpreted code as it is for compiled code.") (li "Foreign procedure expressions cannot be interpreted, so the interpreter invokes the compiler for all foreign procedure expressions (this is done transparently)."))) ((code "interpret") " is sometimes faster than " (code "compile") " when the form to be evaluated is short running, since it avoids some of the work done by " (code "compile") " prior to evaluation.")) ("system:s46" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load " (var "path") " " (var "eval-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "load") " reads and evaluates the contents of the file specified by " (code (var "path")) ". The file may contain source or object code. By default, " (code "load") " employs " (code "eval") " to evaluate each source expression found in a source file. If " (code (var "eval-proc")) " is specified, " (code "load") " uses this procedure instead. " (code (var "eval-proc")) " must accept one argument, the expression to evaluate. The expression passed to " (code (var "eval-proc")) " might be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or an unannotated value.") ("The " (code (var "eval-proc")) " argument facilitates the implementation of embedded Scheme-like languages and the use of alternate evaluation mechanisms to be used for Scheme programs. " (code (var "eval-proc")) " can be put to other uses as well. For example,") ((code "(load \"myfile.ss\"" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (pretty-print" (br) "      (if (annotation? x)" (br) "          (annotation-stripped x)" (br) "          x))" (br) "    (newline)" (br) "    (eval x)))")) ("pretty-prints each expression before evaluating it.") ("The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files not identified by absolute path names or paths that start with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") "  under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows).")) ("system:s48" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-library " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-library " (var "path") " " (var "eval-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "load-library") " is identical to " (code "load") " except that it treats the input file as if it were prefixed by an implicit " (code "#!r6rs") ". This effectively disables any non-R6RS lexical syntax except where subsequently overridden by " (code "#!chezscheme") ".")) ("system:s49" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-program " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-program " (var "path") " " (var "eval-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "load-program") " reads and evaluates the contents of the file specified by " (code (var "path")) ". The file may contain source or object code. If it contains source code, " (code "load-program") " wraps the code in a " (code "top-level-program") " form so that the file's content is treated as an RNRS top-level program (Section " (link "http://scheme.com/tspl4/./libraries.html#g145" "10.3") " of " (var "The Scheme Programming Language, 4th Edition") "). By default, " (code "load-program") " employs " (code "eval") " to evaluate each source expression found in the file. If " (code (var "eval-proc")) " is specified, " (code "load-program") " uses this procedure instead. " (code (var "eval-proc")) " must accept one argument, the expression to evaluate. The expression passed to " (code (var "eval-proc")) " might be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or an unannotated value.") ("The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files not identified by absolute path names or paths that start with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") "  under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows).")) ("system:s51" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(verify-loadability " (var "situation") " " (var "input") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "situation")) " must be one of the symbols " (code "visit") ", " (code "revisit") ", or " (code "load") ". Each " (code (var "input")) " must be a string pathname or a pair of a string pathname and a library search path. Each of the pathnames should name a file containing object code for a set of libraries and top-level programs, such as would be produced by " (code "compile-program") ", " (code "compile-library") ", " (code "compile-whole-program") ", or " (code "compile-whole-library") ". A library search path must be a suitable argument for " (code "library-directories") ".") ((code "verify-loadability") " verifies, without actually loading any code or defining any libraries, whether the object files named by the specified pathnames and their library dependencies, direct or indirect, are present, readable, and mutually compatible. The type of dependencies for each named object file is determined by the " (code (var "situation")) " argument: compile-time dependencies for " (code (var "visit")) ", run-time dependencies for " (code (var "revisit")) " and both for " (code (var "load")) ".") ("For each input pathname that is paired with a search path, the " (code "library-directories") " parameter is parameterized to the library search path during the recursive search for dependencies of the programs and libraries found in the object file named by the pathname.") ("If " (code "verify-loadability") " finds a problem, such as a missing library dependency or compilation-instance mismatch, it raises an exception with an appropriate condition. Otherwise, it returns an unspecified value.") ("Since " (code "verify-loadability") " does not load or run any code from the files it processes, it cannot determine whether errors unrelated to missing or unreadable files or mutual compatibility will occur when the files are actually loaded.")) ("system:s57" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-compiled-from-port " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(load-compiled-from-port " (var "input-port") " " (var "externals") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "result of the last compiled expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "load-compiled-from-port") " reads and evaluates the object-code contents of " (code (var "input-port")) " as previously created by functions like " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-script") ", " (code "compile-library") ", and " (code "compile-to-port") ".") ("The " (code (var "externals")) " argument, if supplied, must be a vector. It should cooperate with an " (code (var "ext-pred")) " procedure passed to " (code "compile-to-port") ", analogous to the way a procedure and vector cooperate with " (code "fasl-write") " and " (code "fasl-read") ".") ("The return value is the value of the last expression whose compiled form is in " (code (var "input-port")) ". If " (code (var "input-port")) " is empty, then the result value is unspecified. The port is left at end-of-file but is not closed.")) ("system:s58" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(visit-compiled-from-port " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "result of the last compiled expression processed" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "visit-compiled-from-port") " reads and evaluates the object-code contents of " (code (var "input-port")) " as previously created by functions like " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-script") ", " (code "compile-library") ", and " (code "compile-to-port") ".  In the process, it skips any revisit (run-time-only) code.") ("The return value is the value of the last expression whose last non-revisit compiled form is in " (code (var "input-port")) ". If there are no such forms, then the result value is unspecified. The port is left at end-of-file but is not closed.")) ("system:s59" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(revisit-compiled-from-port " (var "input-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "result of the last compiled expression processed" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "revisit-compiled-from-port") " reads and evaluates the object-code contents of " (code (var "input-port")) " as previously created by functions like " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-script") ", " (code "compile-library") ", and " (code "compile-to-port") ".  In the process, it skips any visit (compile-time-only) code.") ("The return value is the value of the last expression whose last non-visit compiled form is in " (code (var "input-port")) ". If there are no such forms, then the result value is unspecified. The port is left at end-of-file but is not closed.")) ("system:s60" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(visit " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "visit") " reads the named file, which must contain compiled object code compatible with the current machine type and version, and it runs those portions of the compiled object code that establish compile-time information or correspond to expressions identified as \"visit\" time by " (code "eval-when") " forms contained in the original source file.") ("For example, assume the file " (code "t1.ss") " contains the following forms:") ((code "(define-syntax a (identifier-syntax 3))" (br) "(module m (x) (define x 4))" (br) "(define y 5)")) ("If " (code "t1.ss") " is compiled to " (code "t1.so") ", applying " (code "load") "  to " (code "t1.so") " has the effect of defining all three identifiers. Applying " (code "visit") " to " (code "t1.so") ", however, has the effect of installing the transformer for " (code "a") ", installing the interface for " (code "m") " (for use by " (code "import") "),  and recording " (code "y") " as a variable. " (code "visit") " is useful when separately compiling one file that depends on bindings defined in another without actually loading and evaluating the code in the supporting file.") ("The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files not identified by absolute path names or paths that start with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") "  under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows).")) ("system:s62" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(revisit " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "revisit") " reads the named file, which must contain compiled object code compatible with the current machine type and version, and it runs those portions of the compiled object code that compute run-time values or correspond to expressions identified as \"revisit\" time by " (code "eval-when") " forms contained in the original source file.") ("Continuing the example given for " (code "visit") " above, applying " (code "revisit") " to the object file, " (code "t1.so") ", has the effect of establishing the values of the variable " (code "x") "  exported from " (code "m") " and the top-level variable " (code "y") ", without installing either the interface for " (code "m") " or the transformer for " (code "a") ".") ((code "revisit") " is useful for loading compiled application code without loading unnecessary compile-time information. Care must be taken when using this feature if the application calls " (code "eval") " or uses " (code "top-level-value") ", " (code "set-top-level-value!") ", or " (code "top-level-syntax") " to access top-level bindings at run-time, since these procedures use compile-time information to resolve top-level bindings.") ("The parameter " (code "source-directories") " (Section " (link "./system.html#g121" "12.5") ") determines the set of directories searched for source files not identified by absolute path names or paths that start with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") "  under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows).")) ("system:s64" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-file " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-file " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-filename")) " and " (code (var "output-filename")) " must be strings. " (code (var "input-filename")) " must name an existing, readable file. It must contain a sequence of zero or more source expressions; if this is not the case, " (code "compile-file") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&syntax") ".") ("The normal evaluation process proceeds in two steps: compilation and execution. " (code "compile-file") " performs the compilation process for an entire source file, producing an object file. When the object file is subsequently loaded (see " (code "load") "), the compilation process is not necessary, and the file typically loads several times faster.") ("If the optional " (code (var "output-filename")) " argument is omitted, the actual input and output filenames are determined as follows. If " (code (var "input-filename")) " has no extension, the input filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " followed by " (code ".ss") "  and the output filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " followed by " (code ".so") ". If " (code (var "input-filename")) " has the extension " (code ".so") ", the input filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " and the output filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " followed by " (code ".so") ". Otherwise, the input filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " and the output filename is " (code (var "input-filename")) " without its extension, followed by " (code ".so") ". For example, " (code "(compile-file \"myfile\")") " produces an object file with the name " (code "\"myfile.so\"") " from the source file named " (code "\"myfile.ss\"") ", " (code "(compile-file \"myfile.sls\")") " produces an object file with the name " (code "\"myfile.so\"") " from the source file named " (code "\"myfile.sls\"") ", and " (code "(compile-file \"myfile1\" \"myfile2\")") " produces an object file with the name " (code "\"myfile2\"") " from the source file name " (code "\"myfile1\"") ".") ("Before compiling a file, " (code "compile-file") " saves the values of the following parameters:") ((code "optimize-level" (br) "debug-level" (br) "run-cp0" (br) "cp0-effort-limit" (br) "cp0-score-limit" (br) "cp0-outer-unroll-limit" (br) "generate-inspector-information" (br) "generate-procedure-source-information" (br) "compile-profile" (br) "generate-covin-files" (br) "generate-interrupt-trap" (br) "enable-cross-library-optimization" (br) "enable-arithmetic-left-associative" (br) "enable-error-source-expression" (br) "enable-unsafe-application" (br) "enable-type-recovery")) ("It restores the values after the file has been compiled. This allows the programmer to control the values of these parameters on a per-file basis, e.g., via an " (code "eval-when") " with situation " (code "compile") " embedded in the source file. For example, if") ((code "(eval-when (compile) (optimize-level 3))")) ("appears at the top of a source file, the optimization level is set to 3 just while the remainder of file is compiled.")) ("system:s67" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-script " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-script " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-filename")) " and " (code (var "output-filename")) " must be strings.") ((code "compile-script") " is like " (code "compile-file") " but differs in that it copies the leading " (code "#!") " line from the source-file script into the object file.") ((code "compile-script") " permits compiled script files to be created from source script to reduce script load time. As with source-code scripts, compiled scripts may be run with the " (code "--script") "  command-line option.")) ("system:s69" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-library " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-library " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-filename")) " and " (code (var "output-filename")) " must be strings.") ((code "compile-library") " is identical to " (code "compile-file") " except that it treats the input file as if it were prefixed by an implicit " (code "#!r6rs") ". This effectively disables any non-R6RS lexical syntax except where subsequently overridden by " (code "#!chezscheme") ".")) ("system:s70" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-program " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-program " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of libraries invoked by the program" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-filename")) " and " (code (var "output-filename")) " must be strings.") ((code "compile-program") " is like " (code "compile-script") " but differs in that it implements the semantics of RNRS top-level programs, while " (code "compile-script") " implements the semantics of the interactive top-level. The resulting compiled program will also run faster than if compiled via " (code "compile-file") " or " (code "compile-script") ".") ((code "compile-program") " returns a list of libraries directly invoked by the compiled top-level program, excluding built-in libraries like " (code "(rnrs)") " and " (code "(chezscheme)") ". The procedure " (code "library-requirements") " may be used to determine the indirect requirements, i.e., additional libraries required by the directly invoked libraries. When combined with " (code "library-object-filename") ", this information can be used to determine the set of files that must be distributed with the compiled program file.") ("A program invokes a library only if it references one or more variables exported from the library. The set of libraries invoked by a top-level program, and hence loaded when the program is loaded, might be smaller than the set imported by the program, and it might be larger than the set directly imported by the program.") ("As with source-code top-level programs, compiled top-level programs may be run with the " (code "--program") "  command-line option.")) ("system:s72" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-file " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-file " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-library " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-library " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-program " (var "input-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maybe-compile-program " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are like their non-" (code "maybe") " counterparts but compile the source file only if the object file is out-of-date. An object file " (code (var "X")) " is considered out-of-date if it does not exist or if it is older than the source file or any files included (via " (code "include") ") when " (code (var "X")) " was created. When the value of the parameter " (code "compile-imported-libraries") "  is " (code "#t") ", " (code (var "X")) " is also considered out-of-date if the object file for any library imported when " (code (var "X")) " was compiled is out-of-date. If " (code "maybe-compile-file") " determines that compilation is necessary, it compiles the source file by passing " (code "compile-file") " the input and output filenames. " (code "compile-library") " does so by similarly invoking the value of the " (code "compile-library-handler") " parameter, and " (code "compile-program") " does so by similarly invoking the value of the " (code "compile-program-handler") " parameter.") ("When " (code (var "output-filename")) " is not specified, the input and output filenames are determined in the same manner as for " (code "compile-file") ".")) ("system:s73" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-library-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter must be set to a procedure, and the procedure should accept two string arguments naming a source file and an object file. The procedure should typically invoke " (code "compile-library") " and pass it the two arguments, but it can also use one of the other file or port compilation procedures. For example, it might read the source file using its own parser and use " (code "compile-to-file") " to finish the compilation process. The procedure can perform other actions as well, such as parameterizing compilation parameters, establishing guards, or gathering statistics. The default value of this parameter simply invokes " (code "compile-library") " on the two string arguments without taking any other action.") ("The value of this parameter is called by " (code "maybe-compile-library") "  when the object file is out-of-date. It is also called by the expander to compile an imported library when " (code "compile-imported-libraries") " is " (code "#t") "  and the expander determines the object file is out-of-date.")) ("system:s75" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-program-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter must be set to a procedure, and the procedure should accept two string arguments naming a source file and an object file. The procedure should typically invoke " (code "compile-program") " and pass it the two arguments, but it can also use one of the other file or port compilation procedures. For example, it might read the source file using its own parser and use " (code "compile-to-file") " to finish the compilation process. The procedure can perform other actions as well, such as parameterizing compilation parameters, establishing guards, or gathering statistics. The default value of this parameter simply invokes " (code "compile-program") " on the two string arguments without taking any other action and returns the list of libraries returned by " (code "compile-program") ".") ("The value of this parameter is called by " (code "maybe-compile-program") "  when the object file is out-of-date.")) ("system:s77" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-whole-program " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-whole-program " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") " " (var "libs-visible?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of libraries left to be loaded at run time" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "compile-whole-program") " accepts as input a filename naming a \"whole program optimization\" (wpo) file for a top-level program and produces an object file incorporating the program and each library upon which it depends, provided that a wpo file for the library can be found.") ("If a wpo file for a required library cannot be found, but an object file for the library can, the library is not incorporated in the resulting object file. Such libraries are left to be loaded at run time. " (code "compile-whole-program") " returns a list of such libraries. If there are no such libraries, the resulting object file is self-contained and " (code "compile-whole-program") " returns the empty list.") ("The libraries incorporated into the resulting object file are visible (for use by " (code "environment") " and " (code "eval") ") if the " (code (var "libs-visible?")) "  argument is supplied and non-false. Any library incorporated into the resulting object file and required by an object file left to be loaded at run time is also visible, as are any libraries the object file depends upon, regardless of the value of " (code (var "libs-visible?")) ".") ((code "compile-whole-program") " linearizes the initialization code for the set of incorporated libraries in a way that respects static dependencies among the libraries but not necessary dynamic dependencies deriving from initialization-time uses of " (code "environment") "  or " (code "eval") ". Additional static dependencies can be added in most cases to force an ordering that allows the dynamic imports to succeed, though not in general since a different order might be required each time the program is run. Adding a static dependency of one library on a second requires adding an import of the second in the first as well as a run-time reference to one of the variables exported by the second in the body of the first.") ((code (var "input-filename")) " and " (code (var "output-filename")) " must be strings. " (code (var "input-filename")) " must identify a wpo file, and a wpo or object file must also be present for each required library somewhere in the directories specified by the " (code "library-directories") "  parameter.") ("To the extent possible given the specified set of visible libraries and requirements of libraries to be loaded at run time, " (code "compile-whole-program") " discards unused code and optimizes across program and library boundaries, potentially reducing program load time, run time, and memory requirements. Some optimization also occurs even across the boundaries of libraries that are not incorporated into the output, though this optimization is limited in nature.") ("The procedures " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-program") ", " (code "compile-library") ", " (code "compile-script") ", and " (code "compile-whole-library") " produce wpo files as well as ordinary object files when the " (code "generate-wpo-files") " parameter is set to " (code "#t") " (the default is " (code "#f") "). " (code "compile-port") " and " (code "compile-to-port") " do so when passed an optional wpo port.")) ("system:s79" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-whole-library " (var "input-filename") " " (var "output-filename") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of libraries left to be loaded at run time" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "compile-whole-library") " is like " (code "compile-whole-program") ", except " (code (var "input-filename")) " must specify a wpo file for a library, all libraries are automatically made visible, and a new wpo file is produced (when " (code "generate-wpo-files") " is " (code "#t") ") as well as an object file for the resulting combination of libraries.") ("The comment in the description of " (code "compile-whole-program") "  about the effect of initialization-code linearization on dynamic dependencies applies to " (code "compile-whole-library") " as well.")) ("system:s80" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-port " (var "input-port") " " (var "output-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-port " (var "input-port") " " (var "output-port") " " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-port " (var "input-port") " " (var "output-port") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpo-port") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-port " (var "input-port") " " (var "output-port") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpo-port") " " (var "covop") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-port")) " must be a textual input port. " (code (var "output-port")) " and, if present and non-false, " (code (var "wpo-port")) " must be binary output ports. If present and non-false, " (code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. If present and non-false, " (code (var "covop")) " must be a textual output port.") ((code "compile-port") " is like " (code "compile-file") " except that it takes input from an arbitrary textual input port and sends output to an arbitrary binary output port. If " (code (var "sfd")) " is supplied, it is passed to the reader so that source information can be associated with the expressions read from " (code (var "input-port")) ". It is also used to associate block-profiling information with the input file name encapsulated within " (code (var "sfd")) ". If " (code (var "wpo-port")) " is supplied, " (code "compile-port") " sends whole-program optimization information to " (code (var "wpo-port")) " for use by " (code "compile-whole-program") ", as if (and regardless of whether) " (code "generate-wpo-files") " is set. If " (code (var "covop")) " is supplied, " (code "compile-port") " sends coverage information to " (code (var "covop")) ", as if (and regardless of whether) " (code "generate-covin-files") " is set.") ("The ports are closed automatically after compilation under the assumption the program that opens the ports and invokes " (code "compile-port") "  will take care of closing the ports.")) ("system:s81" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") " " (var "covop") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") " " (var "covop") " " (var "mach") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") " " (var "covop") " " (var "mach") " " (var "hostop") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") " " (var "covop") " " (var "mach") " " (var "hostop") " " (var "ext") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-port " (var "objs") " " (var "op") " " (var "sfd") " " (var "wpoop") " " (var "covop") " " (var "mach") " " (var "hostop") " " (var "ext") " " (var "nortds?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "objs")) " must be a list containing a sequence of objects that represent syntactically valid expressions, each possibly annotated (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") "). If any of the objects does not represent a syntactically valid expression, " (code "compile-to-port") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&syntax") ". " (code (var "op")) " and, if present and non-false, " (code (var "wpoop")) ", " (code (var "covop")) ", and " (code (var "hostop")) " must be binary output ports. If present and non-false, " (code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor. If present and non-false, " (code (var "mach")) " must be a symbol. If present and non-false, " (code (var "ext")) " must be or a procedure, but it must be " (code "#f") " if " (code (var "objs")) " does not have exactlty one element.") ((code "compile-to-port") " is like " (code "compile-file") " except that it takes input from a list of objects and sends output to an arbitrary binary output port. " (code (var "sfd")) " is used to associate block-profiling information with the input file name encapsulated within " (code (var "sfd")) ". If " (code (var "wpoop")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", " (code "compile-to-port") " sends whole-program optimization information to " (code (var "wpoop")) " for use by " (code "compile-whole-program") ", as if (and regardless of whether) " (code "generate-wpo-files") " is set. If " (code (var "covop")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", " (code "compile-to-port") " sends coverage information to " (code (var "covop")) ", as if (and regardless of whether) " (code "generate-covin-files") " is set. If " (code (var "mach")) " is present, " (code "compile-to-port") " compiles for the specified machine type. If " (code (var "hostop")) " is present, " (code "compile-to-port") " compiles for the host machine type to " (code (var "hostp")) " (in addition to writing for " (code (var "machine")) " to " (code (var "op")) ", whether or not the machine types are the same).") ("The ports are not closed automatically after compilation under the assumption the program that opens the port and invokes " (code "compile-to-port") "  will take care of closing the port.") ("If " (code (var "ext")) " is present and not " (code "#f") ", it is used like the " (code (var "externals-pred")) " predicate supplied to " (code "fasl-write") ". In that case, a corresponding vector must be provided to" (br) (code "load-compiled-from-port") " to load the compiled code, analogous to the vector supplied to " (code "fasl-read") ".") ("Similarly, if " (code (var "nortds?")) " is present and true, it is used like the " (code (var "omit-rtds?")) " argument supplied to " (code "fasl-write") ":  Any record types relevant to the compiled code are not written to the output port.  When the compiled code is later loaded, these record types must already be declared in the loading context, and the loading context is assumed to have compatible record-type registrations using the same unique ID.  Behavior if the loading context has an " (var "incompatible") " record-type registration using the same (ostensibly unique) ID is undefined.") ("When " (code (var "objs")) " contains a single list-structured element whose first-element is the symbol " (code "top-level-program") ", " (code "compile-to-port") " returns a list of the libraries the top-level program requires at run time, as with " (code "compile-program") ". Otherwise, the return value is unspecified.")) ("system:s82" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-file " (var "obj-list") " " (var "output-file") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(compile-to-file " (var "obj-list") " " (var "output-file") " " (var "sfd") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "obj-list")) " must be a list containing a sequence of objects that represent syntactically valid expressions, each possibly annotated (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") "). If any of the objects does not represent a syntactically valid expression, " (code "compile-to-file") " raises an exception with condition type " (code "&syntax") ". " (code (var "output-file")) " must be a string. If present, " (code (var "sfd")) " must be a source-file descriptor.") ((code "compile-to-file") " is like " (code "compile-file") " except that it takes input from a list of objects. " (code (var "sfd")) " is used to associate block-profiling information with the input file name encapsulated within " (code (var "sfd")) ".") ("When " (code (var "obj-list")) " contains a single list-structured element whose first-element is the symbol " (code "top-level-program") ", " (code "compile-to-file") " returns a list of the libraries the top-level program requires at run time, as with " (code "compile-program") ". Otherwise, the return value is unspecified.")) ("system:s83" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(concatenate-object-files " (var "out-file") " " (var "in-file" (sub "1")) " " (var "in-file" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "out-file")) " and each " (code (var "in-file")) " must be strings.") ((code "concatenate-object-files") " combines the header information contained in the object files named by each " (code (var "in-file")) ".  It then writes the combined header information to the file named by " (code (var "out-file")) ", followed by the remaining object code from each input file in turn.")) ("system:s84" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-boot-file " (var "output-filename") " " (var "base-boot-list") " " (var "input-filename") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "output-filename")) ", " (code (var "input-filename")) ", and the elements of " (code (var "base-boot-list")) " must be strings.") ((code "make-boot-file") " writes a boot header to the file named by " (code (var "output-filename")) ", followed by the object code for each " (code (var "input-filename")) " in turn. If an input file is not already compiled, " (code "make-boot-file") " compiles the file as it proceeds.") ("The boot header identifies the elements of " (code (var "base-boot-list")) " as alternative boot files upon which the new boot file depends. If the list of strings naming base boot files is empty, the first named input file should be a base boot file, i.e., petite.boot or some boot file derived from petite.boot.") ("Boot files are loaded explicitly via the " (code "--boot") " or " (code "-b") "  command-line options or implicitly based on the name of the executable (Section " (link "./use.html#g14" "2.9") ").") ("See Section " (link "./use.html#g13" "2.8") " for more information on boot files and the use of " (code "make-boot-file") ".")) ("system:s87" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-boot-header " (var "output-filename") " " (var "base-boot" (sub "1")) " " (var "base-boot" (sub "2")) "...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure has been subsumed by " (code "make-boot-file") " and is provided for backward compatibility. The call") ((code "(make-boot-header " (var "output-filename") " " (var "base-boot" (sub "1")) " " (var "base-boot" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("is equivalent to") ((code "(make-boot-file " (var "output-filename") " '(" (var "base-boot" (sub "1")) " " (var "base-boot" (sub "2")) " ...))"))) ("system:s88" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(strip-fasl-file " (var "input-path") " " (var "output-path") " " (var "options") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "input-path")) " and " (code (var "output-path")) " must be strings. " (code (var "input-path")) " must name an existing, readable file containing object code produced by " (code "compile-file") ", one of the other file-compiling procedures, or an earlier run of " (code "strip-fasl-file") ". " (code (var "options")) " must be an enumeration set over the symbols constituting valid strip options, as described in the " (code "fasl-strip-options") "  entry below.") ("The new procedure " (code "strip-fasl-file") " allows the removal of source information of various sorts from a compiled object (fasl) file produced by " (code "compile-file") " or one of the other file compiling procedures. It also allows removal of library visit code from object files containing compiled libraries. Visit code is the code for macro transformers and meta definitions required to compile (but not run) dependent libraries.") ("On most platforms, the input and output paths can be the same, in which case the input file is replaced with a new file containing the stripped object code. Using the same path will likely fail on Windows file systems, which do not generally permit an open file to be removed.") ("If " (code (var "options")) " is empty, the output file is effectively equivalent to the input file, though it will not necessarily be identical.")) ("system:s89" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(fasl-strip-options " (var "symbol") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a fasl-strip-options enumeration set" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Fasl-strip-options enumeration sets are passed to " (code "strip-fasl-file") "  to determine what is stripped. The available options are described below.") ((dt (code "inspector-source") ":") (dd "Strip inspector source information. This includes source expressions that might otherwise be available for procedures and continuations with the \"code\" and \"call\" commands and messages in the interactive and object inspectors. It also includes filename and position information that might otherwise be available for the same via the \"file\" command and \"source\" messages.")) ((dt (code "source-annotations") ":") (dd "Strip source annotations, which typically appear only on syntax objects, e.g., identifiers, in the templates of macro transformers.")) ((dt (code "profile-source") ":") (dd "Strip source file and character position information from profiled code objects. This does not remove the profile counters or eliminate the overhead for incrementing them at run time.")) ((dt (code "compile-time-information") ": ") (dd "This strips compile-time information from compiled libraries, potentially reducing the size of the resulting file but making it impossible to use the file to compile dependent code. This option is useful for creating smaller object files to ship as part of a binary-only package."))) ("system:s90" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-procedure-realm") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter can be set to " (code "#f") " or to a symbol. The realm of a procedure (or, more precisely, a procedure's code object) is accessible though the inspector.")) ("system:s91" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(vfasl-convert-file " (var "input-path") " " (var "output-path") " " (var "base-boots") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Converts a compiled file to one that may load more quickly, especially in the case of a boot file. The converted file is a boot file if " (code (var "base-boots")) " is a list of strings, otherwise " (code (var "base-boots")) " must be " (code "#f") " to create a non-boot file.")) ("system:s92" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(pbchunk-convert-file " (var "in-path") " " (var "out-path") " " (var "c-paths") " " (var "c-names") " " (var "start-index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "ending index as a nonnegative exact integer" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Converts a compiled file " (code (var "in-path")) " for a " (code "pb") " machine variant to a compiled file " (code (var "out-path")) " that uses C chunk functions written to the files in " (code (var "c-paths")) ", where " (code (var "c-paths")) "  is a nonempty list of file names. Chunk code is distributed across the files to avoid making a single, over-large source C file. The " (code (var "c-names")) " list provides a list of function names, one for each output file in " (code (var "c-paths")) ", where each function name is defined as a function of no arguments to register the file's chunks with the system. This registration must be performed before the converted compiled code is run. Every set of chunks registered in this way must use a distinct index range, where " (code (var "start-index")) " provides the starting index for this set, and the result value is one more than the index of the last chunk written to the " (code (var "c-paths")) " sources.")) ("system:s93" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(machine-type)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the current machine type" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Consult the release notes for the current version of " (var "Chez Scheme") "  for a list of supported machine types.")) ("system:s94" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(expand " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(expand " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "expansion of the Scheme form represented by " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "expand") " treats " (code (var "obj")) " as the representation of an expression. It expands the expression in environment " (code (var "env")) " and returns an object representing the expanded form. If no environment is provided, it defaults to the environment returned by " (code "interaction-environment") ".") ((code (var "obj")) " can be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") "), and the default expander makes use of annotations to incorporate source-file information in error messages.") ((code "expand") " actually passes its arguments to the current expander (see " (code "current-expand") "), initially " (code "sc-expand") ".") ("See also " (code "expand-output") " (page " (link "./system.html#desc:expand-output" "400") ") which can be used to request that the compiler or interpreter show expander output.")) ("system:s96" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-expand") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-expand") " determines the expansion procedure used by the compiler, interpreter, and direct calls to " (code "expand") "  to expand syntactic extensions. " (code "current-expand") " is initially bound to the value of " (code "sc-expand") ".") ("It may be set another procedure, but since the format of expanded code expected by the compiler and interpreter is not publicly documented, only " (code "sc-expand") " produces correct output, so the other procedure must ultimately be defined in terms of " (code "sc-expand") ".") ("The first argument to the expansion procedure represents the input expression. It can be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or an unannotated value. the second argument is an environment. Additional arguments might be passed to the expansion procedure by the compiler, interpreter, and " (code "expand") "; their number and roles are unspecified.")) ("system:s99" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sc-expand " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sc-expand " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the expanded form of " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "sc-expand") " is used to expand programs written using " (code "syntax-case") " macros. " (code "sc-expand") " is the default expander, i.e., the initial value of " (code "current-expand") ". " (code (var "obj")) " represents the program to be expanded, and " (code (var "env")) " must be an environment. " (code (var "obj")) " can be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or unannotated value. If not provided, " (code (var "env")) " defaults to the environment returned by " (code "interaction-environment") ".")) ("system:s100" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(expand/optimize " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(expand/optimize " (var "obj") " " (var "env") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "result of expanding and optimizing form represented by " (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "expand/optimize") " treats " (code (var "obj")) " as the representation of an expression. " (code (var "obj")) " can be an annotation (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") or unannotated value. " (code "expand/optimize") " expands the expression in environment " (code (var "env")) "  and passes the expression through the source optimizer " (code "cp0") "  and a type-inferring optimizer " (code "cptypes") "  (unless " (code "cp0") " and " (code "cptypes") " are disabled via " (code "run-cp0") ", or unless " (code "cptypes") " is disabled via " (code "enable-type-recovery") "). It also simplifies " (code "letrec") " and " (code "letrec*") " expressions within the expression and makes their undefined checks explicit. It returns an object representing the expanded, simplified, and optimized form. If no environment is provided, it defaults to the environment returned by " (code "interaction-environment") ".") ((code "expand/optimize") " is primarily useful for understanding what " (code "cp0") " plus " (code "cptypes") " does and does not optimize. Many optimizations are performed later in the compiler, so " (code "expand/optimize") " does not give a complete picture of optimizations performed.") ((code "(expand/optimize" (br) "  '(let ([y '(3 . 4)])" (br) "     (+ (car y) (cdr y)))) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) (br) "(print-gensym #f)" (br) "(expand/optimize" (br) "  '(let ([y '(3 . 4)])" (br) "     (lambda (x)" (br) "       (* (+ (car y) (cdr y)) x)))) " (math "=>") " (lambda (x) (#2%* 7 x))" (br) (br) "(expand/optimize" (br) "  '(let ([n (expt 2 10)])" (br) "     (define even?" (br) "       (lambda (x) (or (zero? x) (not (odd? x)))))" (br) "     (define odd?" (br) "       (lambda (x) (not (even? (- x 1)))))" (br) "     (define f" (br) "       (lambda (x)" (br) "         (lambda (y)" (br) "           (lambda (z)" (br) "             (if (= z 0) (omega) (+ x y z))))))" (br) "     (define omega" (br) "       (lambda ()" (br) "         ((lambda (x) (x x)) (lambda (x) (x x)))))" (br) "     (let ([g (f 1)] [m (f n)])" (br) "       (let ([h (if (> ((g 2) 3) 5)" (br) "                    (lambda (x) (+ x 1))" (br) "                    odd?)])" (br) "         (h n))))) " (math "=>") " 1025")) ("See also " (code "expand/optimize-output") " (page " (link "./system.html#desc:expand/optimize-output" "400") ") which can be used to request that the compiler or interpreter show source-optimizer output.")) ("system:s102" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(eval-when " (var "situations") " " (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "situations")) " must be a list containing some combination of the symbols " (code "eval") ", " (code "compile") ", " (code "load") ", " (code "visit") ", and " (code "revisit") ".") ("When source files are loaded (see " (code "load") "), the forms in the file are read, compiled, and executed sequentially, so that each form in the file is fully evaluated before the next one is read. When a source file is compiled (see " (code "compile-file") "), however, the forms are read and compiled, " (var "but not executed") ", in sequence. This distinction matters only when the execution of one form in the file affects the compilation of later forms, e.g., when the form results in the definition of a module or syntactic form or sets a compilation parameter such as " (code "optimize-level") " or " (code "case-sensitive") ".") ("For example, assume that a file contains the following two forms:") ((code "(define-syntax reverse-define" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ e x) (define x e)]))" (br) (br) "(reverse-define 3 three)")) ("Loading this from source has the effect of defining " (code "reverse-define") " as a syntactic form and binding the identifier " (code "three") " to 3. The situation may be different if the file is compiled with " (code "compile-file") ", however. Unless the system or programmer takes steps to assure that the first form is fully executed before the second expression is compiled, the syntax expander will not recognize " (code "reverse-define") " as a syntactic form and will generate code for a procedure call to " (code "reverse-define") "  instead of generating code to define " (code "three") " to be 3. When the object file is subsequently loaded, the attempt to reference either " (code "reverse-define") " or " (code "three") " will fail.") ("As it happens, when a " (code "define-syntax") ", " (code "module") ", " (code "import") ", or " (code "import-only") " form appears at top level, as in the example above, the compiler does indeed arrange to evaluate it before going on to compile the remainder of the file. If the compiler encounters a variable definition for an identifier that was previously something else, it records that fact as well. The compiler also generates the appropriate code so that the bindings will be present as well when the object file is subsequently loaded. This solves most, but not all, problems of this nature, since most are related to the use of " (code "define-syntax") " and modules. Some problems are not so straightforwardly handled, however. For example, assume that the file contains the following definitions for " (code "nodups?") " and " (code "mvlet") ".") ((code "(define nodups?" (br) "  (lambda (ids)" (br) "    (define bound-id-member?" (br) "      (lambda (id ids)" (br) "        (and (not (null? ids))" (br) "             (or (bound-identifier=? id (car ids))" (br) "                 (bound-id-member? id (cdr ids))))))" (br) "    (or (null? ids)" (br) "        (and (not (bound-id-member? (car ids) (cdr ids)))" (br) "             (nodups? (cdr ids))))))" (br) (br) "(define-syntax mvlet" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(_ ((x ...) expr) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (and (andmap identifier? #'(x ...))" (br) "            (nodups? #'(x ...)))" (br) "       #'(call-with-values" (br) "           (lambda () expr)" (br) "           (lambda (x ...) b1 b2 ...))])))" (br) (br) "(mvlet ((a b c) (values 1 2 3))" (br) "  (list (* a a) (* b b) (* c c)))")) ("When loaded directly, this results in the definition of " (code "nodups?") " as a procedure and " (code "mvlet") " as a syntactic abstraction before evaluation of the " (code "mvlet") " expression. Because " (code "nodups?") " is defined before the " (code "mvlet") "  expression is expanded, the call to " (code "nodups?") " during the expansion of " (code "mvlet") " causes no difficulty. If instead this file were compiled, using " (code "compile-file") ", the compiler would arrange to define " (code "mvlet") " before continuing with the expansion and evaluation of the " (code "mvlet") " expression, but it would not arrange to define " (code "nodups?") ". Thus the expansion of the " (code "mvlet") " expression would fail.") ("In this case it does not help to evaluate the syntactic extension alone. A solution in this case would be to move the definition of " (code "nodups?") " inside the definition for " (code "mvlet") ", just as the definition for " (code "bound-id-member?") " is placed within " (code "nodups?") ", but this does not work for help routines shared among several syntactic definitions. Another solution is to label the " (code "nodups?") " definition a \"meta\" definition (see Section " (link "./syntax.html#g111" "11.8") ") but this does not work for helpers that are used both by syntactic abstractions and by run-time code.") ("A somewhat simpler problem occurs when setting parameters that affect compilation, such as " (code "optimize-level") " and " (code "case-sensitive?") ". If not set prior to compilation, their settings usually will not have the desired effect.") ((code "eval-when") " offers a solution to these problems by allowing the programmer to explicitly control what forms should or should not be evaluated during compilation. " (code "eval-when") " is a syntactic form and is handled directly by the expander. The action of " (code "eval-when") " depends upon the " (code (var "situations")) " argument and whether or not the forms " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") "  are being compiled via " (code "compile-file") " or are being evaluated directly. Let's consider each of the possible situation specifiers " (code "eval") ", " (code "compile") ", " (code "load") ", " (code "visit") ", and " (code "revisit") " in turn.") ((dt (code "eval") ":") (dd "The " (code "eval") " specifier is relevant only when the " (code "eval-when") "  form is being evaluated directly, i.e., if it is typed at the keyboard or loaded from a source file. Its presence causes " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") " to be expanded and this expansion to be included in the expansion of the " (code "eval-when") " form. Thus, the forms will be evaluated directly as if not contained within an " (code "eval-when") " form.")) ((dt (code "compile") ":") (dd "The " (code "compile") " specifier is relevant only when the " (code "eval-when") "  form appears in a file currently being compiled. (Its presence is simply ignored otherwise.) Its presence forces " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") " to be expanded and evaluated immediately.")) ((dt (code "load") ":") (dd "The " (code "load") " specifier is also relevant only when the " (code "eval-when") "  form appears in a file currently being compiled. Its presence causes " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") " to be expanded and this expansion to be included in the expansion of the " (code "eval-when") " form. Any code necessary to record binding information and evaluate syntax transformers for definitions contained in the forms is marked for execution when the file is \"visited,\" and any code necessary to compute the values of variable definitions and the expressions contained within the forms is marked for execution when the file is \"revisited.\"")) ((dt (code "visit") ":") (dd "The " (code "visit") " specifier is also relevant only when the " (code "eval-when") "  form appears in a file currently being compiled. Its presence causes " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") " to be expanded and this expansion to be included in the expansion of the " (code "eval-when") " form, with an annotation that the forms are to be executed when the file is \"visited.\"")) ((dt (code "revisit") ":") (dd "The " (code "revisit") " specifier is also relevant only when the " (code "eval-when") "  form appears in a file currently being compiled. Its presence causes " (code (var "form" (sub "1")) " " (var "form" (sub "2")) " ...") " to be expanded and this expansion to be included in the expansion of the " (code "eval-when") " form, with an annotation that the forms are to be executed when the file is \"revisited.\"")) ("A file is considered \"visited\" when it is brought in by either " (code "load") " or " (code "visit") " and \"revisited\" when it is brought in by either " (code "load") " or " (code "revisit") ".") ("Top-level expressions are treated as if they are wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " with situations " (code "load") " and " (code "eval") ". This means that, by default, forms typed at the keyboard or loaded from a source file are evaluated, and forms appearing in a file to be compiled are not evaluated directly but are compiled for execution when the resulting object file is subsequently loaded.") ("The treatment of top-level definitions is slightly more involved. All definitions result in changes to the compile-time environment. For example, an identifier defined by " (code "define") " is recorded as a variable, and an identifier defined by " (code "define-syntax") "  is recorded as a keyword and associated with the value of its right-hand-side (transformer) expression. These changes are made at eval, compile, and load time as if the definitions were wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " with situations " (code "eval") ", " (code "load") ", and " (code "compile") ". (This behavior can be altered by changing the value of the parameter " (code "eval-syntax-expanders-when") ".) Some definitions also result in changes to the run-time environment. For example, a variable is associated with the value of its right-hand-side expression. These changes are made just at evaluation and load time as if the definitions were wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " with situations " (code "eval") " and " (code "load") ".") ("The treatment of local expressions or definitions (those not at top level) that are wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " depends only upon whether the situation " (code "eval") " is present in the list of situations. If the situation " (code "eval") " is present, the definitions and expressions are evaluated as if they were not wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " form, i.e., the " (code "eval-when") " form is treated as a " (code "begin") " form. If the situation " (code "eval") " is not present, the forms are ignored; in a definition context, the " (code "eval-when") " form is treated as an empty " (code "begin") ", and in an expression context, the " (code "eval-when") "  form is treated as a constant with an unspecified value.") ("Since top-level syntax bindings are established, by default, at compile time as well as eval and load time, top-level variable bindings needed by syntax transformers should be wrapped in an " (code "eval-when") " form with situations " (code "compile") ", " (code "load") ", and " (code "eval") ". We can thus " (code "nodups?") " problem above by enclosing the definition of " (code "nodups?") " in an " (code "eval-when") " as follows.") ((code "(eval-when (compile load eval)" (br) "  (define nodups?" (br) "    (lambda (ids)" (br) "      (define bound-id-member?" (br) "        (lambda (id ids)" (br) "          (and (not (null? ids))" (br) "               (or (bound-identifier=? id (car ids))" (br) "                   (bound-id-member? id (cdr ids))))))" (br) "      (or (null? ids)" (br) "          (and (not (bound-id-member? (car ids) (cdr ids)))" (br) "               (nodups? (cdr ids)))))))")) ("This forces it to be evaluated before it is needed during the expansion of the " (code "mvlet") " expression.") ("Just as it is useful to add " (code "compile") " to the default " (code "load") " and " (code "eval") " situations, omitting options is also useful. Omitting one or more of " (code "compile") ", " (code "load") ", and " (code "eval") " has the effect of preventing the evaluation at the given time. Omitting all of the options has the effect of inhibiting evaluation altogether.") ("One common combination of situations is " (code "(compile eval)") ", which by the inclusion of " (code "compile") " causes the expression to be evaluated at compile time, and by the omission of " (code "load") " inhibits the generation of code by the compiler for execution when the file is subsequently loaded. This is typically used for the definition of syntactic extensions used only within the file in which they appear; in this case their presence in the object file is not necessary. It is also used to set compilation parameters that are intended to be in effect whether the file is loaded from source or compiled via " (code "compile-file")) ((code "(eval-when (compile eval) (case-sensitive #t))")) ("Another common situations list is " (code "(compile)") ", which might be used to set compilation options to be used only when the file is compiled via " (code "compile-file") ".") ((code "(eval-when (compile) (optimize-level 3))")) ("Finally, one other common combination is " (code "(load eval)") ", which might be useful for inhibiting the double evaluation (during the compilation of a file and again when the resulting object file is loaded) of syntax definitions when the syntactic extensions are not needed within the file in which their definitions appear.") ("The behavior of " (code "eval-when") " is usually intuitive but can be understood precisely as follows. The " (code "syntax-case") " expander, which handles " (code "eval-when") "  forms, maintains two state sets, one for compile-time forms and one for run-time forms. The set of possible states in each set are \"L\" for " (code "load") ", \"C\" for " (code "compile") ", \"V\" for " (code "visit") ", \"R\" for " (code "revisit") ", and \"E\" for " (code "eval") ".") ("When compiling a file, the compile-time set initially contains \"L\" and \"C\" and the run-time set initially contains only \"L.\" When not compiling a file (as when a form is evaluated by the read-eval-print loop or loaded from a source file), both sets initially contain only \"E.\" The subforms of an " (code "eval-when") " form at top level are expanded with new compile- and run-time sets determined by the current sets and the situations listed in the " (code "eval-when") " form. Each element of the current set contributes zero or more elements to the new set depending upon the given situations according to the following table.") ((table (row (cell "  ") (cell " " (code "load") "  ") (cell "  " (code "compile") "  ") (cell "  " (code "visit") "  ") (cell "  " (code "revisit") "  ") (cell "  " (code "eval"))) (row (cell "L ") (cell "  L  ") (cell "  C  ") (cell "  V  ") (cell "  R  ") (cell " --- ")) (row (cell "C ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell "  C  ")) (row (cell "V ") (cell "  V  ") (cell "  C  ") (cell "  V  ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ")) (row (cell "R ") (cell "  R  ") (cell "  C  ") (cell " --- ") (cell "  R  ") (cell " --- ")) (row (cell "E ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell " --- ") (cell "  E  ")) (row (cell)))) ("For example, if the current compile-time state set is {L} and the situations are " (code "load") " and " (code "compile") ", the new compile-time state set is {L, C}, since L/" (code "load") "  contributes \"L\" and L/" (code "compile") " contributes \"C.\"") ("The state sets determine how forms are treated by the expander. Compile-time forms such as syntax definitions are evaluated at a time or times determined by the compile-time state set, and run-time forms are evaluated at a time or times determined by the run-time state set. A form is evaluated immediately if \"C\" is in the state set. Code is generated to evaluate the form at visit or revisit time if \"V\" or \"R\" is present. If \"L\" is present in the compile-time set, it is treated as \"V;\" likewise, if \"L\" is present in the run-time set, it is treated as \"R.\" If more than one of states is present in the state set, the form is evaluated at each specified time.") ("\"E\" can appear in the state set only when not compiling a file, i.e., when the expander is invoked from an evaluator such as " (code "compile") "  or " (code "interpret") ". When it does appear, the expanded form is returned from the expander to be processed by the evaluator, e.g., " (code "compile") " or " (code "interpret") ", that invoked the expander.") ("The value of the parameter " (code "eval-syntax-expanders-when") " actually determines the initial compile-time state set. The parameter is bound to a list of situations, which defaults to " (code "(compile load eval)") ". When compiling a file, " (code "compile") " contributes \"C\" to the state set, " (code "load") " contributes \"L,\" " (code "visit") " contributes \"V,\" " (code "revisit") " contributes \"R,\" and " (code "eval") "  contributes nothing. When not compiling a file, " (code "eval") " contributes \"E\" to the state set, and the other situations contribute nothing. There is no corresponding parameter for controlling the initial value of the run-time state set.") ("For RNRS top-level programs, " (code "eval-when") " is essentially ineffective. The entire program is treated as a single expression, so " (code "eval-when") "  becomes a local " (code "eval-when") " for which only the " (code "eval") "  situation has any relevance. As for any local " (code "eval-when") " form, the subforms are ignored if the " (code "eval") " situation is not present; otherwise, they are treated as if the " (code "eval-when") " wrapper were absent.")) ("system:s105" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "eval-syntax-expanders-when") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter must be set to a list representing a set of " (code "eval-when") " situations, e.g., a list containing at most one occurrence of each of the symbols " (code "eval") ", " (code "compile") ", " (code "load") ", " (code "visit") ", and " (code "revisit") ". It is used to determine the evaluation time of syntax definitions, module forms, and import forms are expanded. (See the discussion of " (code "eval-when") " above.) The default value is " (code "(compile load eval)") ", which causes compile-time information in a file to be established when the file is loaded from source, when it is compiled via " (code "compile-file") ", and when a compiled version of the file is loaded via " (code "load") "  or " (code "visit") ".")) ("system:s106" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "current-generate-id") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter must be set to a procedure that accepts one argument, a symbol, and returns a fresh symbol.  This procedure is called by the macro expander to get symbols to use for a top-level binding, as a record uid, or to identify a library compilation. The default procedure converts the symbol to a string and passes it to " (code "gensym") ".") ("For example, while expanding the following " (code "library") ", the " (code "current-generate-id") " procedure is called on the symbols " (code "a-lib") ", " (code "a-var") ", and " (code "a-var") " again.") ((code "(library (a-lib)" (br) "  (export a-var)" (br) "  (import (chezscheme))" (br) "  (define a-var 3)" (br) "  (define-syntax def" (br) "    (syntax-rules ()" (br) "      [(_) (define a-var 'other)]))" (br) "  (def))")) ("The procedure held by the " (code "current-generate-id") " parameter is called on " (code "a-lib") "  to generate a symbol that identifies this particular library compilation, as opposed to the compilation of a different library named " (code "(a-lib)") ". It is called on " (code "a-var") " the first time to select a distinct symbol to hold the value of the " (code "a-var") "  defininion, since " (code "a-var") " itself should not be defined directly at the top level. Finally, the " (code "current-generate-id") " procedure is called on " (code "a-var") " to select a name for the macro-introduced definiton of " (code "a-var") " in the expansion of " (code "(def)") "---which turns out to be completely inaccessible since no reference is introduced by the same expansion step.") ("Setting the parameter to a particular, deterministic generator can cause symbols in the expansion of the library to be always the same. That determinism can be helpful if the expansion semantically matches an earlier expansion, so that uses of an earlier compilation remain compatible with the new one. Of course, reusing a symbol runs the risk of creating collisions that the macro and library system normally prevents via distinct symbols, and compiler optimizations may rely on the expectation of distinct symbols for distinct compilations. Configure " (code "current-generate-id") " at your own risk.") ("As a further example, suppose that the following two forms are compiled separately:") ((code ";; Compile this x.ss to x.so" (br) "(library (x)" (br) "  (export x)" (br) "  (import (chezscheme))" (br) "  (define x '(x)))" (br) (br) ";; Compile this y.ss to y.so" (br) "(top-level-program" (br) " (import (chezscheme)" (br) "         (y))" (br) " (printf \"~s\\n\" x))")) ("If " (code "x.ss") " is modified and recompiled, loading the new " (code "x.so") " and the old " (code "y.so") " will correctly report an error that the compiled " (code "y.so") " requires a different compilation instance that the one already loaded.") ("Suppose, however, that you're willing to live dangerously to avoid recompiling " (code "y.ss") " by generating the same symbols for every compilation of " (code "x.ss") ". While compiling " (code "x.ss") ", you could set " (code "current-generate-id") " to the result of " (code "make-x-generator") ":") ((code "(define (make-x-generator)" (br) "  (let ([x-uid \"gf91a5b83ujz3mogjdaij7-x\"]" (br) "        [counter-ht (make-eq-hashtable)])" (br) "    (lambda (sym)" (br) "      (let* ([n (eq-hashtable-ref counter-ht sym 0)]" (br) "             [s ((if (gensym? sym) gensym->unique-string symbol->string) sym)]" (br) "             [g (gensym (symbol->string sym) (format \"~a-~a-~a\" x-uid s n))])" (br) "        (eq-hashtable-set! counter-ht sym (+ n 1))" (br) "        g))))")) ("As long as the first and second compilations of " (code "x.ss") " use the result of " (code "make-x-generator") ", the first compilation of " (code "y.ss") " might work with the second compilation of " (code "x.ss") ", even if the change adds, removes, or reorders definitions of variables other than " (code "x") ".") ("Beware that if the variable " (code "x") " is originally defined as " (code "(define x 1)") ", then the compilation of " (code "y.ss") " likely inlines the value " (code "1") " in place of its reference to the variable " (code "x") ", so changing " (code "x.ss") " will have no effect without recompiling " (code "y.ss") ". Similarly, if the change to " (code "x.ss") "  deletes the definition of " (code "x") " or introduces a macro-generated definition of " (code "x") " before the direct definition, then the previously compiled " (code "y.ss") " is unlikely to refer to the correct definition of " (code "x") " in the new compilation of " (code "x.ss") ". Configure " (code "current-generate-id") " this way only in situations where the potential for unspecified failure is more tolerable than recompilation.")) ("system:s107" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "expand-omit-library-invocations") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This boolean-valued parameter determines whether library uses are recorded in macro expansion. Normally, when an expression expands to a reference to a library-defined identifier, the expansion is prefixed with a check to ensure that the exporting library is defined and invoked. If " (code "expand-omit-library-invocations") " is set to true, the prefix is omitted.") ("Setting " (code "expand-omit-library-invocations") " to true makes sense only when evaluating many small expressions in a context where all referenced libraries are known to be present and already invoked, and only when it's worth saving the small overhead of representing and running the check.")) ("system:s108" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-omit-concatenate-support") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This boolean-valued parameter determines whether code compiled to a file or port includes information needed to support " (code "concatenate-object-files") ". Omitting the information can provide minor space and load-time savings for small compiled objects.")) ("system:s109" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "source-directories") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "source-directories") " must be a list of strings, each of which names a directory path. " (code "source-directories") " determines the set of directories searched for source or object files when a file is loaded via " (code "load") ", " (code "load-library") ", " (code "load-program") ", " (code "include") ", " (code "visit") ", or " (code "revisit") ", when a syntax error occurs, or when a source file is opened in the interactive inspector.") ("The default value is the list " (code "(\".\")") ", which means source files will be found only in or relative to the current directory, unless named with an absolute path or a path that starts with " (code "./") " (or " (code ".\\") "  under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows).") ("This parameter is never altered by the system, with one exception. The expander temporarily adds (via " (code "parameterize") ") the directory in which a library file resides to the front of the " (code "source-directories") "  list when it compiles (when " (code "compile-imported-libraries") " is true) or loads the library from source, which it does only if the library is not already defined.")) ("system:s110" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-source-path " (var "who") " " (var "name") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "with-source-path") " searches through the current source-directories path, in order, for a file with the specified " (code (var "name")) " and invokes " (code (var "procedure")) " on the result. If no such file is found, an exception is raised with condition types " (code "&assertion") " and " (code "&who") " with " (code (var "who")) " as who value.") ("If " (code (var "name")) " is an absolute pathname or one beginning with " (code "./") "  (or " (code ".\\") " under Windows) or " (code "../") " (or " (code "..\\") " under Windows), or if the list of source directories contains only " (code "\".\"") ", the default, or " (code "\"\"") ", which is equivalent to " (code "\".\"") ", no searching is performed and " (code (var "name")) " is returned.") ((code (var "who")) " must be a symbol, " (code (var "name")) " must be a string, and " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one argument.") ("The following examples assumes that the file \"pie\" exists in the directory \"../spam\" but not in \"../ham\" or the current directory.") ((code "(define find-file" (br) "  (lambda (fn)" (br) "    (with-source-path 'find-file fn values)))" (br) (br) "(find-file \"pie\") " (math "=>") " \"pie\"" (br) (br) "(source-directories '(\".\" \"../ham\"))" (br) "(find-file \"pie\") " (math "=>") " " (var "exception in find-file: pie not found") (br) (br) "(source-directories '(\".\" \"../spam\"))" (br) "(find-file \"pie\") " (math "=>") " \"../spam/pie\"" (br) (br) "(source-directories '(\".\" \"../ham\"))" (br) "(find-file \"/pie\") " (math "=>") " \"/pie\"" (br) (br) "(source-directories '(\".\" \"../ham\"))" (br) "(find-file \"./pie\") " (math "=>") " \"./pie\"" (br) (br) "(source-directories '(\".\" \"../spam\"))" (br) "(find-file \"../pie\") " (math "=>") " \"../ham/pie\""))) ("system:s111" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "optimize-level") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter can take on one of the four values 0, 1, 2, and 3.") ("In theory, this parameter controls the amount of optimization performed by the compiler. In practice, it does so only indirectly, and the only difference is between optimize level 3, at which the compiler generates \"unsafe\" code, and optimize levels 0-2, at which the compiler generates \"safe\" code. Safe code performs full type and bounds checking so that, for example, an attempt to apply a non-procedure, an attempt to take the car of a non-pair, or an attempt to reference beyond the end of a vector each result in an exception being raised. With unsafe code, the same situations may result in invalid memory references, corruption of the Scheme heap (which may cause seemingly unrelated problems later), system crashes, or other undesirable behaviors. Unsafe code is typically faster, but optimize-level 3 should be used with caution and only on sections of well-tested code that must run as quickly as possible.") ("While the compiler produces the same code for optimize levels 0-2, user-defined macro transformers can differentiate among the different levels if desired.") ("One way to use optimize levels is on a per-file basis, using " (code "eval-when") " to force the use of a particular optimize level at compile time. For example, placing:") ((code "(eval-when (compile) (optimize-level 3))")) ("at the front of a file will cause all of the forms in the file to be compiled at optimize level 3 when the file is compiled (using " (code "compile-file") ") but does not affect the optimize level used when the file is loaded from source. Since " (code "compile-file") " parameterizes " (code "optimize-level") " (see " (code "parameterize") "), the above expression does not permanently alter the optimize level in the system in which the " (code "compile-file") " is performed.") ("The optimize level can also be set via the " (code "--optimize-level") "  command-line option (Section " (link "./use.html#g14" "2.9") "). This option is particularly useful for running RNRS top-level programs at optimize-level 3 via the " (code "--program") " command-line option, since " (code "eval-when") " is ineffective for RNRS top-level programs as described on page " (link "./system.html#eval-when-tlp" "389") ".")) ("system:s116" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "($primitive " (var "variable") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "#%" (var "variable")) (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "($primitive 2 " (var "variable") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "#2%" (var "variable")) (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "($primitive 3 " (var "variable") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "#3%" (var "variable")) (br) (bold "returns: ") "the primitive value for " (code (var "variable")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "variable")) " must name a primitive procedure. The " (code "$primitive") " syntactic form allows control over the optimize level at the granularity of individual primitive references, and it can be used to access the original value of a primitive, regardless of the lexical context or the current top-level binding for the variable originally bound to the primitive.") ("The expression " (code "($primitive " (var "variable") ")") " may be abbreviated as " (code "#%" (var "variable")) ". The reader expands " (code "#%") " followed by an object into a " (code "$primitive") " expression, much as it expands " (code "'" (var "object")) "  into a " (code "quote") " expression.") ("If a " (code "2") " or " (code "3") " appears in the form or between the " (code "#") " and " (code "%") " in the abbreviated form, the compiler treats an application of the primitive as if it were compiled at the corresponding optimize level (see the " (code "optimize-level") "  parameter). If no number appears in the form, an application of the primitive is treated as an optimize-level 3 application if the current optimize level is 3; otherwise, it is treated as an optimize-level 2 application.") ((code "(#%car '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "(let ([car cdr]) (car '(a b c))) " (math "=>") " (b c)" (br) "(let ([car cdr]) (#%car '(a b c))) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "(begin (set! car cdr) (#%car '(a b c))) " (math "=>") " a"))) ("system:s117" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "debug-level") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter can take on one of the four values 0, 1, 2, and 3. It is used to tell the compiler how important the preservation of debugging information is, with 0 being least important and 3 being most important. The default value is 1.") ("As of Version 9.0, the value of " (code "debug-level") " is used by the system only to discourage optimizations that affect the continuation as revealed by the inspector. The reduced optimization is intended to produce more informative stack backtraces at the point when an exception is raised, and the reduction applies when the debug level is 2 or 3.")) ("system:s118" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-interrupt-trap") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("To support interrupts, including keyboard, timer, and collect request interrupts, the compiler inserts a short sequence of instructions at the entry to each nonleaf procedure (Section " (link "./system.html#g118" "12.2") "). This small overhead may be eliminated by setting " (code "generate-interrupt-trap") " to " (code "#f") ". The default value of this parameter is " (code "#t") ".") ("It is rarely a good idea to compile code without interrupt trap generation, since a tight loop in the generated code may completely prevent interrupts from being serviced, including the collect request interrupt that causes garbage collections to occur automatically. Disabling trap generation may be useful, however, for routines that act simply as \"wrappers\" for other routines for which code is presumably generated with interrupt trap generation enabled. It may also be useful for short performance-critical routines with embedded loops or recursions that are known to be short running and that make no other calls.")) ("system:s119" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-interpret-simple") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("At all optimize levels, when the value of " (code "compile-interpret-simple") " is set to a true value (the default), " (code "compile") " interprets simple expressions. A simple expression is one that creates no procedures. This can save a significant amount of time over the course of many calls to " (code "compile") " or " (code "eval") " (with " (code "current-eval") "  set to " (code "compile") ", its default value). When set to false, " (code "compile") " compiles all expressions.")) ("system:s121" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-inspector-information") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to a true value (the default), information about the source and contents of procedures and continuations is generated during compilation and retained in tables associated with each code segment. This information allows the inspector to provide more complete information, at the expense of using more memory and producing larger object files (via " (code "compile-file") "). Although compilation and loading may be slower when inspector information is generated, the speed of the compiled code is not affected. If this parameter is changed during the compilation of a file, the original value will be restored. For example, if:") ((code "(eval-when (compile) (generate-inspector-information #f))")) ("is included in a file, generation of inspector information will be disabled only for the remainder of that particular file.")) ("system:s122" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-procedure-source-information") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "generate-inspector-information") " is set to " (code "#f") " and this parameter is set to " (code "#t") ", then a source location is preserved for a procedure, even though other inspector information is not preserved. Source information provides a small amount of debugging support at a much lower cost in memory and object-file size than full inspector information. If this parameter is changed during the compilation of a file, the original value will be restored.")) ("system:s123" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-cross-library-optimization") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls whether information is included with the object code for a compiled library to enable propagation of constants and inlining of procedures defined in the library into dependent libraries. When set to " (code "#t") " (the default), this information is included; when set to " (code "#f") ", the information is not included. Setting the parameter to " (code "#f") " potentially reduces the sizes of the resulting object files and the exposure of near-source information via the object file.")) ("system:s124" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-arithmetic-left-associative") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls whether the compiler is constrained to implement " (code "+") ", " (code "fx+") ", " (code "fl+") ", " (code "cfl+") ", " (code "*") ", " (code "fx*") ", " (code "fl*") ", and " (code "cfl*") " as left-associative operations when given more than two arguments. The default is " (code "#f") ".")) ("system:s125" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-error-source-expression") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls whether the compiler can convert erroneous expressions into a call to an error function that shows the expression as an S-expression. The default is " (code "#t") ".")) ("system:s126" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-unsafe-application") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls whether application forms are compiled as unsafe (i.e., no check whether the target is a procedure), even when the value of " (code "optimize-level") " is less than " (code "3") ". The default is " (code "#f") ".")) ("system:s127" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-unsafe-variable-reference") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter controls whether references to " (code "letrec") "-bound variables are compiled unsafe (i.e., no check whether the variable has a value), even when the value of " (code "optimize-level") " is less than " (code "3") ". The default is " (code "#f") ".")) ("system:s128" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-wpo-files") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to " (code "#t") " (the default is " (code "#f") "), " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-library") ", " (code "compile-program") ", and " (code "compile-script") " produce whole-program optimization (wpo) files for use by " (code "compile-whole-program") ". The name of the " (code "wpo") " file is derived from the output-file name by replacing the object-file extension (normally " (code ".so") ") with " (code ".wpo") ", or adding the extension " (code ".wpo") " if the object filename has no extension or has the extension " (code ".wpo") ".")) ("system:s130" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-file-message") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to true, the default, " (code "compile-file") ", " (code "compile-library") ", " (code "compile-program") ", and " (code "compile-script") " print a message of the form:") ((code "compiling " (var "input-path") " with output to " (var "output-path"))) ("When the parameter is set to " (code "#f") ", the message is not printed.")) ("system:s131" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "run-cp0") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "cp0-effort-limit") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "cp0-score-limit") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "cp0-outer-unroll-limit") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These parameters control the operation of " (code "cp0") " plus " (code "cptypes") ", source optimization passes that run after macro expansion and prior to most other compiler passes.") ((code "cp0") " performs procedure inlining, in which the code of one procedure is inlined at points where it is called by other procedures, as well as copy propagation, constant folding, useless code elimination, and several related optimizations. The algorithm used by the optimizer is described in detail in the paper \"Fast and effective procedure inlining\" [" (link "./bibliography.html#g191" "31") "].") ((code "cptypes") " performs additional optimizations based on type information about primitives, such as the fact that " (code "+") " always returns a number; since it primarily removes unnecessary run-time checks, " (code "cptypes") " primarily benefits compilation in safe mode. The " (code "cptypes") " pass can be independently disabled through the " (code "enable-type-recovery") " parameter.") ("When " (code "cp0") " is enabled, the programmer can count on the compiler to fold constants, eliminate unnecessary " (code "let") " bindings, and eliminate unnecessary and inaccessible code. This is particularly useful when writing macros, since the programmer can usually handle only the general case and let the compiler simplify the code when possible. For example, the programmer can define " (code "case") " as follows:") ((code "(define-syntax case" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ e [(k ...) a1 a2 ...] ... [else b1 b2 ...])" (br) "     (let ([t e])" (br) "       (cond" (br) "         [(memv t '(k ...)) a1 a2 ...]" (br) "         ..." (br) "         [else b1 b2 ...]))]" (br) "    [(_ e [(k ...) a1 a2 ...] ...)" (br) "     (let ([t e])" (br) "       (cond" (br) "         [(memv t '(k ...)) a1 a2 ...]" (br) "         ...))]))")) ("and count on the introduced " (code "let") " expression to be eliminated if " (code "e") " turns out to be an unassigned variable, and count on the entire " (code "case") " expression to be folded if " (code "e") " turns out to be a constant.") ("It is possible to see what " (code "cp0") " plus " (code "cptypes") " does with an expression via the procedure " (code "expand/optimize") ", which expands its argument and passes the result through " (code "cp0") " and " (code "cptypes") ", as illustrated by the following transcript.") ((code "> (print-gensym #f)" (br) "> (expand/optimize" (br) "    '(lambda (x)" (br) "       (case x [(a) 1] [(b c) 2] [(d) 3] [else 4])))" (br) "(lambda (x)" (br) "  (if (#2%member x '(a))" (br) "      1" (br) "      (if (#2%member x '(b c)) 2 (if (#2%member x '(d)) 3 4))))" (br) "> (expand/optimize" (br) "    '(+ (let ([f (lambda (x)" (br) "                (case x [(a) 1] [(b c) 2] [(d) 3] [else 4]))])" (br) "          (f 'b))" (br) "         15))" (br) "17")) ("In the first example, the " (code "let") " expression produced by " (code "case") "  is eliminated, and in the second, the entire expression is optimized down to the constant " (code "17") ". Although not shown by " (code "expand/optimize") ", the " (code "member") " calls in the output code for the first example will be replaced by calls to the less expensive " (code "eq?") " by a later pass of the compiler. Additional examples are given in the description of " (code "expand/optimize") ".") ("The value of " (code "run-cp0") " must be a procedure. Whenever the compiler is invoked on a Scheme form, the value " (code (var "p")) "  of this parameter is called to determine whether and how " (code "cp0") " and " (code "cptypes") " are run. " (code (var "p")) " receives two arguments: " (code (var "cp0")) ", the entry point into the " (code "cp0") " plus " (code "cptypes") " passes, and " (code (var "x")) ", the form being compiled. The default value of " (code "run-cp0") " simply invokes " (code (var "cp0")) " on " (code (var "x")) ", then " (code (var "cp0")) " again on the result. The second run is useful in some cases because the first run may not eliminate bindings for certain variables that appear to be referenced but are not actually referenced after inlining. The marginal benefit of the second run is usually minimal, but so is the cost.") ("Interesting variants include") ((code "(run-cp0 (lambda (cp0 x) x))")) ("which bypasses (disables) " (code "cp0") ", and") ((code "(run-cp0 (lambda (cp0 x) (cp0 x)))")) ("which runs " (code "cp0") " just once.") ("The value of " (code "cp0-effort-limit") " determines the maximum amount of effort spent on each inlining attempt. The time spent optimizing a program is a linear function of this limit and the number of calls in the program's source, so small values for this parameter enforce a tighter bound on compile time. When set to zero, inlining is disabled except when the name of a procedure is referenced only once. The value of " (code "cp0-score-limit") " determines the maximum amount of code produced per inlining attempt. Small values for this parameter limit the amount of overall code expansion. These parameters must be set to nonnegative fixnum values.") ("The parameter " (code "cp0-outer-unroll-limit") "  controls the amount of inlining performed by the optimizer for recursive procedures. With the parameter's value set to the default value of " (code "0") ", recursive procedures are not inlined. A nonzero value for the outer unroll limit allows calls external to a recursive procedure to be inlined. For example, the expression") ((code "(letrec ([fact (lambda (x) (if (zero? x) 1 (* x (fact (- x 1)))))])" (br) "  (fact 10))")) ("would be left unchanged with the outer unroll limit set to zero, but would be converted into") ((code "(letrec ([fact (lambda (x) (if (zero? x) 1 (* x (fact (- x 1)))))])" (br) "  (* 10 (fact 9)))")) ("with the outer unroll limit set to one.") ("Interesting effects can be had by varying several of these parameters at once. For example, setting the effort and outer unroll limits to large values and the score limit to " (code "1") " has the effect of inlining even complex recursive procedures whose values turn out to be constant at compile time without risking any code expansion. For example,") ((code "(letrec ([fact (lambda (x) (if (zero? x) 1 (* x (fact (- x 1)))))])" (br) "  (fact 10))")) ("would be reduced to " (code "3628800") ", but") ((code "(letrec ([fact (lambda (x) (if (zero? x) 1 (* x (fact (- x 1)))))])" (br) "  (fact z))")) ("would be left unchanged, although the optimizer may take a while to reach this decision if the effort and outer unroll limits are large.")) ("system:s133" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "enable-type-recovery") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A parameter that controls whether the compiler attempts to optimize programs by performing a pass similar to type inference, which can avoid more checks and statically resolve more predicates than " (code "cp0") " by itself, but at the cost of extra compile time. The default is " (code "#t") ".")) ("system:s134" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "commonization-level") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("After running the main source optimization pass (cp0) for the last time, the compiler optionally runs a " (var "commonization") " pass. The pass commonizes the code for lambda expressions that have identical structure by abstracting differences at certain leaves of the program, namely constants, references to unassigned variables, and references to primitives. The parameter " (code "commonization-level") " controls whether commonization is run and, if so, how aggressive it is. Its value must be a nonnegative exact integer ranging from 0 through 9. When the parameter is set to 0, the default, commonization is not run. Otherwise, higher values result in more commonization.") ("Commonization can undo some of the effects of cp0's inlining, can add run-time overhead, and can complicate debugging, particularly at higher commonization levels, which is why it is disabled by default. On the other hand, for macros or other meta programs that can generate large, mostly similar lambda expressions, enabling commonization can result in significant savings in object-code size and even reduce run-time overhead by making more efficient use of instruction caches.")) ("system:s135" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "undefined-variable-warnings") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When " (code "undefined-variable-warnings") " is set to " (code "#t") ", the compiler issues a warning message whenever it cannot determine that a variable bound by " (code "letrec") ", " (code "letrec*") ", or an internal definition will not be referenced before it is defined. The default value is " (code "#f") ".") ("Regardless of the setting of this parameter, the compiler inserts code to check for the error, except at optimize level 3. The check is fairly inexpensive and does not typically inhibit inlining or other optimizations. In code that must be carefully tuned, however, it is sometimes useful to reorder bindings or make other changes to eliminate the checks. Enabling undefined-variable warnings can facilitate this process.") ("The checks are also visible in the output of " (code "expand/optimize") ".")) ("system:s136" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "expand-output") (br) (bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "expand/optimize-output") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The parameters " (code "expand-output") " and " (code "expand/optimize-output") "  can be used to request that the compiler and interpreter print expander and source-optimizer output produced during the compilation or interpretation process. Each parameter must be set to either " (code "#f") " (the default) or a textual output port.") ("When " (code "expand-output") " is set to a textual output port, the output of the expander is printed to the port as a side effect of running " (code "compile") ", " (code "interpret") ", or any of the file compiling primitives, e.g., " (code "compile-file") " or " (code "compile-library") ". Similarly, when " (code "expand/optimize-output") " is set to a textual output port, the output of the source optimizer is printed.") ("See also " (code "expand") " (page " (link "./system.html#desc:expand" "383") ") and " (code "expand-optimize") " (page " (link "./system.html#desc:expand/optimize" "384") "), which can be used to run the expander or the expander and source optimizer directly on an individual form.")) ("system:s139" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(pariah " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the last subexpression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "pariah") " expression is just like a " (code "begin") " expression except that it informs the compiler that the code is expected to be executed infrequently. The compiler uses this information to optimize code layout, register assignments, and other aspects of the generated code. The " (code "pariah") " form can be used in performance-critical code to mark the branches of a conditional (e.g., " (code "if") ", " (code "cond") ", or " (code "case") ") that are less likely to be executed than the others.")) ("system:s152" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "compile-profile") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to the symbol " (code "source") " or the boolean value " (code "#t") ", the compiler instruments the code it generates with instructions that count the number of times each section of source code is executed. When set to the symbol " (code "block") ", the compiler similarly instruments the code it generates with instructions that count the number of times each block of code is executed. When set to " (code "#f") " (the default), the compiler does not insert these instructions.") ("The general description of profiling above describes how the source and block profile information can be viewed or used for optimization.") ("The code generated when " (code "compile-profile") " is non-false is larger and less efficient, so this parameter should be set only when profile information is needed.") ("The profile counters for code compiled when profile instrumentation is enabled are retained indefinitely, even if the code with which they are associated is reclaimed by the garbage collector. This results in more complete and accurate profile data but can lead to space leaks in programs that dynamically generate or load code. Such programs can avoid the potential space leak by releasing the counters explicitly via the procedure " (code "profile-release-counters") ".")) ("system:s154" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-covin-files") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to " (code "#t") ", the compiler generates \"coverage-information\" (covin) files that can be used in connection with profile information to measure coverage of a source-code base by a set of tests. One covin file is created for each object file, with the object-file extension replaced by the extension " (code ".covin") ". Each covin file contains the printed representation of a source table (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g115" "11.12") "), compressed using the compression format and level specified by " (code "compress-format") " and " (code "compress-level") ". This information can be read via " (code "get-source-table!") " and used as a universe of source expressions to identify source expressions that are not evaluated during the running of a set of tests.")) ("system:s156" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(profile " (var "source-object") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("A " (code "profile") " form has the effect of accounting to the source position identified by " (code (var "source-object")) " the number of times the " (code "profile") " form is executed. Profile forms are generated implicitly by the expander for source expressions in annotated input, e.g., input read by the compiler or interpreter from a Scheme source file, so this form is typically useful only when unannotated source code is produced by the front end for some language that targets Scheme.")) ("system:s157" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "(generate-profile-forms)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter is set to " (code "#t") ", the default, the expander implicitly introduces " (code "profile") " forms for each annotated input expression, unless the annotation has not been marked for use in profiling  (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") "). It can be set to " (code "#f") " to inhibit the expander's implicit generation of " (code "profile") " forms, typically when explicit " (code "profile") " forms are already present for all source positions that should be profiled.")) ("system:s158" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-clear)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Calling this procedure causes profile information to be cleared, i.e., the counts associated with each section of code are set to zero.")) ("system:s159" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-release-counters)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Calling this procedure causes profile information associated with reclaimed code objects to be dropped.")) ("system:s160" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of pairs of source-object and count" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure produces a dump of all profile information gathered since startup or the last call to " (code "profile-clear") ". It returns a list of pairs, where the car of each pair is a source object (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") and the cdr is an exact nonnegative integer count.") ("The list might contain more than one entry per source object due to macro expansion and procedure inlining, and it might contain more than one (non-eq) source object per file and source position due to separate compilation. In such cases, the counts are not overlapping and can be summed together to obtain the full count.") ("The advantage of " (code "profile-dump") " over " (code "profile-dump-list") "  is that " (code "profile-dump") " performs only minimal processing and preserves complete source objects, including their embedded source-file descriptors. It might be used, for example, to dump profile information to a fasl file on one machine for subsequent processing on another.") ((code "with-profile-tracker") "  can be used to obtain the same set of counts as a source table.")) ("system:s162" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-profile-tracker " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-profile-tracker " (var "preserve-existing?") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a source table and the values returned by " (code (var "thunk")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments. It may return any number of values.") ((code "with-profile-tracker") " invokes " (code (var "thunk")) " without arguments. If " (code (var "thunk")) " returns " (var "n") " values " (code (var "x" (sub "1")) ", " (var "x" (sub "2")) ", ..., " (var "x" (sub "n"))) ", " (code "with-profile-tracker") "  returns " (var "n") " + 1 values " (code (var "st") ", " (var "x" (sub "1")) ", " (var "x" (sub "2")) ", ..., " (var "x" (sub "n"))) ", where " (code (var "st")) " is a source table associating source objects with profile counts. If " (code (var "preserve-existing?")) " is absent or " (code "#f") ", each count represents the number of times the source expression represented by the associated source object is evaluated during the invocation of " (code (var "thunk")) ". Otherwise, each count represents the number of times the source expression represented by the associated source object is evaluated before or during the invocation of " (code (var "thunk")) ".") ("Profile data otherwise cleared by a call to " (code "profile-clear") " or " (code "profile-release-counters") "  during the invocation of " (code (var "thunk")) " is included in the resulting table. That is, invoking these procedures while " (code (var "thunk")) " is running has no effect on the resulting counts. On the other hand, profile data cleared before " (code "with-profile-tracker") "  is invoked is not included in the resulting table.") ("The idiom " (code "(with-profile-tracker #t values)") " can be used to obtain the current set of profile counts as a source table.")) ("system:s165" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(source-table-dump " (var "source-table") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of pairs of source objects and their associated values in " (code (var "source-table")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure can be used to convert a source-table produced by " (code "with-profile-tracker") " or some other mechanism into the form returned by " (code "profile-dump") " for use as an argument to " (code "profile-dump-html") ", " (code "profile-dump-list") ", or " (code "profile-dump-data") ".")) ("system:s171" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-html)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-html " (var "prefix") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-html " (var "prefix") " " (var "dump") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure produces one or more HTML files, including profile.html, which contains color-coded summary information, and one file " (code (var "source")) ".html for each source file " (code (var "source")) " containing a color-coded copy of the source code, as described in the lead-in to this section. If " (code (var "prefix")) " is specified, it must be a string and is prepended to the names of the generated HTML files. For example, if " (code (var "prefix")) " is " (code "\"/tmp/\"") ", the generated files are placed in the directory /tmp. The raw profile information is obtained from " (code (var "dump")) ", which defaults to the value returned by " (code "profile-dump") ".")) ("system:s172" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "(profile-palette)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This value of this parameter must be a nonempty vector of at least three pairs. The car of each pair is a background color and the cdr is a foreground (text) color. Each color must be a string, and each string should contain an HTML cascading style sheet (CSS) color specifier. The first pair is used for unprofiled code, and the second is used for unexecuted profiled code. The third is used for code that is executed least frequently, the fourth for code executed next-least frequently, and so on, with the last being used for code that is executed most frequently. Programmers may wish to supply their own palette to enhance visibility or to change the number of colors used.") ("By default, a black background is used for unprofiled code, and a gray background is used for unexecuted profiled code. Background colors ranging from purple to red are used for executed profiled code, depending on frequency of execution, with red for the most frequently executed code.") ((code "(profile-palette) " (math "=>") (br) "  #((\"#111111\" . \"white\") (\"#607D8B\" . \"white\")" (br) "    (\"#9C27B0\" . \"black\") (\"#673AB7\" . \"white\")" (br) "    (\"#3F51B5\" . \"white\") (\"#2196F3\" . \"black\")" (br) "    (\"#00BCD4\" . \"black\") (\"#4CAF50\" . \"black\")" (br) "    (\"#CDDC39\" . \"black\") (\"#FFEB3B\" . \"black\")" (br) "    (\"#FFC107\" . \"black\") (\"#FF9800\" . \"black\")" (br) "    (\"#F44336\" . \"white\"))" (br) "(profile-palette" (br) " ; set palette with rainbow colors and black text" (br) " ; for all but unprofiled or unexecuted code" (br) "  '#((\"#000000\" . \"white\")    ; black" (br) "     (\"#666666\" . \"white\")    ; gray" (br) "     (\"#8B00FF\" . \"black\")    ; violet" (br) "     (\"#6600FF\" . \"black\")    ; indigo" (br) "     (\"#0000FF\" . \"black\")    ; blue" (br) "     (\"#00FF00\" . \"black\")    ; green" (br) "     (\"#FFFF00\" . \"black\")    ; yellow" (br) "     (\"#FF7F00\" . \"black\")    ; orange" (br) "     (\"#FF0000\" . \"black\")))  ; red"))) ("system:s173" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "(profile-line-number-color)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This value of this parameter must be a string or " (code "#f") ". If it is a string, the string should contain an HTML cascading style sheet (CSS) color specifier. If the parameter is set to a string, " (code "profile-dump-html") " includes line numbers in its html rendering of each source file, using the specified color. If the parameter is set to " (code "#f") ", no line numbers are included.")) ("system:s174" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-list)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-list " (var "warn?") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-list " (var "warn?") " " (var "dump") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of profile entries (see below)" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure produces a dump of all profile information present in " (code (var "dump")) ", which defaults to the value returned by " (code "profile-dump") ". It returns a list of entries, each of which is itself a list containing the following elements identifying one block of code and how many times it has been executed.   " (ul " " (li "execution count ") (li "pathname ") (li "beginning file position in characters (inclusive) ") (li "ending file position in characters (exclusive) ") (li "line number of beginning file position ") (li "character position of beginning file position"))) ((code "profile-dump-list") " may be unable to locate an unmodified copy of the file in the current source directories or at the absolute address, if an absolute address was used when the file was compiled or loaded. If this happens, the line number and character position of the beginning file position are " (code "#f") " and the pathname is the pathname originally used. A warning is also issued (an exception with condition type " (code "&warning") " is raised) unless the " (code "warn?") " argument is provided and is false.") ("Otherwise, the pathname is the path to an unmodified copy of the source and the line and character positions are set to exact nonnegative integers.") ("In either case, the execution count, beginning file position, and ending file position are all exact nonnegative integers, and the pathname is a string.") ("For source positions in files that cannot be found, the list might contain more than one entry per position due to macro expansion, procedure inlining, and separate compilation. In such cases, the counts are not overlapping and can be summed together to obtain the full count.") ("The information returned by " (code "profile-dump-list") " can be used to implement a custom viewer or used as input for offline analysis of profile information.") ("The advantage of " (code "profile-dump-list") " over " (code "profile-dump") "  is that it attempts to determine the line number and character position for each source point and, if successful, aggregates multiple counts for the source point into a single entry.")) ("system:s175" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-data " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-dump-data " (var "path") " " (var "dump") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string.") ("This procedure writes, in a machine-readable form consumable by " (code "profile-load-data") ", profile counts represented by " (code (var "dump")) "  to the file named by " (code (var "path")) ", replacing the file if it already exists. " (code (var "dump")) " defaults to the value returned by " (code "profile-dump") ".")) ("system:s176" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-load-data " (var "path") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each " (code (var "path")) " must be a string.") ("This procedure reads profile information from the files named by " (code (var "path") " ...") " and stores it in the compiler's internal database of profile information. The contents of the files must have been created originally by " (code "profile-dump-data") " using the same version of " (var "Chez Scheme") ".") ("The database stores a weight for each source expression or block rather than the actual count. When a single file is loaded into the database, the weight is the proportion of the actual count over the maximum count for all expressions or blocks represented in the file. When more than one file is loaded, either by one or multiple calls to " (code "profile-load-data") ", the weights are averaged.")) ("system:s177" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-query-weight " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code (var "obj")) "'s profile weight, or " (code "#f") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is not in the database" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The compiler's profile database maps source objects (Section " (link "./syntax.html#g114" "11.11") ") to weights. If " (code (var "obj")) " is a source object, the " (code "profile-query-weight") " returns the weight associated with the source object or " (code "#f") " if the database does not have a weight recorded for the source object. " (code (var "obj")) " can also be an annotation or syntax object, in which case " (code "profile-query-weight") " first extracts the source object, if any, using " (code "syntax->annotation") " and " (code "annotation-source") ", returning " (code "#f") " if no source-object is found.") ("A weight is a flonum in the range 0.0 to 1.0, inclusive, and denotes the ratio of the actual count to the maximum count as described in the description of " (code "profile-load-data") ".") ((code "profile-query-weight") " can be used by a macro to determine the relative frequency with which its subexpressions were executed in the run or runs that generated the information in the database. This information can be used to guide the generation of code that is likely to be more efficient. For example, the " (code "case") " macro uses profile information, when available, to order the clauses so that those whose keys matched more frequently are tested before those whose keys matched less frequently.")) ("system:s178" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(profile-clear-database)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure clears the compiler's profile database. It has no impact on the counts associated with individual sections of instrumented code; " (code "profile-clear") " can be used to reset those counts.")) ("system:s179" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(new-cafe)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(new-cafe " (var "eval-proc") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((var "Chez Scheme") " interacts with the user through a " (var "waiter") ", or read-eval-print loop (REPL). The waiter operates within a context called a " (var "café") ". When the system starts up, the user is placed in a café and given a waiter. " (code "new-cafe") " opens a new Scheme café, stacked on top of the old one. In addition to starting the waiter, " (code "new-cafe") " sets up the café's reset and exit handlers (see " (code "reset-handler") " and " (code "exit-handler") "). Exiting a café resumes the continuation of the call to " (code "new-cafe") " that created the café. Exiting from the initial café leaves Scheme altogether. A café may be exited from either by an explicit call to " (code "exit") " or by receipt of end-of-file (\"control-D\" on Unix systems) in response to the waiter's prompt. In the former case, any values passed to " (code "exit") " are returned from " (code "new-cafe") ".") ("If the optional " (code (var "eval-proc")) " argument is specified, " (code (var "eval-proc")) "  is used to evaluate forms entered from the console. Otherwise, the value of the parameter " (code "current-eval") " is used. " (code (var "eval-proc")) " must accept one argument, the expression to evaluate.") ("Interesting values for " (code (var "eval-proc")) " include " (code "expand") ", which causes the macro expanded value of each expression entered to be printed and " (code "(lambda (x) x)") ", which simply causes each expression entered to be printed. An arbitrary procedure of one argument may be used to facilitate testing of a program on a series of input values.") ((code "> (new-cafe (lambda (x) x))" (br) ">> 3" (br) "3" (br) ">> (a . (b . (c . ())))" (br) "(a b c)")) ((code "(define sum" (br) "  (lambda (ls)" (br) "    (if (null? ls)" (br) "        0" (br) "        (+ (car ls) (sum (cdr ls))))))" (br) "> (new-cafe sum)" (br) ">> (1 2 3)" (br) "6")) ("The default waiter reader (see " (code "waiter-prompt-and-read") ") displays the current waiter prompt (see " (code "waiter-prompt-string") ") to the current value of " (code "console-output-port") " and reads from the current value of " (code "console-input-port") ". The default waiter printer (see " (code "waiter-write") ") sends output to the current value of " (code "console-output-port") ". These parameters, along with " (code "current-eval") ", can be modified to change the behavior of the waiter.")) ("system:s186" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "waiter-prompt-string") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "waiter-prompt-string") " must be a string. It is used by the default waiter prompter (see the parameter " (code "waiter-prompt-and-read") ") to print a prompt. Nested cafés are marked by repeating the prompt string once for each nesting level.") ((code "> (waiter-prompt-string)" (br) "\">\"" (br) "> (waiter-prompt-string \"%\")" (br) "% (waiter-prompt-string)" (br) "\"%\"" (br) "% (new-cafe)" (br) "%% (waiter-prompt-string)" (br) "\"%\""))) ("system:s187" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "waiter-prompt-and-read") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "waiter-prompt-and-read") " must be set to a procedure. It is used by the waiter to print a prompt and read an expression. The value of " (code "waiter-prompt-and-read") " is called by the waiter with a positive integer that indicates the café nesting level. It should return an expression to be evaluated by the current evaluator (see " (code "new-cafe") " and " (code "current-eval") ").")) ("system:s188" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(default-prompt-and-read " (var "level") ")") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "level")) " must be a positive integer indicating the cafeé nesting level as described above.") ("This procedure is the default value of the " (code "waiter-prompt-and-read") "  parameter whenever the expression editor (Section " (link "./use.html#g7" "2.2") ", Chapter " (link "./expeditor.html#g140" "14") ") is " (var "not") " enabled. It might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define default-prompt-and-read" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (unless (and (integer? n) (>= n 0))" (br) "       (assertion-violationf 'default-prompt-and-read" (br) "         \"~s is not a nonnegative integer\"" (br) "         n))" (br) "    (let ([prompt (waiter-prompt-string)])" (br) "      (unless (string=? prompt \"\")" (br) "        (do ([n n (- n 1)])" (br) "            ((= n 0)" (br) "             (write-char #\\space (console-output-port))" (br) "             (flush-output-port (console-output-port)))" (br) "            (display prompt (console-output-port))))" (br) "      (let ([x (read (console-input-port))])" (br) "         (when (and (eof-object? x) (not (string=? prompt \"\")))" (br) "            (newline (console-output-port))" (br) "            (flush-output-port (console-output-port)))" (br) "         x))))"))) ("system:s189" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "waiter-write") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "waiter-write") " must be a procedure. The waiter uses the value of " (code "waiter-write") " to print the results of each expression read and evaluated by the waiter. The following example installs a procedure equivalent to the default " (code "waiter-write") ":") ((code "(waiter-write" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (unless (eq? x (void))" (br) "      (pretty-print x (console-output-port)))" (br) "    (flush-output-port (console-output-port))))"))) ("system:s190" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(reset)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "reset") " invokes the current reset handler (see " (code "reset-handler") ") without arguments.")) ("system:s191" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "reset-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure and should accept zero arguments. The current reset handler is called by " (code "reset") ". The default reset handler resets to the current café.")) ("system:s192" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(exit " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "exit") " invokes the current exit handler (see " (code "exit-handler") "), passing along its arguments, if any.")) ("system:s193" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "exit-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure and should accept any number of arguments. The current exit handler is called by " (code "exit") ".") ("The default exit handler exits from the current café, returning its arguments as the values of the call to " (code "new-cafe") " that created the current café. If the current café is the original café, or if " (code "exit") "  is called from a script, " (code "exit") " exits from Scheme. In this case, the exit code for the Scheme process is 0 if no arguments were supplied or if the first argument is void, the value of the first argument cast to a C int if it is an exact integer of the host machine's bit width, and 1 otherwise.")) ("system:s194" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(abort)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(abort " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "abort") " invokes the current abort handler (see " (code "abort-handler") "), passing along its argument, if any.")) ("system:s195" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "abort-handler") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be a procedure and should accept either zero arguments or one argument. The current abort handler is called by " (code "abort") ".") ("The default abort handler exits the Scheme process. The exit code for the Scheme process is -1 if no arguments were supplied, 0 if the first argument is void, the value of the first argument if it is a 32-bit exact integer, and -1 otherwise.")) ("system:s196" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "scheme-start") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "scheme-start") " is a procedure that determines the system's action upon start-up. The procedure receives zero or more arguments, which are strings representing the file names (or command-line arguments not recognized by the Scheme executable) after given on the command line. The default value first loads the files named by the arguments, then starts up the initial café:") ((code "(lambda fns" (br) "  (for-each load fns)" (br) "  (new-cafe))")) ((code "scheme-start") " may be altered to start up an application or to perform customization prior to normal system start-up.") ("To have any effect, this parameter must be set within a boot file. (See Chapter " (link "./use.html#g5" "2") ".)")) ("system:s197" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "scheme-script") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "scheme-script") " is a procedure that determines the system's action upon start-up, when the " (code "--script") " option is used. The procedure receives one or more arguments. The first is a string identifying the script filename and the remainder are strings representing the remaining file names (or command-line arguments not recognized by the Scheme executable) given on the command line. The default value of this parameter is a procedure that sets the " (code "command-line") " and " (code "command-line-arguments") " parameters, loads the script using " (code "load") ", and returns void, which is translated into a 0 exit status for the script process.") ((code "(lambda (fn . fns)" (br) "  (command-line (cons fn fns))" (br) "  (command-line-arguments fns)" (br) "  (load fn))")) ((code "scheme-script") " may be altered to start up an application or to perform customization prior to normal system start-up.") ("To have any effect, this parameter must be set within a boot file. (See Chapter " (link "./use.html#g5" "2") ".)")) ("system:s201" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "scheme-program") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "scheme-program") " is a procedure that determines the system's action upon start-up when the " (code "--program") " (RNRS top-level program) option is used. The procedure receives one or more arguments. The first is a string identifying the program filename and the remainder are strings representing the remaining file names (or command-line arguments not recognized by the Scheme executable) given on the command line. The default value of this parameter is a procedure that sets the " (code "command-line") " and " (code "command-line-arguments") " parameters, loads the program using " (code "load-program") ", and returns void, which is translated into a 0 exit status for the script process.") ((code "(lambda (fn . fns)" (br) "  (command-line (cons fn fns))" (br) "  (command-line-arguments fns)" (br) "  (load-program fn))")) ((code "scheme-program") " may be altered to start up an application or to perform customization prior to normal system start-up.") ("To have any effect, this parameter must be set within a boot file. (See Chapter " (link "./use.html#g5" "2") ".)")) ("system:s205" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "command-line") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter is set by the default values of " (code "scheme-script") "  and " (code "scheme-program") "  to a list representing the command line, with the script name followed by the command-line arguments, when the " (code "--script") " or " (code "--program") " option is used on system startup.")) ("system:s207" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "command-line-arguments") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This parameter is set by the default values of " (code "scheme-script") "  and " (code "scheme-program") "  to a list of the command-line arguments when the " (code "--script") "  or " (code "--program") " option is used on system startup.")) ("system:s209" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "suppress-greeting") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "suppress-greeting") " is a boolean value that determines whether " (var "Chez Scheme") " prints an identifying banner and copyright notice. The parameter defaults to " (code "#f") " but may be set to " (code "#t") " for use in batch processing applications where the banner would be disruptive.") ("To have any effect, this parameter must be set within a boot file. (See Chapter " (link "./use.html#g5" "2") ".)")) ("system:s211" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(transcript-on " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string.") ((code "transcript-on") " opens the file named by " (code (var "path")) " for output, and it copies to this file all input from the current input port and all output to the current output port. An exception is raised with condition-type " (code "i/o-filename") " if the file cannot be opened for output.")) ("system:s212" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(transcript-off)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "transcript-off") " ends transcription and closes the transcript file.")) ("system:s213" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(transcript-cafe " (var "path") ")") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "path")) " must be a string. " (code "transcript-cafe") " opens a transcript file as with " (code "transcript-on") " and enters a new café; exiting from this café (see " (code "exit") ") also ends transcription and closes the transcript file. Invoking " (code "transcript-off") " while in a transcript café ends transcription and closes the transcript file but does not cause an exit from the café.")) ("system:s214" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-time)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-time " (var "time-type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a time object representing the current time" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "time-type")) " must be one of the time-type symbols listed above and defaults to " (code "time-utc") ".") ((code "(current-time) " (math "=>") " #<time-utc 1198815722.473668000>" (br) "(current-time 'time-process) " (math "=>") " #<time-process 0.120534264>"))) ("system:s215" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-time " (var "type") " " (var "nsec") " " (var "sec") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a time object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "type")) " must be one of the time-type symbols listed above. " (code (var "nsec")) " represents nanoseconds and must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 10" (sup "9") ". " (code (var "sec")) " represents seconds and must be an exact integer.") ((code "(make-time 'time-utc 787511000 1198783214)" (br) "(make-time 'time-duration 10 5)" (br) "(make-time 'time-duration 10 -5)"))) ("system:s216" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a time object, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(time? (current-time)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(time? (make-time 'time-utc 0 0)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(time? \"1400 hours\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("system:s217" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-type " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the time type of " (code (var "time")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-nanosecond " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the nanosecond of " (code (var "time")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-second " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the second of " (code (var "time")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "time")) " must be a time object.") ((code "(time-type (current-time)) " (math "=>") " time-utc" (br) "(time-type (current-time 'time-process)) " (math "=>") " time-process" (br) "(time-type (make-time 'time-duration 0 50)) " (math "=>") " time-duration" (br) "(time-second (current-time)) " (math "=>") " 1198816497" (br) "(time-nanosecond (current-time)) " (math "=>") " 2399000" (br) "(time-second (make-time 'time-duration 10 -5)) " (math "=>") " -5" (br) "(time-nanosecond (make-time 'time-duration 10 -5)) " (math "=>") " 10"))) ("system:s218" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-time-type! " (var "time") " " (var "type") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-time-nanosecond! " (var "time") " " (var "nsec") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-time-second! " (var "time") " " (var "sec") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "time")) " must be a time object. " (code (var "type")) " must be one of the time-type symbols listed above. " (code (var "nsec")) " represents nanoseconds and must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 10" (sup "9") ". " (code (var "sec")) " represents seconds and must be an exact integer.") ("Each of these procedures modifies the time object, changing one aspect while leaving the others unaffected. For example, " (code "set-time-nanosecond!") " changes the nanosecond of " (code (var "time")) " without changing the second or type. In particular, no conversion of values is performed when the type of a time object is changed.")) ("system:s219" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time=? " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time<? " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time<=? " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time>=? " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time>? " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the relation holds, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "time" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "time" (sub "2"))) " must be time objects and must have the same type.") ((code "(let ([t (current-time)])" (br) "  (time=? t t)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(let ([t (current-time)])" (br) "  (let loop ()" (br) "    (when (time=? (current-time) t))" (br) "      (loop))" (br) "  (time>? (current-time) t)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("system:s220" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(copy-time " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a copy of " (code (var "time")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(define t1 (current-time))" (br) "(define t2 (copy-time t1))" (br) "(eq? t2 t1) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(eqv? (time-second t2) (time-second t1)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(eqv? (time-nanosecond t2) (time-nanosecond t1)) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("system:s221" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-difference " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of subtracting " (code (var "time" (sub "2"))) " from " (code (var "time" (sub "1"))) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-difference! " (var "time" (sub "1")) " " (var "time" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of subtracting " (code (var "time" (sub "2"))) " from " (code (var "time" (sub "1"))) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(add-duration " (var "time") " " (var "time" (sub "d")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of adding " (code (var "time" (sub "d"))) " to " (code "time") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(add-duration! " (var "time") " " (var "time" (sub "d")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of adding " (code (var "time" (sub "d"))) " to " (code "time") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(subtract-duration " (var "time") " " (var "time" (sub "d")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of subtracting " (code (var "time" (sub "d"))) " from " (code "time") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(subtract-duration! " (var "time") " " (var "time" (sub "d")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the result of subtracting " (code (var "time" (sub "d"))) " from " (code "time") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("For " (code "time-difference") ", " (code (var "time" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "time" (sub "2"))) " must have the same time type, and the result is a time object with time type " (code "time-duration") ". For " (code "add-duration") ", " (code "add-duration!") ", " (code "subtract-duration") ", and " (code "subtract-duration!") ", " (code (var "time" (sub "d"))) " must have time type " (code "time-duration") ", and the result is a time object with the same time type as " (code (var "time")) ". " (code "time-difference!") ", " (code "add-duration!") ", and " (code "subtract-duration!") " are potentially destructive, i.e., each might modify and return its first argument, or it might allocate a new time object.") ((code "(let ([delay (make-time 'time-duration 0 1)])" (br) "  (let ([t1 (current-time 'time-monotonic)])" (br) "    (sleep delay)" (br) "    (let ([t2 (current-time 'time-monotonic)])" (br) "      (let ([t3 (time-difference t2 t1)])" (br) "        (and" (br) "          (eq? (time-type t3) 'time-duration)" (br) "          (time>=? t3 delay)" (br) "          (time=? (add-duration t1 t3) t2)" (br) "          (time=? (subtract-duration t2 t3) t1)))))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("system:s222" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-date)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-date " (var "offset") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a date object representing the current date" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "offset")) " represents the time-zone offset in seconds east of UTC, as described above. It must be an exact integer in the range -86400 to +86400, inclusive and defaults to the local time-zone offset. UTC may be obtained by passing an offset of zero.") ("If " (code (var "offset")) " is not provided, then the current time zone's offset is used, and " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " report information about the time zone. If " (code (var "offset")) " is provided, then " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " on the resulting date object produce " (code "#f") ".") ("The following examples assume the local time zone is EST.") ((code "(current-date) " (math "=>") " #<date Thu Dec 27 23:23:20 2007>" (br) "(current-date 0) " (math "=>") " #<date Fri Dec 28 04:23:20 2007>" (br) (br) "(date-zone-name (current-date)) " (math "=>") " \"EST\" " (var "or other system-provided string") (br) "(date-zone-name (current-date 0)) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("system:s223" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-date " (var "nsec") " " (var "sec") " " (var "min") " " (var "hour") " " (var "day") " " (var "mon") " " (var "year") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-date " (var "nsec") " " (var "sec") " " (var "min") " " (var "hour") " " (var "day") " " (var "mon") " " (var "year") " " (var "offset") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a date object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "nsec")) " represents nanoseconds and must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 10" (sup "9") ". " (code (var "sec")) " represents seconds and must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 62. " (code (var "min")) " represents minutes and must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 60. " (code (var "hour")) " must be an exact nonnegative integer less than 24. " (code (var "day")) " must be an exact integer, 1 " (math "<=") " " (var "day") " " (math "<=") " 31. (The actual upper limit may be less depending on the month and year.) " (code (var "mon")) " represents the month must be an exact integer, 1 " (math "<=") " " (var "mon") " " (math "<=") " 12. " (code (var "year")) " must be an exact integer. It should be at least 1970. " (code (var "offset")) " represents the time-zone offset in seconds east of UTC, as described above. It must be an exact integer in the range -86400 to +86400, inclusive. UTC may be specified by passing an offset of zero.") ("If " (code (var "offset")) " is not provided, then the current time zone's offset is used, and " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " report information about the time zone. If " (code (var "offset")) " is provided, then " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " on the resulting date object produce " (code "#f") ".") ((code "(make-date 0 0 0 0 1 1 1970 0) " (math "=>") " #<date Thu Jan  1 00:00:00 1970>" (br) "(make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400) " (math "=>") " #<date Sun Sep 23 09:07:30 2007>" (br) (br) "(date-zone-name (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(string? (date-zone-name (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("system:s224" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a date object, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "(date? (current-date))" (br) "(date? (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400))" (br) "(date? \"Sun Sep 23 09:07:30 2007\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("system:s225" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-nanosecond " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the nanosecond of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-second " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the second of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-minute " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the minute of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-hour " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the hour of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-day " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the day of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-month " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the month of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-year " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the year of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-zone-offset " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the time-zone offset of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "date")) " must be a date object.") ((code "(define d (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400))" (br) "(date-nanosecond d) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(date-second d) " (math "=>") " 30" (br) "(date-minute d) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) "(date-hour d) " (math "=>") " 9" (br) "(date-day d) " (math "=>") " 23" (br) "(date-month d) " (math "=>") " 9" (br) "(date-year d) " (math "=>") " 2007" (br) "(date-zone-offset d) " (math "=>") " -14400"))) ("system:s226" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-week-day " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the week-day of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-year-day " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the year-day of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures allow the day-of-week or day-of-year to be determined for the date represented by " (code (var "date")) ". A week-day is an exact nonnegative integer less than 7, where 0 represents Sunday, 1 represents Monday, and so on. A year-day is an exact nonnegative integer less than 367, where 0 represents the first day of the year (January 1), 1 the second day, 2 the third, and so on.") ((code "(define d1 (make-date 0 0 0 0 1 1 1970 -18000))" (br) "d1 " (math "=>") " #<date Thu Jan  1 00:00:00 1970>" (br) "(date-week-day d1) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) "(date-year-day d1) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) (br) "(define d2 (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400))" (br) "d2 " (math "=>") " #<date Sun Sep 23 09:07:30 2007>" (br) "(date-week-day d2) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(date-year-day d2) " (math "=>") " 265"))) ("system:s227" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-dst? " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "whether " (code (var "date")) " is in Daylight Saving Time" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-zone-name " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#f") " or a string naming the time zone of " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures report time-zone information for the date represented by " (code (var "date")) " for a date object that is constructed without an explicit time-zone offset. When a date object is created instead with explicit time-zone offset, these procedures produce " (code "#f") ".") ("Daylight Saving Time status for the current time zone and a name string for the time zone are computed using platform-specific routines. In particular, the format of the zone name is platform-specific.") ((code "(define d (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007))" (br) "(date-zone-offset d) " (math "=>") " -14400 " (var "assuming Eastern U.S. time zone") (br) "(date-dst? d) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(date-zone-name d) " (math "=>") " \"EDT\" " (var "or some system-provided string")))) ("system:s228" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-utc->date " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(time-utc->date " (var "time") " " (var "offset") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a date object corresponding to " (code (var "time")) (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date->time-utc " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a time object corresponding to " (code (var "date")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures are used to convert between time and date objects. The " (code (var "time")) " argument to " (code "time-utc->date") " must have time-type " (code "utc") ", and " (code "date->time-utc") " always returns a time object with time-type " (code "utc") ".") ("For " (code "time-utc->date") ", " (code (var "offset")) " represents the time-zone offset in seconds east of UTC, as described at the beginning of this section. It must be an exact integer in the range -86400 to +86400, inclusive and defaults to the local time-zone offset. UTC may be obtained by passing an offset of zero.") ("If " (code (var "offset")) " is not provided to " (code "time-utc->date") ", then the current time zone's offset is used, and " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " report information about the time zone. If " (code (var "offset")) " is provided, then " (code "date-dst?") " and " (code "date-zone-name") " on the resulting date object produce " (code "#f") ".") ((code "(define d (make-date 0 30 7 9 23 9 2007 -14400))" (br) "(date->time-utc d) " (math "=>") " #<time-utc 1190552850.000000000>" (br) "(define t (make-time 'time-utc 0 1190552850))" (br) "(time-utc->date t) " (math "=>") " #<date Sun Sep 23 09:07:30 2007>" (br) "(time-utc->date t 0) " (math "=>") " #<date Sun Sep 23 13:07:30 2007>"))) ("system:s229" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-and-time)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(date-and-time " (var "date") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a string giving the current date and time" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The string is always in the format illustrated by the examples below and always has length 24.") ((code "(date-and-time) " (math "=>") " \"Fri Jul 13 13:13:13 2001\"" (br) "(define d (make-date 0 0 0 0 1 1 2007 0))" (br) "(date-and-time d) " (math "=>") " \"Mon Jan 01 00:00:00 2007\""))) ("system:s230" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sleep " (var "time") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "time")) " must be a time object with type " (code "time-duration") ". " (code (var "sleep")) " causes the invoking thread to suspend operation for approximately the amount of time indicated by the time object, unless the process receives a signal that interrupts the sleep operation. The actual time slept depends on the granularity of the system clock and how busy the system is running other threads and processes.")) ("system:s231" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(time " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of " (code (var "expr")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "time") " evaluates " (code (var "expr")) " and, as a side-effect, prints (to the console-output port) the amount of cpu time, the amount of real time, the number of bytes allocated, and the amount of collection overhead associated with evaluating " (code (var "expr")) ".") ((code "> (time (collect))" (br) "(time (collect))" (br) "    1 collection" (br) "    1 ms elapsed cpu time, including 1 ms collecting" (br) "    1 ms elapsed real time, including 1 ms collecting" (br) "    160 bytes allocated, including 8184 bytes reclaimed"))) ("system:s232" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-statistics)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(display-statistics " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure displays a running total of the amount of cpu time, real time, bytes allocated, and collection overhead. If " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port.")) ("system:s233" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cpu-time)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the amount of cpu time consumed since system start-up" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The amount is in milliseconds. The amount includes \"system\" as well as \"user\" time, i.e., time spent in the kernel on behalf of the process as well as time spent in the process itself.") ("See also " (code "current-time") ", which returns more precise information.")) ("system:s234" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(real-time)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the amount of real time that has elapsed since system start-up" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The amount is in milliseconds.") ("See also " (code "current-time") ", which returns more precise information.")) ("system:s235" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bytes-allocated)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bytes-allocated " (var "g") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of bytes currently allocated" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("If " (code (var "g")) " is supplied, " (code "bytes-allocated") " returns the number of bytes currently allocated for Scheme objects in the specified generation plus externally allocated bytes as represented by phantom bytevectors in the generation. " (code (var "g")) " must be a nonnegative exact integer no greater than the maximum nonstatic generation, i.e., the value returned by " (code "collect-maximum-generation") ", or the symbol " (code "static") ". If " (code (var "g")) " is not supplied, " (code "bytes-allocated") " returns the total number of bytes allocated in all generations.")) ("system:s236" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(initial-bytes-allocated)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the total number of bytes allocated after loading boot files" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s237" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bytes-deallocated)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the total number of bytes deallocated by the garbage collector" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The total number of bytes allocated by the current process, whether still in use or not, can be obtained by summing " (code "(bytes-deallocated)") " and " (code "(bytes-allocated)") "  and possibly subtracting " (code "(initial-bytes-allocated)") ".")) ("system:s238" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(bytes-finalized)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of bytes queued in guardians" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The number of bytes associated with objects that were registered in guardians as otherwise inaccessible (including the bytes for objects reachable only through registered objects) during the most recent garbage collection.")) ("system:s239" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(current-memory-bytes)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the total number of bytes currently allocated, including overhead" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "current-memory-bytes") " returns the total size of the heap in bytes, including not only the bytes occupied for Scheme objects but also various forms of overhead, including fragmentation and reserved but not currently occupied memory, and is thus an accurate measure of the amount of heap memory currently reserved from the operating system for the current process. The result is only for Scheme's storage management, however, so it does not include sizes for externally allocated objects (even those that are represented by phantom bytevectors).")) ("system:s240" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(maximum-memory-bytes)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the maximum number of bytes ever allocated, including overhead" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "maximum-memory-bytes") " returns the maximum size of the heap in bytes, i.e., the maximum value that " (code "current-memory-bytes") "  returned or could have returned, since the last call to " (code "reset-maximum-memory-bytes!") " or, if there has been no such call, since the process started.")) ("system:s241" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(reset-maximum-memory-bytes!)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "reset-maximum-memory-bytes!") " resets the maximum recorded size of the heap to the current size of the heap.")) ("system:s242" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(collections)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number garbage collections so far" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s243" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(statistics)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a sstats record containing current statistics" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "statistics") " packages together various timing and allocation statistics into a single " (code "sstats") " record. A " (code "sstats") " record has the following fields:") ((dt (code "cpu") ",") (dd " the cpu time consumed,") (dt (code "real") ",") (dd " the elapsed real time,") (dt (code "bytes") ",") (dd " the number of bytes allocated,") (dt (code "gc-count") ",") (dd " the number of collections,") (dt (code "gc-cpu") ",") (dd " the cpu time consumed during collections,") (dt (code "gc-real") ",") (dd " the elapsed real time during collections, and") (dt (code "gc-bytes") ",") (dd " the number of bytes reclaimed by the collector.")) ("All values are computed since system start-up. The time values are time objects (Section " (link "./system.html#g126" "12.10") "), and the bytes and count values are exact integers.") ((code "statistics") " might be defined as follows:") ((code "(define statistics" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (make-sstats" (br) "      (current-time 'time-thread)" (br) "      (current-time 'time-monotonic)" (br) "      (- (+ (bytes-allocated) (bytes-deallocated))" (br) "         (initial-bytes-allocated))" (br) "      (collections)" (br) "      (current-time 'time-collector-cpu)" (br) "      (current-time 'time-collector-real)" (br) "      (bytes-deallocated))))"))) ("system:s244" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-sstats " (var "cpu") " " (var "real") " " (var "bytes") " " (var "gc-count") " " (var "gc-cpu") " " (var "gc-real") " " (var "gc-bytes") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a sstats record" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The time arguments (" (code (var "cpu")) ", " (code (var "real")) ", " (code (var "gc-cpu")) ", and " (code (var "gc-real")) ") must be time objects. The other arguments must be exact integers.")) ("system:s245" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a sstats record, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s246" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-cpu " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-real " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-bytes " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-gc-count " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-gc-cpu " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-gc-real " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-gc-bytes " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value of the corresponding field of " (code (var "s")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "s")) " must be a sstats record.")) ("system:s247" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-cpu! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-real! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-bytes! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-gc-count! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-gc-cpu! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-gc-real! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-sstats-gc-bytes! " (var "s") " " (var "new-value") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "s")) " must be a sstats record, the " (code (var "new-value")) " arguments for the time fields (" (code (var "cpu")) ", " (code (var "real")) ", " (code (var "gc-cpu")) ", and " (code (var "gc-real")) ") must be time objects, and the other " (code (var "new-value")) " arguments must be exact integers. Each procedure sets the value of the corresponding field of " (code (var "s")) " to " (code (var "new-value")) ".")) ("system:s248" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-difference " (var "s" (sub "1")) " " (var "s" (sub "2")) ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a sstats record representing the difference between " (code (var "s" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "s" (sub "2"))) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "s" (sub "1"))) " and " (code (var "s" (sub "2"))) " must be sstats records. " (code "sstats-difference") " subtracts each field of " (code (var "s" (sub "2"))) " from the corresponding field of " (code (var "s" (sub "1"))) " to produce the resulting " (code "sstats") "  record.")) ("system:s249" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-print " (var "s") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(sstats-print " (var "s") " " (var "textual-output-port") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "s")) " must be a " (code "sstats") " record. If " (code (var "textual-output-port")) " is not supplied, it defaults to the current output port. " (code "sstats-print") " displays the fields of " (code "s") " in a manner similar to " (code "display-statistics") " and " (code "time") ".")) ("system:s250" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "enable-object-counts") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "enable-object-counts") " is a boolean value that determines whether the collector records object counts as it runs and hence whether the object counts returned by the procedure " (code "object-counts") " are accurate. The parameter is set to " (code "#f") " by default, since enabling object counts adds overhead to collection.") ("Counts for the static generation are always correct. Counts for a nonstatic generation " (var "n") " are correct immediately after a collection of generation " (var "m") " " (math ">=") " " (var "n") " (regardless of whether the target generation is " (var "m") " or " (var "m") " + 1) if " (code "enable-object-counts") "  was set to " (code "#t") " during the collection.") ("One strategy for collecting object counts with minimal overhead is to enable object counts only while collecting the maximum nonstatic generation and to obtain the object counts immediately after that collection.")) ("system:s251" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(object-counts)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "object-counts") " returns a nested association list representing object counts and bytes allocated for each heap-allocated primitive type and record type with at least one live instance in one or more generations. (Heap-allocated primitive types include, e.g., pairs and vectors, but not, e.g., fixnums or characters.) Object counts are gathered by the collector only when " (code "enable-object-counts") " is " (code "#t") ". The description of " (code "enable-object-counts") " details the circumstances under which the counts are accurate.") ("The association list returned by " (code "object-counts") " has the following structure:") ((code "((" (var "type") " (" (var "generation") " " (var "count") " . " (var "bytes") ") ...) ...)")) ((code (var "type")) " is either the name of a primitive type, represented as a symbol, e.g., " (code "pair") ", or a record-type descriptor (rtd). " (code (var "generation")) " is a nonnegative fixnum between 0 and the value of " (code "(collect-maximum-generation)") ", inclusive, or the symbol " (code "static") " representing the static generation. " (code (var "count")) " and " (code (var "bytes")) " are nonnegative fixnums.") ((code "(collect-request-handler void)" (br) "(enable-object-counts #t)" (br) "(define-record-type frob (fields x))" (br) "(define x (make-frob (make-frob #f)))" (br) "(collect 3 3)" (br) "(cdr (assoc 3" (br) "       (cdr (assoc (record-type-descriptor frob)" (br) "              (object-counts)))))                " (math "=>") " (2 . 16)"))) ("system:s252" ((bold "global parameter") ": " (code "enable-object-backreferences") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of " (code "enable-object-backreferences") " is a boolean value that determines whether the collector records information about which other object caused an object to be retained and hence whether the backreferences reported by the procedure " (code "object-backreferences") " are accurate. The parameter is set to " (code "#f") " by default, since backreference recording adds overhead to collection.") ("Beware that backreference recording can have small performance effects even after it is disabled---at least until the next collection over the same generations---since backreference records constrain the way that the collector stores some objects.")) ("system:s253" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(object-backreferences)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of list of pairs" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The procedure " (code "object-backreferences") " returns a list of backreference lists. Each backreference list is a list of pairs, where the " (code "car") " of the pair is a referenced object, and the " (code "cdr") " of the pair is either " (code "#f") " or a value that caused the " (code "car") "'s value to be retained during collection. The " (code "cdr") " of a backreference pair is " (code "#f") " if the object in the " (code "car") " is retained by a root reference within the system's implementation or static generation. By locating the " (code "cdr") " of one pair as the " (code "car") " of another, chains of objects as discovered by the collector can be traced back to roots.") ("The list returned by " (code "object-backreferences") " contains one backreference list for each nonstatic generation (in order, starting with generation " (code "0") "). An object is recorded in a backreference list for the destination generation to which it is moved by collection. The collector records backreference information only when " (code "enable-object-backreferences") " is set to a true value, and only for objects that start in generations that are collected.") ("For example, assuming that backreferences have not been previously enabled, the result of") ((code "(collect-request-handler void)" (br) "(enable-object-backreferences #t)" (br) "(collect 0)" (br) "(enable-object-backreferences #f)" (br) "(object-backreferences)")) ("will have a non-empty backreference list only for the second list in the result (i.e., the list for generation " (code "1") ").") ("Although " (code "object-backreferences") " reports generation-specific information to reflect its cooperation with generational collection, backreference information is most useful after a collection of all generations up to the maximum nonstatic generation. In that case, backreference information can be used to discover why a particular value or kind of value remains allocated or remains in a weak pair after garbage collection.") ((code "(collect-request-handler void)" (br) "(enable-object-backreferences #t)" (br) "(define b (box \"hello\"))" (br) "(collect 0)" (br) "(assq (unbox b) (cadr (object-backreferences))) " (math "=>") " (\"hello\" . #&\"hello\")"))) ("system:s254" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-allocation-counts") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter has a true value, the compiler inserts a short sequence of instructions at each allocation point in generated code to track the amount of allocation that occurs. This parameter is initially false.")) ("system:s255" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "generate-instruction-counts") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("When this parameter has a true value, the compiler inserts a short sequence of instructions in each block of generated code to track the number of instructions executed by that block. This parameter is initially false.")) ("system:s256" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-cost-center)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new cost center" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The recorded costs of the new cost center are initialized to zero.")) ("system:s257" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cost-center? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a cost center, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s258" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-cost-center " (var "cost-center") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-cost-center " (var "timed?") " " (var "cost-center") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure that accepts zero arguments. " (code "with-cost-center") " invokes " (code (var "thunk")) " without arguments and returns its values. It also tracks, dynamically, the bytes allocated, instructions executed, and cpu time elapsed while evaluating the invocation of " (code (var "thunk")) " and adds the tracked costs to the cost center's running record of these costs.") ("As described above, allocation counts are tracked only for code compiled with the parameter " (code "generate-allocation-counts") " set to true, and instruction counts are tracked only for code compiled with " (code "generate-instruction-counts") " set to true. Cpu time is tracked only if " (code (var "timed?")) " is provided and not false and includes cpu time spent in instrumented, uninstrumented, and non-Scheme code.")) ("system:s259" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cost-center-instruction-count " (var "cost-center") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of instructions tracked by " (code (var "cost-center")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s260" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cost-center-allocation-count " (var "cost-center") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of allocated bytes tracked by " (code (var "cost-center")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s261" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(cost-center-time " (var "cost-center") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the cpu time tracked by " (code (var "cost-center")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The cpu time is returned as a time object with time-type " (code "time-duration") ".")) ("system:s262" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(reset-cost-center! " (var "cost-center") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This procedure resets the costs recorded by " (code (var "cost-center")) " to zero.")) ("system:s263" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(black-box " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code (var "obj")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The " (code "black-box") " procedure simply returns its argument, but optimization passes make no assumptions about how " (code "black-box") "  uses its argument, whether " (code "black-box") " has side effects, or what " (code "black-box") " returns beyond the fact that it is a single value.") ("The intended use of " (code "black-box") " is for benchmarking and testing tasks that need to simulate an arbitrary consumption context for a computation, but without otherwise adding overhead to the computation by adding a non-inlined function call or other obfuscating work.") ("Examples:") ((code "(time" (br) " (let ([to-power 100])" (br) "   (let loop ([i 1000])" (br) "     (unless (zero? i)" (br) "       ; call to `expt` is likely optimized away entirely, since there's" (br) "       ; no effect and the result is unused:" (br) "       (expt 2 to-power)" (br) "       (loop (sub1 i))))))" (br) (br) "(time" (br) " (let ([to-power 100])" (br) "   (let loop ([i 1000])" (br) "     (unless (zero? i)" (br) "       ; likely optimized to just returning a folded constant, instead of" (br) "       ; calling `expt` each iteration:" (br) "       (black-box (expt 2 to-power))" (br) "       (loop (sub1 i))))))" (br) (br) "(time" (br) " (let ([to-power (black-box 100)])" (br) "   (let loop ([i 1000])" (br) "     (unless (zero? i)" (br) "       ; in safe mode, likely calls `expt`, because `to-power` is not known" (br) "       ; to be a number, but likely optimized away with `(optimize-level 3)`:" (br) "       (expt 2 to-power)" (br) "       (loop (sub1 i))))))" (br) (br) "(time" (br) " (let ([to-power (black-box 100)])" (br) "   (let loop ([i 1000])" (br) "     (unless (zero? i)" (br) "       ; arithmetic really performed every iteration, since `to-power` value" (br) "       ; is assumed unknown, and `expt` result is assumed to be used" (br) "       (black-box (expt 2 to-power))" (br) "       (loop (sub1 i))))))"))) ("system:s264" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-parameter " (var "object") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-parameter " (var "object") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a parameter (procedure)" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "make-parameter") " accepts one or two arguments. The first argument is the initial value of the internal variable, and the second, if present, is a " (var "filter") " applied to the initial value and all subsequent values. The filter should accept one argument. If the value is not appropriate, the filter should raise an exception or convert the value into a more appropriate form.") ("For example, the default value of " (code "print-length") " is defined as follows:") ((code "(define print-length" (br) "  (make-parameter" (br) "    #f" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (unless (or (not x) (and (fixnum? x) (fx>= x 0)))" (br) "        (assertion-violationf 'print-length" (br) "          \"~s is not a positive fixnum or #f\"" (br) "          x))" (br) "      x)))")) ((code "(print-length)  " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(print-length 3)" (br) "(print-length)  " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(format \"~s\" '(1 2 3 4 5 6))  " (math "=>") " \"(1 2 3 ...)\"" (br) "(print-length #f)" (br) "(format \"~s\" '(1 2 3 4 5 6))  " (math "=>") " \"(1 2 3 4 5 6)\"")) ("The definition of " (code "make-parameter") " is straightforward using " (code "case-lambda") ":") ((code "(define make-parameter" (br) "  (case-lambda" (br) "    [(init guard)" (br) "     (let ([v (guard init)])" (br) "       (case-lambda" (br) "         [() v]" (br) "         [(u) (set! v (guard u))]))]" (br) "    [(init)" (br) "     (make-parameter init (lambda (x) x))]))")) ("In threaded versions of " (var "Chez Scheme") ", " (code "make-parameter") " creates global parameters. The procedure " (code "make-thread-parameter") ", described in Section " (link "./threads.html#g153" "15.8") ", may be used to make thread parameters.")) ("system:s266" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(parameterize ((" (var "param") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Using the syntactic form " (code "parameterize") ", the values of parameters can be changed in a manner analogous to " (code "fluid-let") " for ordinary variables. Each " (code (var "param")) " is set to the value of the corresponding " (code (var "expr")) " while the body is evaluated. When control leaves the body by normal return or by the invocation of a continuation created outside of the body, the parameters are restored to their original values. If control returns to the body via a continuation created during the execution of the body, the parameters are again set to their temporary values.") ((code "(define test" (br) "  (make-parameter 0))" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(test 1)" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(parameterize ([test 2])" (br) "  (test))  " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(parameterize ([test 2])" (br) "  (test 3)" (br) "  (test))  " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(define k (lambda (x) x))" (br) "(begin (set! k (call/cc k))" (br) "       'k)  " (math "=>") " k" (br) "(parameterize ([test 2])" (br) "  (test (call/cc k))" (br) "  (test))  " (math "=>") " k" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "(k 3)  " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(test)  " (math "=>") " 1")) ("The definition of " (code "parameterize") " is similar to the definition of " (code "fluid-let") " (page " (link "./binding.html#defn:fluid-let" "123") "):") ((code "(define-syntax parameterize" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (syntax-case x ()" (br) "      [(_ () b1 b2 ...) #'(begin b1 b2 ...)]" (br) "      [(_ ((x e) ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "       (with-syntax ([(p ...) (generate-temporaries #'(x ...))]" (br) "                     [(y ...) (generate-temporaries #'(x ...))])" (br) "         #'(let ([p x] ... [y e] ...)" (br) "             (let ([swap (lambda ()" (br) "                           (let ([t (p)]) (p y) (set! y t))" (br) "                           ...)])" (br) "               (dynamic-wind swap (lambda () b1 b2 ...) swap))))])))"))) ("system:s267" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(virtual-register-count)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the number of virtual registers" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("As of Version 9.0, the number of virtual registers is set at 16. It cannot be changed except by recompiling " (var "Chez Scheme") " from source.")) ("system:s268" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(set-virtual-register! " (var "k") " " (var "x") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "set-virtual-register!") " stores " (code (var "x")) " in virtual register " (code (var "k")) ". " (code (var "k")) " must be a nonnegative fixnum less than the value of " (code "(virtual-register-count)") ".")) ("system:s269" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(virtual-register " (var "k") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "virtual-register") " returns the value most recently stored in virtual register " (code (var "k")) " (on the current thread, in threaded versions of the system).")) ("system:s270" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-version)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-version " (var "show-pre-release?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a version string" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The version string is in the form") ((code "\"Chez Scheme Version " (var "version") "\"")) ("for " (var "Chez Scheme") ", and") ((code "\"Petite Chez Scheme Version " (var "version") "\"")) ("for " (var "Petite Chez Scheme") ".") ((code (var "version")) " includes the major, minor, and patch version numbers separated by " (code ".") ".  If " (code (var "show-pre-release?")) " is provided and is a true value, and the current version is a pre-release version (i.e., " (code "(scheme-pre-release)") " is a number), then " (code (var "version")) "  additionally has " (code "-pre-release.") " and the pre-release number appended.") ((code "(scheme-version) " (math "=>") " \"Chez Scheme Version 9.9.9\"" (br) "(scheme-version #t) " (math "=>") " \"Chez Scheme Version 9.9.9-pre-release.11\""))) ("system:s271" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-version-number)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "three values: the major, minor, and patch version numbers" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each of the three return values is a nonnegative fixnum.") ("In " (var "Chez Scheme") " Version 9.7.3 or 9.7.3-pre-release.3:") ((code "(scheme-version-number) " (math "=>") " 9" (br) "                        " (math "  ") " 7" (br) "                        " (math "  ") " 3"))) ("system:s272" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-pre-release)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a pre-release number or " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The return value is a positive fixnum for a pre-release version, or it is " (code "#f") " for a release version (which is " (var "after") " any version that has a pre-release number and the same major, minor, and patch number).") ("In " (var "Chez Scheme") " Version 9.7.3:") ((code "(scheme-pre-release) " (math "=>") " #f")) ("In " (var "Chez Scheme") " Version 9.7.3-pre-release.3:") ((code "(scheme-pre-release) " (math "=>") " 3"))) ("system:s273" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(petite?)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if called in " (var "Petite Chez Scheme") ", " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The only difference between " (var "Petite Chez Scheme") " and " (var "Chez Scheme") " is that the compiler is not available in the former, so this predicate can serve as a way to determine if the compiler is available.")) ("system:s274" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(threaded?)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if called in a threaded version of the system, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s275" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(interactive?)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if system is run interactively, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("This predicate returns " (code "#t") " if the Scheme process's stdin and stdout are connected to a tty (Unix-based systems) or console (Windows). Otherwise, it returns " (code "#f") ".")) ("system:s276" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-process-id)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the operating system process id of the current process" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("system:s277" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(getenv " (var "key") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "environment value of " (code (var "key")) " or " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "key")) " must be a string. " (code "getenv") " returns the operating system shell's environment value associated with " (code (var "key")) ", or " (code "#f") " if no environment value is associated with " (code (var "key")) ".") ((code "(getenv \"HOME\") " (math "=>") " \"/u/freddy\""))) ("system:s278" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(putenv " (var "key") " " (var "value") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "key")) " and " (code (var "value")) " must be strings.") ((code "putenv") " stores the " (code (var "key")) ", " (code (var "value")) " pair in the environment of the process, where it is available to the current process (e.g., via " (code (var "getenv")) ") and any spawned processes.") ((code "(putenv \"SCHEME\" \"rocks!\")" (br) "(getenv \"SCHEME\") " (math "=>") " \"rocks!\""))) ("system:s279" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-registry " (var "key") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "registry value of " (code (var "key")) " or " (code "#f") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(put-registry! " (var "key") " " (var "val") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(remove-registry! " (var "key") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "key")) " and " (code (var "val")) " must be strings.") ((code "get-registry") " returns a string containing the registry value of " (code (var "key")) " if the value exists. If no registry value for " (code (var "key")) " exists, " (code "get-registry") " returns " (code "#f") ".") ((code "put-registry!") " sets the registry value of " (code (var "key")) " to " (code (var "val")) ". It raises an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the value cannot be set, which may happen if the user has insufficient access.") ((code "remove-registry!") " removes the registry key or value named by " (code (var "key")) ". It raises an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if the value cannot be removed. Reasons for failure include the key not being present, the user having insufficient access, or " (code (var "key")) " being a key with subkeys.") ("These routines are defined for Windows only.") ((code "(get-registry \"hkey_local_machine\\\\Software\\\\North\\\\South\") " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(put-registry! \"hkey_local_machine\\\\Software\\\\North\\\\South\" \"east\")" (br) "(get-registry \"hkey_local_machine\\\\Software\\\\North\\\\South\") " (math "=>") " \"east\"" (br) "(remove-registry! \"hkey_local_machine\\\\Software\\\\North\")" (br) "(get-registry \"hkey_local_machine\\\\Software\\\\North\\\\South\") " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("system:s280" ((bold "thread parameter") ": " (code "subset-mode") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The value of this parameter must be " (code "#f") " (the default) or the symbol " (code "system") ". Setting " (code "subset-mode") " to " (code "system") " allows the manipulation of various undocumented system variables, data structures, and settings. It is typically used only for system debugging.         ")) ("threads:s2" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fork-thread " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a thread object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "thunk")) " must be a procedure that accepts zero arguments.") ((code "fork-thread") " invokes " (code (var "thunk")) " in a new thread and returns a thread object.") ("Threads created by foreign code using some means other than " (code "fork-thread") " must call " (code "Sactivate_thread") "  (Section " (link "./foreign.html#g37" "4.9") ") before touching any Scheme data or calling any Scheme procedures.")) ("threads:s3" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(thread-join " (var "thread") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Waits until " (code (var "thread")) " has completed.")) ("threads:s4" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-initial-thread)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a thread object for the initial thread" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("threads:s5" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(thread? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a thread object, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("threads:s6" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(get-thread-id)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the thread id of the current thread" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("The thread id is a thread number assigned by thread id, and has no relationship to the process id returned by " (code "get-process-id") ", which is the same in all threads.")) ("threads:s8" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(thread-preserve-ownership!)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(thread-preserve-ownership! " (var "thread") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Provides a hint to the storage manager that " (code (var "thread")) " (which defaults to the current thread if not supplied) can particularly benefit from tracking the objects that it allocates for parallel collection.")) ("threads:s9" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-mutex)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-mutex " (var "name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new mutex object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "name")) ", if supplied, must be a symbol which identifies the mutex, or " (code "#f") " for no name. The name is printed every time the mutex is printed, which is useful for debugging.")) ("threads:s10" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mutex? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a mutex, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("threads:s11" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mutex-acquire " (var "mutex") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mutex-acquire " (var "mutex") " " (var "block?") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "mutex")) " must be a mutex.") ((code (var "mutex-acquire")) " acquires the mutex identified by " (code (var "mutex")) ". The optional boolean argument " (code (var "block?")) " defaults to " (code "#t") " and specifies whether the thread should block waiting for the mutex. If " (code (var "block?")) " is omitted or is true, the thread blocks until the mutex has been acquired, and an unspecified value is returned.") ("If " (code "block?") " is false and the mutex currently belongs to a different thread, the current thread does not block. Instead, " (code "mutex-acquire") " returns immediately with the value " (code "#f") " to indicate that the mutex is not available. If " (code (var "block?")) " is false and the mutex is successfully acquired, " (code "mutex-acquire") " returns " (code "#t") ".") ("Mutexes are " (var "recursive") " in Posix threads terminology, which means that the calling thread can use " (code "mutex-acquire") " to (re)acquire a mutex it already has. In this case, an equal number of " (code "mutex-release") " calls is necessary to release the mutex.")) ("threads:s12" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mutex-release " (var "mutex") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "mutex")) " must be a mutex.") ((code "mutex-release") " releases the mutex identified by " (code (var "mutex")) ". Unpredictable behavior results if the mutex is not owned by the calling thread.")) ("threads:s13" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(with-mutex " (var "mutex") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code "with-mutex") " evaluates the expression " (code (var "mutex")) ", which must evaluate to a mutex, acquires the mutex, evaluates the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") ", and releases the mutex. The mutex is released whether the body returns normally or via a control operation (that is, throw to a continuation, perhaps because of an error) that results in a nonlocal exit from the " (code "with-mutex") " form. If control subsequently returns to the body via a continuation invocation, the mutex is reacquired.") ("Using " (code "with-mutex") " is generally more convenient and safer than using " (code "mutex-acquire") " and " (code "mutex-release") " directly.")) ("threads:s14" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(mutex-name " (var "mutex") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the name associated with " (code (var "mutex")) ", if any; otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "mutex")) " must be a mutex.")) ("threads:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-condition)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-condition " (var "name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new condition object" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "name")) ", if supplied, must be a symbol which identifies the condition object, or " (code "#f") " for no name. The name is printed every time the condition is printed, which is useful for debugging.")) ("threads:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(thread-condition? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition object, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)"))) ("threads:s17" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-wait " (var "cond") " " (var "mutex") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-wait " (var "cond") " " (var "mutex") " " (var "timeout") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the calling thread was awakened by the condition, " (code "#f") " if the calling thread timed out waiting" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "cond")) " must be a condition object, and " (code (var "mutex")) " must be a mutex. The optional argument " (code (var "timeout")) " is a time record of type " (code "time-duration") " or " (code "time-utc") ", or " (code "#f") " for no timeout. It defaults to " (code "#f") ".") ((code "condition-wait") " waits up to the specified " (code (var "timeout")) " for the condition identified by the condition object " (code (var "cond")) ". The calling thread must have acquired the mutex identified by the mutex " (code (var "mutex")) " at the time " (code "condition-wait") " is called. " (code (var "mutex")) " is released as a side effect of the call to " (code "condition-wait") ". When a thread is later released from the condition variable by one of the procedures described below or the timeout expires, " (code (var "mutex")) " is reacquired and " (code "condition-wait") " returns.")) ("threads:s18" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-signal " (var "cond") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "cond")) " must be a condition object.") ((code "condition-signal") " releases one of the threads waiting for the condition identified by " (code (var "cond")) ".")) ("threads:s19" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-broadcast " (var "cond") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "cond")) " must be a condition object.") ((code "condition-broadcast") " releases all of the threads waiting for the condition identified by " (code (var "cond")) ".")) ("threads:s20" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition-name " (var "condition") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the name associated with " (code (var "condition")) ", if any; otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "condition")) " must be a condition.")) ("threads:s22" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-init-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-init-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the lock is not already locked, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-spin-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-spin-lock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-unlock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-unlock! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each of these has a syntax like and behaves similarly to " (code "ftype-set!") " (page " (link "./foreign.html#defn:ftype-set!" "88") "), though with an implicit " (code (var "val-expr")) ". In particular, the restrictions on and handling of " (code (var "fptr-expr")) "  and the accessors " (code (var "a") " ...") " is similar, with one important restriction: the field specified by the last accessor, upon which the form operates,  must be a word-size integer, i.e., an " (code "iptr") ", " (code "uptr") ", or the equivalent, with the native endianness.") ((code "ftype-init-lock!") " should be used to initialize the lock prior to the use of any of the other operators; if this is not done, the behavior of the other operators is undefined.") ((code "ftype-lock!") " can be used to lock the lock. If it finds the lock unlocked at the time of the operation, it (normally) locks the lock and returns " (code "#t") "; if it finds the lock already locked, it returns " (code "#f") " without changing the lock. On an architecture with a weak memory model, " (code "ftype-lock!") " can spuriously fail, leaving a lock unchanged and returning " (code "#f") " even if the lock is currently unlocked. On success, no memory ordering is implied, which means that " (code "memory-order-acquire") " may be needed to complete an intended synchronization.") ((code "ftype-spin-lock!") " can also be used to lock the lock. If it finds the lock unlocked at the time of the operation, it locks the lock and returns; if it finds the lock already locked, it waits until the lock is unlocked, then locks the lock and returns. If no other thread or process unlocks the lock, the operation does not return and cannot be interrupted by normal means, including by the storage manager for the purpose of initiating a garbage collection. There are also no guarantees of fairness, so a process might hang indefinitely even if other processes are actively locking and unlocking the lock.") ((code "ftype-unlock!") " is used to unlock a lock. If it finds the lock locked, it unlocks the lock and returns. Otherwise, it returns without changing the lock. On an architecture with a weak memory model, no memory ordering is implied, and " (code "memory-order-release") " may be needed to complete an intended synchronization.")) ("threads:s23" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-locked-incr! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-locked-incr! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the updated value is 0, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-locked-decr! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-locked-decr! " (var "ftype-name") " (" (var "a") " ...) " (var "fptr-expr") " " (var "index") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if the updated value is 0, " (code "#f") " otherwise" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("Each of these has a syntax like and behaves similarly to " (code "ftype-set!") " (page " (link "./foreign.html#defn:ftype-set!" "88") "), though with an implicit " (code (var "val-expr")) ". In particular, the restrictions on and handling of " (code (var "fptr-expr")) "  and the accessors " (code (var "a") " ...") " is similar, with one important restriction: the field specified by the last accessor, upon which the form operates,  must be a word-size integer, i.e., an " (code "iptr") ", " (code "uptr") ", or the equivalent, with the native endianness.") ((code "ftype-locked-incr!") " atomically reads the value of the specified field, adds 1 to the value, and writes the new value back into the field. Similarly, " (code "ftype-locked-decr!") " atomically reads the value of the specified field, subtracts 1 from the value, and writes the new value back into the field. Both return " (code "#t") " if the new value is 0, otherwise " (code "#f") ".")) ("threads:s28" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(ftype-guardian " (var "ftype-name") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new ftype guardian" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ((code (var "ftype-name")) " must name an ftype. The first base field of the ftype (or one of the first base fields in the case of unions) must be a word-sized integer (iptr or uptr) with native endianness. This field is assumed to hold a reference count.") ("The return value is a new ftype guardian " (code (var "g")) ", with which ftype-pointers of type " (code (var "ftype-name")) " (or some subtype of " (code (var "ftype-name")) ") can be registered. An ftype pointer is registered with " (code (var "g")) " by invoking " (code (var "g")) "  with the ftype pointer as an argument.") ("An ftype guardian does not automatically protect from collection the ftype pointers registered with it,  as a normal guardian would do. Instead, for each registered ftype pointer that becomes inaccessible via normal (non-weak, non-guardian pointers), the guardian decrements the reference count of the object to which the ftype pointer points. If the resulting reference-count value is zero, the ftype pointer is preserved and can be retrieved from the guardian. If the resulting reference-count value is non-zero, however, the ftype pointer is not preserved. Objects retrieved from an ftype guardian (by calling it without arguments) are guaranteed to have zero reference counts, assuming reference counts are maintained properly by code outside the collector.") ("The collector decrements the reference count using the equivalent of " (code "ftype-locked-decr!") "  to support systems in which non-Scheme objects are stored in memory shared by multiple processes. In such systems, programs should themselves use " (code "ftype-locked-incr!") " and " (code "ftype-locked-decr!") " or non-Scheme equivalents (e.g., the C " (code "LOCKED_INCR") " and " (code "LOCKED_DECR") " macros in scheme.h, which are described in Section " (link "./foreign.html#g37" "4.9") ") to maintain reference counts.") ("The following example defines a simple ftype and an allocator for objects of that ftype that frees any objects of that ftype that were previously allocated and no longer accessible.") ((code "(module (A make-A free-dropped-As)" (br) "  (define-ftype A" (br) "    (struct" (br) "      [refcount uptr]" (br) "      [data int]))" (br) "  (define g (ftype-guardian A))" (br) "  (define free-dropped-As" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (let ([a (g)])" (br) "        (when a" (br) "          (printf \"freeing ~s\\n\" (ftype-ref A (data) a))" (br) "          (foreign-free (ftype-pointer-address a))" (br) "          (free-dropped-As)))))" (br) "  (define make-A" (br) "    (lambda (n)" (br) "      (free-dropped-As)" (br) "      (let ([a (make-ftype-pointer A (foreign-alloc (ftype-sizeof A)))])" (br) "        (ftype-set! A (refcount) a 1)" (br) "        (ftype-set! A (data) a n)" (br) "        (g a)" (br) "        a))))")) ("We can test this by allocating, dropping, and immediately collecting ftype pointers to A.") ((code "> (do ([i 10 (fx- i 1)])" (br) "      ((fx= i 0))" (br) "    (make-A i)" (br) "    (collect))" (br) "freeing 10" (br) "freeing 9" (br) "freeing 8" (br) "freeing 7" (br) "freeing 6" (br) "freeing 5" (br) "freeing 4" (br) "freeing 3" (br) "freeing 2" (br) "> (free-dropped-As)" (br) "freeing 1")) ("Objects guarded by an ftype guardian might contain pointers to other objects whose reference counts should also be incremented upon allocation of the containing object and decremented upon freeing of the containing object.")) ("threads:s33" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(memory-order-acquire)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(memory-order-release)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("These procedures fence memory operations in a way that is consistent with acquire-release patterns. Specifically, " (code "memory-order-acquire") " ensures at least a load-load and load-store fence, and " (code "memory-order-release") " ensures at least a store-store and store-load fence.")) ("threads:s34" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-thread-parameter " (var "object") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(make-thread-parameter " (var "object") " " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a new thread parameter" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(chezscheme)")) ("See Section " (link "./system.html#g130" "12.14") " for a general discussion of parameters and the use of the optional second argument.") ("When a thread parameter is created, a separate location is set aside in each current and future thread to hold the value of the parameter's internal state variable. (This location may be eliminated by the storage manager when the parameter becomes inaccessible.) Changes to the thread parameter in one thread are not seen by any other thread.") ("When a new thread is created (see " (code "fork-thread") "), the current value (not location) of each thread parameter is inherited from the forking thread by the new thread. Similarly, when a thread created by some other means is activated for the first time (see " (code "Sactivate_thread") " in Section " (link "./foreign.html#g37" "4.9") "), the current value (not location) of each thread parameter is inherited from the main (original) thread by the new thread.") ("Most built-in parameters are thread parameters, but some are global. All are marked as global or thread where they are defined. There is no distinction between built-in global and thread parameters in the nonthreaded versions of the system."))) (tspl ("binding:s2" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code (var "variable")) (br) (bold "returns: ") "the value of " (code (var "variable"))) ("Any identifier appearing as an expression in a program is a variable if a visible variable binding for the identifier exists, e.g., the identifier appears within the scope of a binding created by " (code "define") ", " (code "lambda") ", " (code "let") ", or some other variable-binding construct.") ((code "list " (math "=>") " #<procedure>" (br) "(define x 'a)" (br) "(list x x) " (math "=>") " (a a)" (br) "(let ([x 'b])" (br) "  (list x x)) " (math "=>") " (b b)" (br) "(let ([let 'let]) let) " (math "=>") " let")) ("It is a syntax violation for an identifier reference to appear within a " (code "library") " form or top-level program if it is not bound as a variable, keyword, record name, or other entity. Since the scope of the definitions in a " (code "library") ", top-level program, " (code "lambda") ", or other local body is the entire body, it is not necessary for the definition of a variable to appear before its first reference appears, as long as the reference is not actually evaluated until the definition has been completed. So, for example, the reference to " (code "g") " within the definition of " (code "f") " below") ((code "(define f" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (g x)))" (br) "(define g" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (+ x x)))")) ("is okay, but the reference to " (code "g") " in the definition of " (code "q") " below is not.") ((code "(define q (g 3))" (br) "(define g" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (+ x x)))"))) ("binding:s3" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(lambda " (var "formals") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The " (code "lambda") " syntactic form is used to create procedures. Any operation that creates a procedure or establishes local variable bindings is ultimately defined in terms of " (code "lambda") " or " (code "case-lambda") ".") ("The variables in " (code (var "formals")) " are the formal parameters of the procedure, and the sequence of subforms " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") " is its body.") ("The body may begin with a sequence of definitions, in which case the bindings created by the definitions are local to the body. If definitions are present, the keyword bindings are used and discarded while expanding the body, and the body is expanded into a " (code "letrec*") " expression formed from the variable definitions and the remaining expressions, as described on page " (link "./syntax.html#body-expansion" "292") ". The remainder of this description of " (code "lambda") " assumes that this transformation has taken place, if necessary, so that the body is a sequence of expressions without definitions.") ("When the procedure is created, the bindings of all variables occurring free within the body, excluding the formal parameters, are retained with the procedure. Subsequently, whenever the procedure is applied to a sequence of actual parameters, the formal parameters are bound to the actual parameters, the retained bindings are restored, and the body is evaluated.") ("Upon application, the formal parameters defined by " (code (var "formals")) " are bound to the actual parameters as follows.   " (ul (li "If " (code (var "formals")) " is a proper list of variables, e.g., " (code "(x y z)") ", each variable is bound to the corresponding actual parameter. An exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised if too few or too many actual parameters are supplied.") (li "If " (code (var "formals")) " is a single variable (not in a list), e.g., " (code "z") ", it is bound to a list of the actual parameters.") (li "If " (code (var "formals")) " is an improper list of variables terminated by a variable, e.g., " (code "(x y . z)") ", each variable but the last is bound to the corresponding actual parameter. The last variable is bound to a list of the remaining actual parameters. An exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised if too few actual parameters are supplied."))) ("When the body is evaluated, the expressions in the body are evaluated in sequence, and the procedure returns the values of the last expression.") ("Procedures do not have a printed representation in the usual sense. Scheme systems print procedures in different ways; this book uses the notation " (code "#<procedure>") ".") ((code "(lambda (x) (+ x 3)) " (math "=>") " #<procedure>" (br) "((lambda (x) (+ x 3)) 7) " (math "=>") " 10" (br) "((lambda (x y) (* x (+ x y))) 7 13) " (math "=>") " 140" (br) "((lambda (f x) (f x x)) + 11) " (math "=>") " 22" (br) "((lambda () (+ 3 4))) " (math "=>") " 7" (br) (br) "((lambda (x . y) (list x y))" (br) " 28 37) " (math "=>") " (28 (37))" (br) "((lambda (x . y) (list x y))" (br) " 28 37 47 28) " (math "=>") " (28 (37 47 28))" (br) "((lambda (x y . z) (list x y z))" (br) " 1 2 3 4) " (math "=>") " (1 2 (3 4))" (br) "((lambda x x) 7 13) " (math "=>") " (7 13)"))) ("binding:s13" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(case-lambda " (var "clause") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a procedure" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs control)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("A " (code "case-lambda") " expression consists of a set of clauses, each resembling a " (code "lambda") " expression. Each " (code (var "clause")) " has the form below.") ((code "[" (var "formals") " " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...]")) ("The formal parameters of a clause are defined by " (code (var "formals")) " in the same manner as for a " (code "lambda") " expression. The number of arguments accepted by the procedure value of a " (code "case-lambda") " expression is determined by the numbers of arguments accepted by the individual clauses.") ("When a procedure created with " (code "case-lambda") " is invoked, the clauses are considered in order. The first clause that accepts the given number of actual parameters is selected, the formal parameters defined by its " (code (var "formals")) " are bound to the corresponding actual parameters, and the body is evaluated as described for " (code "lambda") " above. If " (code (var "formals")) " in a clause is a proper list of identifiers, then the clause accepts exactly as many actual parameters as there are formal parameters (identifiers) in " (code (var "formals")) ". As with a " (code "lambda") " " (code (var "formals")) ", a " (code "case-lambda") " clause " (code (var "formals")) "  may be a single identifier, in which case the clause accepts any number of arguments, or an improper list of identifiers terminated by an identifier, in which case the clause accepts any number of arguments greater than or equal to the number of formal parameters excluding the terminating identifier. If no clause accepts the number of actual parameters supplied, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("The following definition for " (code "make-list") " uses " (code "case-lambda") " to support an optional fill parameter.") ((code "(define make-list" (br) "  (case-lambda" (br) "    [(n) (make-list n #f)]" (br) "    [(n x)" (br) "     (do ([n n (- n 1)] [ls '() (cons x ls)])" (br) "         ((zero? n) ls))]))")) ("The " (code "substring") " procedure may be extended with " (code "case-lambda") " to accept either no " (code (var "end")) " index, in which case it defaults to the end of the string, or no " (code (var "start")) " and " (code (var "end")) " indices, in which case " (code "substring") " is equivalent to " (code "string-copy") ":") ((code "(define substring1" (br) "  (case-lambda" (br) "    [(s) (substring1 s 0 (string-length s))]" (br) "    [(s start) (substring1 s start (string-length s))]" (br) "    [(s start end) (substring s start end)]))")) ("It is also possible to default the " (code (var "start")) " index rather than the " (code (var "end")) " index when only one index is supplied:") ((code "(define substring2" (br) "  (case-lambda" (br) "    [(s) (substring2 s 0 (string-length s))]" (br) "    [(s end) (substring2 s 0 end)]" (br) "    [(s start end) (substring s start end)]))")) ("It is even possible to require that both or neither of the " (code (var "start")) "  and " (code (var "end")) " indices be supplied, simply by leaving out the middle clause:") ((code "(define substring3" (br) "  (case-lambda" (br) "    [(s) (substring3 s 0 (string-length s))]" (br) "    [(s start end) (substring s start end)]))"))) ("binding:s16" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(let ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "let") " establishes local variable bindings. Each variable " (code (var "var")) " is bound to the value of the corresponding expression " (code (var "expr")) ". The body of the " (code "let") ", in which the variables are bound, is the sequence of subforms " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") " and is processed and evaluated like a " (code "lambda") " body.") ("The forms " (code "let") ", " (code "let*") ", " (code "letrec") ", and " (code "letrec*") "  (the others are described after " (code "let") ") are similar but serve slightly different purposes. With " (code "let") ", in contrast with " (code "let*") ", " (code "letrec") ", and " (code "letrec*") ", the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") " are all outside the scope of the variables " (code (var "var") " ...") ". Also, in contrast with " (code "let*") " and " (code "letrec*") ", no ordering is implied for the evaluation of the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") ". They may be evaluated from left to right, from right to left, or in any other order at the discretion of the implementation. Use " (code "let") " whenever the values are independent of the variables and the order of evaluation is unimportant.") ((code "(let ([x (* 3.0 3.0)] [y (* 4.0 4.0)])" (br) "  (sqrt (+ x y))) " (math "=>") " 5.0" (br) (br) "(let ([x 'a] [y '(b c)])" (br) "  (cons x y)) " (math "=>") " (a b c)" (br) (br) "(let ([x 0] [y 1])" (br) "  (let ([x y] [y x])" (br) "    (list x y))) " (math "=>") " (1 0)")) ("The following definition of " (code "let") " shows the typical derivation of " (code "let") " from " (code "lambda") ".") ((code "(define-syntax let" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ ((x e) ...) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "     ((lambda (x ...) b1 b2 ...) e ...)]))")) ("Another form of " (code "let") ", " (var "named") " " (code "let") ", is described in Section " (link "./control.html#g100" "5.4") ", and a definition of the full " (code "let") " can be found on page " (link "./syntax.html#defn:let" "312") ".")) ("binding:s18" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(let* ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "let*") " is similar to " (code "let") " except that the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") " are evaluated in sequence from left to right, and each of these expressions is within the scope of the variables to the left. Use " (code "let*") " when there is a linear dependency among the values or when the order of evaluation is important.") ((code "(let* ([x (* 5.0 5.0)]" (br) "       [y (- x (* 4.0 4.0))])" (br) "  (sqrt y)) " (math "=>") " 3.0" (br) (br) "(let ([x 0] [y 1])" (br) "  (let* ([x y] [y x])" (br) "    (list x y))) " (math "=>") " (1 1)")) ("Any " (code "let*") " expression may be converted to a set of nested " (code "let") " expressions. The following definition of " (code "let*") " demonstrates the typical transformation.") ((code "(define-syntax let*" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ () e1 e2 ...)" (br) "     (let () e1 e2 ...)]" (br) "    [(_ ((x1 v1) (x2 v2) ...) e1 e2 ...)" (br) "     (let ((x1 v1))" (br) "       (let* ((x2 v2) ...) e1 e2 ...))]))"))) ("binding:s20" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(letrec ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "letrec") " is similar to " (code "let") " and " (code "let*") ", except that all of the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") "  are within the scope of all of the variables " (code (var "var") " ...") ". " (code "letrec") " allows the definition of mutually recursive procedures.") ((code "(letrec ([sum (lambda (x)" (br) "                (if (zero? x)" (br) "                    0" (br) "                    (+ x (sum (- x 1)))))])" (br) "  (sum 5)) " (math "=>") " 15")) ("The order of evaluation of the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") " is unspecified, so a program must not evaluate a reference to any of the variables bound by the " (code "letrec") " expression before all of the values have been computed. (Occurrence of a variable within a " (code "lambda") " expression does not count as a reference, unless the resulting procedure is applied before all of the values have been computed.) If this restriction is violated, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("An " (code (var "expr")) " should not return more than once. That is, it should not return both normally and via the invocation of a continuation obtained during its evaluation, and it should not return twice via two invocations of such a continuation. Implementations are not required to detect a violation of this restriction, but if they do, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("Choose " (code "letrec") " over " (code "let") " or " (code "let*") " when there is a circular dependency among the variables and their values and when the order of evaluation is unimportant. Choose " (code "letrec*") " over " (code "letrec") " when there is a circular dependency and the bindings need to be evaluated from left to right.") ("A " (code "letrec") " expression of the form") ((code "(letrec ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("may be expressed in terms of " (code "let") " and " (code "set!") " as") ((code "(let ((" (var "var") " #f) ...)" (br) "  (let ((" (var "temp") " " (var "expr") ") ...)" (br) "    (set! " (var "var") " " (var "temp") ") ..." (br) "    (let () " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)))")) ("where " (code (var "temp") " ...") " are fresh variables, i.e., ones that do not already appear in the " (code "letrec") " expression, one for each " (code "(" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ")") " pair. The outer " (code "let") " expression establishes the variable bindings. The initial value given each variable is unimportant, so any value suffices in place of " (code "#f") ". The bindings are established first so that " (code (var "expr") " ...") "  may contain occurrences of the variables, i.e., so that the expressions are computed within the scope of the variables. The middle " (code "let") " evaluates the values and binds them to the temporary variables, and the " (code "set!") " expressions assign each variable to the corresponding value. The inner " (code "let") " is present in case the body contains internal definitions.") ("A definition of " (code "letrec") " that uses this transformation is shown on page " (link "./syntax.html#defn:letrec" "310") ".") ("This transformation does not enforce the restriction that the " (code (var "expr")) " expressions must not evaluate any references of or assignments to the variables. More elaborate transformations that enforce this restriction and actually produce more efficient code are possible [" (link "./bibliography.html#g249" "31") "].")) ("binding:s22" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(letrec* ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "letrec*") " is similar to " (code "letrec") ", except that " (code "letrec*") " evaluates " (code (var "expr") " ...") " in sequence from left to right. While programs must still not evaluate a reference to any " (code (var "var")) "  before the corresponding " (code (var "expr")) " has been evaluated, references to " (code (var "var")) " may be evaluated any time thereafter, including during the evaluation of the " (code (var "expr")) " of any subsequent binding.") ("A " (code "letrec*") " expression of the form") ((code "(letrec* ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("may be expressed in terms of " (code "let") " and " (code "set!") " as") ((code "(let ((" (var "var") " #f) ...)" (br) "  (set! " (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ..." (br) "  (let () " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...))")) ("The outer " (code "let") " expression creates the bindings, each assignment evaluates an expression and immediately sets the corresponding variable to its value, in sequence, and the inner let evaluates the body. " (code "let") " is used in the latter case rather than " (code "begin") "  since the body may include internal definitions as well as expressions.") ((code "(letrec* ([sum (lambda (x)" (br) "                 (if (zero? x)" (br) "                     0" (br) "                     (+ x (sum (- x 1)))))]" (br) "          [f (lambda () (cons n n-sum))]" (br) "          [n 15]" (br) "          [n-sum (sum n)])" (br) "  (f)) " (math "=>") " (15 . 120)" (br) (br) "(letrec* ([f (lambda () (lambda () g))]" (br) "          [g (f)])" (br) "  (eq? (g) g)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(letrec* ([g (f)]" (br) "          [f (lambda () (lambda () g))])" (br) "  (eq? (g) g)) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception: attempt to reference undefined variable f")))) ("binding:s23" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(let-values ((" (var "formals") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(let*-values ((" (var "formals") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "let-values") " is a convenient way to receive multiple values and bind them to variables. It is structured like " (code "let") " but permits an arbitrary formals list (like " (code "lambda") ") on each left-hand side. " (code "let*-values") " is similar but performs the bindings in left-to-right order, as with " (code "let*") ". An exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised if the number of values returned by an " (code (var "expr")) " is not appropriate for the corresponding " (code (var "formals")) ", as described in the entry for " (code "lambda") " above. A definition of " (code "let-values") " is given on page " (link "./syntax.html#fullletvalues" "310") ".") ((code "(let-values ([(a b) (values 1 2)] [c (values 1 2 3)])" (br) "  (list a b c)) " (math "=>") " (1 2 (1 2 3))" (br) (br) "(let*-values ([(a b) (values 1 2)] [(a b) (values b a)])" (br) "  (list a b)) " (math "=>") " (2 1)"))) ("binding:s24" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define " (var "var") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define " (var "var") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define (" (var "var" (sub "0")) " " (var "var" (sub "1")) " ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define (" (var "var" (sub "0")) " . " (var "var" (sub "r")) ") " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define (" (var "var" (sub "0")) " " (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ... . " (var "var" (sub "r")) ") " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("In the first form, " (code "define") " creates a new binding of " (code (var "var")) " to the value of " (code (var "expr")) ". The " (code (var "expr")) " should not return more than once. That is, it should not return both normally and via the invocation of a continuation obtained during its evaluation, and it should not return twice via two invocations of such a continuation. Implementations are not required to detect a violation of this restriction, but if they do, an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " is raised.") ("The second form is equivalent to " (code "(define " (var "var") " " (var "unspecified") ")") ", where " (code (var "unspecified")) " is some unspecified value. The remaining are shorthand forms for binding variables to procedures; they are identical to the following definition in terms of " (code "lambda") ".") ((code "(define " (var "var") (br) "  (lambda " (var "formals") (br) "    " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...))")) ("where " (code (var "formals")) " is " (code "(" (var "var" (sub "1")) " ...)") ", " (code (var "var" (sub "r"))) ", or " (code "(" (var "var" (sub "1")) " " (var "var" (sub "2")) " ... . " (var "var" (sub "r")) ")") " for the third, fourth, and fifth " (code "define") " formats.") ("Definitions may appear at the front of a " (code "library") " body, anywhere among the forms of a top-level-program body, and at the front of a " (code "lambda") " or " (code "case-lambda") " body or the body of any form derived from " (code "lambda") ", e.g., " (code "let") ", or " (code "letrec*") ". Any body that begins with a sequence of definitions is transformed during macro expansion into a " (code "letrec*") " expression as described on page " (link "./syntax.html#body-expansion" "292") ".") ("Syntax definitions may appear along with variable definitions wherever variable definitions may appear; see Chapter " (link "./syntax.html#g133" "8") ".") ((code "(define x 3)" (br) "x " (math "=>") " 3" (br) (br) "(define f" (br) "  (lambda (x y)" (br) "    (* (+ x y) 2)))" (br) "(f 5 4) " (math "=>") " 18" (br) (br) "(define (sum-of-squares x y)" (br) "  (+ (* x x) (* y y)))" (br) "(sum-of-squares 3 4) " (math "=>") " 25" (br) (br) "(define f" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (+ x 1)))" (br) "(let ([x 2])" (br) "  (define f" (br) "    (lambda (y)" (br) "      (+ y x)))" (br) "  (f 3)) " (math "=>") " 5" (br) "(f 3) " (math "=>") " 4")) ("A set of definitions may be grouped by enclosing them in a " (code "begin") " form. Definitions grouped in this manner may appear wherever ordinary variable and syntax definitions may appear. They are treated as if written separately, i.e., without the enclosing " (code "begin") " form. This feature allows syntactic extensions to expand into groups of definitions.") ((code "(define-syntax multi-define-syntax" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ (var expr) ...)" (br) "     (begin" (br) "       (define-syntax var expr)" (br) "       ...)]))" (br) "(let ()" (br) "  (define plus" (br) "    (lambda (x y)" (br) "        (if (zero? x)" (br) "            y" (br) "            (plus (sub1 x) (add1 y)))))" (br) "  (multi-define-syntax" (br) "    (add1 (syntax-rules () [(_ e) (+ e 1)]))" (br) "    (sub1 (syntax-rules () [(_ e) (- e 1)])))" (br) "  (plus 7 8)) " (math "=>") " 15")) ("Many implementations support an interactive \"top level\" in which variable and other definitions may be entered interactively or loaded from files. The behavior of these top-level definitions is outside the scope of the Revised" (sup "6") " Report, but as long as top-level variables are defined before any references or assignments to them are evaluated, the behavior is consistent across most implementations. So, for example, the reference to " (code "g") " in the top-level definition of " (code "f") " below is okay if " (code (var "g")) " is not already defined, and " (code "g") "  is assumed to name a variable to be defined at some later point.") ((code "(define f" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (g x)))")) ("If this is then followed by a definition of " (code "g") " before " (code "f") " is evaluated, the assumption that " (code "g") " would be defined as a variable is proven correct, and a call to " (code "f") " works as expected.") ((code "(define g" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (+ x x)))" (br) "(f 3) " (math "=>") " 6")) ("If " (code "g") " were defined instead as the keyword for a syntactic extension, the assumption that " (code "g") " would be bound as a variable is proven false, and if " (code "f") " is not redefined before it is invoked, the implementation is likely to raise an exception.")) ("binding:s28" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(set! " (var "var") " " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "set!") " does not establish a new binding for " (code (var "var")) " but rather alters the value of an existing binding. It first evaluates " (code (var "expr")) ", then assigns " (code (var "var")) " to the value of " (code (var "expr")) ". Any subsequent reference to " (code (var "var")) " within the scope of the altered binding evaluates to the new value.") ("Assignments are not employed as frequently in Scheme as in most other languages, but they are useful for implementing state changes.") ((code "(define flip-flop" (br) "  (let ([state #f])" (br) "    (lambda ()" (br) "      (set! state (not state))" (br) "      state)))" (br) (br) "(flip-flop) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(flip-flop) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(flip-flop) " (math "=>") " #t")) ("Assignments are also useful for caching values. The example below uses a technique called " (var "memoization") ", in which a procedure records the values associated with old input values so it need not recompute them, to implement a fast version of the otherwise exponential doubly recursive definition of the Fibonacci function (see page " (link "./further.html#fibonacci" "69") ").") ((code "(define memoize" (br) "  (lambda (proc)" (br) "    (let ([cache '()])" (br) "      (lambda (x)" (br) "        (cond" (br) "          [(assq x cache) => cdr]" (br) "          [else" (br) "           (let ([ans (proc x)])" (br) "             (set! cache (cons (cons x ans) cache))" (br) "             ans)])))))" (br) (br) "(define fibonacci" (br) "  (memoize" (br) "    (lambda (n)" (br) "      (if (< n 2)" (br) "          1" (br) "          (+ (fibonacci (- n 1)) (fibonacci (- n 2)))))))" (br) (br) "(fibonacci 100) " (math "=>") " 573147844013817084101"))) ("control:s1" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "