f " (code "begin") " is primarily used by syntactic extensions that must expand into multiple definitions. (See page " (link "./binding.html#multi-define-syntax" "101") ".)") ("The bodies of many syntactic forms, including " (code "lambda") ", " (code "case-lambda") ", " (code "let") ", " (code "let*") ", " (code "letrec") ", and " (code "letrec*") ", as well as the result clauses of " (code "cond") ", " (code "case") ", and " (code "do") ", are treated as if they were inside an implicit " (code "begin") "; i.e., the expressions making up the body or result clause are executed in sequence, with the values of the last expression being returned.") ((code "(define swap-pair!" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (let ([temp (car x)])" (br) "      (set-car! x (cdr x))" (br) "      (set-cdr! x temp)" (br) "      x)))" (br) "(swap-pair! (cons 'a 'b)) " (math "=>") " (b . a)"))) ("control:s8" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(if " (var "test") " " (var "consequent") " " (var "alternative") ")") (br) (bold "syntax") ": " (code "(if " (var "test") " " (var "consequent") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of " (code (var "consequent")) " or " (code (var "alternative")) " depending on the value of " (code (var "test")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The " (code (var "test")) ", " (code (var "consequent")) ", and " (code (var "alternative")) " subforms must be expressions. If " (code (var "test")) " evaluates to a true value (anything other than " (code "#f") "), " (code "consequent") " is evaluated and its values are returned. Otherwise, " (code "alternative") " is evaluated and its values are returned. With the second, \"one-armed,\" form, which has no " (code (var "alternative")) ", the result is unspecified if " (code (var "test")) " evaluates to false.") ((code "(let ([ls '(a b c)])" (br) "  (if (null? ls)" (br) "      '()" (br) "      (cdr ls))) " (math "=>") " (b c)" (br) (br) "(let ([ls '()])" (br) "  (if (null? ls)" (br) "      '()" 