:") ((code "(define-syntax unless" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ e0 e1 e2 ...)" (br) "     (when (not e0) e1 e2 ...)]))"))) ("control:s18" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(case " (var "expr" (sub "0")) " " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("Each clause but the last must take the form") ((code "((" (var "key") " ...) " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("where each " (code (var "key")) " is a datum distinct from the other keys. The last clause may be in the above form or it may be an " (code "else") " clause of the form") ((code "(else " (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ((code (var "expr" (sub "0"))) " is evaluated and the result is compared (using " (code "eqv?") ") against the keys of each clause in order. If a clause containing a matching key is found, the expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") " are evaluated in sequence and the values of the last expression are returned.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and an " (code "else") " clause is present, the expressions " (code (var "expr" (sub "1")) " " (var "expr" (sub "2")) " ...") " of the " (code "else") " clause are evaluated in sequence and the values of the last expression are returned.") ("If none of the clauses contains a matching key and no " (code "else") " clause is present, the value or values are unspecified.") ("See page " (link "./syntax.html#defn:case" "306") " for a syntax definition of " (code "case") ".") ((code "(let ([x 4] [y 5])" (br) "  (case (+ x y)" (br) "    [(1 3 5 7 9) 'odd]" (br) "    [(0 2 4 6 8) 'even]" (br) "    [else 'out-of-range])) " (math "=>") " odd"))) ("control:s20" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(let " (var "name") " ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "values of the final body expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("This form of " (code "let") ", called " (code (var "named")) "  " (code "let") ", is a general-purpose iteration and recursion construct. It is similar to the more common form of " (code "let") "  (see Section " (link "./binding.html#g92" "4.4") ") in the binding of the variables " (code (var "var") " ...") " to the values of " (code (var "expr") " ...") " within the body " (code (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...") ", which is processed and evaluated like a " (code "lambda") " body. In addition, the variable " (code (var "name")) " is bound within the body to a procedure that may be called to recur or iterate; the arguments to the procedure become the new values of the variables " (code (var "var") " ...") ".") ("A named " (code "let") " expression of the form") ((code "(let " (var "name") " ((" (var "var") " " (var "expr") ") ...)" (br) "  " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)")) ("can be rewritten with " (code "letrec") " as follows.") ((code "((letrec ((" (var "name") " (lambda (" (var "var") " ...) " (var "body" (sub "1")) " " (var "body" (sub "2")) " ...)))" (br) "   " (var "name") ")" (br) " " (var "expr") " ...)")) ("A syntax definition of " (code "let") " that implements this transformation and handles unnamed " (code "let") " as well can be found on page " (link "./syntax.html#defn:let" "312") ".") ("The procedure " (code "divisors") " defined below uses named " (code "let") " to compute the nontrivial divisors of a nonnegative integer.") ((code "(define divisors" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (let f ([i 2])" (br) "      (cond" (br) "        [(>= i n) '()]" (br) "        [(integer? (/ n i)) (cons i (f (+ i 1)))]" (br) "        [else (f (+ i 1))]))))" (br) (br) "(divisors 5) " (math "=>") " ()" (br) "(divisors 32) " (math "=>") " (2 4 8 16)")) ("The version above is non-tail-recursive when a divisor is found and tail-recursive when a divisor is not found. The version below is fully tail-recursive. It builds up the list in reverse order, but this is easy to remedy, if desired, by reversing the list on exit.") ((code "(define divisors" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (let f ([i 2] [ls '()])" (br) "      (cond" (br) "        [(>= i n) ls]" (br) "        [(integer? (/ n i)) (f (+ i 1) (cons i ls))]" (br) "        [else (f (+ i 1) ls)]))))"))) ("control:s25" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(do ((" (var "var") " " (var "init") " " (var "update") ") ...) (" (var "test") " " (var "result") " ...) " (var "expr") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "the values of the last " (code (var "result")) " expression" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs control)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "do") " allows a common restricted form of iteration to be expressed succinctly. The variables " (code (var "var") " ...") " are bound initially to the values of " (code (var "init") " ...") " and are rebound on each subsequent iteration to the values of " (code (var "update") " ...") ". The expressions " (code (var "test")) ",  " (code (var "update") " ...") ", " (code (var "expr") " ...") ", and " (code (var "result") " ...") "  are all within the scope of the bindings established for " (code (var "var") " ...") ".") ("On each step, the test expression " (code (var "test")) " is evaluated. If the value of " (code (var "test")) " is true, iteration ceases, the expressions " (code (var "result") " ...") " are evaluated in sequence, and the values of the last expression are returned. If no result expressions are present, the value or values of the " (code "do") "  expression are unspecified.") ("If the value of " (code (var "test")) " is false, the expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") "  are evaluated in sequence, the expressions " (code (var "update") " ...") " are evaluated, new bindings for " (code (var "var") " ...") " to the values of " (code (var "update") " ...") " are created, and iteration continues.") ("The expressions " (code (var "expr") " ...") " are evaluated only for effect and are often omitted entirely. Any " (code (var "update")) " expression may be omitted, in which case the effect is the same as if the " (code (var "update")) " were simply the corresponding " (code (var "var")) ".") ("Although looping constructs in most languages require that the loop iterands be updated via assignment, " (code "do") " requires the loop iterands " (code (var "var") " ...") " to be updated via rebinding. In fact, no side effects are involved in the evaluation of a " (code "do") " expression unless they are performed explicitly by its subexpressions.") ("See page " (link "./syntax.html#defn:do" "313") " for a syntax definition of " (code "do") ".") ("The definitions of " (code "factorial") " and " (code "fibonacci") " below are straightforward translations of the tail-recursive named-" (code "let") " versions given in Section " (link "./further.html#g55" "3.2") ".") ((code "(define factorial" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (do ([i n (- i 1)] [a 1 (* a i)])" (br) "        ((zero? i) a))))" (br) (br) "(factorial 10) " (math "=>") " 3628800" (br) (br) "(define fibonacci" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (if (= n 0)" (br) "        0" (br) "        (do ([i n (- i 1)] [a1 1 (+ a1 a2)] [a2 0 a1])" (br) "            ((= i 1) a1)))))" (br) (br) "(fibonacci 6) " (math "=>") " 8")) ("The definition of " (code "divisors") " below is similar to the tail-recursive definition of " (code "divisors") " given with the description of named " (code "let") " above.") ((code "(define divisors" (br) "  (lambda (n)" (br) "    (do ([i 2 (+ i 1)]" (br) "         [ls '()" (br) "             (if (integer? (/ n i))" (br) "                 (cons i ls)" (br) "                 ls)])" (br) "        ((>= i n) ls))))")) ("The definition of " (code "scale-vector!") " below, which scales each element of a vector " (var "v") " by a constant " (var "k") ", demonstrates a nonempty " (code "do") " body.") ((code "(define scale-vector!" (br) "  (lambda (v k)" (br) "    (let ([n (vector-length v)])" (br) "      (do ([i 0 (+ i 1)])" (br) "          ((= i n))" (br) "        (vector-set! v i (* (vector-ref v i) k))))))" (br) (br) "(define vec (vector 1 2 3 4 5))" (br) "(scale-vector! vec 2)" (br) "vec " (math "=>") " #(2 4 6 8 10)"))) ("control:s30" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(map " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "list of results" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "map") " applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to corresponding elements of the lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " and returns a list of the resulting values. The lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " must be of the same length. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept as many arguments as there are lists, should return a single value, and should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments.") ((code "(map abs '(1 -2 3 -4 5 -6)) " (math "=>") " (1 2 3 4 5 6)" (br) (br) "(map (lambda (x y) (* x y))" (br) "     '(1 2 3 4)" (br) "     '(8 7 6 5)) " (math "=>") " (8 14 18 20)")) ("While the order in which the applications themselves occur is not specified, the order of the values in the output list is the same as that of the corresponding values in the input lists.") ((code "map") " might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define map" (br) "  (lambda (f ls . more)" (br) "    (if (null? more)" (br) "        (let map1 ([ls ls])" (br) "          (if (null? ls)" (br) "              '()" (br) "              (cons (f (car ls))" (br) "                    (map1 (cdr ls)))))" (br) "        (let map-more ([ls ls] [more more])" (br) "          (if (null? ls)" (br) "              '()" (br) "              (cons" (br) "                (apply f (car ls) (map car more))" (br) "                (map-more (cdr ls) (map cdr more))))))))")) ("No error checking is done by this version of " (code "map") "; " (code "f") " is assumed to be a procedure and the other arguments are assumed to be proper lists of the same length. An interesting feature of this definition is that " (code "map") " uses itself to pull out the cars and cdrs of the list of input lists; this works because of the special treatment of the single-list case.")) ("control:s33" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(for-each " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "for-each") " is similar to " (code "map") " except that " (code "for-each") " does not create and return a list of the resulting values, and " (code "for-each") "  guarantees to perform the applications in sequence over the elements from left to right. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept as many arguments as there are lists and should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments. " (code "for-each") " may be defined without error checks as follows.") ((code "(define for-each" (br) "  (lambda (f ls . more)" (br) "    (do ([ls ls (cdr ls)] [more more (map cdr more)])" (br) "        ((null? ls))" (br) "      (apply f (car ls) (map car more)))))" (br) (br) "(let ([same-count 0])" (br) "  (for-each" (br) "    (lambda (x y)" (br) "      (when (= x y)" (br) "        (set! same-count (+ same-count 1))))" (br) "    '(1 2 3 4 5 6)" (br) "    '(2 3 3 4 7 6))" (br) "  same-count) " (math "=>") " 3"))) ("control:s36" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(exists " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs lists)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " must be of the same length. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept as many arguments as there are lists and should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments. If the lists are empty, " (code "exists") " returns " (code "#f") ". Otherwise, " (code "exists") " applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to corresponding elements of the lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " in sequence until either the lists each have only one element or " (code (var "procedure")) " returns a true value " (code (var "t")) ". In the former case, " (code "exists") " tail-calls " (code (var "procedure")) ", applying it to the remaining element of each list. In the latter case, " (code "exists") " returns " (code (var "t")) ".") ((code "(exists symbol? '(1.0 #\\a \"hi\" '())) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) (br) "(exists member" (br) "        '(a b c)" (br) "        '((c b) (b a) (a c))) " (math "=>") " (b a)" (br) (br) "(exists (lambda (x y z) (= (+ x y) z))" (br) "        '(1 2 3 4)" (br) "        '(1.2 2.3 3.4 4.5)" (br) "        '(2.3 4.4 6.4 8.6)) " (math "=>") " #t")) ((code "exists") " may be defined (somewhat inefficiently and without error checks) as follows:") ((code "(define exists" (br) "  (lambda (f ls . more)" (br) "    (and (not (null? ls))" (br) "      (let exists ([x (car ls)] [ls (cdr ls)] [more more])" (br) "        (if (null? ls)" (br) "            (apply f x (map car more))" (br) "            (or (apply f x (map car more))" (br) "                (exists (car ls) (cdr ls) (map cdr more))))))))"))) ("control:s37" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(for-all " (var "procedure") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs lists)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " must be of the same length. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept as many arguments as there are lists and should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments. If the lists are empty, " (code "for-all") " returns " (code "#t") ". Otherwise, " (code "for-all") " applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to corresponding elements of the lists " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " in sequence until either the lists each have only one element left or " (code (var "procedure")) " returns " (code "#f") ". In the former case, " (code "for-all") " tail-calls " (code (var "procedure")) ", applying it to the remaining element of each list. In the latter case, " (code "for-all") " returns " (code "#f") ".") ((code "(for-all symbol? '(a b c d)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(for-all =" (br) "         '(1 2 3 4)" (br) "         '(1.0 2.0 3.0 4.0)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) (br) "(for-all (lambda (x y z) (= (+ x y) z))" (br) "         '(1 2 3 4)" (br) "         '(1.2 2.3 3.4 4.5)" (br) "         '(2.2 4.3 6.5 8.5)) " (math "=>") " #f")) ((code "for-all") " may be defined (somewhat inefficiently and without error checks) as follows:") ((code "(define for-all" (br) "  (lambda (f ls . more)" (br) "    (or (null? ls)" (br) "      (let for-all ([x (car ls)] [ls (cdr ls)] [more more])" (br) "        (if (null? ls)" (br) "            (apply f x (map car more))" (br) "            (and (apply f x (map car more))" (br) "                 (for-all (car ls) (cdr ls) (map cdr more))))))))"))) ("control:s38" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fold-left " (var "procedure") " " (var "obj") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs lists)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The " (code (var "list")) " arguments should all have the same length. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one more argument than the number of " (code (var "list")) "  arguments and return a single value. It should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments.") ((code "fold-left") " returns " (code (var "obj")) " if the " (code (var "list")) " arguments are empty. If they are not empty, " (code "fold-left") " applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to " (code (var "obj")) " and the cars of " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") ", then recurs with the value returned by " (code (var "procedure")) " in place of " (code (var "obj")) " and the cdr of each " (code (var "list")) " in place of the " (code (var "list")) ".") ((code "(fold-left cons '() '(1 2 3 4)) " (math "=>") " ((((() . 1) . 2) . 3) . 4)" (br) (br) "(fold-left" (br) "  (lambda (a x) (+ a (* x x)))" (br) "  0 '(1 2 3 4 5)) " (math "=>") " 55" (br) (br) "(fold-left" (br) "  (lambda (a . args) (append args a))" (br) "  '(question)" (br) "  '(that not to)" (br) "  '(is to be)" (br) "  '(the be: or)) " (math "=>") " (to be or not to be: that is the question)"))) ("control:s41" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(fold-right " (var "procedure") " " (var "obj") " " (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs lists)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The " (code (var "list")) " arguments should all have the same length. " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept one more argument than the number of " (code (var "list")) "  arguments and return a single value. It should not mutate the " (code (var "list")) " arguments.") ((code "fold-right") " returns " (code (var "obj")) " if the " (code (var "list")) " arguments are empty. If they are not empty, " (code "fold-right") " recurs with the cdr of each " (code (var "list")) " replacing the " (code (var "list")) ", then applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to the cars of " (code (var "list" (sub "1")) " " (var "list" (sub "2")) " ...") " and the result returned by the recursion.") ((code "(fold-right cons '() '(1 2 3 4)) " (math "=>") " (1 2 3 4)" (br) (br) "(fold-right" (br) "  (lambda (x a) (+ a (* x x)))" (br) "  0 '(1 2 3 4 5)) " (math "=>") " 55" (br) (br) "(fold-right" (br) "  (lambda (x y a) (cons* x y a))   " (math "=>") " (parting is such sweet sorrow" (br) "  '((with apologies))              " (math "  ") "  gotta go see ya tomorrow" (br) "  '(parting such sorrow go ya)     " (math "  ") "  (with apologies))" (br) "  '(is sweet gotta see tomorrow))"))) ("control:s44" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(vector-map " (var "procedure") " " (var "vector" (sub "1")) " " (var "vector" (sub "1")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "vector of results" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "vector-map") " applies " (code (var "procedure")) " to corresponding elements of " (code (var "vector" (sub "1")) " " (var "vector" (sub "2")) " ...") " and returns a vector of the resulting values. The vectors " (code (var "vector" (sub "1")) " " (var "vector" (sub "2")) " ...") " must be of the same length, and " (code (var "procedure")) " should accept as many arguments as there are vectors and return a single value.") ((code "(vector-map abs '#(1 -2 3 -4 5 -6)) " (math "=>") " #(1 2 3 4 5 6)" (br) "(vector-map (lambda (x y) (* x y))" (br) "  '#(1 2 3 4)" (br) "  '#(8 7 6 5)) " (math "=>") " #(8 14 18 20)")) ("While the order in which the applications themselves occur is not specified, the order of the values in the output vector is the same as that of the corresponding values in the input vectors.")) ("control:s47" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(vector-for-each " (var "procedure") " " (var "vector" (sub "1")) " " (var "vector" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "vector-for-each") " is similar to " (code "vector-map") " except that " (code "vector-for-each") " does not create and return a vector of the resulting values, and " (code "vector-for-each") "  guarantees to perform the applications in sequence over the elements from left to right.") ((code "(let ([same-count 0])" (br) "  (vector-for-each" (br) "    (lambda (x y)" (br) "      (when (= x y)" (br) "        (set! same-count (+ same-count 1))))" (br) "    '#(1 2 3 4 5 6)" (br) "    '#(2 3 3 4 7 6))" (br) "  same-count) " (math "=>") " 3"))) ("control:s50" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(string-for-each " (var "procedure") " " (var "string" (sub "1")) " " (var "string" (sub "2")) " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "unspecified" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "string-for-each") " is similar to " (code "for-each") " and " (code "vector-for-each") "  except that the inputs are strings rather than lists or vectors.") ((code "(let ([ls '()])" (br) "  (string-for-each" (br) "    (lambda r (set! ls (cons r ls)))" (br) "    \"abcd\"" (br) "    \"====\"" (br) "    \"1234\")" (br) "  (map list->string (reverse ls))) " (math "=>") " (\"a=1\" \"b=2\" \"c=3\" \"d=4\")"))) ("control:s54" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call/cc " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-current-continuation " (var "procedure") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("These procedures are the same. The shorter name is often used for the obvious reason that it requires fewer keystrokes to type.") ((code "call/cc") " obtains its continuation and passes it to " (code (var "procedure")) ", which should accept one argument. The continuation itself is represented by a procedure. Each time this procedure is applied to zero or more values, it returns the values to the continuation of the " (code "call/cc") " application. That is, when the continuation procedure is called, it returns its arguments as the values of the application of " (code "call/cc") ".") ("If " (code (var "procedure")) " returns normally when passed the continuation procedure, the values returned by " (code "call/cc") " are the values returned by " (code (var "procedure")) ".") ("Continuations allow the implementation of nonlocal exits, backtracking [" (link "./bibliography.html#g232" "14") "," (link "./bibliography.html#g247" "29") "], coroutines [" (link "./bibliography.html#g234" "16") "], and multitasking [" (link "./bibliography.html#g228" "10") "," (link "./bibliography.html#g250" "32") "].") ("The example below illustrates the use of a continuation to perform a nonlocal exit from a loop.") ((code "(define member" (br) "  (lambda (x ls)" (br) "    (call/cc" (br) "      (lambda (break)" (br) "        (do ([ls ls (cdr ls)])" (br) "            ((null? ls) #f)" (br) "          (when (equal? x (car ls))" (br) "            (break ls)))))))" (br) (br) "(member 'd '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(member 'b '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " (b c)")) ("Additional examples are given in Sections " (link "./further.html#g63" "3.3") "  and " (link "./examples.html#g208" "12.11") ".") ("The current continuation is typically represented internally as a stack of procedure activation records, and obtaining the continuation involves encapsulating the stack within a procedural object. Since an encapsulated stack has indefinite extent, some mechanism must be used to preserve the stack contents indefinitely. This can be done with surprising ease and efficiency and with no impact on programs that do not use continuations [" (link "./bibliography.html#g235" "17") "].")) ("control:s56" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(dynamic-wind " (var "in") " " (var "body") " " (var "out") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "values resulting from the application of " (code (var "body")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "dynamic-wind") " offers \"protection\" from continuation invocation. It is useful for performing tasks that must be performed whenever control enters or leaves " (code (var "body")) ", either normally or by continuation application.") ("The three arguments " (code (var "in")) ", " (code (var "body")) ", and " (code (var "out")) " must be procedures and should accept zero arguments, i.e., they should be " (code (var "thunks")) ". Before applying " (code (var "body")) ", and each time " (code (var "body")) " is entered subsequently by the application of a continuation created within " (code (var "body")) ", the " (code (var "in")) "  thunk is applied. Upon normal exit from " (code (var "body")) " and each time " (code (var "body")) " is exited by the application of a continuation created outside " (code (var "body")) ", the " (code (var "out")) "  thunk is applied.") ("Thus, it is guaranteed that " (code (var "in")) " is invoked at least once. In addition, if " (code (var "body")) " ever returns, " (code (var "out")) " is invoked at least once.") ("The following example demonstrates the use of " (code "dynamic-wind") " to be sure that an input port is closed after processing, regardless of whether the processing completes normally.") ((code "(let ([p (open-input-file \"input-file\")])" (br) "  (dynamic-wind" (br) "    (lambda () #f)" (br) "    (lambda () (process p))" (br) "    (lambda () (close-port p))))")) ("Common Lisp provides a similar facility (" (code "unwind-protect") ") for protection from nonlocal exits. This is often sufficient. " (code "unwind-protect") " provides only the equivalent to " (code (var "out")) ", however, since Common Lisp does not support fully general continuations. Here is how " (code "unwind-protect") " might be specified with " (code "dynamic-wind") ".") ((code "(define-syntax unwind-protect" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ body cleanup ...)" (br) "     (dynamic-wind" (br) "       (lambda () #f)" (br) "       (lambda () body)" (br) "       (lambda () cleanup ...))]))" (br) (br) "((call/cc" (br) "   (let ([x 'a])" (br) "     (lambda (k)" (br) "       (unwind-protect" (br) "         (k (lambda () x))" (br) "         (set! x 'b)))))) " (math "=>") " b")) ("Some Scheme implementations support a controlled form of assignment known as " (var "fluid binding") ", in which a variable takes on a temporary value during a given computation and reverts to the old value after the computation has completed. The syntactic form " (code "fluid-let") " defined below in terms of " (code "dynamic-wind") "  permits the fluid binding of a single variable " (code "x") " to the value of an expression " (code "e") " within a the body " (code "b1 b2 ...") ".") ((code "(define-syntax fluid-let" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ ((x e)) b1 b2 ...)" (br) "     (let ([y e])" (br) "       (let ([swap (lambda () (let ([t x]) (set! x y) (set! y t)))])" (br) "         (dynamic-wind swap (lambda () b1 b2 ...) swap)))]))")) ("Implementations that support " (code "fluid-let") " typically extend it to allow an indefinite number of " (code "(x e)") " pairs, as with " (code "let") ".") ("If no continuations are invoked within the body of a " (code "fluid-let") ", the behavior is the same as if the variable were simply assigned the new value on entry and assigned the old value on return.") ((code "(let ([x 3])" (br) "  (+ (fluid-let ([x 5])" (br) "       x)" (br) "     x)) " (math "=>") " 8")) ("A fluid-bound variable also reverts to the old value if a continuation created outside of the " (code "fluid-let") " is invoked.") ((code "(let ([x 'a])" (br) "  (let ([f (lambda () x)])" (br) "    (cons (call/cc" (br) "            (lambda (k)" (br) "              (fluid-let ([x 'b])" (br) "                (k (f)))))" (br) "          (f)))) " (math "=>") " (b . a)")) ("If control has left a " (code "fluid-let") " body, either normally or by the invocation of a continuation, and control reenters the body by the invocation of a continuation, the temporary value of the fluid-bound variable is reinstated. Furthermore, any changes to the temporary value are maintained and reflected upon reentry.") ((code "(define reenter #f)" (br) "(define x 0)" (br) "(fluid-let ([x 1])" (br) "  (call/cc (lambda (k) (set! reenter k)))" (br) "  (set! x (+ x 1))" (br) "  x) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) "x " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "(reenter '*) " (math "=>") " 3" (br) "(reenter '*) " (math "=>") " 4" (br) "x " (math "=>") " 0")) ("A library showing how " (code "dynamic-wind") " might be implemented were it not already built in is given below. In addition to defining " (code "dynamic-wind") ", the code defines a version of " (code "call/cc") "  that does its part to support " (code "dynamic-wind") ".") ((code "(library (dynamic-wind)" (br) "  (export dynamic-wind call/cc" (br) "    (rename (call/cc call-with-current-continuation)))" (br) "  (import (rename (except (rnrs) dynamic-wind) (call/cc rnrs:call/cc)))" (br) (br) "  (define winders '())" (br) (br) "  (define common-tail" (br) "    (lambda (x y)" (br) "      (let ([lx (length x)] [ly (length y)])" (br) "        (do ([x (if (> lx ly) (list-tail x (- lx ly)) x) (cdr x)]" (br) "             [y (if (> ly lx) (list-tail y (- ly lx)) y) (cdr y)])" (br) "            ((eq? x y) x)))))" (br) (br) "  (define do-wind" (br) "    (lambda (new)" (br) "      (let ([tail (common-tail new winders)])" (br) "        (let f ([ls winders])" (br) "          (if (not (eq? ls tail))" (br) "              (begin" (br) "                (set! winders (cdr ls))" (br) "                ((cdar ls))" (br) "                (f (cdr ls)))))" (br) "        (let f ([ls new])" (br) "          (if (not (eq? ls tail))" (br) "              (begin" (br) "                (f (cdr ls))" (br) "                ((caar ls))" (br) "                (set! winders ls)))))))")) ((code "  (define call/cc" (br) "    (lambda (f)" (br) "      (rnrs:call/cc" (br) "        (lambda (k)" (br) "          (f (let ([save winders])" (br) "               (lambda (x)" (br) "                 (unless (eq? save winders) (do-wind save))" (br) "                 (k x))))))))" (br) (br) "  (define dynamic-wind" (br) "    (lambda (in body out)" (br) "      (in)" (br) "      (set! winders (cons (cons in out) winders))" (br) "      (let-values ([ans* (body)])" (br) "        (set! winders (cdr winders))" (br) "        (out)" (br) "        (apply values ans*)))))")) ("Together, " (code "dynamic-wind") " and " (code "call/cc") " manage a list of " (var "winders") ". A winder is a pair of " (var "in") " and " (var "out") " thunks established by a call to " (code "dynamic-wind") ". Whenever " (code "dynamic-wind") " is invoked, the " (var "in") " thunk is invoked, a new winder containing the " (var "in") " and " (var "out") " thunks is placed on the winders list, the " (var "body") " thunk is invoked, the winder is removed from the winders list, and the " (var "out") "  thunk is invoked. This ordering ensures that the winder is on the winders list only when control has passed through " (var "in") " and not yet entered " (var "out") ". Whenever a continuation is obtained, the winders list is saved, and whenever the continuation is invoked, the saved winders list is reinstated. During reinstatement, the " (var "out") " thunk of each winder on the current winders list that is not also on the saved winders list is invoked, followed by the " (var "in") " thunk of each winder on the saved winders list that is not also on the current winders list. The winders list is updated incrementally, again to ensure that a winder is on the current winders list only if control has passed through its " (var "in") " thunk and not entered its " (var "out") " thunk.") ("The test " (code "(not (eq? save winders))") " performed in " (code "call/cc") "  is not strictly necessary but makes invoking a continuation less costly whenever the saved winders list is the same as the current winders list.")) ("control:s65" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(delay " (var "expr") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a promise" (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(force " (var "promise") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "result of forcing " (code (var "promise")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs r5rs)")) ("The first time a promise created by " (code "delay") " is " (var "forced") " (with " (code "force") "), it evaluates " (code (var "expr")) ", \"remembering\" the resulting value. Thereafter, each time the promise is forced, it returns the remembered value instead of reevaluating " (code (var "expr")) ".") ((code "delay") " and " (code "force") " are typically used only in the absence of side effects, e.g., assignments, so that the order of evaluation is unimportant.") ("The benefit of using " (code "delay") " and " (code "force") " is that some amount of computation might be avoided altogether if it is delayed until absolutely required. Delayed evaluation may be used to construct conceptually infinite lists, or " (var "streams") ". The example below shows how a stream abstraction may be built with " (code "delay") " and " (code "force") ". A stream is a promise that, when forced, returns a pair whose cdr is a stream.") ((code "(define stream-car" (br) "  (lambda (s)" (br) "    (car (force s))))" (br) (br) "(define stream-cdr" (br) "  (lambda (s)" (br) "    (cdr (force s))))" (br) (br) "(define counters" (br) "  (let next ([n 1])" (br) "    (delay (cons n (next (+ n 1))))))" (br) (br) "(stream-car counters) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) (br) "(stream-car (stream-cdr counters)) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) (br) "(define stream-add" (br) "  (lambda (s1 s2)" (br) "    (delay (cons" (br) "             (+ (stream-car s1) (stream-car s2))" (br) "             (stream-add (stream-cdr s1) (stream-cdr s2))))))" (br) (br) "(define even-counters" (br) "  (stream-add counters counters))" (br) (br) "(stream-car even-counters) " (math "=>") " 2" (br) (br) "(stream-car (stream-cdr even-counters)) " (math "=>") " 4")) ((code "delay") " may be defined by") ((code "(define-syntax delay" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ expr) (make-promise (lambda () expr))]))")) ("where " (code "make-promise") " might be defined as follows.") ((code "(define make-promise" (br) "  (lambda (p)" (br) "    (let ([val #f] [set? #f])" (br) "      (lambda ()" (br) "        (unless set?" (br) "          (let ([x (p)])" (br) "            (unless set?" (br) "              (set! val x)" (br) "              (set! set? #t))))" (br) "        val))))")) ("With this definition of " (code "delay") ", " (code "force") " simply invokes the promise to force evaluation or to retrieve the saved value.") ((code "(define force" (br) "  (lambda (promise)" (br) "    (promise)))")) ("The second test of the variable " (code "set?") " in " (code "make-promise") " is necessary in the event that, as a result of applying " (code (var "p")) ", the promise is recursively forced. Since a promise must always return the same value, the result of the first application of " (code (var "p")) " to complete is returned.") ("Whether " (code "delay") " and " (code "force") " handle multiple return values is unspecified; the implementation given above does not, but the following version does, with the help of " (code "call-with-values") " and " (code "apply") ".") ((code "(define make-promise" (br) "  (lambda (p)" (br) "    (let ([vals #f] [set? #f])" (br) "      (lambda ()" (br) "        (unless set?" (br) "          (call-with-values p" (br) "            (lambda x" (br) "              (unless set?" (br) "                (set! vals x)" (br) "                (set! set? #t)))))" (br) "        (apply values vals)))))" (br) (br) "(define p (delay (values 1 2 3)))" (br) "(force p) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "          " (math "  ") " 2" (br) "          " (math "  ") " 3" (br) "(call-with-values (lambda () (force p)) +) " (math "=>") " 6")) ("Neither implementation is quite right, since " (code "force") " must raise an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if its argument is not a promise. Since distinguishing procedures created by " (code "make-promise") " from other procedures is impossible, " (code "force") " cannot do so reliably. The following reimplementation of " (code "make-promise") " and " (code "force") "  represents promises as records of the type " (code "promise") " to allow " (code "force") " to make the required check.") ((code "(define-record-type promise" (br) "  (fields (immutable p) (mutable vals) (mutable set?))" (br) "  (protocol (lambda (new) (lambda (p) (new p #f #f)))))" (br) (br) "(define force" (br) "  (lambda (promise)" (br) "    (unless (promise? promise)" (br) "      (assertion-violation 'promise \"invalid argument\" promise))" (br) "    (unless (promise-set? promise)" (br) "      (call-with-values (promise-p promise)" (br) "        (lambda x" (br) "          (unless (promise-set? promise)" (br) "            (promise-vals-set! promise x)" (br) "            (promise-set?-set! promise #t)))))" (br) "    (apply values (promise-vals promise))))"))) ("control:s70" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(values " (var "obj") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code (var "obj") " ...") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("The procedure " (code "values") " accepts any number of arguments and simply passes (returns) the arguments to its continuation.") ((code "(values) " (math "=>") (br) (br) "(values 1) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) (br) "(values 1 2 3) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) "               " (math "  ") " 2" (br) "               " (math "  ") " 3" (br) (br) "(define head&tail" (br) "  (lambda (ls)" (br) "    (values (car ls) (cdr ls))))" (br) (br) "(head&tail '(a b c)) " (math "=>") " a" (br) "                     " (math "  ") " (b c)"))) ("control:s71" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(call-with-values " (var "producer") " " (var "consumer") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code (var "producer")) " and " (code (var "consumer")) " must be procedures. " (code "call-with-values") " applies " (code (var "consumer")) " to the values returned by invoking " (code (var "producer")) " without arguments.") ((code "(call-with-values" (br) "  (lambda () (values 'bond 'james))" (br) "  (lambda (x y) (cons y x))) " (math "=>") " (james . bond)" (br) (br) "(call-with-values values list) " (math "=>") " '()")) ("In the second example, " (code "values") " itself serves as the producer. It receives no arguments and thus returns no values. " (code "list") " is thus applied to no arguments and so returns the empty list.") ("The procedure " (code "dxdy") " defined below computes the change in " (code (var "x")) " and " (code (var "y")) " coordinates for a pair of points whose coordinates are represented by " (code "(" (var "x") " . " (var "y") ")") " pairs.") ((code "(define dxdy" (br) "  (lambda (p1 p2)" (br) "    (values (- (car p2) (car p1))" (br) "            (- (cdr p2) (cdr p1)))))" (br) (br) "(dxdy '(0 . 0) '(0 . 5)) " (math "=>") " 0" (br) "                         " (math "  ") " 5")) ((code "dxdy") " can be used to compute the length and slope of a segment represented by two endpoints.") ((code "(define segment-length" (br) "  (lambda (p1 p2)" (br) "    (call-with-values" (br) "      (lambda () (dxdy p1 p2))" (br) "      (lambda (dx dy) (sqrt (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)))))))" (br) (br) "(define segment-slope" (br) "  (lambda (p1 p2)" (br) "    (call-with-values" (br) "      (lambda () (dxdy p1 p2))" (br) "      (lambda (dx dy) (/ dy dx)))))" (br) (br) "(segment-length '(1 . 4) '(4 . 8)) " (math "=>") " 5" (br) "(segment-slope '(1 . 4) '(4 . 8)) " (math "=>") " 4/3")) ("We can of course combine these to form one procedure that returns two values.") ((code "(define describe-segment" (br) "  (lambda (p1 p2)" (br) "    (call-with-values" (br) "      (lambda () (dxdy p1 p2))" (br) "      (lambda (dx dy)" (br) "        (values" (br) "          (sqrt (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)))" (br) "          (/ dy dx))))))" (br) (br) "(describe-segment '(1 . 4) '(4 . 8)) " (math "=>") " 5" (br) "                                     " (math "=>") " 4/3")) ("The example below employs multiple values to divide a list nondestructively into two sublists of alternating elements.") ((code "(define split" (br) "  (lambda (ls)" (br) "    (if (or (null? ls) (null? (cdr ls)))" (br) "        (values ls '())" (br) "        (call-with-values" (br) "          (lambda () (split (cddr ls)))" (br) "          (lambda (odds evens)" (br) "            (values (cons (car ls) odds)" (br) "                    (cons (cadr ls) evens)))))))" (br) (br) "(split '(a b c d e f)) " (math "=>") " (a c e)" (br) "                       " (math "  ") " (b d f)")) ("At each level of recursion, the procedure " (code "split") " returns two values:  a list of the odd-numbered elements from the argument list and a list of the even-numbered elements.") ("The continuation of a call to " (code "values") " need not be one established by a call to " (code "call-with-values") ", nor must only " (code "values") " be used to return to a continuation established by " (code "call-with-values") ". In particular, " (code "(values " (var "e") ")") " and " (code (var "e")) " are equivalent expressions. For example:") ((code "(+ (values 2) 4) " (math "=>") " 6" (br) (br) "(if (values #t) 1 2) " (math "=>") " 1" (br) (br) "(call-with-values" (br) "  (lambda () 4)" (br) "  (lambda (x) x)) " (math "=>") " 4")) ("Similarly, " (code "values") " may be used to pass any number of values to a continuation that ignores the values, as in the following.") ((code "(begin (values 1 2 3) 4) " (math "=>") " 4")) ("Because a continuation may accept zero or more than one value, continuations obtained via " (code "call/cc") " may accept zero or more than one argument.") ((code "(call-with-values" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (call/cc (lambda (k) (k 2 3))))" (br) "  (lambda (x y) (list x y))) " (math "=>") " (2 3)")) ("The behavior is unspecified when a continuation expecting exactly one value receives zero values or more than one value. For example, the behavior of each of the following expressions is unspecified. Some implementations raise an exception, while others silently suppress additional values or supply defaults for missing values.") ((code "(if (values 1 2) 'x 'y)" (br) (br) "(+ (values) 5)")) ("Programs that wish to force extra values to be ignored in particular contexts can do so easily by calling " (code "call-with-values") " explicitly. A syntactic form, which we might call " (code "first") ", can be defined to abstract the discarding of more than one value when only one is desired.") ((code "(define-syntax first" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ expr)" (br) "     (call-with-values" (br) "       (lambda () expr)" (br) "       (lambda (x . y) x))]))" (br) (br) "(if (first (values #t #f)) 'a 'b) " (math "=>") " a")) ("Since implementations are required to raise an exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " if a procedure does not accept the number of arguments passed to it, each of the following raises an exception.") ((code "(call-with-values" (br) "  (lambda () (values 2 3 4))" (br) "  (lambda (x y) x))" (br) (br) "(call-with-values" (br) "  (lambda () (call/cc (lambda (k) (k 0))))" (br) "  (lambda (x y) x))")) ("Since " (code (var "producer")) " is most often a " (code "lambda") " expression, it is often convenient to use a syntactic extension that suppresses the lambda expression in the interest of readability.") ((code "(define-syntax with-values" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ expr consumer)" (br) "     (call-with-values (lambda () expr) consumer)]))" (br) (br) "(with-values (values 1 2) list) " (math "=>") " (1 2)" (br) "(with-values (split '(1 2 3 4))" (br) "  (lambda (odds evens)" (br) "    evens)) " (math "=>") " (2 4)")) ("If the " (code (var "consumer")) " is also a " (code "lambda") " expression, the multiple-value variants of " (code "let") " and " (code "let*") " described in Section " (link "./binding.html#g93" "4.5") " are usually even more convenient.") ((code "(let-values ([(odds evens) (split '(1 2 3 4))])" (br) "  evens) " (math "=>") " (2 4)" (br) (br) "(let-values ([ls (values 'a 'b 'c)])" (br) "  ls) " (math "=>") " (a b c)")) ("Many standard syntactic forms and procedures  pass along multiple values. Most of these are \"automatic,\" in the sense that nothing special must be done by the implementation to make this happen. The usual expansion of " (code "let") " into a direct " (code "lambda") " call automatically propagates multiple values produced by the body of the " (code "let") ". Other operators must be coded specially to pass along multiple values. The " (code "call-with-port") " procedure (page " (link "./io.html#g127" "7.6") "), for example, calls its procedure argument, then closes the port argument before returning the procedure's values, so it must save the values temporarily. This is easily accomplished via " (code "let-values") ", " (code "apply") ", and " (code "values") ":") ((code "(define call-with-port" (br) "  (lambda (port proc)" (br) "    (let-values ([val* (proc port)])" (br) "      (close-port port)" (br) "      (apply values val*))))")) ("If this seems like too much overhead when a single value is returned, the code can use " (code "call-with-values") " and " (code "case-lambda") " to handle the single-value case more efficiently:") ((code "(define call-with-port" (br) "  (lambda (port proc)" (br) "    (call-with-values (lambda () (proc port))" (br) "      (case-lambda" (br) "        [(val) (close-port port) val]" (br) "        [val* (close-port port) (apply values val*)]))))")) ("The definitions of " (code "values") " and " (code "call-with-values") " (and concomitant redefinition of " (code "call/cc") ") in the library below demonstrate that the multiple-return-values interface could be implemented in Scheme if it were not already built in. No error checking can be done, however, for the case in which more than one value is returned to a single-value context, such as the test part of an " (code "if") " expression.") ((code "(library (mrvs)" (br) "  (export call-with-values values call/cc" (br) "    (rename (call/cc call-with-current-continuation)))" (br) "  (import" (br) "    (rename" (br) "      (except (rnrs) values call-with-values)" (br) "      (call/cc rnrs:call/cc)))" (br) (br) "  (define magic (cons 'multiple 'values))" (br) (br) "  (define magic?" (br) "    (lambda (x)" (br) "      (and (pair? x) (eq? (car x) magic))))")) ((code "  (define call/cc" (br) "    (lambda (p)" (br) "      (rnrs:call/cc" (br) "        (lambda (k)" (br) "          (p (lambda args" (br) "               (k (apply values args))))))))" (br) (br) "  (define values" (br) "    (lambda args" (br) "      (if (and (not (null? args)) (null? (cdr args)))" (br) "          (car args)" (br) "          (cons magic args))))" (br) (br) "  (define call-with-values" (br) "    (lambda (producer consumer)" (br) "      (let ([x (producer)])" (br) "        (if (magic? x)" (br) "            (apply consumer (cdr x))" (br) "            (consumer x))))))")) ("Multiple values can be implemented more efficiently [" (link "./bibliography.html#g220" "2") "], but this code serves to illustrate the meanings of the operators and may be used to provide multiple values in older, nonstandard implementations that do not support them.")) ("control:s80" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(eval " (var "obj") " " (var "environment") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "values of the Scheme expression represented by " (code (var "obj")) " in " (code (var "environment")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs eval)")) ("If " (code (var "obj")) " does not represent a syntactically valid expression, " (code "eval") "  raises an exception with condition type " (code "&syntax") ". The environments returned by " (code "environment") ", " (code "scheme-report-environment") ", and " (code "null-environment") " are immutable. Thus, " (code "eval") " also raises an exception with condition type " (code "&syntax") " if an assignment to any of the variables in the environment appears within the expression.") ((code "(define cons 'not-cons)" (br) "(eval '(let ([x 3]) (cons x 4)) (environment '(rnrs))) " (math "=>") " (3 . 4)" (br) (br) "(define lambda 'not-lambda)" (br) "(eval '(lambda (x) x) (environment '(rnrs))) " (math "=>") " #<procedure>" (br) (br) "(eval '(cons 3 4) (environment)) " (math "=>") " " (var "exception")))) ("control:s81" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(environment " (var "import-spec") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an environment" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs eval)")) ((code "environment") " returns an environment formed from the combined bindings of the given import specifiers. Each " (code (var "import-spec")) " must be an s-expression representing a valid import specifier (see Chapter " (link "./libraries.html#g142" "10") ").") ((code "(define env (environment '(rnrs) '(prefix (rnrs lists) $)))" (br) "(eval '($cons* 3 4 (* 5 8)) env) " (math "=>") " (3 4 . 40)"))) ("control:s82" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(null-environment " (var "version") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(scheme-report-environment " (var "version") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "an R5RS compatibility environment" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs r5rs)")) ((code (var "version")) " must be the exact integer " (code "5") ".") ((code "null-environment") " returns an environment containing bindings for the keywords whose meanings are defined by the Revised" (sup "5") " Report on Scheme, along with bindings for the auxiliary keywords " (code "else") ", " (code "=>") ", " (code "...") ", and " (code "_") ".") ((code "scheme-report-environment") " returns an environment containing the same keyword bindings as the environment returned by " (code "null-environment") " along with bindings for the variables whose meanings are defined by the Revised" (sup "5") " Report on Scheme, except those not defined by the Revised" (sup "6") " Report: " (code "load") ", " (code "interaction-environment") ", " (code "transcript-on") ", " (code "transcript-off") ", and " (code "char-ready?") ".") ("The bindings for each of the identifiers in the environments returned by these procedures are those of the corresponding Revised" (sup "6") " Report library, so this does not provide full backward compatibility, even if the excepted identifier bindings are not used.         ")) ("exceptions:s3" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(raise " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(raise-continuable " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs exceptions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("Both of these procedures raise an exception, effectively invoking the current exception handler, passing " (code (var "obj")) " as the only argument. For " (code "raise") ", the exception is " (var "non-continuable") ", while for " (code "raise-continuable") ", the exception is " (var "continuable") ". An exception handler may return (with zero or more values) to the continuation of a continuable exception. If an exception handler attempts to return to the continuation of a non-continuable exception, however, a new exception with condition type " (code "&non-continuable") " is raised. Thus, " (code "raise") " never returns, while " (code "raise-continuable") "  may return zero or more values, depending upon the exception handler.") ("If the current exception handler, " (code (var "p")) ", was established via a " (code "guard") " form or call to " (code "with-exception-handler") ", the current exception handler is reset to the handler that was current when " (code (var "p")) " was established before " (code "raise") " or " (code "raise-continuable") " invokes " (code (var "p")) ". This allows " (code (var "p")) " to defer to the preexisting exception handler simply by reraising the exception, and it helps prevent infinite regression when an exception handler inadvertently causes a different exception to be raised. If " (code (var "p")) " returns and the exception is continuable, " (code (var "p")) " is reinstated as the current exception handler.") ((code "(raise" (br) "  (condition" (br) "    (make-error)" (br) "    (make-message-condition \"no go\"))) " (math "=>") " " (var "error: no go") (br) "(raise-continuable" (br) "  (condition" (br) "    (make-violation)" (br) "    (make-message-condition \"oops\"))) " (math "=>") " " (var "violation: oops") (br) "(list" (br) "  (call/cc" (br) "    (lambda (k)" (br) "      (vector" (br) "        (with-exception-handler" (br) "          (lambda (x) (k (+ x 5)))" (br) "          (lambda () (+ (raise 17) 8))))))) " (math "=>") " (22)" (br) "(list" (br) "  (vector" (br) "    (with-exception-handler" (br) "      (lambda (x) (+ x 5))" (br) "      (lambda () (+ (raise-continuable 17) 8))))) " (math "=>") " (#(30))" (br) "(list" (br) "  (vector" (br) "    (with-exception-handler" (br) "      (lambda (x) (+ x 5))" (br) "      (lambda () (+ (raise 17) 8))))) " (math "=>") " " (var "violation: non-continuable")))) ("exceptions:s4" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(error " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(assertion-violation " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "irritant") " ...)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "error") " raises a non-continuable exception with condition type " (code "&error") " and should be used to describe situations for which the " (code "&error") " condition type is appropriate, typically a situation involving the program's interaction with something outside of the program. " (code "assertion-violation") " raises a non-continuable exception with condition type " (code "&assertion") " and should be used to describe situations for which the " (code "&assertion") " condition type is appropriate, typically an invalid argument to a procedure or invalid value of a subexpression of a syntactic form.") ("The continuation object with which the exception is raised also includes a " (code "&who") " condition whose who field is " (code (var "who")) " if " (code (var "who")) " is not " (code "#f") ", a " (code "&message") " condition whose message field is " (code (var "msg")) ", and an " (code "&irritants") " condition whose irritants field is " (code "(" (var "irritant") " ...)") ".") ((code (var "who")) " must be a string, a symbol, or " (code "#f") " identifying the procedure or syntactic form reporting the error upon whose behalf the error is being reported. It is usually best to identify a procedure the programmer has called rather than some other procedure the programmer may not be aware is involved in carrying out the operation. " (code (var "msg")) " must be a string and should describe the exceptional situation. The irritants may be any Scheme objects and should include values that may have caused or been materially involved in the exceptional situation.")) ("exceptions:s5" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(assert " (var "expression") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs base)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "assert") " evaluates " (code (var "expression")) " and returns the value of " (code (var "expression")) " if the value is not " (code "#f") ". If the value of " (code (var "expression")) " is " (code "#f") ", " (code "assert") " raises a non-continuable exception with condition types " (code "&assertion") " and " (code "&message") ", with an implementation-dependent value in its message field. Implementations are encouraged to provide information about the location of the " (code "assert") " call within the condition whenever possible.")) ("exceptions:s6" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax-violation " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "form") ")") (br) (bold "procedure") ": " (code "(syntax-violation " (var "who") " " (var "msg") " " (var "form") " " (var "subform") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "does not return" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs syntax-case)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("This procedure raises a non-continuable exception with a condition of type " (code "&syntax") ". It should be used to report a syntax error detected by the transformer of a syntactic extension. The value of the condition's form field is " (code (var "form")) ", and the value of its subform field is " (code (var "subform")) ", or " (code "#f") " if " (code (var "subform")) " is not provided.") ("The continuation object with which the exception is raised also includes a " (code "&who") " condition whose who field is " (code (var "who")) ", if " (code (var "who")) " is not " (code "#f") " or is inferred from " (code (var "form")) ", and a " (code "&message") "  condition whose message field is " (code (var "msg")) ".") ((code (var "who")) " must be a string, a symbol, or " (code "#f") ". If " (code (var "who")) " is " (code "#f") ", it is inferred to be the symbolic name of " (code (var "form")) "  if " (code (var "form")) " is an identifier or the symbolic name of the first subform of " (code (var "form")) " if " (code (var "form")) " is a list-structured form whose first subform is an identifier. " (code (var "message")) " must be a string. " (code (var "form")) " should be the syntax object or datum representation of the syntactic form within which the syntax violation occurred, and " (code (var "subform")) ", if not " (code "#f") ", should be a syntax object or datum representation of a subform more specifically involved in the violation. For example, if a duplicate formal parameter is found in a " (code "lambda") " expression, " (code (var "form")) " might be the " (code "lambda") " expression and " (code (var "subform")) " might be the duplicated parameter.") ("Some implementations attach source information to syntax objects, e.g., line, character, and filename for forms originating in a file, in which case this information might also be present as some implementation-dependent condition type within the condition object.")) ("exceptions:s7" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(with-exception-handler " (var "procedure") " " (var "thunk") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs exceptions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("This procedure establishes " (code (var "procedure")) ", which should accept one argument, as the current exception handler in place of the old current exception handler, " (code (var "old-proc")) ", and invokes " (code (var "thunk")) " without arguments. If the call to " (code (var "thunk")) " returns, " (code (var "old-proc")) " is reestablished as the current exception handler and the values returned by " (code (var "thunk")) " are returned. If control leaves or subsequently reenters the call to " (code (var "thunk")) " via the invocation of a continuation obtained via " (code "call/cc") ", the procedure that was the current exception handler when the continuation was captured is reinstated.") ((code "(define (try thunk)" (br) "  (call/cc" (br) "    (lambda (k)" (br) "      (with-exception-handler" (br) "        (lambda (x) (if (error? x) (k #f) (raise x)))" (br) "        thunk))))" (br) "(try (lambda () 17)) " (math "=>") " 17" (br) "(try (lambda () (raise (make-error)))) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(try (lambda () (raise (make-violation)))) " (math "=>") " " (var "violation") (br) "(with-exception-handler" (br) "  (lambda (x)" (br) "    (raise" (br) "      (apply condition" (br) "        (make-message-condition \"oops\")" (br) "        (simple-conditions x))))" (br) "  (lambda ()" (br) "    (try (lambda () (raise (make-violation)))))) " (math "=>") " " (var "violation: oops")))) ("exceptions:s8" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(guard (" (var "var") " " (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...) " (var "b1") " " (var "b2") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "see below" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs exceptions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("A " (code "guard") " expression establishes a new current exception handler, " (code (var "procedure")) " (described below), in place of the old current exception handler, " (code (var "old-proc")) ", and evaluates the body " (code (var "b1") " " (var "b2") " ...") ". If the body returns, " (code "guard") " reestablishes " (code (var "old-proc")) " as the current exception handler. If control leaves or subsequently reenters the body via the invocation of a continuation obtained via " (code "call/cc") ", the procedure that was the current exception handler when the continuation was captured is reinstated.") ("The procedure " (code (var "procedure")) " established by " (code "guard") " binds " (code (var "var")) "  to the value it receives and, within the scope of that binding, processes the clauses " (code (var "clause" (sub "1")) " " (var "clause" (sub "2")) " ...") " in turn, as if contained within an implicit " (code "cond") " expression. This implicit " (code "cond") " expression is evaluated in the continuation of the " (code "guard") " expression, with " (code (var "old-proc")) " as the current exception handler.") ("If no " (code "else") " clause is provided, " (code "guard") " supplies one that reraises the exception with the same value, as if with " (code "raise-continuable") ", in the continuation of the call to " (code (var "procedure")) ", with " (code (var "old-proc")) " as the current exception handler.") ((code "(guard (x [else x]) (raise \"oops\")) " (math "=>") " \"oops\"" (br) "(guard (x [#f #f]) (raise (make-error))) " (math "=>") "  " (var "error") (br) "(define-syntax try" (br) "  (syntax-rules ()" (br) "    [(_ e1 e2 ...)" (br) "     (guard (x [(error? x) #f]) e1 e2 ...)]))" (br) "(define open-one" (br) "  (lambda fn*" (br) "    (let loop ([ls fn*])" (br) "      (if (null? ls)" (br) "          (error 'open-one \"all open attempts failed\" fn*)" (br) "          (or (try (open-input-file (car ls)))" (br) "              (loop (cdr ls)))))))" (br) "; say bar.ss exists but not foo.ss:" (br) "(open-one \"foo.ss\" \"bar.ss\") " (math "=>") " #<input port bar.ss>"))) ("exceptions:s13" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "&condition") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs conditions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "&condition") " is a record-type name (Chapter " (link "./records.html#g138" "9") ") and the root of the condition-type hierarchy. All simple condition types are extensions of this type, and all conditions, whether simple or compound, are considered instances of this type.")) ("exceptions:s14" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition? " (var "obj") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") (code "#t") " if " (code (var "obj")) " is a condition object, otherwise " (code "#f") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs conditions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("A condition object is an instance of a subtype of " (code "&condition") "  or a compound condition, possibly created by user code with " (code "condition") ".") ((code "(condition? 'stable) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(condition? (make-error)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(condition? (make-message-condition \"oops\")) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(condition?" (br) "  (condition" (br) "    (make-error)" (br) "    (make-message-condition \"no such element\"))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("exceptions:s15" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(condition " (var "condition") " ...)") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a condition, possibly compound" (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs conditions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "condition") " is used to create condition objects that may consist of multiple simple conditions. Each argument " (code (var "condition")) " may be simple or complex; if simple, it is treated as a compound condition with itself as its only simple condition. The simple conditions of the result condition are the simple conditions of the " (code (var "condition")) " arguments, flattened into a single list and appearing in order, with the simple conditions of the first " (code (var "condition")) " followed by the simple conditions of the second, and so on.") ("If the list has exactly one element, the result condition may be simple or compound; otherwise it is compound. The distinction between simple and compound conditions is not usually important but can be detected, if " (code "define-record-type") " rather than " (code "define-condition-type") " is used to extend an existing condition type, via the predicate defined by " (code "define-record-type") ".") ((code "(condition) " (math "=>") " #<condition>" (br) "(condition" (br) "  (make-error)" (br) "  (make-message-condition \"oops\")) " (math "=>") " #<condition>" (br) (br) "(define-record-type (&xcond make-xcond xcond?) (parent &condition))" (br) "(xcond? (make-xcond)) " (math "=>") " #t" (br) "(xcond? (condition (make-xcond))) " (math "=>") " #t " (var "or") " #f" (br) "(xcond? (condition)) " (math "=>") " #f" (br) "(xcond? (condition (make-error) (make-xcond))) " (math "=>") " #f"))) ("exceptions:s16" ((bold "procedure") ": " (code "(simple-conditions " (var "condition") ")") (br) (bold "returns: ") "a list of the simple conditions of " (code (var "condition")) (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs conditions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ((code "(simple-conditions (condition)) " (math "=>") " '()" (br) "(simple-conditions (make-error)) " (math "=>") " (#<condition &error>)" (br) "(simple-conditions (condition (make-error))) " (math "=>") " (#<condition &error>)" (br) "(simple-conditions" (br) "  (condition" (br) "    (make-error)" (br) "    (make-message-condition" (br) "      \"oops\"))) " (math "=>") " (#<condition &error> #<condition &message>)" (br) (br) "(let ([c1 (make-error)]" (br) "      [c2 (make-who-condition \"f\")]" (br) "      [c3 (make-message-condition \"invalid argument\")]" (br) "      [c4 (make-message-condition" (br) "            \"error occurred while reading from file\")]" (br) "      [c5 (make-irritants-condition '(\"a.ss\"))])" (br) "  (equal?" (br) "    (simple-conditions" (br) "      (condition" (br) "        (condition (condition c1 c2) c3)" (br) "        (condition c4 (condition c5))))" (br) "    (list c1 c2 c3 c4 c5))) " (math "=>") " #t"))) ("exceptions:s17" ((bold "syntax") ": " (code "(define-condition-type " (var "name") " " (var "parent") " " (var "constructor") " " (var "pred") " " (var "field") " ...)") (br) (bold "libraries: ") (code "(rnrs conditions)") ", " (code "(rnrs)")) ("A " (code "define-condition-type") " form is a definition and may appear anywhere other definitions may appear. It is used to define new simple condition types.") ("The subforms " (code (var "name")) ", " (code (var "parent")) ", " (code (var "constructor")) ", and " (code (var "pred")) "  must be identifiers. Each " (code (var "field")) " must be of the form " (code "(" (var "field-name") " " (var "accessor-name") ")") ", where " (code (var "field-name")) " and " (code (var "accessor-name")) " are identifiers.") ((code "define-condition-type") " defines " (code (var "name")) " as a new record type whose parent record type is " (code (var "parent")) ", whose constructor name is " (code (var "constructor")) ", whose predicate name is " (code (var "pred")) ", whose fields are " (code (var "field-name") " ...") ", and whose field accessors are named by " (code (var "accessor-name") " ...") ".") ("With the exception of the predicate and field accessors, " (code "define-condition-type") " is essentially an ordinary record definition equivalent to") ((code "(define-record-type (" (var "name") " " (var "constructor") " " (var "pred") ")" (br) "  (parent " (var "parent") ")" (br) "  (fields ((immutable " (var "field-name") " " (var "accessor-name") ") ...)))")) ("The predicate differs from one that would be generated by a " (code "define-record-type") " form in that it returns " (code "#t") " not only for an instance of the new type but also for compound conditions whose simple conditions include an instance of the new type. Similarly, field accessors accept instances of the new type as well as compound conditions whose simple conditions include at least one instance of the new record type. If an accessor receives a compound condition whose simple conditions list includes one or more instances of the new type, the accessor operates on the first instance in the list.") ((code "(define-condition-type &mistake &condition make-mistake mistake?" (br) "  (type mistake-type))" (br) (br) "(mistake? 'booboo) " (math "